window.LESSONS = [{"id":1,"title":"Explain the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) in Python?","body":"**What is the GIL?**\nThe GIL is a mutex (mutual exclusion lock) that prevents multiple native threads from executing Python bytecodes simultaneously.\nThe primary purpose of the GIL is to simplify memory management in `CPython` by ensuring only one thread can execute Python bytecode at any given time.\nThis prevents issues like race conditions in memory management, which can lead to unexpected results or crashes.\n\n1. **Why Does Python Have a GIL?**\nPython’s memory management system is not thread-safe by default. Managing memory with thread safety would require a more complex approach,\nsuch as locking every object during access, which can be slow and difficult to maintain.\nThe GIL allows `CPython` to use a simpler memory model with reference counting. With a single lock, `CPython` can keep track of the references\nto each object and manage memory deallocation efficiently.\n2. **How the GIL Works?**\nWhen a Python program starts, a single instance of the GIL is created.\nWhen a thread in a Python program wants to run, it must acquire the GIL. Once it has the GIL, it can execute Python bytecode.\nAfter a certain period or when an I/O operation occurs (like file reading, network calls, etc.), the GIL is released, and another thread can acquire it.\nPython’s interpreter switches the GIL between threads frequently. However, this switch can lead to performance bottlenecks, especially in CPU-bound tasks.\n3. **Impact of the GIL on Multi-Threading**\nCPU-bound Programs: In programs that are CPU-bound (where threads are busy with computations), the GIL can severely limit the performance gains\nfrom multi-threading. Even if there are multiple threads, they have to wait for the GIL to be released, meaning that only one thread can execute at any time. This is why multi-threaded Python programs may not see performance improvements on multi-core processors when doing CPU-intensive tasks.\nI/O-bound Programs: Programs that are I/O-bound (waiting for input/output operations) tend to perform better with the GIL because threads\nspend much of their time waiting. During this wait time, the GIL can be released and handed over to another thread, allowing I/O-bound programs\nto benefit more from Python’s threading model.\n4. **Workarounds and Alternatives to the GIL**\nSince the GIL is specific to `CPython`, there are several methods and strategies to mitigate its limitations:\n\nMultiprocessing: ****The multiprocessing module creates separate processes instead of threads. Each process has its own Python interpreter and memory space,\neffectively bypassing the GIL. Multiprocessing is ideal for CPU-bound tasks, as it can utilize multiple cores.\nUsing C Extensions: Some C-based libraries release the GIL while performing time-consuming tasks, allowing other threads to run in parallel.\nFor example, libraries like `NumPy` can operate outside the GIL for certain heavy operations.\n\nAlternative Python Implementations: Some Python implementations don’t have a GIL. For instance:\nJython: Python implemented on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).\nIronPython: Python for the .NET framework.\nPyPy STM (Software Transactional Memory): An experimental version of `PyPy` that avoids the GIL.\n\n1. **Why Not Remove the GIL Entirely?**\nRemoving the GIL is theoretically possible, but it would require a complete overhaul of `CPython’s` memory management system.\nThe absence of the GIL could slow down single-threaded programs due to the additional overhead of locking mechanisms that would be necessary for thread safety.\nPython’s design philosophy emphasizes simplicity and readability, and the GIL simplifies Python’s internals, making it easier to maintain.\n2. **Future of the GIL**\nEfforts have been made over the years to either remove the GIL or reduce its impact, and there are ongoing discussions in the Python community about it.\nFor example, recent versions of Python (starting with 3.2) introduced “per-interpreter GIL” support, where each Python interpreter in a process\ncan have its own GIL. This allows certain use cases to improve multi-core usage but requires significant changes to Python codebases.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe GIL is a central feature of Python’s `CPython` interpreter, enabling easier memory management but limiting multi-threaded CPU-bound performance.\nWhile there are alternative approaches to work around the GIL for specific use cases, it remains an important feature that influences how concurrency\nis handled in Python programs. For CPU-bound applications requiring high concurrency, Python’s multiprocessing or moving to alternative\n\n**I/O-bound tasks (like reading files, making network requests, or waiting for a database response):** Multithreading can be helpful here.\nThreads can perform tasks while waiting for I/O to complete. During these waits, the GIL can be released, allowing other threads to run.\nThis approach improves responsiveness and can speed up I/O-bound programs.\n\n**CPU-bound tasks (like complex calculations or data processing):** Multithreading is less effective because the GIL allows only one thread to execute Python code\nat a time, so CPU-bound tasks don’t see significant speed improvements with threads. Here, multiprocessing is preferred because each process has its own GIL\nand can run on a separate core.\n\n**In Summary:**\nMultithreading is useful in Python for I/O-bound tasks, but for CPU-bound tasks, multiprocessing or other approaches are better.\n\nCPU-bound Task with Threads:\n\n```python\nimport threading\nimport time\n\n# Function for CPU-bound task\ndef cpu_bound_task(n):\n    total = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        total += i * i\n    return total\n\n# Wrapping the task in a thread\ndef thread_task(n):\n    print(f\"Thread starting for {n}\")\n    result = cpu_bound_task(n)\n    print(f\"Result for {n}: {result}\")\n\nn = 10**6  # Large number for CPU-intensive computation\n\n# Single-threaded execution\nstart_time = time.time()\ncpu_bound_task(n)\ncpu_bound_task(n)\nend_time = time.time()\nprint(\"Single-threaded time:\", end_time - start_time)\n\n# Multi-threaded execution (2 threads)\nstart_time = time.time()\nt1 = threading.Thread(target=cpu_bound_task, args=(n,))\nt2 = threading.Thread(target=cpu_bound_task, args=(n,))\nt1.start()\nt2.start()\nt1.join()\nt2.join()\nend_time = time.time()\nprint(\"Multi-threaded time:\", end_time - start_time)\n\nOutput:\n\nSingle-threaded time: 0.45 seconds  # approximate time\n\nThread starting for 1000000\nThread starting for 1000000\nResult for 1000000: (some large number)\nResult for 1000000: (some large number)\nMulti-threaded time: 0.47 seconds  # nearly the same as single-threaded time\n\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n- The single-threaded and multi-threaded times are nearly the same because the GIL prevents both threads from executing CPU-bound code simultaneously.\n- Multi-threading doesn’t provide any speedup here, showing how the GIL restricts parallelism in CPU-bound tasks.\n\nI/O-bound Task with Threads:\n\n```python\n# Function for I/O-bound task\ndef io_bound_task(duration):\n    print(f\"Sleeping for {duration} seconds\")\n    time.sleep(duration)\n    print(f\"Finished sleeping for {duration} seconds\")\n\n# Single-threaded execution\nstart_time = time.time()\nio_bound_task(2)\nio_bound_task(2)\nend_time = time.time()\nprint(\"Single-threaded time:\", end_time - start_time)\n\n# Multi-threaded execution (2 threads)\nstart_time = time.time()\nt1 = threading.Thread(target=io_bound_task, args=(2,))\nt2 = threading.Thread(target=io_bound_task, args=(2,))\nt1.start()\nt2.start()\nt1.join()\nt2.join()\nend_time = time.time()\nprint(\"Multi-threaded time:\", end_time - start_time)\n\nOutput:\n\nSleeping for 2 seconds\nFinished sleeping for 2 seconds\nSleeping for 2 seconds\nFinished sleeping for 2 seconds\nSingle-threaded time: 4.0 seconds  # sequential sleep\n\nSleeping for 2 seconds\nSleeping for 2 seconds\nFinished sleeping for 2 seconds\nFinished sleeping for 2 seconds\nMulti-threaded time: 2.0 seconds  # concurrent sleep\n\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n- In the single-threaded execution, each `time.sleep(2)` runs one after another, taking a total of around 4 seconds.\n- In the multi-threaded execution, both threads sleep concurrently, reducing the total time to approximately 2 seconds.\n- This demonstrates that the GIL is less restrictive for I/O-bound tasks, as it is released during `time.sleep`, allowing threads to run in parallel.\n\nCPU-bound Task with Multiprocessing:\n\n```python\nimport multiprocessing\nimport time\n\n# Function for CPU-bound task\ndef cpu_bound_task(n):\n    total = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        total += i * i\n    return total\n\n# Function to execute in a process\ndef process_task(n):\n    print(f\"Process starting for {n}\")\n    result = cpu_bound_task(n)\n    print(f\"Result for {n}: {result}\")\n\nn = 10**6  # Large number for CPU-intensive computation\n\n# Single-process execution\nstart_time = time.time()\ncpu_bound_task(n)\ncpu_bound_task(n)\nend_time = time.time()\nprint(\"Single-process time:\", end_time - start_time)\n\n# Multi-processing execution (2 processes)\nstart_time = time.time()\np1 = multiprocessing.Process(target=cpu_bound_task, args=(n,))\np2 = multiprocessing.Process(target=cpu_bound_task, args=(n,))\np1.start()\np2.start()\np1.join()\np2.join()\nend_time = time.time()\nprint(\"Multi-processing time:\", end_time - start_time)\n\nOutput:\n\nSingle-process time: 0.45 seconds  # approximate time\n\nProcess starting for 1000000\nProcess starting for 1000000\nResult for 1000000: (some large number)\nResult for 1000000: (some large number)\nMulti-processing time: 0.23 seconds  # approximately half of single-process time\n\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n- In the single-process execution, each `cpu_bound_task` runs sequentially, taking about 0.45 seconds.\n- In the multi-processing execution, each task is run in a separate process, achieving parallel execution and roughly halving the time.\n- This demonstrates that multiprocessing can bypass the GIL to achieve parallelism for CPU-bound tasks by leveraging separate processes.\n\n### Summary:\n\nThese examples illustrate:\n\n- **CPU-bound tasks**: Threads provide no performance gain due to the GIL, but using multiple processes enables parallelism.\n- **I/O-bound tasks**: Threads can improve performance as they release the GIL during I/O operations, allowing concurrent execution.\n\n### The GIL and I/O-bound Tasks\n\nWhile it's true that only one thread can hold the GIL and execute Python bytecode at any given time, **the GIL is released temporarily during certain operations**, particularly I/O operations. Here’s how it works:\n\n1. **For CPU-bound tasks** (where threads are primarily doing calculations or other CPU-intensive work), the GIL is a limiting factor. Only one thread can execute Python bytecode at any moment, and thus only one thread can be actively working on CPU-bound code within a single process, regardless of the number of cores.\n2. **For I/O-bound tasks** (like waiting for network responses, reading/writing files, or sleeping), threads often need to wait for external resources. During these waiting periods, the thread holding the GIL temporarily releases it, allowing other threads to take over and execute.\n\n### Why the GIL is Released in I/O-bound Tasks\n\nThe reason the GIL is released during I/O operations is that, while waiting for I/O, a thread isn’t doing CPU work. Python's interpreter allows another thread to acquire the GIL and execute code during this wait time. This leads to **concurrent execution of multiple threads during I/O operations**, as each thread alternates between executing code and waiting for I/O resources.\n\nSo, while technically only one thread at a time holds the GIL to execute bytecode, Python can effectively handle multiple threads in I/O-bound tasks by releasing the GIL during I/O waits, allowing other threads to run during those pauses.\n\n### Example in Practice:\n\nIn an I/O-bound task (like network requests or file reads), threads can keep switching control whenever one of them is waiting on I/O. This is why our example of multi-threaded `time.sleep` saw a speedup: `time.sleep()` causes the thread to release the GIL, allowing the other thread to execute concurrently, thus reducing overall wait time.\n\nThis distinction is crucial:\n\n- **CPU-bound tasks are limited by the GIL.**\n- **I/O-bound tasks benefit from the GIL being released during I/O operations, enabling threads to run concurrently even in the presence of the GIL.**\n\nThis is why threading can still be useful in I/O-bound Python programs despite the GIL’s presence.","category":"Concurrency","label":"The Global Interpreter Lock","examples":[{"title":"Compute safely with a thread pool","code":"from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef square(n):\n    return n * n\n\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=2) as pool:\n    print(list(pool.map(square, [2, 3, 4])))","output":"[4, 9, 16]","explanation":["Two workers may be scheduled, but this tiny pure-Python computation is not evidence of CPU parallelism on a conventional GIL-enabled build. map preserves input order in its returned results.","Thread scheduling and elapsed time vary by machine, so the example verifies results rather than promising a speedup. Threads are often more useful when tasks spend time waiting on I/O."]},{"title":"Protect a shared update with a lock","code":"from threading import Thread, Lock\n\ncount = 0\nlock = Lock()\ndef work():\n    global count\n    for _ in range(1000):\n        with lock:\n            count += 1\n\nthreads = [Thread(target=work) for _ in range(2)]\nfor thread in threads:\n    thread.start()\nfor thread in threads:\n    thread.join()\nprint(count)","output":"2000","explanation":["Each update is performed while holding the same lock, and join waits for both workers before printing the result.","The GIL is not a substitute for protecting application-level shared invariants. Explicit synchronization also makes the intended contract clear across Python builds."]},{"title":"Use separate processes for independent CPU work","code":"from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\n\ndef square(n):\n    return n * n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=2) as pool:\n        print(list(pool.map(square, [2, 3, 4])))","output":"[4, 9, 16]","explanation":["Save this example as a .py file and run it. Worker processes have separate interpreter state, so CPU work can use different cores without sharing one interpreter’s GIL.","The main guard avoids recursively starting workers on spawn-based systems such as Windows. Startup and data-transfer costs mean this tiny example is illustrative, not a performance benchmark."]}],"summary":"In conventional GIL-enabled CPython, one thread per interpreter runs Python bytecode at a time. Threads can overlap waiting; processes can run CPU work separately. Free-threaded builds change this model.","minutes":10},{"id":2,"title":"How does Python handle memory management?","body":"1. **Automatic Memory Management**\nPython uses automatic memory management, which means it takes care of allocating and freeing memory without requiring the programmer to do it manually.\nThis makes Python easier to use compared to languages like C or C++, where you must manage memory directly.\n2. **Reference Counting**\nPython uses reference counting to keep track of the number of references to each object in memory. Each object has a reference count that increases when a new reference to it is created and decreases when a reference is deleted. When an object’s reference count reaches zero (meaning no references to it exist), Python automatically deallocates the object and frees the memory.\n\n```python\nx = [1, 2, 3]  # Reference count for the list increases\ny = x  # Reference count increases again\ndel x  # Reference count decreases\ndel y  # Reference count reaches zero, memory is freed\n```\n\n1. **Garbage Collection (GC)**\nPython has a garbage collector to handle cases where reference counting alone can’t free memory. Circular references (when two objects refer to each other) cannot be cleaned up by reference counting, so Python’s garbage collector identifies and removes these cycles. The garbage collector is part of Python’s memory management system and runs automatically but can also be controlled with the `gc` module.\n2. **Memory Pooling**\nPython uses memory pooling to reduce the overhead of frequent memory allocation and deallocation, especially for small objects.\nThe memory allocator manages small objects more efficiently by allocating memory in larger chunks and reusing freed memory for new objects.\nFor example, integers and short strings are often stored in pools so they can be reused, which improves performance.\n3. **Object-Specific Memory Allocation**\nDifferent types of objects have their own memory allocation strategies to optimize performance.\nFor instance, Python uses a separate memory pool for small integers (often between -5 and 256) because they are frequently used.\nThese integers are interned (stored once and reused), reducing memory usage.\n4. **Memory Management Modules**\nPython provides modules like **`gc`** and **`sys`** to help control and monitor memory usage. The **`gc`** module allows you to interact with the garbage collector, such as enabling or disabling it, forcing a collection, and checking for unreachable objects. The **`sys`** module offers functions to monitor memory usage, like **`sys.getrefcount()`** to check an object’s reference count.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nx = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(sys.getrefcount(x))  # Shows the reference count for x\n```\n\n- In Python, you can check an object's reference count using the `sys.getrefcount()` function from the `sys` module. This function returns the number of references to the specified object.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\n# Create an object\nx = [1, 2, 3]\n\n# Check the reference count for the object\nprint(sys.getrefcount(x))  # Output will show the reference count of `x`\n\noutput: 2\n```\n\nThe output is 2 instead of 1 because when you call `sys.getrefcount(x)`, Python temporarily creates an additional reference to x in order to pass it as an argument to the `getrefcount` function. Here’s a breakdown:\n\n**Original Reference:** The variable x itself holds one reference to the list [1, 2, 3].\nTemporary Reference: When you call `sys.getrefcount(x)`, Python internally creates a temporary reference to x to pass it as an argument to the function. This additional reference is why `sys.getrefcount(x)` returns 2 instead of 1.\n\n**Summary**:\nThe \"temporary reference\" created when calling `sys.getrefcount()` explains why the count is always one higher than expected. This reference is created just for the duration of the function call and removed immediately afterward.","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Memory management","examples":[{"title":"Separate names from the object they reference","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Item:\n    pass\n\na = Item()\nb = a\nref = weakref.ref(a)\ndel a\nprint(ref() is b)\ndel b\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["a and b initially point to the same instance. Deleting a removes one binding, but b still keeps the object alive.","After deleting b and collecting garbage, the weak reference shows that the object is gone. The weak reference itself does not own the instance."]},{"title":"Collect an unreachable cycle","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Node:\n    pass\n\nnode = Node()\nnode.link = node\nref = weakref.ref(node)\ndel node\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True","explanation":["The node references itself, so its reference count alone cannot fall to zero when the external name is removed.","gc.collect asks the cyclic collector to examine unreachable tracked objects. It can reclaim this isolated self-cycle; the weak reference then returns None."]},{"title":"Release temporary references deliberately","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Record:\n    pass\n\nrecord = Record()\nref = weakref.ref(record)\ncache = [record]\ndel record\nprint(ref() is not None)\ncache.clear()\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["Containers also hold references. The list keeps the instance alive even after its original variable disappears.","Clearing the list removes that ownership. This demonstrates why a growing cache can retain memory even when the objects are no longer needed elsewhere."]}],"summary":"Variables refer to objects. CPython uses reference counting plus a cycle collector to reclaim unreachable objects; resource cleanup should still be explicit.","minutes":4},{"id":3,"title":"What are generators in Python, and how do they work?","body":"### What is a Generator?\n\nA generator in Python is a special type of function that can be paused and resumed. It generates values one at a time instead of all at once, which helps save memory.\n\n### How to Create a Generator\n\nGenerators are created using a function with the `yield` keyword instead of `return`.\n\n### Example 1: Basic Generator Function\n\n```python\ndef simple_generator():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\n    yield 3\n\n# Using the generator\ngen = simple_generator()\n\n# Getting values one by one\nprint(next(gen))  # Output: 1\nprint(next(gen))  # Output: 2\nprint(next(gen))  # Output: 3\n\nOutput:\n1\n2\n3\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- Each time `next(gen)` is called, the function runs until it hits `yield`, then pauses and returns that value.\n- When it’s called again, it resumes right after the last `yield`.\n\n### Example 2: Using a Loop with a Generator\n\n```python\ndef simple_generator():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\n    yield 3\n\n# Using the generator with a for loop\nfor value in simple_generator():\n    print(value)\n\nOutput:\n1\n2\n3\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- The `for` loop automatically calls `next()` on the generator until there are no more values to yield.\n- When the generator has no more values, it stops automatically.\n\n### Example 3: Generator for a Range of Numbers\n\n```python\ndef number_generator(limit):\n    n = 1\n    while n <= limit:\n        yield n\n        n += 1\n\n# Creating a generator to yield numbers from 1 to 5\ngen = number_generator(5)\n\nfor num in gen:\n    print(num)\n\nOutput:\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- The generator starts from `1` and keeps yielding numbers until it reaches the specified `limit`.\n- Each time it hits `yield`, it pauses, saving the current value of `n`, and then resumes from where it left off in the next iteration.\n\n### Example 4: Infinite Generator (only print a few values)\n\n```python\ndef infinite_counter():\n    n = 1\n    while True:\n        yield n\n        n += 1\n\n# Create an infinite generator\ngen = infinite_counter()\n\n# Print the first 5 values only\nfor _ in range(5):\n    print(next(gen))\n    \n\nOutput:\n\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- `infinite_counter` will keep counting up forever because of the `while True` loop.\n- We only print the first 5 values here to avoid an infinite loop.\n\n### Why Use Generators?\n\nGenerators are useful because:\n\n1. **They save memory**: Values are generated one at a time instead of all at once.\n2. **They’re efficient for large data**: You can process data as it comes without waiting for everything to load.\n\n### Summary\n\n- **Generators** use `yield` instead of `return`.\n- **Each call** to `next()` on a generator retrieves the next value.\n- **They’re memory efficient** and ideal for working with large or infinite sequences.","category":"Iteration","label":"Generators","examples":[{"title":"Pause and resume at yield","code":"def steps():\n    print(\"start\")\n    yield 10\n    print(\"resume\")\n    yield 20\n\ng = steps()\nprint(\"created\")\nprint(next(g))\nprint(next(g))","output":"created\nstart\n10\nresume\n20","explanation":["Calling steps creates a generator without executing its body. The first next starts execution, prints start, and pauses at the first yield.","The second next continues after that yield, prints resume, and yields 20. Local state and execution position survive the pause."]},{"title":"Consume the remaining values only once","code":"g = (n * n for n in range(4))\nprint(next(g))\nprint(list(g))\nprint(list(g))","output":"0\n[1, 4, 9]\n[]","explanation":["next consumes the first item. list then drains only the remaining items, so 0 does not appear in the second result.","A second list call finds an exhausted iterator. Recreate the generator expression when another independent pass is required."]},{"title":"Process a stream without a result list","code":"def running_total(values):\n    total = 0\n    for value in values:\n        total += value\n        yield total\n\nfor total in running_total([2, 3, 4]):\n    print(total)","output":"2\n5\n9","explanation":["The generator retains total between iterations, yielding each intermediate accumulation as soon as it is available.","It does not build a separate list of running totals. This makes generators useful for incremental pipelines, though retaining yielded values elsewhere can still consume memory."]}],"summary":"A generator produces values one at a time and remembers where it paused. Once consumed, it does not restart automatically.","minutes":4},{"id":4,"title":"Explain decorators and how they work in Python?","body":"Decorators in Python are functions that modify the behavior of another function or class. They allow you to \"wrap\" another function to extend its behavior without modifying its actual code. Decorators are widely used for tasks like logging, access control, and timing.\n\n### Key Points About Decorators\n\n1. **Decorators are just functions** that take another function as an argument, add some functionality, and return a new function.\n2. They are commonly used with the `@decorator_name` syntax, placed above the function you want to decorate.\n\nLet’s walk through decorators with simple examples.\n\n### Basic Example of a Decorator\n\nSuppose we have a function that says \"Hello\". We’ll create a decorator that adds a line saying \"Starting function\" before saying \"Hello\".\n\n```python\n# Step 1: Define a basic decorator\ndef my_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        print(\"Starting function\")\n        func()  # call the original function\n        print(\"Function ended\")\n    return wrapper\n\n# Step 2: Apply the decorator to a function\n@my_decorator\ndef say_hello():\n    print(\"Hello!\")\n\n# Call the decorated function\nsay_hello()\n\nOutput:\n\nStarting function\nHello!\nFunction ended\n\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n- **`my_decorator`** takes a function (`func`) as input.\n- Inside `my_decorator`, we define a `wrapper` function that adds extra functionality (printing \"Starting function\" and \"Function ended\").\n- **`@my_decorator`** before `say_hello` is the same as writing `say_hello = my_decorator(say_hello)`.\n- When we call `say_hello()`, the `wrapper` function runs, adding the extra print statements before and after calling the original function.\n\n### Example 2: Decorator with Arguments\n\nDecorators can also be used with functions that have arguments.\n\n```python\ndef my_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper(name):\n        print(\"Starting function\")\n        func(name)  # call the original function with arguments\n        print(\"Function ended\")\n    return wrapper\n\n@my_decorator\ndef greet(name):\n    print(f\"Hello, {name}!\")\n\n# Call the decorated function with an argument\ngreet(\"Alice\")\n\nOutput:\n\nStarting function\nHello, Alice!\nFunction ended\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- Here, `greet` is a function that takes a `name` argument.\n- The decorator’s `wrapper` function also takes `name` as an argument to pass it to `func`.\n- When `greet(\"Alice\")` is called, `wrapper` runs, adding print statements around the original `greet` function.\n\n### Example 3: Using Multiple Decorators\n\nYou can stack multiple decorators on a single function. Each decorator will be applied from top to bottom.\n\n```python\ndef bold_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        return f\"<b>{func()}</b>\"\n    return wrapper\n\ndef italic_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        return f\"<i>{func()}</i>\"\n    return wrapper\n\n@bold_decorator\n@italic_decorator\ndef say_text():\n    return \"Hello, world!\"\n\nprint(say_text())\n\nOutput:\n<b><i>Hello, world!</i></b>\n\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- **`italic_decorator`** wraps the text with `<i>` tags, and **`bold_decorator`** wraps it with `<b>` tags.\n- Because of the stacking order, `say_text()` first becomes italic and then bold.\n- The result is a bold and italicized \"Hello, world!\" text.\n\n### Example 4: Decorator with `functools.wraps`\n\nUsing `functools.wraps` helps to preserve the original function’s metadata (like its name and docstring) when it’s wrapped by a decorator.\n\n```python\nfrom functools import wraps\n\ndef my_decorator(func):\n    @wraps(func)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        \"\"\"Wrapper function\"\"\"\n        print(\"Starting function\")\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n        print(\"Function ended\")\n        return result\n    return wrapper\n\n@my_decorator\ndef greet(name):\n    \"\"\"Greets a person by name\"\"\"\n    print(f\"Hello, {name}!\")\n\n# Check function metadata\nprint(greet.__name__)   # Output: greet\nprint(greet.__doc__)    # Output: Greets a person by name\n\n```\n\n> \n> \n> \n> ### What Happens if Keyword Arguments Come First?\n> \n> If you try to call a function with a **keyword argument first**, followed by a **positional argument**, like this:\n> \n> ```python\n> \n> example_function(param1={\"key\": \"value\"}, 10)\n> \n> ```\n> \n> Python will raise a **`SyntaxError`**:\n> \n> ```jsx\n> \n> SyntaxError: positional argument follows keyword argument\n> \n> ```\n> \n> This is because **keyword arguments must follow positional arguments** when calling a function.\n> \n> ### Conclusion:\n> \n> - **`args` cannot capture keyword arguments**. They are captured by `*kwargs`.\n> - **Keyword arguments must always follow positional arguments** when calling a function.\n> - Therefore, it's impossible to pass a keyword argument first and then a positional argument—Python enforces this rule to avoid ambiguity.\n> \n> In summary, if someone tries to pass a **keyword argument** first and then a **positional argument**, Python will raise an error, and `*args` and `**kwargs` cannot handle that scenario because it violates Python's argument passing rules.\n> \n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- Without `@wraps`, the decorated function would have the name and docstring of `wrapper`, not `greet`.\n- **`@wraps(func)`** copies the original function’s metadata to the wrapper function.\n\n### Summary\n\n- **Decorators** modify a function’s behavior without changing its code.\n- **Basic syntax**: Define a decorator function and apply it with `@decorator_name`.\n- **Flexible**: Can handle functions with arguments, return values, and multiple decorators.\n- **Helpful tools**: Use `functools.wraps` to keep the original function’s metadata.\n\n**Extra code example:**\n\n```python\ndef logger(func):\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        print(f\"Executing {func.__name__} with arguments {args} {kwargs}\")\n        return func(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\n@logger\ndef add(x, y):\n    return x + y\n\n```","category":"Functions","label":"Decorators","examples":[{"title":"Add behavior before and after a call","code":"def announce(fn):\n    def wrapper():\n        print(\"Before\")\n        result = fn()\n        print(\"After\")\n        return result\n    return wrapper\n\n@announce\ndef greet():\n    return \"Hello\"\n\nprint(greet())","output":"Before\nAfter\nHello","explanation":["@announce replaces greet with announce(greet), so calling greet enters wrapper first. The original function is still reachable through the enclosing fn binding.","The wrapper saves and returns the original result. Its own messages print before the outer print receives and displays Hello."]},{"title":"Forward positional and keyword arguments","code":"from functools import wraps\n\ndef double_result(fn):\n    @wraps(fn)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        return 2 * fn(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\n@double_result\ndef add(a, b=1):\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add(3, b=4))\nprint(add.__name__)","output":"14\nadd","explanation":["*args and **kwargs preserve flexible calling conventions: 3 is positional and b=4 is forwarded by name. The original sum is 7 and the wrapper doubles it.","wraps preserves metadata such as the original function name; it does not perform the doubling or enforce argument types."]},{"title":"Understand stacked decorator order","code":"def tag(name):\n    def decorate(fn):\n        def wrapper():\n            return name + \"(\" + fn() + \")\"\n        return wrapper\n    return decorate\n\n@tag(\"outer\")\n@tag(\"inner\")\ndef value():\n    return \"core\"\n\nprint(value())","output":"outer(inner(core))","explanation":["The decorator closest to def is applied first: value becomes tag(\"outer\")(tag(\"inner\")(value)).","At call time, the outer wrapper invokes the inner wrapper, which invokes the original function. The returned string makes that nesting visible."]}],"summary":"A decorator takes a function and gives back a function with extra behavior. The @ syntax applies that transformation for you.","minutes":5},{"id":5,"title":"What is metaprogramming in Python, and how are meta-classes used?","body":"In Python, **metaprogramming** refers to techniques that allow you to write code that manipulates other code at runtime, often to dynamically modify or generate classes, functions, and their behavior. Metaprogramming can simplify tasks that would otherwise require repetitive or complex code. **Meta-classes** are a powerful tool in Python for metaprogramming, allowing you to control the behavior and creation of classes themselves.\n\n### What is a Meta-class?\n\nA **meta-class** is essentially a \"class of a class.\" Just as classes are templates for creating instances (objects), meta-classes are templates for creating classes. By default, Python classes are instances of the `type` meta-class. Meta-classes provide a way to customize or extend how classes behave at the time of their creation.\n\n### Why Use Meta-classes?\n\nMeta-classes allow you to:\n\n1. **Modify class attributes and methods** at the time of class creation.\n2. **Enforce rules or constraints** on class definitions.\n3. **Implement design patterns** (e.g., Singleton) or automatically add logging, method decorators, etc.\n4. **Track class creation** and modify behavior globally across classes.\n\nMeta-classes can add a level of dynamism and customization to Python code, especially when working with libraries or frameworks.\n\n### How Do Meta-classes Work?\n\nWhen a class is created, Python:\n\n1. Calls the `__new__` method of the meta-class to create the class object.\n2. Calls the `__init__` method of the meta-class to initialize the class object.\n\nYou can customize these methods to modify class creation or to add attributes and methods dynamically.\n\n### Structure of a Meta-class\n\nA meta-class is created by sub-classing `type`. Here’s the basic structure of a meta-class:\n\n```python\nclass MyMeta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        # Called before the class is created\n        # Custom behavior here\n        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, dct)\n\n    def __init__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        # Called after the class is created\n        # Custom behavior here\n        super().__init__(name, bases, dct)\n\n```\n\n- **`__new__`** is used to customize class creation (modifying or adding attributes, enforcing rules, etc.).\n- **`__init__`** is called after the class is created and can add final modifications.\n\n### Examples of Meta-classes\n\n### Example 1: Simple Meta-class for Logging Class Creation\n\n```python\nclass LoggingMeta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        print(f\"Creating class {name}\")\n        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, dct)\n\n# Using the metaclass\nclass MyClass(metaclass=LoggingMeta):\n    pass\n\nOutput:\n\nCreating class MyClass\n\n```\n\nHere, `LoggingMeta` logs the creation of `MyClass` by printing a message whenever a class using `LoggingMeta` is created.\n\n**Every class in Python is itself an object**—specifically, an instance of a **metaclass**.\n\nHere's the simplest way to understand it:\n\n- In Python, **everything is an object**. This includes **classes** themselves.\n- Classes are actually **created by a special class called a metaclass**.\n\n### Metaclasses in Simple Terms\n\n- You can think of a **metaclass** as a **\"class of a class\"**.\n- **Metaclasses create classes**, just like classes create instances (objects).\n\n### Example Breakdown:\n\n1. **Normal Objects**:\n    - If you have a class, like `MyClass`, you can create **instances** of that class.\n    \n    ```python\n    \n    class MyClass:\n        pass\n    \n    my_instance = MyClass()  # my_instance is an instance of MyClass\n    \n    ```\n    \n    - Here, `my_instance` is an **object** of `MyClass`.\n2. **Classes are Objects Too**:\n    - But what about `MyClass` itself?\n    - When you write `class MyClass: ...`, Python **creates `MyClass` as an object** of a **metaclass**.\n    - By default, this **metaclass** is called `type`.\n3. **`type` Metaclass**:\n    - In Python, most classes are instances of the `type` metaclass.\n    - You can verify this:\n    \n    ```python\n    \n    print(type(MyClass))  # Output: <class 'type'>\n    \n    ```\n    \n    - This means that `MyClass` is an **object** of `type`.\n\n### So, What Does This Mean?\n\n- **Classes are objects**, just like instances.\n- Classes are **created** by **metaclasses**.\n- By default, the **metaclass** for most classes is `type`.\n\n### Example of a Custom Metaclass:\n\nIn your original example with `LoggingMeta`:\n\n- `LoggingMeta` is a **custom metaclass** that **controls how `MyClass` is created**.\n- When you define `MyClass`, Python calls the `__new__` method of `LoggingMeta` to **create the `MyClass` object**.\n\n### Visualization:\n\n- **`type`** is the default metaclass.\n- **`LoggingMeta`** is a custom metaclass.\n\n```\n\nLoggingMeta (metaclass) --> creates --> MyClass (class)\nMyClass (class) --> creates --> my_instance (object)\n\n```\n\n### Summary\n\n- A **class itself** is an **object**, and that object is **created by a `metaclass`**.\n- The **`metaclass`** is like the factory that builds the **class object**.\n- The default `metaclass` in Python is `type`, but you can define your own custom `metaclasses`, like `LoggingMeta`, to control the **creation** of classes in special ways.\n1. **Everything in Python is an object**, including classes and even `metaclasses`.\n2. **Classes are created by `metaclasses`**.\n    - When you define a class like `class MyClass: pass`, it is actually **created by a `metaclass`**.\n    - By default, Python uses the `metaclass` **`type`** to create classes.\n3. **`type` is a `metaclass`** that creates all other classes. It is like a **blueprint for classes**.\n4. **`type` itself is also an instance of `type`**.\n    - This means that `type` is both a **class** and a **metaclass**, making it **self-referential**.\n5. This **self-referential structure** makes Python consistent, where **everything is treated as an object**—even the mechanisms that create objects.\n\nSo, in short:\n\n- **Classes are objects** created by **`metaclasses`**.\n- The **default `metaclass`** is `type`.\n- **`type` is an instance of itself**, which keeps everything unified as an object.\n\nIt might seem a bit tricky at first, but it ensures Python's object model is consistent and powerful.\n\n### Example 2: Adding Attributes Dynamically with a Meta-class\n\n```python\nclass AttributeMeta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        dct['new_attr'] = \"Added by Meta\"\n        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, dct)\n\nclass MyClass(metaclass=AttributeMeta):\n    pass\n\nprint(MyClass.new_attr)  # Output: Added by Meta\n\n```\n\nHere, `AttributeMeta` adds `new_attr` to every class created with it. `MyClass` automatically has this attribute without any modification to its code.\n\n### Example 3: Enforcing Required Methods in Classes\n\n```python\nclass RequireSpeakMeta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        if 'speak' not in dct:\n            raise TypeError(\"Must define 'speak' method\")\n        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, dct)\n\n# This will raise an error because 'speak' is not defined\n# class MyClass(metaclass=RequireSpeakMeta):\n#     pass\n\n# This works because 'speak' is defined\nclass MyClassWithSpeak(metaclass=RequireSpeakMeta):\n    def speak(self):\n        print(\"Hello!\")\n\nobj = MyClassWithSpeak()\nobj.speak()  # Output: Hello!\n\n```\n\nIn this example, `RequireSpeakMeta` checks if the `speak` method is defined in any class using it. If not, it raises a `TypeError`.\n\n### Example 4: Singleton Pattern with a Meta-class\n\nA **Singleton** pattern ensures that a class has only one instance. We can implement this using a meta-class.\n\n```python\nclass SingletonMeta(type):\n    _instances = {}\n    \n    def __call__(cls, *args, **kwargs):\n        if cls not in cls._instances:\n            cls._instances[cls] = super().__call__(*args, **kwargs)\n        return cls._instances[cls]\n\nclass SingletonClass(metaclass=SingletonMeta):\n    pass\n\n# Testing Singleton behavior\nobj1 = SingletonClass()\nobj2 = SingletonClass()\nprint(obj1 is obj2)  # Output: True\n\n```\n\n`SingletonMeta` overrides `__call__` (used to create instances). If an instance already exists, it returns that instance instead of creating a new one.\n\n- **Metaclass Assignment (`metaclass=SingletonMeta`)**: This is not inheritance. Instead, it tells Python to use **`SingletonMeta` as the metaclass** when creating `SingletonClass`.\n    \n    ```python\n    \n    class SingletonClass(metaclass=SingletonMeta):\n        pass\n    \n    ```\n    \n    Here, `SingletonClass` is not **inheriting** from `SingletonMeta`. Instead, it is using `SingletonMeta` as its **metaclass**. This means that the behavior of **creating and managing instances** of `SingletonClass` is controlled by `SingletonMeta`.\n    \n\n### What Does the Metaclass Do?\n\n- **Metaclasses** are responsible for **defining the behavior of classes themselves**, not the instances.\n- In the example code, `SingletonMeta` is a **metaclass**, which means that it defines how `SingletonClass` behaves when you try to **create an instance**.\n\nThe line:\n\n```python\n\nclass SingletonClass(metaclass=SingletonMeta):\n    pass\n\n```\n\nis telling Python, **\"Use `SingletonMeta` as the blueprint to create `SingletonClass`.\"**\n\n### What’s Happening in Detail\n\n1. **`SingletonMeta` is a Metaclass**:\n    - It inherits from `type`, which means it can control how other classes are created and how their instances are managed.\n    - `SingletonMeta` defines a special `__call__` method that makes it act like a **Singleton**, meaning it allows **only one instance** of the class to be created.\n2. **`SingletonClass` Uses `SingletonMeta` as Its Metaclass**:\n    - When you define `SingletonClass` using `metaclass=SingletonMeta`, it means that **all instance creation** of `SingletonClass` will be managed by the `__call__` method of `SingletonMeta`.\n    - This `__call__` method is where the **singleton behavior** is implemented. It ensures that if an instance of `SingletonClass` already exists, that same instance is returned, rather than creating a new one.\n\n### Difference Between Inheritance and Metaclass\n\n- **Inheritance** is used to create a **relationship between two classes** where one class (child) extends the behavior of another (parent).\n    - For example: `class Dog(Animal):` — Here, `Dog` inherits from `Animal`.\n- **`Metaclass`** is used to create a **relationship between a class and how it’s constructed**.\n    - For example: `class SingletonClass(metaclass=SingletonMeta):` — Here, `SingletonMeta` defines **how to build `SingletonClass`** and **control its instances**.\n\n### Summary\n\n- **`SingletonClass(metaclass=SingletonMeta)`** is **not inheritance**.\n- It means that **`SingletonMeta` is used as the `metaclass`** to create and manage `SingletonClass`.\n- The **`metaclass` controls the construction** of `SingletonClass` and its instances, implementing the **singleton pattern** to ensure that only **one instance** of `SingletonClass` can exist.\n\nIn short, **`metaclasses`** are about controlling **how classes are defined and instantiated**, while **inheritance** is about **sharing behavior and attributes** between a parent class and a child class.","category":"Classes & design","label":"Metaprogramming & metaclasses","examples":[{"title":"See that classes are objects too","code":"class User:\n    pass\n\nuser = User()\nprint(type(user).__name__)\nprint(type(User).__name__)\nprint(isinstance(User, type))","output":"User\ntype\nTrue","explanation":["user is an instance of User, and User is itself an instance of type. type is the usual metaclass that constructs class objects.","This is the extra level metaprogramming works with: code can inspect or customize a class object, not just instances created from it."]},{"title":"Add an attribute during class creation","code":"class Tagged(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        namespace[\"tag\"] = name.lower()\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n\nclass Invoice(metaclass=Tagged):\n    pass\n\nprint(Invoice.tag)\nprint(Invoice().tag)","output":"invoice\ninvoice","explanation":["Tagged receives the new class name, base classes, and namespace before the class object is created. It inserts a class attribute into that namespace.","The attribute is available through both the class and its instances via normal attribute lookup. A simpler class decorator may be sufficient for many such transformations."]},{"title":"Reject an incomplete class definition","code":"class RequireRun(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        if \"run\" not in namespace:\n            raise TypeError(\"run is required\")\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n\ntry:\n    class Broken(metaclass=RequireRun):\n        pass\nexcept TypeError as error:\n    print(error)\n\nclass Job(metaclass=RequireRun):\n    def run(self):\n        return \"done\"\n\nprint(Job().run())","output":"run is required\ndone","explanation":["The metaclass validates the class namespace when the class statement runs, before any instance is created. Broken fails because it does not declare run.","This deliberately checks only the new namespace, not inherited methods. For ordinary interface requirements, abstract base classes often express the intent more clearly."]}],"summary":"A class makes instances; a metaclass makes classes. Use a metaclass only when class creation itself needs rules or customization.","minutes":9},{"id":6,"title":"What is monkey patching in Python?","body":"**Monkey patching** in Python is a technique that allows you to modify or extend the behavior of existing classes or modules at runtime, without changing the original source code. This is often done to add features or fix bugs dynamically.\n\n### How Does Monkey Patching Work?\n\nMonkey patching works by directly assigning new functions or attributes to existing classes or instances. This is especially useful when you want to adjust code in a library or built-in module without modifying the original codebase.\n\n### Example of Monkey Patching with Step-by-Step Explanation\n\nLet's go through a step-by-step example to understand monkey patching. We'll start by defining a simple class and then modify its behavior using monkey patching.\n\n### Step 1: Define an Original Class\n\nHere's a simple `Person` class with a method `greet()`.\n\n```python\nclass Person:\n    def greet(self):\n        return \"Hello!\"\n\n# Creating an instance of Person\nperson = Person()\nprint(person.greet())  # Output: Hello!\n\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- We define a `Person` class with a method `greet()` that returns \"Hello!\".\n- When we create an instance `person` and call `greet()`, it outputs `\"Hello!\"`.\n\n### Step 2: Monkey Patch the `greet` Method\n\nNow, let's modify the behavior of `greet()` using monkey patching.\n\n```python\n# Define a new function to replace the greet method\ndef new_greet():\n    return \"Hi there!\"\n\n# Monkey patch the greet method\nPerson.greet = new_greet\n\n# Testing the modified behavior\nprint(person.greet())  # Output: Hi there!\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- We define a new function `new_greet()` that returns `\"Hi there!\"`.\n- We then monkey patch the `greet` method by reassigning `Person.greet` to `new_greet`.\n- Now, when we call `person.greet()`, it uses the new method and outputs `\"Hi there!\"`.\n\n### Step 3: Adding a New Method Dynamically\n\nYou can also add a completely new method to a class at runtime using monkey patching.\n\n```python\n# Define a new function to add as a method\ndef say_goodbye(self):\n    return \"Goodbye!\"\n\n# Monkey patch the new method into the Person class\nPerson.say_goodbye = say_goodbye\n\n# Testing the new method\nprint(person.say_goodbye())  # Output: Goodbye!\n\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- We define `say_goodbye`, a new function that we want to add to the `Person` class.\n- We add it to `Person` by assigning `Person.say_goodbye = say_goodbye`.\n- Now, `person` can call `say_goodbye()`, and it outputs `\"Goodbye!\"`.\n\n### Summary\n\n- **Monkey patching** lets you modify or add new methods to classes or modules at runtime.\n- **Uses**: Fixing bugs, extending library features, or testing without altering the original code.\n- **Caution**: While powerful, monkey patching can lead to unpredictable behavior, so it should be used carefully.\n\nThis technique shows how flexible Python is, allowing you to modify classes on the fly.","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Monkey patching","examples":[{"title":"Replace a method at runtime","code":"class Greeter:\n    def hello(self):\n        return \"Hello\"\n\ng = Greeter()\nprint(g.hello())\nGreeter.hello = lambda self: \"Hi\"\nprint(g.hello())","output":"Hello\nHi","explanation":["The method is looked up on Greeter when g.hello is accessed. Replacing the class attribute changes what the existing instance calls.","This affects other instances using that class method too. It is a runtime modification, whereas a subclass override creates a separate specialization."]},{"title":"Scope a temporary test patch","code":"from unittest.mock import patch\n\nclass Clock:\n    def now(self):\n        return 100\n\nclock = Clock()\nwith patch.object(Clock, \"now\", return_value=7):\n    print(clock.now())\nprint(clock.now())","output":"7\n100","explanation":["patch.object temporarily substitutes a mock for the method. The with block establishes exactly where the replacement applies.","On exit, the original method is restored even if the block raises. Scoped patches reduce the risk of one test changing another test’s behavior."]},{"title":"Prefer explicit dependencies when possible","code":"def report(now):\n    return \"time=\" + str(now())\n\nprint(report(lambda: 7))\nprint(report(lambda: 12))","output":"time=7\ntime=12","explanation":["Instead of replacing a global clock, report accepts the behavior it depends on as an argument. Each caller can supply a deterministic clock.","Dependency injection makes the substitution visible in the function signature and avoids modifying a shared class or module. Use patching when that seam is not available."]}],"summary":"Monkey patching replaces behavior on an existing object, class, or module while the program runs. Keep patches narrow and restore the original behavior afterward.","minutes":4},{"id":7,"title":"How does the @staticmethod differ from @classmethod in Python?","body":"In Python, `@staticmethod` and `@classmethod` are two types of methods that can be defined in a class, but they serve different purposes and behave differently.\n\n### Key Differences:\n\n1. **@staticmethod**: A static method doesn’t access or modify the instance (`self`) or the class (`cls`). It behaves like a regular function, but it’s part of the class namespace.\n2. **@classmethod**: A class method has access to the class itself through `cls` and can modify class-level attributes. It doesn’t need an instance to be called.\n\n### Example: @staticmethod\n\nA `@staticmethod` is just like a regular function but belongs to the class. It doesn’t take `self` or `cls` as its first parameter and doesn’t have access to instance or class-level data.\n\n```python\nclass MyClass:\n    @staticmethod\n    def static_method():\n        return \"This is a static method\"\n\n# Calling the static method\nprint(MyClass.static_method())\n\nOutput:\n\nThis is a static method\n\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- **Define**: `static_method` is defined with the `@staticmethod` decorator.\n- **Call**: We call `MyClass.static_method()` without needing an instance.\n- **Result**: It returns a message but has no access to any instance or class data.\n\n---\n\n### Example: @classmethod\n\nA `@classmethod` has access to the class itself via `cls` and can modify or access class-level data.\n\n```python\nclass MyClass:\n    class_variable = \"Hello from class variable\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def class_method(cls):\n        return cls.class_variable\n\n# Calling the class method\nprint(MyClass.class_method())\n\nOutput:\n\nHello from class variable\n\n```\n\n- **Define**: `class_method` is defined with the `@classmethod` decorator, taking `cls` as its parameter.\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- **Access Class Data**: It accesses `class_variable`, a class-level attribute.\n- **Call**: We call `MyClass.class_method()` directly on the class.\n- **Result**: It returns the value of `class_variable`.\n\n### Comparison: @staticmethod vs @classmethod\n\n| Feature | @staticmethod | @classmethod |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Access to `self` | No | No |\n| Access to `cls` | No | Yes |\n| Can modify class | No | Yes (using `cls`) |\n| Usage | General utility functions within class | Operations related to class-level data or setup |\n\n### When to Use Each\n\n- **Use `@staticmethod`** for utility functions that don’t need access to class or instance data.\n- **Use `@classmethod`** when you need to modify class-level attributes or when the method logically relates to the class itself rather than an instance.\n\nThese decorators provide flexible ways to organize methods within a class, based on whether they need to interact with instance-specific or class-specific data.","category":"Classes & design","label":"Static methods vs class methods","examples":[{"title":"Compare all three kinds of method","code":"class Demo:\n    label = \"class\"\n    def instance(self):\n        return self.label\n    @classmethod\n    def class_name(cls):\n        return cls.__name__\n    @staticmethod\n    def add(a, b):\n        return a + b\n\nd = Demo()\nd.label = \"instance\"\nprint(d.instance())\nprint(Demo.class_name())\nprint(Demo.add(2, 3))","output":"instance\nDemo\n5","explanation":["The instance method reads d’s own label. The class method receives Demo and reads its name. The static method just uses the explicitly supplied numbers.","A static method does not automatically know the instance or calling class. It groups a utility with the class without binding self or cls."]},{"title":"Make an inheritance-friendly alternate constructor","code":"class User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n    @classmethod\n    def from_text(cls, text):\n        return cls(text.strip())\n\nclass Admin(User):\n    pass\n\nuser = Admin.from_text(\" Ada \")\nprint(type(user).__name__)\nprint(user.name)","output":"Admin\nAda","explanation":["Calling the inherited class method through Admin binds cls to Admin. Constructing cls(...) therefore returns the appropriate subclass.","Hard-coding User(...) would lose this behavior. Alternate constructors are a common reason to choose classmethod over staticmethod."]},{"title":"Keep utilities independent from state","code":"class Temperature:\n    @staticmethod\n    def celsius_to_fahrenheit(celsius):\n        return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32\n\nprint(Temperature.celsius_to_fahrenheit(0))\nprint(Temperature().celsius_to_fahrenheit(100))","output":"32.0\n212.0","explanation":["Both calls pass only the explicit temperature value; no instance is injected when the static method is accessed through an instance.","The utility could also be a module-level function. Keeping it on the class is an organizational choice, not a requirement for accessing object state."]}],"summary":"An instance method receives self, a class method receives cls, and a static method receives neither automatically. Choose based on which state the method needs.","minutes":4},{"id":8,"title":"Explain the difference between `__str__` and `__repr__` in Python?","body":"In Python, `__str__` and `__repr__` are two special methods used to define how an object is represented as a string. They serve different purposes:\n\n1. **`__str__`**: Used to create a “user-friendly” or human-readable string representation of an object, meant for display to end users.\n2. **`__repr__`**: Used to create a more detailed and “developer-friendly” string representation of an object, often meant for debugging.\n\n### Key Differences:\n\n- **Purpose**: `__str__` is intended for readability, and `__repr__` is intended for accuracy (often showing how the object could be recreated).\n- **Fallback**: If `__str__` is not defined, `__repr__` is used as a fallback in `print()` and `str()` functions.\n\n### Example to Understand the Difference\n\nLet’s create a `Book` class and define both `__str__` and `__repr__` methods to see how they behave differently.\n\n```python\nclass Book:\n    def __init__(self, title, author):\n        self.title = title\n        self.author = author\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f\"'{self.title}' by {self.author}\"\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f\"Book(title='{self.title}', author='{self.author}')\"\n\n# Create an instance of Book\nmy_book = Book(\"The Great Gatsby\", \"F. Scott Fitzgerald\")\n\n# Using str() and print() to get __str__ output\nprint(str(my_book))      # Output: 'The Great Gatsby' by F. Scott Fitzgerald\nprint(my_book)           # Output: 'The Great Gatsby' by F. Scott Fitzgerald\n\n# Using repr() to get __repr__ output\nprint(repr(my_book))     # Output: Book(title='The Great Gatsby', author='F. Scott Fitzgerald')\n\nOutput:\n\n'The Great Gatsby' by F. Scott Fitzgerald       # From __str__\n'The Great Gatsby' by F. Scott Fitzgerald       # From __str__ (when using print directly)\nBook(title='The Great Gatsby', author='F. Scott Fitzgerald')  # From __repr__\n\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n1. **`__str__`**: Returns a readable representation of the `Book` object, formatted as `\"'The Great Gatsby' by F. Scott Fitzgerald\"`, which is user-friendly.\n    - When we use `print(my_book)` or `str(my_book)`, it calls `__str__`.\n2. **`__repr__`**: Returns a more detailed and accurate string representation: `\"Book(title='The Great Gatsby', author='F. Scott Fitzgerald')\"`.\n    - When we call `repr(my_book)`, it shows the exact values, providing an unambiguous description of the object.\n\n### Summary:\n\n- **`__str__`**: For end-users, meant to be readable and user-friendly.\n- **`__repr__`**: For developers, meant to be detailed and unambiguous, often providing information useful for debugging.","category":"Classes & design","label":"__str__ vs __repr__","examples":[{"title":"Separate user display from debugging","code":"class Book:\n    def __init__(self, title):\n        self.title = title\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.title\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f\"Book({self.title!r})\"\n\nb = Book(\"Python\")\nprint(str(b))\nprint(repr(b))","output":"Python\nBook('Python')","explanation":["str uses the readable title, while repr includes the class-like structure and quotes around the string. !r applies repr to the title itself.","A helpful representation shows important state without depending on a memory address. It need not be executable in every class, but should be informative."]},{"title":"Understand representation inside containers","code":"class Item:\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"friendly\"\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return \"Item()\"\n\nitem = Item()\nprint(item)\nprint([item])","output":"friendly\n[Item()]","explanation":["Printing the object directly uses its string representation. Printing a list uses the representation of each contained item.","This is why a good __repr__ matters when debugging collections: a friendly __str__ alone does not control every display context."]},{"title":"Use the fallback when only repr is defined","code":"class Point:\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return \"Point(2, 3)\"\n\np = Point()\nprint(str(p))\nprint(repr(p))","output":"Point(2, 3)\nPoint(2, 3)","explanation":["Without a custom __str__, the inherited default uses the object’s representation. Both calls therefore produce the same text.","Start with a useful __repr__ for a data object, then add __str__ when users need a distinct, simpler display."]}],"summary":"__str__ gives a friendly display. __repr__ gives a useful debugging representation. If __str__ is absent, Python can fall back to __repr__.","minutes":3},{"id":9,"title":"How are coroutines different from threads in Python?","body":"### Threads\n\n- **What are they?** Threads are like “mini-programs” within a program. In Python, threads can run tasks that seem to be running at the same time.\n- **Why the limitation?** Python has something called the **Global Interpreter Lock (GIL)**. This GIL allows only one thread to execute Python code at a time, even if you have multiple threads.\n- **When to use?** Threads are still useful for tasks that involve waiting (like downloading files or reading databases) because Python can switch between threads during those waiting times, so it doesn’t sit idle.\n\n### Coroutines\n\n- **What are they?** Coroutines are a special way of writing functions that “pause” and “resume” using `await`, letting other tasks run in between.\n- **How are they different?** Coroutines don’t use multiple threads at all. They work within a single thread, switching between tasks only at specific points (when `await` is used).\n- **When to use?** Coroutines are best for tasks that involve a lot of waiting, like handling many network requests. They’re faster and more efficient for this because they don’t have the overhead of threads.\n\n### Summary:\n\n- **Threads**: Allow you to do many things that wait (like downloading files) at the same time, but only one thread runs Python code at once due to the GIL.\n- **Coroutines**: Do the same “waiting tasks” within a single thread but are more efficient because they pause only when necessary (at `await`).\n\nIn short:\n\n- Use **threads** if you need tasks to *appear* to run at the same time.\n- Use **coroutines** when you have many tasks waiting (like network requests) and want it to be lightweight and fast.\n\nWe’ll create two tasks that each:\n\n1. Print a starting message.\n2. Wait for 2 seconds (simulating a time-consuming task like a network request).\n3. Print a completion message.\n\nWe’ll first do this using **threads** and then using **coroutines** with `asyncio`.\n\n### Example with Threads\n\nIn this example, we’ll use the `threading` module to run both tasks concurrently.\n\n```python\nimport threading\nimport time\n\n# Define two tasks\ndef task1():\n    print(\"Starting task 1\")\n    time.sleep(2)  # Simulate a wait (like downloading a file)\n    print(\"Task 1 complete\")\n\ndef task2():\n    print(\"Starting task 2\")\n    time.sleep(2)  # Simulate a wait\n    print(\"Task 2 complete\")\n\n# Create and start threads\nt1 = threading.Thread(target=task1)\nt2 = threading.Thread(target=task2)\n\nt1.start()\nt2.start()\n\n# Wait for both threads to complete\nt1.join()\nt2.join()\n\nOutput:\n\nStarting task 1\nStarting task 2\nTask 1 complete\nTask 2 complete\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- **Concurrency**: Both `task1` and `task2` start at nearly the same time in separate threads.\n- **Time Taken**: Since each task waits for 2 seconds, but they’re running at the same time, the total time taken is about 2 seconds instead of 4.\n- **Overhead**: Threads add some memory and CPU overhead, as each thread manages its state.\n\n---\n\n### Example with Coroutines (`Asyncio`)\n\nNow, let’s see the same example using **coroutines** and `asyncio`. Coroutines are single-threaded but switch between tasks efficiently when waiting (like during a network request or a sleep).\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\n# Define asynchronous tasks\nasync def task1():\n    print(\"Starting task 1\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # Simulate a wait (like downloading a file)\n    print(\"Task 1 complete\")\n\nasync def task2():\n    print(\"Starting task 2\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # Simulate a wait\n    print(\"Task 2 complete\")\n\n# Run tasks concurrently in an asyncio event loop\nasync def main():\n    await asyncio.gather(task1(), task2())\n\n# Start the asyncio event loop\nasyncio.run(main())\n\nOutput:\n\nStarting task 1\nStarting task 2\nTask 1 complete\nTask 2 complete\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- **Concurrency in Single Thread**: Both `task1` and `task2` start at the same time, running in a single thread. They “pause” at `await asyncio.sleep(2)` and switch to the other task.\n- **Time Taken**: The tasks complete in about 2 seconds, as they switch control during the waiting periods.\n- **Efficiency**: Coroutines use less memory and CPU than threads, making them more efficient for I/O-bound tasks (like network requests or file reads).\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Feature | **Threads** | **Coroutines** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Execution** | Multiple threads, switches during waits | Single thread, switches at `await` |\n| **Best for** | I/O-bound tasks with some parallelism needs | I/O-bound tasks needing minimal overhead |\n| **Performance** | Higher memory and CPU usage | Lower memory and CPU usage |\n| **Code Complexity** | Requires managing threads | Managed within an `asyncio` event loop |\n\n### When to Use Each\n\n- Use **threads** when you want multiple tasks to run in parallel (though true parallelism is limited by Python's GIL).\n- Use **coroutines** when you have many tasks that mostly involve waiting, as they’re lightweight and efficient within a single thread.","category":"Concurrency","label":"Coroutines vs threads","examples":[{"title":"Collect thread results in input order","code":"from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef fetch_label(n):\n    return \"item-\" + str(n)\n\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=2) as pool:\n    print(list(pool.map(fetch_label, [1, 2])))","output":"['item-1', 'item-2']","explanation":["The pool schedules normal functions on threads. The main thread receives map results in input order even if workers complete at different times.","This function is a deterministic stand-in for blocking I/O; it does not perform a real network request or demonstrate a timing improvement."]},{"title":"Collect coroutine results with gather","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def fetch_label(n):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return \"item-\" + str(n)\n\nasync def main():\n    return await asyncio.gather(fetch_label(1), fetch_label(2))\n\nprint(asyncio.run(main()))","output":"['item-1', 'item-2']","explanation":["gather schedules both coroutines and returns their results in argument order. sleep(0) provides a suspension point where another ready task may run.","The event loop coordinates these tasks on one thread in this example. Cooperative concurrency is different from executing two Python bodies in parallel."]},{"title":"Move a blocking function away from the event loop","code":"import asyncio\n\ndef blocking_style_work(value):\n    return value.upper()\n\nasync def main():\n    return await asyncio.to_thread(blocking_style_work, \"hello\")\n\nprint(asyncio.run(main()))","output":"HELLO","explanation":["to_thread executes the normal callable in a worker thread and lets the coroutine await its result. This is useful for existing blocking I/O APIs.","The short function here only illustrates the interface. Moving pure-Python CPU work to a thread is not a general GIL-enabled multicore speedup strategy."]}],"summary":"Threads are scheduled by the operating system; asyncio tasks cooperate by suspending at await points. Both can help overlap waiting, but they have different coordination rules.","minutes":5},{"id":10,"title":"What is `memoization` and how does it work in Python?","body":"### What is `Memoization`?\n\n**`Memoization`** is a technique where you store the result of a function call so that if you call the same function with the same input again, you can return the stored result instead of recalculating it.\n\nThis is useful when you have functions that take a long time to compute, especially when you call them multiple times with the same inputs.\n\n---\n\n### Example Without `Memoization`\n\nHere’s a simple example using the Fibonacci sequence, which calculates each number by adding the previous two numbers. This can be slow without optimization, as it repeatedly recalculates values.\n\n```python\n# Fibonacci function without memoization\ndef fibonacci(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2)\n\nprint(fibonacci(10))  # Output: 55\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- If you call `fibonacci(10)`, the function recalculates values like `fibonacci(9)`, `fibonacci(8)`, etc., over and over again.\n- This makes it slow and inefficient, especially for larger numbers.\n\n---\n\n### Example With `Memoization` Using `lru_cache`\n\nTo make this faster, we can use **`memoization`** with Python’s `functools.lru_cache` decorator. This caches (stores) the results, so repeated calls with the same input are faster.\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)  # No limit on cache size\ndef fibonacci(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2)\n\nprint(fibonacci(10))  # Output: 55\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n1. **@lru_cache**: This decorator caches the results of `fibonacci(n)`.\n2. **First Call**: When you call `fibonacci(10)` for the first time, it calculates all intermediate values (`fibonacci(9)`, `fibonacci(8)`, etc.) and stores them.\n3. **Subsequent Calls**: If you call `fibonacci(10)` again, it retrieves the result directly from the cache instead of recalculating.\n\n### Summary\n\n- **`Memoization`** saves previous results so the function doesn’t repeat calculations.\n- **In Python**: You can use `@lru_cache` to easily add `memoization` to your functions.\n- **Result**: The function runs much faster for repeated calls with the same input.\n\n### Summary of Differences\n\n| Aspect | **Without `Memoization`** | **With `Memoization`** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Time to calculate** | Slow (recalculates results repeatedly) | Fast (caches results to reuse them) |\n| **Redundant Calculations** | Yes (many repeated calls for same input) | No (each result is calculated once) |\n| **Efficiency** | Low | High |\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n# Regular Fibonacci function without memoization\ndef fibonacci_no_memo(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    return fibonacci_no_memo(n - 1) + fibonacci_no_memo(n - 2)\n\n# Fibonacci function with memoization\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef fibonacci_with_memo(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    return fibonacci_with_memo(n - 1) + fibonacci_with_memo(n - 2)\n\n# Calculating without memoization\nstart_time = time.time()\nresult_no_memo = fibonacci_no_memo(35)\nend_time = time.time()\nprint(f\"Result (No Memoization): {result_no_memo}\")\nprint(f\"Time taken without memoization: {end_time - start_time:.4f} seconds\\n\")\n\n# Calculating with memoization\nstart_time = time.time()\nresult_with_memo = fibonacci_with_memo(35)\nend_time = time.time()\nprint(f\"Result (With Memoization): {result_with_memo}\")\nprint(f\"Time taken with memoization: {end_time - start_time:.4f} seconds\")\n\nOutput:\n\nResult (No Memoization): 9227465\n**Time taken without memoization: ~3-5 seconds (depends on your machine)**\n\nResult (With Memoization): 9227465\n**Time taken with memoization: ~0.001 seconds (almost instantaneous)**\n\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n1. **Without `Memoization`** (`fibonacci_no_memo`):\n    - This function recalculates values repeatedly, which takes longer for large inputs (like 35), resulting in noticeable delay.\n2. **With `Memoization`** (`fibonacci_with_memo`):\n    - This version caches each result using `lru_cache`, avoiding redundant calculations and returning the result almost instantly.\n\n`Memoization` makes a big difference in performance, especially for recursive functions like Fibonacci that involve repetitive calculations.","category":"Functions","label":"Memoization","examples":[{"title":"Reuse an expensive result","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=2)\ndef square(n):\n    print(\"compute\", n)\n    return n * n\n\nprint(square(4))\nprint(square(4))\ninfo = square.cache_info()\nprint(info.hits, info.misses)","output":"compute 4\n16\n16\n1 1","explanation":["The first call misses the cache, executes square, and stores 16. The second call returns the stored result without executing the body again.","The compute message appears only once. cache_info reports one hit and one miss; those counts show reuse without relying on unreliable timing measurements."]},{"title":"Watch least-recently-used eviction","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=2)\ndef square(n):\n    print(\"compute\", n)\n    return n * n\n\nfor n in [1, 2, 1, 3, 2]:\n    square(n)\nprint(square.cache_info().hits, square.cache_info().misses)","output":"compute 1\ncompute 2\ncompute 3\ncompute 2\n1 4","explanation":["After caching 1 and 2, revisiting 1 makes it more recent. Inserting 3 therefore evicts 2, which must be computed again on the final request.","LRU tracks recency, not frequency or numerical order. A small cache can repeatedly recompute values when the working set exceeds its capacity."]},{"title":"Use hashable keys and clear stale results","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=4)\ndef total(values):\n    return sum(values)\n\nprint(total((2, 3)))\ntry:\n    total([2, 3])\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Use a hashable key\")\ntotal.cache_clear()\nprint(total.cache_info().currsize)","output":"5\nUse a hashable key\n0","explanation":["The tuple of integers is hashable and can form a cache key. A mutable list cannot, so the list call raises TypeError before entering the function body.","cache_clear removes existing entries and resets statistics. Prefer caching repeatable computations; mutable external state can otherwise make remembered answers stale."]}],"summary":"Memoization remembers answers to repeated calls. An LRU cache limits memory by discarding the answer that has gone unused the longest.","minutes":4},{"id":11,"title":"What are context managers, and how are they implemented in Python?","body":"In Python, **context managers** help you manage resources efficiently by ensuring that resources like files, network connections, or locks are properly set up and cleaned up when you're done with them. The most common use case is working with files, but context managers can be used in many scenarios.\n\n### How Context Managers Work\n\nContext managers use two main methods:\n\n1. `__enter__()`: This is called when entering the context (the setup phase).\n2. `__exit__()`: This is called when exiting the context (the cleanup phase).\n\nThe `with` statement in Python simplifies the use of context managers by automatically handling setup and cleanup.\n\n```python\n# Writing to a file using a context manager\nwith open(\"example.txt\", \"w\") as file:\n    file.write(\"Hello, context managers!\")\n\n# Reading from the file to confirm its content\nwith open(\"example.txt\", \"r\") as file:\n    content = file.read()\n    print(content)  # Output: Hello, context managers!\n\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n- The `with open(...) as file:` statement opens the file and assigns it to `file`.\n- After exiting the `with` block, Python automatically closes the file for you, even if an error occurs.\n\n### Custom Context Manager with `__enter__` and `__exit__`\n\nYou can create your own context managers by defining a class with `__enter__` and `__exit__` methods. Here’s a basic example:\n\n```python\nclass SimpleContext:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"Entering the context...\")\n        return \"Inside the context\"\n\n    def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc_value, traceback):\n        print(\"Exiting the context...\")\n        if exc_type:\n            print(f\"An error occurred: {exc_value}\")\n        return True  # Suppresses any exception if one occurred\n\n# Using the custom context manager\nwith SimpleContext() as value:\n    print(value)\n    # Output:\n    # Entering the context...\n    # Inside the context\n\n# Output after exiting the block:\n# Exiting the context...\n\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n- `__enter__` is called when you enter the `with` block and can return a value (in this case, `\"Inside the context\"`) to be used in the block.\n- `__exit__` is called when leaving the `with` block. It can handle exceptions if they occur.\n\n### Output of the Custom Context Manager:\n\n```python\nEntering the context...\nInside the context\nExiting the context...\n\n```\n\nContext managers are a clean, effective way to handle resources without worrying about releasing them manually!\n\nHere's a table to help break down the concept of context managers in Python, including a comparison between the built-in `with` statement and a custom context manager implementation.\n\n| **Concept** | **Explanation** | **Code Example** | **Output** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Basic Idea** | Context managers handle setup and cleanup of resources automatically (e.g., opening/closing files). | `with open(\"file.txt\") as f: f.write(\"Hello!\")` | File closes automatically |\n| **Built-in Context Manager** | Simplifies resource handling using `with` keyword (e.g., file handling). | `with open(\"example.txt\", \"w\") as file: file.write(\"Hello!\")` | File writes \"Hello!\" and closes. |\n| **Custom Context Manager** | Uses `__enter__` and `__exit__` methods to create a custom context manager class. | `class MyContext: def __enter__(self): return \"Inside\" def __exit__(self, *args): print(\"Exiting\")` | `InsideExiting` |\n| **`__enter__` method** | Runs at the start of the `with` block and sets up the resource (optional return value can be assigned). | `def __enter__(self): print(\"Entering\") return \"Resource\"` | `Entering` |\n| **`__exit__` method** | Runs at the end of the `with` block to clean up, handle exceptions if needed (`exc_type`, `exc_value`, `traceback` params for errors). | `def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc_value, traceback): print(\"Exiting\") return True` | `Exiting` |\n| **Handling Exceptions** | `__exit__` can handle exceptions by checking `exc_type`, `exc_value`, and `traceback`.If `True` is returned, exceptions are suppressed. | `def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc_value, traceback): if exc_type: print(f\"Error: {exc_value}\")` | Prints error message, if any |\n| **Example of Usage** | Custom context manager can be used with `with` statement. Returned value from `__enter__` is accessible inside the `with` block. | `with MyContext() as value: print(value)` | `EnteringInsideExiting` |\n\nThis table illustrates the basic flow of how context managers work, from the setup (`__enter__`), to usage in a `with` block, and finally cleanup with the `__exit__` method.\n\n```python\n# Define a custom context manager for simulating database connection management\nclass DatabaseConnectionManager:\n    def __init__(self, connection_string):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize the context manager with a connection string.\n\n        Args:\n            connection_string (str): A simulated database connection string.\n        \n        Attributes:\n            self.connection_string: Stores the connection details.\n            self.connection: Placeholder for the database connection (initially None).\n        \"\"\"\n        self.connection_string = connection_string\n        self.connection = None  # This will hold the simulated database connection\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Setup method, called when the 'with' block starts.\n        It establishes a database connection and provides a cursor for executing queries.\n\n        Returns:\n            MockCursor: A simulated database cursor object.\n        \"\"\"\n        print(f\"Connecting to the database: {self.connection_string}\")\n        # Simulate connecting to the database\n        self.connection = self._connect_to_database()\n        # Get a simulated cursor object for executing queries\n        self.cursor = self.connection.cursor()\n        print(\"Database connection established.\")\n        return self.cursor  # Return the cursor to be used inside the 'with' block\n\n    def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc_value, traceback):\n        \"\"\"\n        Teardown method, called when exiting the 'with' block.\n        Handles transactions (commit/rollback) and ensures the connection is closed.\n\n        Args:\n            exc_type: Type of the exception (if any).\n            exc_value: Exception instance (if any).\n            traceback: Traceback object (if any).\n        \n        Returns:\n            False: Allows exceptions to propagate after cleanup.\n        \"\"\"\n        if exc_type is not None:\n            # If an exception occurred, rollback the transaction\n            print(f\"Exception occurred: {exc_value}. Rolling back the transaction.\")\n            self.connection.rollback()\n        else:\n            # If no exceptions, commit the transaction\n            print(\"Committing the transaction.\")\n            self.connection.commit()\n\n        # Always close the connection, regardless of success or error\n        print(\"Closing the database connection.\")\n        self.connection.close()\n        return False  # Returning False propagates any exception that occurred\n\n    def _connect_to_database(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Simulates connecting to a database by returning a mock connection object.\n\n        Returns:\n            MockConnection: A simulated database connection object.\n        \"\"\"\n        class MockConnection:\n            def cursor(self):\n                # Return a simulated cursor for query execution\n                return MockCursor()\n\n            def commit(self):\n                # Simulate committing a transaction\n                print(\"Transaction committed.\")\n\n            def rollback(self):\n                # Simulate rolling back a transaction\n                print(\"Transaction rolled back.\")\n\n            def close(self):\n                # Simulate closing the connection\n                print(\"Connection closed.\")\n\n        return MockConnection()  # Return an instance of the mock connection\n\n# Define a simulated cursor class for executing queries\nclass MockCursor:\n    def execute(self, query):\n        \"\"\"\n        Simulates executing an SQL query.\n\n        Args:\n            query (str): The SQL query to execute.\n        \"\"\"\n        print(f\"Executing query: {query}\")\n\n    def fetchall(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Simulates fetching results from a query.\n\n        Returns:\n            list: A list of mock results.\n        \"\"\"\n        print(\"Fetching all results.\")\n        return [(\"Row1\", \"Data1\"), (\"Row2\", \"Data2\")]  # Return mock results\n\n# Use the custom context manager to simulate database interactions\nconnection_string = \"DB_HOST=127.0.0.1;DB_NAME=testdb;USER=admin;PASSWORD=secret\"\n\nprint(\"=== Execution Output ===\")\nwith DatabaseConnectionManager(connection_string) as cursor:\n    # This block is executed within the context of the connection manager\n    cursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users;\")  # Simulate running a query\n    results = cursor.fetchall()  # Simulate fetching query results\n    print(\"Query Results:\", results)  # Display the mock query results\n\n# === Expected Output ===\n# Connecting to the database: DB_HOST=127.0.0.1;DB_NAME=testdb;USER=admin;PASSWORD=secret\n# Database connection established.\n# Executing query: SELECT * FROM users;\n# Fetching all results.\n# Query Results: [('Row1', 'Data1'), ('Row2', 'Data2')]\n# Committing the transaction.\n# Closing the database connection.\n# Connection closed.\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Context managers","examples":[{"title":"Implement the context-manager protocol","code":"class Session:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"enter\")\n        return \"ready\"\n    def __exit__(self, kind, value, traceback):\n        print(\"exit\")\n        return False\n\nwith Session() as status:\n    print(status)","output":"enter\nready\nexit","explanation":["The object’s __enter__ runs before the block, and its return value is bound to status. __exit__ runs when the block finishes.","Returning False means exceptions are not suppressed. It is normal for an exit method to return None with the same propagation behavior."]},{"title":"Use contextlib.contextmanager with finally","code":"from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef session():\n    print(\"open\")\n    try:\n        yield \"ready\"\n    finally:\n        print(\"close\")\n\ntry:\n    with session() as value:\n        print(value)\n        raise ValueError(\"failure\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"handled\")","output":"open\nready\nclose\nhandled","explanation":["The generator yields exactly once to enter the block. When the body raises, the exception is passed back at the yield point and finally still runs.","The exception then propagates to the outer handler. Putting cleanup after yield without finally would not provide the same guarantee when the body fails."]},{"title":"Suppress only a deliberate exception type","code":"from contextlib import suppress\n\nwith suppress(KeyError):\n    print({}[\"missing\"])\nprint(\"continued\")\n\ntry:\n    with suppress(KeyError):\n        int(\"bad\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"ValueError was not suppressed\")","output":"continued\nValueError was not suppressed","explanation":["suppress(KeyError) treats only KeyError as handled. The missing dictionary access is skipped and execution continues after the with block.","The unrelated ValueError still propagates. Narrow exception handling avoids concealing programming errors that were never intended to be ignored."]},{"title":"Manage a dynamic group of cleanup actions","code":"from contextlib import ExitStack\n\nwith ExitStack() as stack:\n    stack.callback(print, \"close first\")\n    stack.callback(print, \"close second\")\n    print(\"work\")","output":"work\nclose second\nclose first","explanation":["ExitStack records callbacks and runs them in reverse registration order when the stack exits. That mirrors nested with blocks.","This helps when the number of resources is discovered at runtime. Register each cleanup as soon as its corresponding resource is acquired."]}],"summary":"A context manager defines setup and cleanup around a with block. Cleanup happens on normal exit and while an exception unwinds the block.","minutes":7},{"id":12,"title":"Explain the difference between deep copy and shallow copy in Python.","body":"### Shallow Copy\n\n- A **shallow copy** creates a new object, but it doesn’t create copies of nested objects.\n- It copies references of nested objects, so changes in nested objects will affect both the original and copied objects.\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\n# Original list with nested lists\noriginal_list = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nshallow_copied_list = copy.copy(original_list)  # Shallow copy\n\n# Modify a nested element\nshallow_copied_list[0][0] = 'X'\n\n# Output both lists\nprint(\"Original List:\", original_list)\nprint(\"Shallow Copied List:\", shallow_copied_list)\n\nOutput:\n\nOriginal List: [['X', 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nShallow Copied List: [['X', 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\n\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n- When we modified `shallow_copied_list[0][0]`, it also affected `original_list` because both lists share references to the nested lists.\n- The shallow copy only created a new top-level list, not new copies of the nested lists.\n\n### Deep Copy\n\n- A **deep copy** creates a new object as well as new copies of all nested objects.\n- Changes made to the nested objects in a deep copy do not affect the original object.\n\n```python\n# Original list with nested lists\noriginal_list = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\ndeep_copied_list = copy.deepcopy(original_list)  # Deep copy\n\n# Modify a nested element\ndeep_copied_list[0][0] = 'X'\n\n# Output both lists\nprint(\"Original List:\", original_list)\nprint(\"Deep Copied List:\", deep_copied_list)\n\nOutput:\n\nOriginal List: [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nDeep Copied List: [['X', 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\n\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n- In this case, when we modified `deep_copied_list[0][0]`, it did not affect `original_list`.\n- The `deepcopy` function created a completely independent copy of `original_list`, including all nested lists.\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| **Copy Type** | **Behavior** | **Effect** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shallow Copy** | Copies the top-level object but not nested objects (only references to nested objects). | Changes in nested objects affect both the original and the copy. |\n| **Deep Copy** | Copies the top-level object and all nested objects (creates independent copies of each). | Changes in nested objects do not affect the original object. |\n\n### Key Points\n\n- Use `copy.copy()` for a shallow copy.\n- Use `copy.deepcopy()` for a deep copy.\n- Deep copies are more memory-intensive because they duplicate every nested object, whereas shallow copies are faster but can lead to unintended side effects if nested objects are modified.\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\n# Original list with nested lists\noriginal_list = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\n\n# Shallow copy of the original list\nshallow_copied_list = copy.copy(original_list)\n\n# Modify an element in the nested list of shallow_copied_list\nshallow_copied_list[0][0] = 'X'\n\n# Printing results for shallow copy\nprint(\"Original List after shallow copy modification:\", original_list)\n# Output: Original List after shallow copy modification: [['X', 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nprint(\"Shallow Copied List:\", shallow_copied_list)\n# Output: Shallow Copied List: [['X', 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\n\n# Explanation for Shallow Copy:\n# - Changing shallow_copied_list[0][0] also changes original_list because \n#   both lists reference the same nested lists.\n\n# Resetting original_list for deep copy demonstration\noriginal_list = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\n\n# Deep copy of the original list\ndeep_copied_list = copy.deepcopy(original_list)\n\n# Modify an element in the nested list of deep_copied_list\ndeep_copied_list[0][0] = 'Y'\n\n# Printing results for deep copy\nprint(\"Original List after deep copy modification:\", original_list)\n# Output: Original List after deep copy modification: [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nprint(\"Deep Copied List:\", deep_copied_list)\n# Output: Deep Copied List: [['Y', 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\n\n# Explanation for Deep Copy:\n# - Changing deep_copied_list[0][0] does NOT affect original_list because \n#   deepcopy created completely independent copies of the nested lists.\n\n```\n\n### Explanation of Output\n\n1. **Shallow Copy:**\n    - `shallow_copied_list[0][0] = 'X'` modified both `shallow_copied_list` and `original_list`.\n    - This happened because the shallow copy only duplicated the top-level list, keeping references to the original nested lists.\n2. **Deep Copy:**\n    - `deep_copied_list[0][0] = 'Y'` did **not** affect `original_list`.\n    - This is because the deep copy duplicated the entire structure, including nested lists, making `deep_copied_list` entirely separate from `original_list`.\n\nThis demonstrates how a shallow copy keeps references to nested objects, while a deep copy creates independent duplicates of everything.","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Shallow vs deep copying","examples":[{"title":"Compare assignment, shallow copy, and deep copy","code":"import copy\n\noriginal = [[1], [2]]\nalias = original\nshallow = copy.copy(original)\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\nprint(alias is original)\nprint(shallow is original, shallow[0] is original[0])\nprint(deep[0] is original[0])","output":"True\nFalse True\nFalse","explanation":["Assignment creates another name for the same outer list. copy.copy creates a different outer list but retains references to the same inner lists.","deepcopy creates independent inner lists here. Test identity at both levels to understand which mutations will be shared."]},{"title":"Mutate a nested value","code":"import copy\n\na = {\"scores\": [10, 20]}\nb = copy.copy(a)\nc = copy.deepcopy(a)\nb[\"scores\"].append(30)\nprint(a[\"scores\"])\nprint(b[\"scores\"])\nprint(c[\"scores\"])","output":"[10, 20, 30]\n[10, 20, 30]\n[10, 20]","explanation":["a and b are separate dictionaries containing the same scores list. Appending through b changes that shared list, so a observes it too.","c contains a different list from the deep copy and retains its original values. Replacing a top-level dictionary entry would behave differently from mutating the referenced list."]},{"title":"Distinguish replacing an item from mutating it","code":"import copy\n\na = [[1], [2]]\nb = copy.copy(a)\nb[0] = [99]\nprint(a)\nprint(b)\nb[1].append(8)\nprint(a)\nprint(b)","output":"[[1], [2]]\n[[99], [2]]\n[[1], [2, 8]]\n[[99], [2, 8]]","explanation":["Assigning b[0] changes one slot in b’s outer list; it does not modify a’s slot or the old inner list.","The second inner list remains shared. Appending to b[1] mutates that object and is visible through a[1] as well."]}],"summary":"A shallow copy duplicates the outer container and shares nested objects. A deep copy also copies nested objects in this example, so later nested changes stay separate.","minutes":5},{"id":13,"title":"What are slots in Python, and how do they optimize memory usage?","body":"In Python, `__slots__` is a special mechanism used in classes to optimize memory usage. It’s particularly useful when you create a large number of instances of a class and want to reduce the memory footprint.\n\n### How `__slots__` Works\n\nNormally, Python objects use a built-in dictionary (`__dict__`) to store their attributes. This dictionary is flexible and allows you to add new attributes dynamically. However, dictionaries consume more memory because they store both keys (attribute names) and values.\n\nWhen you define `__slots__` in a class, Python doesn’t create a `__dict__` for instances of that class. Instead, it allocates a fixed amount of memory for the specific attributes listed in `__slots__`. This reduces memory usage and speeds up attribute access, but it also restricts the class to only those attributes specified in `__slots__`.\n\n```python\nclass WithoutSlots:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\nclass WithSlots:\n    __slots__ = ['name', 'age']  # Restricting attributes to only 'name' and 'age'\n    \n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n# Creating instances of both classes\nwithout_slots_instance = WithoutSlots(\"Alice\", 30)\nwith_slots_instance = WithSlots(\"Bob\", 25)\n\n# Checking the memory usage of each instance\nimport sys\nprint(\"Memory usage without __slots__:\", sys.getsizeof(without_slots_instance.__dict__))  # Output: memory usage with __dict__\nprint(\"Memory usage with __slots__:\", sys.getsizeof(with_slots_instance))  # Output: reduced memory usage without __dict__\n\nOutput:\n\nMemory usage without __slots__: 88\nMemory usage with __slots__: 56\n\n```\n\n### Explanation of the Output\n\n1. **Memory Usage without `__slots__`**:\n    - In the `WithoutSlots` class, Python creates a `__dict__` to store attributes (`name` and `age`), which consumes more memory.\n2. **Memory Usage with `__slots__`**:\n    - In the `WithSlots` class, defining `__slots__` removes the need for a `__dict__`, which reduces memory usage.\n    - `sys.getsizeof(without_slots_instance.__dict__)` gives a higher value because the dictionary takes up extra space.\n    - `sys.getsizeof(with_slots_instance)` shows lower memory usage since there’s no dictionary overhead.\n\n### Benefits of Using `__slots__`\n\n- **Memory Efficiency**: Each instance uses less memory since there’s no `__dict__`.\n- **Faster Attribute Access**: Accessing attributes is faster because Python knows exactly where each attribute is stored.\n- **Limitations on Attributes**: Only the specified attributes can be set, which can prevent bugs from accidentally adding new attributes.\n\n### Limitations of `__slots__`\n\n- **No Dynamic Attributes**: You can only set the attributes specified in `__slots__`. Trying to add new attributes not in `__slots__` will raise an `AttributeError`.\n- **Inheritance Issues**: If a subclass doesn’t define `__slots__`, it will revert to using a `__dict__`, negating the memory benefit.\n\n### Summary\n\nUsing `__slots__` is an effective way to optimize memory usage in classes where you don’t need dynamic attributes and where you’re creating many instances. It’s especially helpful for large-scale applications that need to manage memory more efficiently.\n\nHere's the code with detailed explanations and the **output embedded directly within the code** for clarity:\n\n```python\n\nclass WithoutSlots:\n    \"\"\"A class without using __slots__.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name  # Dynamically added to __dict__\n        self.age = age    # Dynamically added to __dict__\n\nclass WithSlots:\n    \"\"\"A class using __slots__ to restrict attributes.\"\"\"\n    __slots__ = ['name', 'age']  # Define the only allowed attributes\n\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name  # Uses __slots__ for storage\n        self.age = age    # Uses __slots__ for storage\n\n# Example 1: Instance without __slots__\nprint(\"=== Without __slots__ ===\")\nperson1 = WithoutSlots(\"Alice\", 30)\n# Output: Shows that attributes are stored in the __dict__\nprint(\"Attributes in __dict__:\", person1.__dict__)\n# Dynamically add a new attribute\nperson1.height = 170\n# Output: __dict__ updated with new attribute\nprint(\"New attribute added. Updated __dict__:\", person1.__dict__)\n\n# Example 2: Instance with __slots__\nprint(\"\\n=== With __slots__ ===\")\nperson2 = WithSlots(\"Bob\", 25)\n# Output: Access the allowed attributes\nprint(\"Allowed attributes:\")\nprint(\"Name:\", person2.name)\nprint(\"Age:\", person2.age)\n\n# Trying to add a new attribute not in __slots__\ntry:\n    person2.height = 180  # This will raise an AttributeError\nexcept AttributeError as e:\n    # Output: Error message\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n\n# Example 3: Memory usage comparison\nimport sys\nprint(\"\\n=== Memory Usage Comparison ===\")\n# Output: Memory usage for both instances\nprint(\"Memory used by WithoutSlots instance:\", sys.getsizeof(person1.__dict__), \"bytes\")\nprint(\"Memory used by WithSlots instance:\", sys.getsizeof(person2), \"bytes\")\n\n# === Expected Output ===\n# === Without __slots__ ===\n# Attributes in __dict__: {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 30}\n# New attribute added. Updated __dict__: {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 30, 'height': 170}\n#\n# === With __slots__ ===\n# Allowed attributes:\n# Name: Bob\n# Age: 25\n# Error: 'WithSlots' object has no attribute 'height'\n#\n# === Memory Usage Comparison ===\n# Memory used by WithoutSlots instance: 104 bytes\n# Memory used by WithSlots instance: 48 bytes\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Explanation of the Output**\n\n### **1. Without `__slots__`**\n\n- **Attributes in `__dict__`:**\n    \n    ```arduino\n    \n    {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 30}\n    \n    ```\n    \n    Attributes `name` and `age` are stored dynamically in the `__dict__`.\n    \n- **Adding New Attribute:**\n    \n    ```arduino\n    \n    {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 30, 'height': 170}\n    \n    ```\n    \n    New attribute `height` is added dynamically to the `__dict__`.\n    \n\n### **2. With `__slots__`**\n\n- **Accessing Attributes:**\n    \n    ```makefile\n    \n    Name: Bob\n    Age: 25\n    \n    ```\n    \n    Attributes `name` and `age` are accessed as defined in `__slots__`.\n    \n- **Adding New Attribute:**\n    \n    ```tsx\n    \n    Error: 'WithSlots' object has no attribute 'height'\n    \n    ```\n    \n    Since `height` is not defined in `__slots__`, attempting to add it raises an `AttributeError`.\n    \n\n### **3. Memory Usage Comparison**\n\n- **Without `__slots__`:**\n    \n    ```csharp\n    \n    Memory used by WithoutSlots instance: 104 bytes\n    \n    ```\n    \n    More memory is used because of the dynamic `__dict__`.\n    \n- **With `__slots__`:**\n    \n    ```csharp\n    \n    Memory used by WithSlots instance: 48 bytes\n    \n    ```\n    \n    Less memory is used because `__dict__` is replaced by a more efficient structure.\n    \n    `__slots__` in Python is a mechanism to restrict the attributes of a class, improving memory usage and performance by replacing the default `__dict__` with a fixed structure. Defined as a list or tuple of allowed attributes, `__slots__` prevents adding dynamic attributes and reduces the memory footprint for each instance, making it ideal for lightweight classes with many instances. While it enforces strict attribute control and speeds up attribute access, it disallows flexible attribute addition and must be explicitly defined in subclasses. This makes `__slots__` particularly useful for optimization in memory-critical or high-performance scenarios with fixed attribute requirements.","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Memory optimization with slots","examples":[{"title":"Restrict instance attributes","code":"class Point:\n    __slots__ = (\"x\", \"y\")\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x, self.y = x, y\n\np = Point(2, 3)\nprint(p.x, p.y)\nprint(hasattr(p, \"__dict__\"))\ntry:\n    p.z = 4\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"z is not a declared slot\")","output":"2 3\nFalse\nz is not a declared slot","explanation":["The class reserves storage for x and y without a normal per-instance attribute dictionary. Assigning an undeclared z therefore fails.","This can save memory across many small instances, but the exact saving depends on the Python implementation and class hierarchy."]},{"title":"Slots are still writable","code":"class Account:\n    __slots__ = (\"balance\",)\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self.balance = balance\n\na = Account(10)\na.balance += 5\nprint(a.balance)","output":"15","explanation":["A slot controls where an attribute can be stored; it is not a read-only declaration. Updating the declared balance attribute works normally.","Use properties, validation, or an appropriate immutable data design when you need restrictions on changing values."]},{"title":"Extend slots explicitly in subclasses","code":"class Base:\n    __slots__ = (\"x\",)\n\nclass Child(Base):\n    __slots__ = (\"y\",)\n\nc = Child()\nc.x, c.y = 1, 2\nprint(c.x + c.y)\nprint(hasattr(c, \"__dict__\"))","output":"3\nFalse","explanation":["Child inherits the x slot and adds its own y slot. Defining slots again avoids introducing an ordinary instance dictionary in this subclass.","A subclass that omits __slots__ normally gets a __dict__, so memory and attribute restrictions must be considered across the whole inheritance chain."]}],"summary":"__slots__ reserves named instance attributes and can reduce per-instance memory overhead. It does not automatically make instances immutable or faster in every workload.","minutes":6},{"id":14,"title":"How do you implement method overloading in Python?","body":"In Python, **method overloading** (defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameters) is not directly supported as in some other programming languages like Java or C++. However, Python provides ways to achieve similar functionality using **default parameters**, **`*args` and `**kwargs`**, or by using the **`@singledispatch` decorator** from the `functools` module.\n\n### 1. Using Default Parameters\n\nYou can simulate method overloading by setting default values for parameters in a method. This way, you can call the method with different numbers of arguments.\n\n```python\nclass Calculator:\n    def add(self, a, b=0, c=0):\n        return a + b + c\n\n# Usage\ncalc = Calculator()\nprint(calc.add(5))           # Output: 5 (only one parameter, so b and c are 0)\nprint(calc.add(5, 10))       # Output: 15 (a=5, b=10, c=0)\nprint(calc.add(5, 10, 15))   # Output: 30 (a=5, b=10, c=15)\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- The `add` method can take one, two, or three parameters.\n- If only one parameter is given, `b` and `c` default to 0, providing flexibility similar to overloading.\n\n### 2. Using `args` (Variable-Length Arguments)\n\nWith `*args`, you can pass a variable number of arguments to a method. This is useful when the exact number of arguments is unknown.\n\n```python\nclass Calculator:\n    def add(self, *args):\n        return sum(args)\n\n# Usage\ncalc = Calculator()\nprint(calc.add(5))             # Output: 5\nprint(calc.add(5, 10))         # Output: 15\nprint(calc.add(5, 10, 15))     # Output: 30\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `args` allows the `add` method to accept any number of arguments.\n- The method sums up all the arguments, making it flexible and similar to method overloading.\n\n### 3. Using `@singledispatch` Decorator\n\nThe `@singledispatch` decorator from the `functools` module lets you create a base function and register multiple implementations for different types of parameters.\n\n```python\nfrom functools import singledispatch\n\n@singledispatch\ndef area(shape):\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"Cannot calculate area of unknown shape\")\n\n@area.register\ndef _(shape: int):  # Circle with radius (int)\n    return 3.14 * shape * shape\n\n@area.register\ndef _(shape: tuple):  # Rectangle with (length, width) as tuple\n    length, width = shape\n    return length * width\n\n@area.register\ndef _(shape: list):  # Triangle with [base, height] as list\n    base, height = shape\n    return 0.5 * base * height\n\n# Usage\nprint(area(5))                  # Output: 78.5 (Circle area)\nprint(area((4, 5)))             # Output: 20 (Rectangle area)\nprint(area([6, 8]))             # Output: 24.0 (Triangle area)\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `@singledispatch` allows the `area` function to handle different types of inputs based on type.\n- We register different implementations based on the type of `shape` (int, tuple, or list).\n- This approach mimics overloading by choosing the function implementation based on the type of input.\n\n### Summary\n\n| **Method** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Default Parameters** | Allows defining optional parameters to simulate multiple versions of a method. |\n| **`*args`** | Enables handling an arbitrary number of arguments, providing flexibility for method-like overloading. |\n| **`@singledispatch`** | Allows method overloading based on parameter type by registering different implementations for each type. |\n\nThese methods provide flexibility in Python to achieve similar behavior to method overloading, allowing you to define methods that work with various input configurations.","category":"Functions","label":"Method overloading","examples":[{"title":"Support several call shapes with defaults","code":"def area(width, height=None):\n    if height is None:\n        height = width\n    return width * height\n\nprint(area(4))\nprint(area(4, 3))","output":"16\n12","explanation":["The same function handles both calls. A missing height selects square behavior, while an explicit height selects rectangle behavior.","Using None as the missing-value marker avoids treating a legitimate zero height as though it were omitted. This is conditional behavior, not signature-based overload resolution."]},{"title":"Collect a variable number of inputs","code":"def add(*values):\n    return sum(values)\n\nprint(add())\nprint(add(2, 3))\nprint(add(2, 3, 4))","output":"0\n5\n9","explanation":["*values collects additional positional arguments into a tuple. sum works for an empty tuple and for tuples of any length containing compatible numeric values.","One definition covers all these arities. Document the accepted element types because *args itself does not validate their meaning."]},{"title":"See why redefining a method is not overloading","code":"class Greeter:\n    def hello(self, name):\n        return \"Hi \" + name\n\n    def hello(self):\n        return \"Hi everyone\"\n\ng = Greeter()\nprint(g.hello())\ntry:\n    g.hello(\"Ada\")\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Earlier definition was replaced\")","output":"Hi everyone\nEarlier definition was replaced","explanation":["A class body binds names in a namespace. The second def binds hello again, replacing the first function rather than adding another signature.","The surviving method accepts only self. Passing Ada gives it an unexpected positional argument and raises TypeError."]}],"summary":"Python does not automatically keep several same-named methods based on their signatures. Use defaults, variable arguments, or explicit type dispatch.","minutes":4},{"id":15,"title":"What is a closure in Python, and why are they useful?","body":"A closure in Python is a nested function that captures and remembers the scope in which it was created, even after that scope has finished executing. Closures allow for data encapsulation and maintaining a state across function calls. They are often used to create factory functions, where a function returns another function with its own enclosed data. Closures are a foundation for decorators and function factories.\n\n```python\ndef make_multiplier(factor):\n    def multiply(x):\n        return x * factor\n    return multiply\n\ndoubler = make_multiplier(2)\nprint(doubler(5))  # Output: 10\n\n```","category":"Functions","label":"Closures","examples":[{"title":"Build a configurable function","code":"def multiplier(factor):\n    def apply(value):\n        return value * factor\n    return apply\n\ndouble = multiplier(2)\ntriple = multiplier(3)\nprint(double(5))\nprint(triple(5))","output":"10\n15","explanation":["Each call to multiplier creates its own enclosing factor binding. The returned functions retain access to those separate bindings after multiplier finishes.","double and triple run the same inner function logic with different remembered configuration. This is useful for callbacks and small function factories."]},{"title":"Update captured state using nonlocal","code":"def make_counter():\n    count = 0\n    def next_value():\n        nonlocal count\n        count += 1\n        return count\n    return next_value\n\na = make_counter()\nb = make_counter()\nprint(a(), a(), b())","output":"1 2 1","explanation":["nonlocal directs assignment to the count binding in the enclosing function instead of creating a local count inside next_value.","a and b come from separate factory calls and have independent state. Two calls to a advance only its counter; b still starts at 1."]},{"title":"Avoid late-binding surprises in loops","code":"late = [lambda: n for n in range(3)]\nfixed = [lambda n=n: n for n in range(3)]\n\nprint([fn() for fn in late])\nprint([fn() for fn in fixed])","output":"[2, 2, 2]\n[0, 1, 2]","explanation":["The first set of lambdas looks up the shared loop binding when called, after the loop has finished with n equal to 2. A closure is not automatically a snapshot.","The default argument n=n is evaluated separately when each lambda is created. It preserves the value for that function call without relying on the later loop binding."]}],"summary":"A closure is an inner function that remembers variables from its surrounding function. It lets you keep small pieces of private state without a class.","minutes":2},{"id":16,"title":"Explain the purpose of the `async` and `await` keywords in Python.","body":"`async` and `await` are keywords used to define asynchronous functions and handle asynchronous code execution in Python. `async` marks a function as asynchronous, allowing it to use `await` expressions. When an `await` statement is encountered, it pauses the function execution until the awaited coroutine completes. This non-blocking behavior is ideal for I/O-bound operations, as it allows other code to run while waiting. These keywords enable asynchronous programming, improving performance by handling multiple tasks concurrently without requiring multiple threads.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def greet():\n    print(\"Hello\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    print(\"World!\")\n\nasyncio.run(greet())\n\n```\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport time\n\n# An asynchronous function simulating a network request\nasync def fetch_data(delay, data):\n    print(f\"Starting to fetch {data}...\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(delay)  # Simulates the network delay\n    print(f\"Finished fetching {data}\")\n    return data\n\n# Main asynchronous function to perform multiple fetches concurrently\nasync def main():\n    start_time = time.time()\n    \n    # Schedule multiple fetch tasks to run concurrently\n    task1 = asyncio.create_task(fetch_data(2, \"Data A\"))\n    task2 = asyncio.create_task(fetch_data(3, \"Data B\"))\n    task3 = asyncio.create_task(fetch_data(1, \"Data C\"))\n    \n    # Await results from all tasks\n    results = await asyncio.gather(task1, task2, task3)\n    \n    print(\"All data fetched:\", results)\n    print(\"Total time taken:\", time.time() - start_time)\n\n# Running the async main function\nasyncio.run(main())\n\n# Expected Output:\n# Starting to fetch Data A...\n# Starting to fetch Data B...\n# Starting to fetch Data C...\n# Finished fetching Data C\n# Finished fetching Data A\n# Finished fetching Data B\n# All data fetched: ['Data A', 'Data B', 'Data C']\n# Total time taken: ~3 seconds (not 6 seconds because tasks ran concurrently)\n\n```\n\n### Explanation and Output:\n\n1. **`async def fetch_data(...)`**: Defines an asynchronous function that simulates fetching data, with a delay to mimic a network request.\n    - **`await asyncio.sleep(delay)`**: Pauses execution for the specified time (`delay`) without blocking other tasks.\n2. **`async def main()`**: This main function manages the asynchronous tasks.\n    - **`asyncio.create_task(fetch_data(...))`**: Starts each fetch task without waiting for it to finish, allowing all tasks to run concurrently.\n    - **`await asyncio.gather(...)`**: Waits for all tasks to complete and gathers their results.\n3. **Output Explanation**:\n    - Fetches are started concurrently, so the program doesn’t wait for each to finish one by one.\n    - Total time taken is around 3 seconds instead of 6 because tasks run concurrently, demonstrating the efficiency of `async` and `await` for I/O-bound tasks.\n\n### 1. `async def`\n\n- The `async` keyword before a function (e.g., `async def fetch_data(...)`) defines it as an **asynchronous function** or **coroutine**.\n- Calling an `async` function returns a coroutine object, which must be awaited or scheduled to run in an event loop.\n- Asynchronous functions allow Python to perform non-blocking operations, meaning the program can continue running other tasks while waiting for these functions to complete.\n\n### 2. `await`\n\n- `await` is used inside an `async` function to pause the function's execution until the awaited coroutine completes.\n- When we use `await asyncio.sleep(delay)`, for instance, it pauses `fetch_data` for the specified `delay` without blocking other concurrent tasks.\n- `await` allows other asynchronous functions to run during the wait time, enabling concurrency.\n\n### 3. `asyncio.sleep(delay)`\n\n- `asyncio.sleep()` is a non-blocking, asynchronous version of `time.sleep()`.\n- Instead of pausing the whole program, `asyncio.sleep(delay)` pauses only the current coroutine, letting other tasks continue during the delay.\n- This function is commonly used to simulate network or I/O delays in asynchronous examples.\n\n### 4. `asyncio.create_task()`\n\n- `asyncio.create_task(coroutine)` schedules a coroutine (like `fetch_data`) to run **concurrently** as an independent task.\n- It doesn’t wait for the task to complete immediately; instead, it runs in the background, allowing the main function to keep executing.\n- This is helpful for starting multiple tasks at once and letting them run independently.\n\n**Example**:\n\n```python\ntask1 = asyncio.create_task(fetch_data(2, \"Data A\"))\n\n```\n\nThis creates a task for `fetch_data` with a 2-second delay for \"Data A\", which runs concurrently with other tasks.\n\n### 5. `asyncio.gather()`\n\n- `asyncio.gather()` is used to run multiple asynchronous tasks concurrently and collect their results once all are completed.\n- It takes in multiple coroutine tasks and returns a list of results in the order the tasks were passed.\n- In our example, `await asyncio.gather(task1, task2, task3)` waits for `task1`, `task2`, and `task3` to complete, ensuring all tasks finish before moving forward.\n\n**Example**:\n\n```python\nresults = await asyncio.gather(task1, task2, task3)\n```\n\nThis gathers results from each task, which are then stored in the `results` list.\n\n### 6. `asyncio.run()`\n\n- `asyncio.run()` is used to run an `async` function from a synchronous context, such as the main block of code.\n- It sets up an event loop, runs the provided `async` function (in this case, `main()`), and closes the event loop once the function is done.\n- This function is ideal for running top-level asynchronous code without needing to manually set up an event loop.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```python\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nThis runs `main()` asynchronously, allowing it to handle multiple concurrent tasks using `async`/`await`.\n\n### Summary of Each Function\n\n| **Function** | **Purpose** |\n| --- | --- |\n| `async def` | Defines a coroutine function that can be awaited. |\n| `await` | Pauses execution in the coroutine until the awaited task completes, allowing other tasks to run. |\n| `asyncio.sleep(delay)` | Asynchronous delay; only pauses the current coroutine, letting others run during the delay. |\n| `asyncio.create_task()` | Schedules a coroutine to run concurrently as a task without waiting for it to complete immediately. |\n| `asyncio.gather()` | Runs multiple coroutines concurrently and collects their results once all tasks are complete. |\n| `asyncio.run()` | Runs an `async` function in the main program, setting up and managing the event loop. |\n\nThese functions, when combined, allow Python to handle I/O-bound operations concurrently, which is efficient for programs that involve network requests, file I/O, or other time-consuming operations.\n\nIn programming, **concurrently** means that multiple tasks or operations are happening during the same time period but don’t necessarily run at the exact same instant.\n\n### In Simple Terms:\n\n- **Concurrently**: Tasks are run **in overlapping time periods**. They may start, pause, and resume at different times but are handled in a way that gives the appearance of simultaneous execution.\n\n### Example:\n\nLet’s say you want to:\n\n1. Download a file.\n2. Send an email.\n3. Process some data.\n\nWhen you run these tasks concurrently, they might overlap in their execution:\n\n- While downloading the file, the program can also work on sending the email.\n- It doesn’t wait for each task to finish completely before starting the next one.\n- Instead, it switches between tasks, handling bits of each, giving the illusion of doing all at once.\n\n### Concurrently vs. Parallelly:\n\n- **Concurrent** execution is like juggling – you’re switching between tasks, handling parts of each one at different times.\n- **Parallel** execution is when multiple tasks actually run at the exact same time, usually requiring multiple processors or cores.\n\n### Why Use Concurrently?\n\nFor tasks that involve waiting (like network requests or file I/O), running them concurrently can make your program more efficient, as it doesn’t waste time waiting for one task to finish before starting the next.","category":"Concurrency","label":"Async & await","examples":[{"title":"Create a coroutine, then run it","code":"import asyncio\nimport inspect\n\nasync def answer():\n    return 7\n\ncoroutine = answer()\nprint(inspect.iscoroutine(coroutine))\nprint(asyncio.run(coroutine))","output":"True\n7","explanation":["answer() returns a coroutine object, not 7 immediately. asyncio.run creates an event loop, runs the coroutine, and returns its final result.","In an environment that already has an event loop, such as some notebooks, await answer() instead of nesting asyncio.run."]},{"title":"Schedule concurrent waits and retain result order","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def task(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return name.upper()\n\nasync def main():\n    results = await asyncio.gather(task(\"a\"), task(\"b\"))\n    print(results)\n\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"['A', 'B']","explanation":["gather schedules both awaitables and waits for their results. The output list follows argument order rather than completion order.","The tasks yield at sleep(0). CPU-heavy loops with no suspension would instead monopolize the event-loop thread until they finish."]},{"title":"Cancel a task and still run cleanup","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def worker():\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(60)\n    finally:\n        print(\"cleanup\")\n\nasync def main():\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker())\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    task.cancel()\n    try:\n        await task\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        print(\"cancelled\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"cleanup\ncancelled","explanation":["The initial sleep(0) gives worker a chance to enter its try block. Cancellation then interrupts its suspended sleep, triggering finally.","Awaiting the task observes CancelledError. The example does not wait 60 seconds: cancellation requests termination and the awaited task handles the unwind."]}],"summary":"Calling async def creates a coroutine. Awaiting or scheduling it runs the work; await lets other tasks proceed when the operation actually suspends.","minutes":7},{"id":17,"title":"What are f-strings, and how do they differ from other formatting methods in Python?","body":"F-strings (formatted string literals) were introduced in Python 3.6 and offer a concise and readable way to embed expressions directly within string literals by prefixing the string with an `f` or `F` and using curly braces `{}` to insert variables or expressions. F-strings are faster than older formatting methods (`%` formatting and `str.format()`) and allow inline evaluation of expressions, making them more flexible and efficient.\n\n```python\nname = \"Alice\"\nage = 30\nprint(f\"Name: {name}, Age: {age + 5}\")  # Output: Name: Alice, Age: 35\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"F-strings","examples":[{"title":"Insert variables and expressions","code":"name = \"Ada\"\ncount = 3\nprint(f\"Hello {name}\")\nprint(f\"Next count: {count + 1}\")","output":"Hello Ada\nNext count: 4","explanation":["Expressions in braces are evaluated in the current scope when the f-string is created. They are not stored as live links to variables.","Keep expressions readable. Complex business logic is usually clearer when computed separately before formatting."]},{"title":"Control precision and alignment","code":"price = 12.5\nprint(f\"Price: {price:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Code: {7:04d}\")\nprint(f\"|{'Py':>5}|\")","output":"Price: 12.50\nCode: 0007\n|   Py|","explanation":["The colon introduces a format specification. .2f displays two decimal places, 04d pads an integer with zeroes, and >5 right-aligns text to width five.","Formatting changes the displayed string, not the original numeric value. Use suitable numeric types when exact decimal arithmetic is required."]},{"title":"Show debugging representations and literal braces","code":"value = \"hello\"\nprint(f\"repr={value!r}\")\nprint(f\"{{value}} = {value}\")\nvalue = \"changed\"\nmessage = f\"now={value}\"\nvalue = \"later\"\nprint(message)","output":"repr='hello'\n{value} = hello\nnow=changed","explanation":["!r uses repr, which includes quotes around the string. Doubled braces insert literal braces rather than opening an expression.","The final message retains the value captured when it was formatted. Rebinding value later does not rewrite an already-created string."]}],"summary":"An f-string evaluates expressions inside braces when the string is built. Format specifiers control how those values are displayed.","minutes":2},{"id":18,"title":"How does `__call__` work in Python, and when would you use it?","body":"The `__call__` method in Python allows an instance of a class to be called as if it were a function. This is achieved by defining the `__call__()` method in a class, enabling instances to act as callable objects. It’s commonly used to create function-like objects, such as in scenarios where you want an object to behave with dynamic behavior based on instance state, or when building classes that represent operations (e.g., decorators).\n\n```python\nclass Adder:\n    def __init__(self, increment):\n        self.increment = increment\n\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        return x + self.increment\n\nadd_five = Adder(5)\nprint(add_five(10))  # Output: 15\n\n```\n\nIn Python, the `__call__` method allows an instance of a class to be called like a function. When a class implements the `__call__` method, the instance behaves like a function and can be called using parentheses `()`, triggering the `__call__` method.\n\n### Purpose of `__call__`:\n\n1. **Callable Instances**: You can make an object of a class act like a function.\n2. **Flexibility**: It allows you to encapsulate behavior and reuse objects like functions.\n3. **Advanced Use Cases**: Useful for scenarios like function wrappers, creating decorators, or making instances with special functionality.\n\n### Example of `__call__` in Use\n\nHere’s a simple example of how `__call__` works, with comments explaining the output within the code.\n\n```python\nclass Greeter:\n    def __init__(self, greeting):\n        self.greeting = greeting\n\n    # Define __call__ to make the instance callable\n    def __call__(self, name):\n        return f\"{self.greeting}, {name}!\"\n\n# Create an instance of Greeter\nhello_greeter = Greeter(\"Hello\")\n\n# Calling the instance as if it were a function\nprint(hello_greeter(\"Alice\"))  # Output: \"Hello, Alice!\"\nprint(hello_greeter(\"Bob\"))    # Output: \"Hello, Bob!\"\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n1. **`__init__` method**: Initializes each `Greeter` instance with a specific greeting message (e.g., \"Hello\").\n2. **`__call__` method**: Defines what happens when we call an instance of `Greeter` as if it were a function.\n    - It takes an additional argument `name` and returns a greeting string combining `self.greeting` and `name`.\n\n**Usage**:\n\n- `hello_greeter(\"Alice\")` behaves like calling a function with `\"Alice\"` as an argument.\n- Behind the scenes, `hello_greeter(\"Alice\")` triggers `hello_greeter.__call__(\"Alice\")`.\n\n**Output Explanation**:\n\n- `hello_greeter(\"Alice\")` returns `\"Hello, Alice!\"`, and `hello_greeter(\"Bob\")` returns `\"Hello, Bob!\"`, showing how `__call__` lets the instance act like a function.\n\n### When to Use `__call__`\n\n- **Configurable Functions**: When you want to create objects that act like functions with parameters set at initialization (e.g., a specific greeter).\n- **Reusable Logic**: Encapsulate behavior in objects that can be reused by simply calling the instance.\n- **Decorators and Wrappers**: You can use `__call__` to make objects that modify or wrap the behavior of other functions.\n\nThis example demonstrates how `__call__` makes an instance behave like a function, making your code more flexible and expressive in certain cases.","category":"Classes & design","label":"Callable objects","examples":[{"title":"Keep state across calls","code":"class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 0\n    def __call__(self):\n        self.value += 1\n        return self.value\n\ncounter = Counter()\nprint(callable(counter))\nprint(counter(), counter())","output":"True\n1 2","explanation":["counter() invokes Counter.__call__ on that instance. The value attribute persists between calls, so each invocation increments the same counter.","callable reports that the object supports calling; it does not guarantee that every possible argument list will be accepted."]},{"title":"Configure a callable object","code":"class Multiply:\n    def __init__(self, factor):\n        self.factor = factor\n    def __call__(self, value):\n        return value * self.factor\n\ndouble = Multiply(2)\nprint(double(7))\ndouble.factor = 3\nprint(double(7))","output":"14\n21","explanation":["The instance stores configuration in factor and applies it each time it is called. Updating the attribute changes future behavior.","This resembles a closure-based function factory, but a class makes state explicit through attributes and can expose additional methods."]},{"title":"Use callable instances in higher-order functions","code":"class LongerThan:\n    def __init__(self, size):\n        self.size = size\n    def __call__(self, text):\n        return len(text) > self.size\n\nwords = [\"a\", \"cat\", \"python\"]\nprint(list(filter(LongerThan(2), words)))","output":"['cat', 'python']","explanation":["filter calls the supplied object for each item just as it would call a function. The stored size determines which words pass.","This is useful when a callback needs reusable configuration. The consuming API only needs callable behavior, not a particular concrete type."]}],"summary":"Define __call__ on a class to make its instances callable. This gives you function-like syntax with object state.","minutes":4},{"id":19,"title":"How does Python handle function annotations?","body":"Python function annotations are a way to attach metadata to function parameters and return values. They do not enforce types but are used by IDEs, linters, and type checkers to improve readability and aid in static analysis. Annotations can be accessed via the `__annotations__` attribute and are often used in type hinting and documentation.\n\n```python\ndef greet(name: str) -> str:\n    return f\"Hello, {name}\"\n\nprint(greet.__annotations__)  # Output: {'name': <class 'str'>, 'return': <class 'str'>}\n\n```\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Optional, Union, Tuple\n\nclass DataProcessor:\n    def add_numbers(self, a: int, b: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Adds two numbers and returns an integer.\"\"\"\n        return a + b\n\n    def greet_user(self, name: str, title: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Generates a greeting, with an optional title.\"\"\"\n        if title:\n            return f\"Hello, {title} {name}!\"\n        return f\"Hello, {name}!\"\n\n    def process_list(self, items: List[Union[int, float]]) -> List[float]:\n        \"\"\"Processes a list of integers and floats and returns a list of floats.\"\"\"\n        return [float(item) * 1.5 for item in items]\n\n    def get_student_grades(self, grades: Dict[Tuple[str, int], str]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Prints the grades of students by name and age.\"\"\"\n        for (name, age), grade in grades.items():\n            print(f\"{name} ({age} years): Grade {grade}\")\n\n    def get_coordinates(self) -> Tuple[int, int, str]:\n        \"\"\"Returns a tuple with x, y coordinates and a label.\"\"\"\n        return (10, 20, \"Location A\")\n\n# Creating an instance of DataProcessor and calling each method\nprocessor = DataProcessor()\n\n# Method 1: add_numbers\nprint(processor.add_numbers.__annotations__)\n# Output: {'a': <class 'int'>, 'b': <class 'int'>, 'return': <class 'int'>}\nprint(processor.add_numbers(5, 10))  # Output: 15\n\n# Method 2: greet_user\nprint(processor.greet_user.__annotations__)\n# Output: {'name': <class 'str'>, 'title': typing.Optional[str], 'return': <class 'str'>}\nprint(processor.greet_user(\"Alice\"))  # Output: \"Hello, Alice!\"\nprint(processor.greet_user(\"Bob\", \"Mr.\"))  # Output: \"Hello, Mr. Bob!\"\n\n# Method 3: process_list\nprint(processor.process_list.__annotations__)\n# Output: {'items': typing.List[typing.Union[int, float]], 'return': typing.List[float]}\nprint(processor.process_list([1, 2.5, 3]))  # Output: [1.5, 3.75, 4.5]\n\n# Method 4: get_student_grades\nprint(processor.get_student_grades.__annotations__)\n# Output: {'grades': typing.Dict[typing.Tuple[str, int], str], 'return': <class 'NoneType'>}\ngrades = {(\"Alice\", 14): \"A\", (\"Bob\", 15): \"B\"}\nprocessor.get_student_grades(grades)\n# Output:\n# Alice (14 years): Grade A\n# Bob (15 years): Grade B\n\n# Method 5: get_coordinates\nprint(processor.get_coordinates.__annotations__)\n# Output: {'return': typing.Tuple[int, int, str]}\nprint(processor.get_coordinates())  # Output: (10, 20, \"Location A\")\n\n```\n\n### Explanation of Each Method and Output\n\n1. **`add_numbers`**:\n    - **Annotation**: `a: int, b: int`, `> int`\n    - **Description**: Takes two integers and returns their sum as an integer.\n    - **Annotations Output**: `{'a': <class 'int'>, 'b': <class 'int'>, 'return': <class 'int'>}`\n    - **Example Output**: `processor.add_numbers(5, 10)` gives `15`.\n2. **`greet_user`**:\n    - **Annotation**: `name: str, title: Optional[str]`, `> str`\n    - **Description**: Greets a user, with an optional title.\n    - **Annotations Output**: `{'name': <class 'str'>, 'title': typing.Optional[str], 'return': <class 'str'>}`\n    - **Example Outputs**:\n        - `processor.greet_user(\"Alice\")` gives `\"Hello, Alice!\"`\n        - `processor.greet_user(\"Bob\", \"Mr.\")` gives `\"Hello, Mr. Bob!\"`\n3. **`process_list`**:\n    - **Annotation**: `items: List[Union[int, float]]`, `> List[float]`\n    - **Description**: Takes a list of integers and floats, returns a list of floats.\n    - **Annotations Output**: `{'items': typing.List[typing.Union[int, float]], 'return': typing.List[float]}`\n    - **Example Output**: `processor.process_list([1, 2.5, 3])` gives `[1.5, 3.75, 4.5]`\n4. **`get_student_grades`**:\n    - **Annotation**: `grades: Dict[Tuple[str, int], str]`, `> None`\n    - **Description**: Takes a dictionary of student names and ages as keys with grades as values; prints them.\n    - **Annotations Output**: `{'grades': typing.Dict[typing.Tuple[str, int], str], 'return': <class 'NoneType'>}`\n\n```python\nAlice (14 years): Grade A\nBob (15 years): Grade B\n\n```\n\n1. **`get_coordinates`**:\n    - **Annotation**: `> Tuple[int, int, str]`\n    - **Description**: Returns a tuple containing two integers (x and y coordinates) and a label.\n    - **Annotations Output**: `{'return': typing.Tuple[int, int, str]}`\n    - **Example Output**: `processor.get_coordinates()` gives `(10, 20, \"Location A\")`\n\n### Summary of Class Methods\n\n| **Method** | **Annotations** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `add_numbers` | `a: int, b: int`, `-> int` | Adds two integers |\n| `greet_user` | `name: str, title: Optional[str]`, `-> str` | Greets with optional title |\n| `process_list` | `items: List[Union[int, float]]`, `-> List[float]` | Processes list of numbers, returns floats |\n| `get_student_grades` | `grades: Dict[Tuple[str, int], str]`, `-> None` | Prints student grades |\n| `get_coordinates` | `-> Tuple[int, int, str]` | Returns coordinates and a label |\n\nEach method uses annotations to indicate expected argument types and return types, providing hints that improve readability and enable static type checking in Python.","category":"Functions","label":"Function annotations","examples":[{"title":"Read a function’s declared types","code":"from typing import get_type_hints\n\ndef twice(value: int) -> int:\n    return value * 2\n\nhints = get_type_hints(twice)\nprint(hints[\"value\"].__name__)\nprint(hints[\"return\"].__name__)\nprint(twice(4))","output":"int\nint\n8","explanation":["get_type_hints retrieves resolved annotation information. Looking at the type names keeps the output readable rather than printing implementation-specific annotation storage.","The function’s body performs the actual multiplication. The annotations document the intended input and return types for readers and static checking tools."]},{"title":"Observe that annotations do not enforce types","code":"def twice(value: int) -> int:\n    return value * 2\n\nprint(twice(\"ha\"))\nprint(type(twice(\"ha\")).__name__)","output":"haha\nstr","explanation":["The call succeeds because multiplying a string by 2 is valid Python. The int annotation does not insert a runtime check.","A static checker can flag this mismatch before execution. If runtime rejection matters, explicitly validate the value rather than assuming annotations do it."]},{"title":"Add explicit runtime validation","code":"def positive(value: int) -> int:\n    if type(value) is not int:\n        raise TypeError(\"Expected an integer\")\n    if value <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a positive value\")\n    return value\n\nprint(positive(3))\nfor value in [\"3\", -1]:\n    try:\n        positive(value)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError) as error:\n        print(type(error).__name__)","output":"3\nTypeError\nValueError","explanation":["The checks in the function body enforce the contract: the string fails the type check, while -1 passes the type check but fails the value constraint.","Using type(value) is int deliberately excludes bool and subclasses here. Use isinstance when your API should accept compatible subclasses instead."]}],"summary":"Annotations describe intended types. They help readers and checking tools, but ordinary Python function calls do not automatically enforce them.","minutes":5},{"id":20,"title":"What are `dunder` (double underscore) methods, and why are they important in Python?","body":"`Dunder` methods (short for “double underscore”) are special methods in Python that begin and end with `__`, such as `__init__`, `__str__`, `__repr__`, etc. They allow objects to implement and interact with built-in operations and support operator overloading, enabling custom behavior for standard operations like addition, comparison, and iteration. For example, `__add__` lets you define behavior for the `+` operator, while `__iter__` makes an object iterable. `Dunder` methods are essential for making classes work intuitively with Python’s syntax and provide an object-oriented interface.\n\n**`Dunder` methods** (short for \"double underscore\" methods) are special methods in Python with names surrounded by double underscores, like `__init__`, `__str__`, and `__add__`. These methods, also known as **magic methods**, allow you to define how objects of your class interact with built-in Python operations, such as initialization, string representation, addition, subtraction, and comparison. They play a key role in **operator overloading** and **customizing object behavior**.\n\n### Why `Dunder` Methods are Important:\n\n1. **Operator Overloading**: Allow custom classes to work with operators (`+`, , , etc.).\n2. **Built-in Function Behavior**: Define how objects interact with functions like `len()`, `str()`, and `repr()`.\n3. **Custom Object Behavior**: Customize initialization, representation, and other key actions\n\n```python\nclass Vector:\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        return Vector(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f\"Vector({self.x}, {self.y})\"\n\nv1 = Vector(2, 3)\nv2 = Vector(4, 5)\nprint(v1 + v2)  # Output: Vector(6, 8)\n\n```\n\n```python\nclass Vector:\n    def __init__(self, x: int, y: int):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n\n    # __str__ for readable string representation\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f\"Vector({self.x}, {self.y})\"\n    \n    # __repr__ for unambiguous string representation\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f\"Vector(x={self.x}, y={self.y})\"\n    \n    # __add__ for vector addition\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, Vector):\n            return Vector(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)\n        raise TypeError(\"Both operands must be Vector instances\")\n\n    # __sub__ for vector subtraction\n    def __sub__(self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, Vector):\n            return Vector(self.x - other.x, self.y - other.y)\n        raise TypeError(\"Both operands must be Vector instances\")\n\n    # __eq__ for equality comparison\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return isinstance(other, Vector) and self.x == other.x and self.y == other.y\n\n    # __len__ to define the length based on magnitude\n    def __len__(self):\n        return int((self.x**2 + self.y**2)**0.5)\n\n# Using the Vector class and testing dunder methods\nvec1 = Vector(3, 4)\nvec2 = Vector(1, 2)\n\n# __str__ and __repr__ methods\nprint(str(vec1))      # Output: Vector(3, 4)\nprint(repr(vec1))     # Output: Vector(x=3, y=4)\n\n# __add__ method for vector addition\nvec3 = vec1 + vec2\nprint(vec3)           # Output: Vector(4, 6)\n\n# __sub__ method for vector subtraction\nvec4 = vec1 - vec2\nprint(vec4)           # Output: Vector(2, 2)\n\n# __eq__ method for equality comparison\nprint(vec1 == vec2)   # Output: False\nprint(vec1 == Vector(3, 4))  # Output: True\n\n# __len__ method for magnitude-based length (distance from origin)\nprint(len(vec1))      # Output: 5 (since √(3² + 4²) = 5)\nprint(len(vec2))      # Output: 2 (since √(1² + 2²) ≈ 2)\n\n```\n\n### Explanation of Each `Dunder` Method\n\n1. **`__init__`**:\n    - This is the initializer (constructor) method, used here to set the `x` and `y` values when creating a `Vector` instance.\n    - **Example**: `vec1 = Vector(3, 4)` initializes a vector at coordinates `(3, 4)`.\n2. **`__str__`**:\n    - Defines a readable string representation for the object, used by `print()` and `str()`.\n    - **Output**: `print(vec1)` results in `Vector(3, 4)`.\n3. **`__repr__`**:\n    - Defines an unambiguous string representation, often used for debugging. Called by `repr()`.\n    - **Output**: `repr(vec1)` results in `Vector(x=3, y=4)`.\n4. **`__add__`**:\n    - Defines the behavior for the `+` operator when adding two `Vector` objects.\n    - **Example**: `vec3 = vec1 + vec2` results in `Vector(4, 6)`.\n    - **Output**: `print(vec3)` shows `Vector(4, 6)`.\n5. **`__sub__`**:\n    - Defines the behavior for the  operator for subtracting one `Vector` from another.\n    - **Example**: `vec4 = vec1 - vec2` results in `Vector(2, 2)`.\n    - **Output**: `print(vec4)` shows `Vector(2, 2)`.\n6. **`__eq__`**:\n    - Defines the behavior of the `==` operator for checking equality.\n    - **Examples**:\n        - `vec1 == vec2` checks if both vectors are equal, resulting in `False`.\n        - `vec1 == Vector(3, 4)` checks equality with another vector of the same values, resulting in `True`.\n7. **`__len__`**:\n    - Defines the behavior for `len()` to get the magnitude of the vector (distance from the origin).\n    - **Examples**:\n        - `len(vec1)` gives `5` since the magnitude of `(3, 4)` is `5`.\n        - `len(vec2)` gives `2` since the magnitude of `(1, 2)` is approximately `2`.\n\n### Summary of `Dunder` Methods in the Example\n\n| **`Dunder` Method** | **Purpose** | **Example Use** | **Output** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `__init__` | Initializes a new instance with `x` and `y` attributes | `vec1 = Vector(3, 4)` | Creates `Vector(3, 4)` |\n| `__str__` | Provides a readable string representation | `print(vec1)` | `Vector(3, 4)` |\n| `__repr__` | Provides an unambiguous string representation | `repr(vec1)` | `Vector(x=3, y=4)` |\n| `__add__` | Defines vector addition with `+` | `vec1 + vec2` | `Vector(4, 6)` |\n| `__sub__` | Defines vector subtraction with `-` | `vec1 - vec2` | `Vector(2, 2)` |\n| `__eq__` | Checks equality with `==` | `vec1 == vec2` | `False` |\n| `__len__` | Returns magnitude-based length with `len()` | `len(vec1)` | `5` (magnitude of `(3, 4)`) |\n\n`Dunder` methods allow you to define custom behaviors for your class, enabling it to interact seamlessly with Python’s built-in operators and functions. This makes your objects more intuitive and powerful when used in code.","category":"Classes & design","label":"Dunder methods","examples":[{"title":"Support len and indexing","code":"class Team:\n    def __init__(self, names):\n        self.names = list(names)\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.names)\n    def __getitem__(self, index):\n        return self.names[index]\n\nteam = Team([\"Ada\", \"Grace\"])\nprint(len(team))\nprint(team[1])","output":"2\nGrace","explanation":["len(team) uses __len__, and team[1] uses __getitem__. The methods delegate to the internal list.","These hooks let your type fit ordinary Python expressions. Keep their behavior unsurprising: indexing should select an element and length should describe a non-negative size."]},{"title":"Define addition for a small value type","code":"class Score:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Score):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return Score(self.value + other.value)\n\nprint((Score(2) + Score(3)).value)\ntry:\n    Score(2) + 3\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Unsupported operands\")","output":"5\nUnsupported operands","explanation":["The + operator finds __add__. It returns a new Score for a supported pair rather than mutating either input.","NotImplemented tells Python that this operand combination is unsupported, allowing other operator protocol steps before a final TypeError."]},{"title":"Control truthiness through length","code":"class Basket:\n    def __init__(self, items):\n        self.items = items\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.items)\n\nprint(bool(Basket([])))\nprint(bool(Basket([\"apple\"])))","output":"False\nTrue","explanation":["When __bool__ is not defined, Python can use __len__ to determine truthiness: zero is false and a positive length is true.","This makes if basket behave like if list. A separate __bool__ can override the meaning when length is not the right signal."]}],"summary":"Dunder methods connect your class to Python syntax and built-in operations. Implement the protocol that matches the behavior your object naturally supports.","minutes":6},{"id":21,"title":"Explain method resolution order (MRO) and the C3 linearization in Python.","body":"### **What is MRO?**\n\nWhen you call a method on a class or an object, Python looks for that method in a specific order. This order is called the **Method Resolution Order (MRO)**.\n\nIf a class has multiple parent classes (multiple inheritance), Python decides the order in which it checks the parent classes using the **C3 Linearization**.\n\n```python\nclass A:\n    def greet(self):\n        print(\"Hello from A\")\n\nclass B(A):\n    def greet(self):\n        print(\"Hello from B\")\n\nclass C(A):\n    def greet(self):\n        print(\"Hello from C\")\n\nclass D(B, C):  # Inherits from B and C\n    pass\n\n# Create an object of D\nd = D()\n\n# Call greet() method\nd.greet()\n\n# Print the MRO\nprint(D.mro())\n\nOutput:\n\nHello from B\n[<class '__main__.D'>, <class '__main__.B'>, <class '__main__.C'>, <class '__main__.A'>, <class 'object'>]\n\n```\n\n### **Explanation**\n\n1. **What happens when `d.greet()` is called?**\n    - Python checks the MRO of class `D` to find the `greet` method.\n    - The MRO for `D` is `[D, B, C, A, object]`.\n2. **How is the MRO calculated?**\n    - Start with `D`.\n    - Look at the order of inheritance: `B` first, then `C`.\n    - Add their MROs while ensuring no duplicates and preserving the order.\n    - The final MRO is: `[D, B, C, A, object]`.\n3. **Result**\n    - Python finds the `greet` method in class `B` first (since `B` comes before `C` in the MRO).\n    - So, `d.greet()` prints: `Hello from B`.\n\n### **Key Points to Remember**\n\n- MRO is the order Python follows to look for methods or attributes.\n- C3 Linearization ensures that the order respects inheritance and avoids duplicates.\n- Use `ClassName.mro()` or `ClassName.__mro__` to see the MRO of any class.\n\n### **C3 Linearization in the Simplest Way**\n\nC3 Linearization is a rule Python follows to figure out the **Method Resolution Order (MRO)** (the order Python checks for methods in classes with multiple inheritance).\n\nIt ensures:\n\n1. **Child classes are checked before parent classes**.\n2. **The order of parent classes is respected**.\n3. **Each class appears only once in the order**.\n\n```python\nclass A:\n    def show(self):\n        print(\"A\")\n\nclass B(A):\n    def show(self):\n        print(\"B\")\n\nclass C(A):\n    def show(self):\n        print(\"C\")\n\nclass D(B, C):  # Inherits from B and C\n    pass\n\n# Check the MRO\nprint(D.mro())\n\nOutput:\n[<class '__main__.D'>, <class '__main__.B'>, <class '__main__.C'>, <class '__main__.A'>, <class 'object'>]\n\n```\n\n### **How It Works**\n\n1. Start with the child class `D`.\n2. Look at its parent classes `B` and `C`.\n3. Merge the MROs of `B` and `C`:\n    - MRO of `B` = `[B, A, object]`\n    - MRO of `C` = `[C, A, object]`\n\nMerge these lists while keeping the order and avoiding duplicates:\n\n- Start with `B` (first in `D`'s inheritance).\n- Then `C` (next parent of `D`).\n- Then `A` (parent of both `B` and `C`).\n- Finally, `object`.\n\n**Resulting MRO for `D`:** `[D, B, C, A, object]`\n\n### **What Does This Mean?**\n\nWhen you call `d.show()`, Python follows this order:\n\n1. Check `D` (nothing there).\n2. Check `B` (finds `show` and stops searching).\n3. So, it prints: **\"B\"**.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Takeaway**\n\nC3 Linearization just decides the order Python looks at classes. In our example, the method in `B` is called first because `B` is higher in the MRO than `C`. Python figures this out automatically using the C3 rule!","category":"Classes & design","label":"MRO & C3 linearization","examples":[{"title":"Inspect a diamond hierarchy","code":"class A: pass\nclass B(A): pass\nclass C(A): pass\nclass D(B, C): pass\n\nprint([cls.__name__ for cls in D.__mro__])","output":"['D', 'B', 'C', 'A', 'object']","explanation":["D lists B before C, and both bases depend on A. C3 places the shared ancestor after both intermediate classes.","The order is neither a simple depth-first traversal nor a repeated visit to A. Read __mro__ when multiple inheritance behavior is unclear."]},{"title":"Resolve the first matching method","code":"class A:\n    def label(self):\n        return \"A\"\nclass B(A):\n    pass\nclass C(A):\n    def label(self):\n        return \"C\"\nclass D(B, C):\n    pass\n\nprint(D().label())","output":"C","explanation":["Lookup checks D, then B, then C, then A. B does not define label, so C’s version is found before A’s.","Even though B inherits A, Python does not complete B’s entire ancestry before considering C. The single combined MRO controls lookup."]},{"title":"Reject contradictory base orders","code":"class X: pass\nclass Y: pass\nclass A(X, Y): pass\nclass B(Y, X): pass\n\ntry:\n    class Broken(A, B):\n        pass\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"No consistent MRO\")","output":"No consistent MRO","explanation":["A requires X before Y, while B requires Y before X. A subclass combining both cannot satisfy those constraints at once.","Python raises at class creation rather than picking an arbitrary order. Redesign conflicting base relationships instead of relying on a guessed lookup path."]}],"summary":"MRO is the order Python searches through classes for attributes and methods. In multiple inheritance, it prevents inconsistent parent ordering.","minutes":4},{"id":22,"title":"What is duck typing, and how does Python use it?","body":"**Duck Typing** is a programming concept where the type of an object is determined by its behavior (methods and properties) rather than its actual class or inheritance. The name comes from the saying:\n\n> \"If it looks like a duck, swims like a duck, and quacks like a duck, then it is probably a duck.\"\n> \n\nIn Python, you don’t check an object’s type explicitly. Instead, you rely on its methods or attributes to decide if it can perform the desired action.\n\n---\n\n### **How Python Uses Duck Typing**\n\nPython doesn’t care about the object’s actual type. If an object can perform the required operation, Python allows it. This makes Python highly flexible.\n\n```python\nclass Duck:\n    def sound(self):\n        return \"Quack!\"\n\nclass Dog:\n    def sound(self):\n        return \"Bark!\"\n\n# A function that doesn't care about the type of object\ndef make_sound(animal):\n    print(animal.sound())\n\n# Using the function with different objects\nduck = Duck()\ndog = Dog()\n\nmake_sound(duck)  # Output: Quack!\nmake_sound(dog)   # Output: Bark!\n\nOutput:\nQuack!\nBark!\n\n```\n\n### **Explanation**\n\n1. **Duck Typing in Action:**\n    - The `make_sound` function doesn’t check if `animal` is a `Duck` or a `Dog`.\n    - It only calls the `sound()` method. As long as the object passed has a `sound()` method, it works.\n2. **Flexibility:**\n    - Python doesn’t care about the specific type (`Duck` or `Dog`).\n    - It just checks if the method exists and can be called.\n\n### **Duck Typing in Python**\n\n**Duck Typing** is a programming concept where the type of an object is determined by its behavior (methods and properties) rather than its actual class or inheritance. The name comes from the saying:\n\n> \"If it looks like a duck, swims like a duck, and quacks like a duck, then it is probably a duck.\"\n> \n\nIn Python, you don’t check an object’s type explicitly. Instead, you rely on its methods or attributes to decide if it can perform the desired action.\n\n---\n\n### **How Python Uses Duck Typing**\n\nPython doesn’t care about the object’s actual type. If an object can perform the required operation, Python allows it. This makes Python highly flexible.\n\n---\n\n### **Example**\n\nLet’s see a simple example:\n\n```python\n\nclass Duck:\n    def sound(self):\n        return \"Quack!\"\n\nclass Dog:\n    def sound(self):\n        return \"Bark!\"\n\n# A function that doesn't care about the type of object\ndef make_sound(animal):\n    print(animal.sound())\n\n# Using the function with different objects\nduck = Duck()\ndog = Dog()\n\nmake_sound(duck)  # Output: Quack!\nmake_sound(dog)   # Output: Bark!\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Explanation**\n\n1. **Duck Typing in Action:**\n    - The `make_sound` function doesn’t check if `animal` is a `Duck` or a `Dog`.\n    - It only calls the `sound()` method. As long as the object passed has a `sound()` method, it works.\n2. **Flexibility:**\n    - Python doesn’t care about the specific type (`Duck` or `Dog`).\n    - It just checks if the method exists and can be called.\n\n---\n\n### **Output**\n\n```python\nQuack!\nBark!\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Key Points**\n\n1. Duck typing allows writing flexible and reusable code.\n2. Instead of focusing on the type of an object, focus on what it **can do**.\n3. Errors might occur if an object without the required method is passed, but this aligns with Python’s philosophy: *\"It’s easier to ask for forgiveness than permission\"*.\n\n### **Another Example**\n\nEven unrelated objects can work in duck typing:\n\n```python\n\nclass Bird:\n    def fly(self):\n        print(\"Flying!\")\n\nclass Airplane:\n    def fly(self):\n        print(\"Zooming in the sky!\")\n\ndef do_fly(flying_thing):\n    flying_thing.fly()\n\nbird = Bird()\nplane = Airplane()\n\ndo_fly(bird)    # Output: Flying!\ndo_fly(plane)   # Output: Zooming in the sky!\n\n```\n\nAs long as the object has the `fly()` method, it works!\n\n### **Why Duck Typing is Good**\n\n1. **Flexibility and Polymorphism**\n    \n    Duck typing lets you write code that works with a variety of objects, as long as they have the required behavior.\n    \n    - Example: A `sound()` method can work with any object that defines `sound()`, without worrying about whether it's a `Dog`, `Duck`, or something else.\n    - **Result:** You can pass any object with a `sound()` method to a function without modifying the function.\n2. **Less Code, More Reusability**\n    \n    You don’t need to write conditional logic to handle specific types. The function becomes simpler and reusable.\n    \n    - Instead of:You just write:\n        \n        ```python\n        \n        if isinstance(animal, Dog):\n            print(animal.bark())\n        elif isinstance(animal, Duck):\n            print(animal.quack())\n        \n        ```\n        \n        ```python\n        \n        print(animal.sound())\n        \n        ```\n        \n3. **Fewer Dependencies**\n    \n    Duck typing decouples the code from specific types or classes. You don’t need to tightly couple your logic to a particular class hierarchy.\n    \n4. **Aligns with Python’s Philosophy**\n    \n    Python embraces a \"we’re all adults here\" philosophy:\n    \n    - If you pass an object without the required behavior, it will raise an error, and that’s okay.\n    - This eliminates unnecessary type checks and makes the code concise.\n\n---\n\n### **What Happens When You Check Types Explicitly?**\n\n1. **Less Flexibility**\n    \n    Explicit type checks limit your function to work with specific types, which means you lose the benefit of polymorphism. For example:\n    \n    ```python\n    \n    def make_sound(animal):\n        if isinstance(animal, Duck):\n            print(animal.sound())\n        elif isinstance(animal, Dog):\n            print(animal.sound())\n    \n    ```\n    \n    If you introduce a new class (`Cat`) with a `sound()` method, you need to update the function to support it. With duck typing, this is unnecessary.\n    \n2. **Code Becomes Harder to Extend**\n    \n    When you rely on type checks, you need to update every function that checks for specific types whenever a new type is added.\n    \n3. **Violation of Open-Closed Principle**\n    \n    Type checks often violate the Open-Closed Principle, which states:\n    \n    > Code should be open for extension but closed for modification.\n    With duck typing, the function works for any new type that follows the required behavior, without modifying the existing code.\n    > \n\n---\n\n### **When Type Checking Can Be Useful**\n\nWhile duck typing is generally advantageous, there are cases where explicit type checks are useful:\n\n1. **Critical Systems:** In mission-critical applications, you might want strict type validation to ensure correctness.\n2. **Third-Party Code:** If you’re consuming third-party code and aren’t sure about the behavior of an object, you might validate its type to avoid runtime errors.\n3. **Complex Systems:** For very large systems, explicit type checks can make the code more predictable and easier to debug.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\n- **Duck typing** promotes **flexibility**, **reusability**, and cleaner code. It’s a natural fit for Python’s dynamic nature.\n- Explicit type checking adds **rigor** but often comes at the cost of **code complexity** and **reduced extensibility**.\n\nIn most Python projects, duck typing is preferred because it keeps the code simple and easy to extend. However, when predictability is critical, explicit type checks might be justified.","category":"Classes & design","label":"Duck typing","examples":[{"title":"Accept different implementations of the same behavior","code":"class Dog:\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"woof\"\nclass Robot:\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"beep\"\n\ndef announce(thing):\n    print(thing.speak())\n\nannounce(Dog())\nannounce(Robot())","output":"woof\nbeep","explanation":["announce depends on a callable speak method with suitable behavior. Dog and Robot satisfy that contract without a common custom base class.","This reduces coupling, but the behavioral contract still matters: a method with the same name but incompatible arguments is not automatically suitable."]},{"title":"Read text from any suitable stream","code":"from io import StringIO\n\ndef first_line(source):\n    return source.readline().strip()\n\nprint(first_line(StringIO(\"hello\\nworld\")))","output":"hello","explanation":["The function needs readline, not an actual file on disk. StringIO supplies that behavior using an in-memory string.","A real text file can work with the same function. Programming to a small protocol makes testing easier and avoids unnecessary concrete type checks."]},{"title":"Report an unsupported operation clearly","code":"def area(shape):\n    return shape.area()\n\nclass Square:\n    def area(self):\n        return 9\n\nprint(area(Square()))\ntry:\n    area(object())\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"An area() method is required\")","output":"9\nAn area() method is required","explanation":["The generic function tries the required operation directly. A plain object lacks area and raises AttributeError.","This illustrates the protocol boundary. In a larger program, avoid broad exception handling that could hide an unrelated AttributeError raised inside a valid implementation."]}],"summary":"Duck typing asks whether an object supports the needed behavior. A useful object does not always need to inherit a specific class.","minutes":7},{"id":23,"title":"What is the difference between a generator and an iterator?","body":"Both **generators** and **iterators** are used to iterate through data in Python, but they have differences in how they work and how they are created.\n\n---\n\n### **1. Iterator**\n\n- **What is it?**An **iterator** is an object in Python that implements the `__iter__()` and `__next__()` methods.\n- **How to create it?**You create an iterator manually by defining a class with the `__iter__()` and `__next__()` methods or by using Python’s built-in functions (like iterables).\n\n---\n\n### **2. Generator**\n\n- **What is it?**A **generator** is a simpler way to create an iterator. It is written like a function but uses the `yield` keyword to produce values one at a time.\n- **How to create it?**You create a generator using a **generator function** or a **generator expression**.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Differences**\n\n| **Feature** | **Iterator** | **Generator** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Creation | Defined with a class and methods | Defined using a function with `yield` |\n| Syntax Complexity | Requires `__iter__()` and `__next__` | Simpler with `yield` |\n| State Management | Manually handled | Automatically handles the state |\n| Memory Efficiency | Can be memory-intensive | More memory-efficient |\n\n---\n\n### **Code Examples**\n\n### **Iterator Example**\n\n```python\n\n# Custom Iterator\nclass MyIterator:\n    def __init__(self, max_value):\n        self.current = 0\n        self.max_value = max_value\n\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return self\n\n    def __next__(self):\n        if self.current < self.max_value:\n            self.current += 1\n            return self.current\n        else:\n            raise StopIteration\n\n# Using the Iterator\niterator = MyIterator(3)\nfor value in iterator:\n    print(value)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```\n\n1\n2\n3\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Generator Example**\n\n```python\n\n# Generator Function\ndef my_generator(max_value):\n    current = 0\n    while current < max_value:\n        current += 1\n        yield current\n\n# Using the Generator\ngen = my_generator(3)\nfor value in gen:\n    print(value)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```\n\n1\n2\n3\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Simplified Comparison**\n\n- **Iterator:** You manually define `__next__` to return values and handle when it stops.\n- **Generator:** You use `yield`, and Python handles the iteration for you.\n\n---\n\n### **Why Use Generators?**\n\n1. **Simpler Code:** Generators require less boilerplate.\n2. **Memory Efficient:** They don’t store all values in memory; they generate values on demand.\n3. **Automatic State Management:** You don’t need to manage the current state yourself.","category":"Iteration","label":"Generators vs iterators","examples":[{"title":"Separate the iterable from its iterator","code":"values = [10, 20]\niterator = iter(values)\nprint(next(iterator))\nprint(next(iterator))\nprint(next(iterator, \"finished\"))\nprint(list(values))","output":"10\n20\nfinished\n[10, 20]","explanation":["iter creates a cursor over the list. Consuming that cursor does not remove values from the list itself.","next with a default returns finished when this iterator is exhausted. Without the default, the same situation raises StopIteration."]},{"title":"Implement a custom iterator","code":"class Countdown:\n    def __init__(self, start):\n        self.current = start\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return self\n    def __next__(self):\n        if self.current <= 0:\n            raise StopIteration\n        value = self.current\n        self.current -= 1\n        return value\n\nc = Countdown(3)\nprint(list(c))\nprint(list(c))","output":"[3, 2, 1]\n[]","explanation":["__next__ updates the instance’s cursor after returning each value. __iter__ returns self because the object is its own iterator.","The cursor remains exhausted after the first list call. This single-pass behavior is also characteristic of generators."]},{"title":"Create a reusable iterable with fresh iterators","code":"class Countdown:\n    def __init__(self, start):\n        self.start = start\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return iter(range(self.start, 0, -1))\n\nc = Countdown(3)\nprint(list(c))\nprint(list(c))","output":"[3, 2, 1]\n[3, 2, 1]","explanation":["Here the object stores configuration, not a shared mutable cursor. Each __iter__ call creates a new iterator over the range.","Repeated loops therefore get independent traversal state. Choose between an iterable and an iterator deliberately when designing a collection-like API."]}],"summary":"An iterable can supply an iterator. An iterator keeps a current position and returns items through next until it raises StopIteration.","minutes":3},{"id":24,"title":"How do property decorators work, and what are their uses?","body":"### **Property Decorators in Python**\n\nThe `@property` decorator is used to define a **getter** method in a class that allows access to a private attribute as if it were a public attribute. It provides a way to encapsulate (hide) the internal implementation of a class attribute while allowing controlled access.\n\n---\n\n### **How Does It Work?**\n\n1. **Encapsulation:** The `@property` decorator allows you to define methods that look like regular attributes.\n2. **Getter and Setter:** You can use `@property` for a getter method and the `@<property_name>.setter` decorator to define a setter method for the same property.\n3. **Control:** It gives you fine-grained control over how an attribute is accessed or updated.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example**\n\n```python\n\nclass Rectangle:\n    def __init__(self, width, height):\n        self._width = width  # Private attribute\n        self._height = height  # Private attribute\n\n    # Getter for area\n    @property\n    def area(self):\n        return self._width * self._height\n\n    # Getter for width\n    @property\n    def width(self):\n        return self._width\n\n    # Setter for width\n    @width.setter\n    def width(self, value):\n        if value > 0:\n            self._width = value\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(\"Width must be positive!\")\n\n# Using the class\nrect = Rectangle(5, 10)\n\n# Accessing the area (getter)\nprint(rect.area)  # Output: 50\n\n# Accessing and updating width\nprint(rect.width)  # Output: 5\nrect.width = 7     # Updates width\nprint(rect.area)   # Output: 70\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Explanation**\n\n1. **Private Attributes (`_width` and `_height`):**\n    - These are used internally in the class.\n    - They are not meant to be accessed directly.\n2. **Getter (`@property`):**\n    - The `area` property is defined with `@property`. It calculates the area dynamically.\n    - The `width` property allows controlled access to `_width`.\n3. **Setter (`@width.setter`):**\n    - Allows setting the value of `width` with validation.\n    - Ensures only positive values are accepted.\n4. **Using as Attributes:**\n    - Even though `area` and `width` are methods, they are accessed like regular attributes (`rect.area`, `rect.width`).\n\n---\n\n### **Why Use Property Decorators?**\n\n1. **Encapsulation:** Hides implementation details and provides controlled access to attributes.\n2. **Validation:** Add checks when setting values (e.g., ensuring a positive width).\n3. **Read-Only Properties:** Make some attributes read-only by defining only a getter.\n\n---\n\n### **Read-Only Property Example**\n\n```python\n\nclass Circle:\n    def __init__(self, radius):\n        self._radius = radius\n\n    @property\n    def radius(self):\n        return self._radius\n\n    @property\n    def circumference(self):\n        return 2 * 3.14 * self._radius\n\n# Using the class\ncircle = Circle(5)\nprint(circle.radius)        # Output: 5\nprint(circle.circumference) # Output: 31.400000000000002\n\n# Trying to set a read-only property\n# circle.radius = 10  # This will raise an AttributeError\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Key Points**\n\n- **Getter with `@property`:** Allows you to access a method as if it’s an attribute.\n- **Setter with `@<property_name>.setter`:** Allows controlled updates with validation.\n- **Encapsulation and Readability:** Makes your code cleaner and better encapsulated.\n\nWe can implement **getter** and **setter** methods without using the `@property` decorator. However, the **`@property` decorator** offers significant advantages that make it special and preferable in Python. Let's explore why.\n\n---\n\n### **Why Use the `@property` Decorator?**\n\n### 1. **Cleaner and More Readable Code**\n\nWithout `@property`:\n\n```python\n\nclass Rectangle:\n    def __init__(self, width, height):\n        self._width = width\n        self._height = height\n\n    def get_width(self):\n        return self._width\n\n    def set_width(self, value):\n        if value > 0:\n            self._width = value\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(\"Width must be positive!\")\n\nrect = Rectangle(5, 10)\nprint(rect.get_width())  # Accessing width\nrect.set_width(7)        # Updating width\n\n```\n\nWith `@property`:\n\n```python\n\nclass Rectangle:\n    def __init__(self, width, height):\n        self._width = width\n        self._height = height\n\n    @property\n    def width(self):\n        return self._width\n\n    @width.setter\n    def width(self, value):\n        if value > 0:\n            self._width = value\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(\"Width must be positive!\")\n\nrect = Rectangle(5, 10)\nprint(rect.width)  # Accessing width\nrect.width = 7     # Updating width\n\n```\n\n**Comparison:**\n\n- Without `@property`: You need to explicitly call `get_width()` and `set_width()`.\n- With `@property`: You access and modify `width` like a regular attribute (`rect.width`), making the code simpler and more Pythonic.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Encapsulation Without Changing the Interface**\n\nIf you later decide to add validation or make calculations for an attribute, `@property` allows you to do so **without changing how the attribute is accessed** by existing code.\n\nExample:\n\n```python\n\nclass Circle:\n    def __init__(self, radius):\n        self._radius = radius\n\n    @property\n    def radius(self):\n        return self._radius\n\n    @radius.setter\n    def radius(self, value):\n        if value > 0:\n            self._radius = value\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(\"Radius must be positive!\")\n\ncircle = Circle(5)\nprint(circle.radius)  # Accessed as an attribute\ncircle.radius = 10    # Updated as an attribute\n\n```\n\n**Key Benefit:**\n\nIf you later need to calculate the `radius` dynamically or validate its value, you can modify the getter or setter without affecting code that already uses the `radius` property.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Read-Only Properties**\n\nThe `@property` decorator allows you to define **read-only attributes** by skipping the setter:\n\n```python\n\nclass Square:\n    def __init__(self, side_length):\n        self._side_length = side_length\n\n    @property\n    def area(self):\n        return self._side_length ** 2\n\nsquare = Square(4)\nprint(square.area)  # Output: 16\n# square.area = 25  # Raises AttributeError (read-only)\n\n```\n\n**Without `@property`:** You'd need to create a separate method (`get_area`) and ensure users don't mistakenly update `area`.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Follows Pythonic Principles**\n\nThe `@property` decorator makes code more Pythonic by adhering to the principle of **\"uniform access\"**:\n\n- Accessing an attribute (`obj.width`) should be as simple as calling a method (`obj.width()`), with no visible distinction to the user.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Reduces Boilerplate**\n\nWith `@property`, you avoid writing explicit getter and setter methods, reducing unnecessary boilerplate and keeping the class definition concise.\n\n---\n\n### **Comparison Table**\n\n| **Feature** | **Without `@property`** | **With `@property`** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Access Syntax | `obj.get_attr()` and `obj.set_attr(value)` | `obj.attr` and `obj.attr = value` |\n| Read-Only Attributes | Not straightforward | Simple by defining only the getter |\n| Adding Validation Later | Requires API changes | No changes needed |\n| Code Readability | Verbose | Cleaner and more Pythonic |\n\n---\n\n### **When Should You Use `@property`?**\n\n1. When you want to **control access** to an attribute.\n2. When you need **validation** while setting an attribute.\n3. When you want to create **read-only attributes**.\n4. When you want to maintain a **consistent interface** (even if the internal logic changes).\n5. When you want your code to be **clean and Pythonic**.\n\n---\n\n### **Summary**\n\nThe `@property` decorator is special because it allows:\n\n1. Attribute-like access to methods, making code clean and intuitive.\n2. Encapsulation without breaking the interface.\n3. Validation and read-only properties easily.\n\nIt’s not mandatory, but it makes Python code more elegant and in line with Pythonic practices. If you care about simplicity and maintainability, `@property` is a powerful tool!","category":"Classes & design","label":"Property decorators","examples":[{"title":"Expose a computed attribute","code":"class Rectangle:\n    def __init__(self, width, height):\n        self.width, self.height = width, height\n    @property\n    def area(self):\n        return self.width * self.height\n\nr = Rectangle(3, 4)\nprint(r.area)\nr.width = 5\nprint(r.area)","output":"12\n20","explanation":["Accessing r.area calls the getter without parentheses. The result is recomputed from current attributes, so changing width changes the next result.","@property does not automatically cache values. Use it for a cheap computed attribute or for controlled access with a familiar interface."]},{"title":"Validate assignments with a setter","code":"class Account:\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self.balance = balance\n    @property\n    def balance(self):\n        return self._balance\n    @balance.setter\n    def balance(self, value):\n        if value < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Negative balance\")\n        self._balance = value\n\na = Account(10)\nprint(a.balance)\ntry:\n    a.balance = -1\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)\nprint(a.balance)","output":"10\nNegative balance\n10","explanation":["Both initialization and later assignment go through the setter. The rejected value is never stored because validation runs before updating _balance.","The backing name _balance avoids recursively assigning the property itself. The leading underscore signals an internal implementation detail, not enforced privacy."]},{"title":"Create a read-only public attribute","code":"class User:\n    def __init__(self, user_id):\n        self._user_id = user_id\n    @property\n    def user_id(self):\n        return self._user_id\n\nuser = User(7)\nprint(user.user_id)\ntry:\n    user.user_id = 8\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"Read-only property\")","output":"7\nRead-only property","explanation":["A property with no setter rejects ordinary assignment through that attribute name. The getter still permits reading.","This is API-level control, not deep immutability or a security boundary: internal code can still change the backing attribute if the design permits it."]}],"summary":"A property lets ordinary attribute access run controlled getter or setter logic. Callers keep using obj.value while the class manages how that value is read or changed.","minutes":7},{"id":25,"title":"What is `eval()` in Python, and why should it be used cautiously?","body":"### **What is `eval()` in Python?**\n\nThe `eval()` function in Python takes a string and evaluates it as a Python expression. It executes the string as code.\n\n---\n\n### **Syntax**\n\n```python\n\neval(expression, globals=None, locals=None)\n\n```\n\n- **`expression`**: A string containing a valid Python expression.\n- **`globals`** (optional): A dictionary for global variables.\n- **`locals`** (optional): A dictionary for local variables.\n\n---\n\n### **How `eval()` Works**\n\n```python\n\n# Example of eval()\nx = 10\nresult = eval(\"x + 5\")  # Evaluates the expression \"x + 5\"\nprint(result)  # Output: 15\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Why Should `eval()` Be Used Cautiously?**\n\n1. **Security Risk**:\n    \n    `eval()` executes any Python code, so if it evaluates malicious code (like deleting files), it can harm your system.\n    \n    ```python\n    \n    # Dangerous eval example\n    eval(\"__import__('os').system('rm -rf /')\")  # Can delete critical files\n    \n    ```\n    \n2. **Untrusted Input**:\n    \n    If you use `eval()` with user input, attackers can inject harmful code.\n    \n\n---\n\n### **Safer Alternatives to `eval()`**\n\n1. **Use `literal_eval()` from `ast` for safer evaluation**:\n    \n    This only allows basic Python literals like strings, numbers, and tuples.\n    \n    ```python\n    \n    import ast\n    \n    expression = \"{'key': 'value'}\"\n    result = ast.literal_eval(expression)\n    print(result)  # Output: {'key': 'value'}\n    \n    ```\n    \n2. **Manually Parse and Validate Input**:\n    \n    Instead of blindly evaluating, explicitly handle user input.\n    \n\n---\n\n### **When to Use `eval()`**\n\n- If you **fully trust** the input, such as internal scripts or pre-defined expressions.\n- To execute dynamic but controlled Python expressions (e.g., calculators).\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example with Warning**\n\n```python\n\n# A calculator using eval() (dangerous if input is untrusted)\ndef calculate(expression):\n    try:\n        return eval(expression)\n    except Exception as e:\n        return f\"Error: {e}\"\n\n# Safe Example\nexpression = \"2 + 3 * 4\"  # Trusted input\nprint(calculate(expression))  # Output: 14\n\n# Dangerous Example\nexpression = \"__import__('os').system('rm -rf /')\"  # Malicious input\n# DO NOT RUN THIS. It can delete your system files!\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Key Points**\n\n1. **`eval()` is powerful** but dangerous because it executes arbitrary Python code.\n2. **Use it only if you completely trust the input**.\n3. Prefer **`ast.literal_eval()`** for safer, controlled evaluation of simple data structures.","category":"Python toolkit","label":"eval & its risks","examples":[{"title":"Understand what eval evaluates","code":"value = eval(\"2 + 3 * 4\", {\"__builtins__\": {}})\nprint(value)","output":"14","explanation":["This fixed, trusted expression follows normal arithmetic precedence. eval evaluates an expression and returns its result.","The empty builtins mapping here is not presented as a secure sandbox. Do not pass attacker-controlled expressions to eval, even after superficial restrictions."]},{"title":"Parse JSON data instead of executing code","code":"import json\n\ntext = '{\"name\": \"Ada\", \"active\": true}'\ndata = json.loads(text)\nprint(data[\"name\"])\nprint(data[\"active\"])","output":"Ada\nTrue","explanation":["json.loads interprets a defined data format rather than arbitrary Python expressions. JSON true becomes Python True.","Use this for JSON messages and configuration, then validate required fields and sizes according to your application’s contract."]},{"title":"Read Python literal data with literal_eval","code":"import ast\n\nprint(ast.literal_eval(\"[1, 2, 3]\"))\ntry:\n    ast.literal_eval(\"sum([1, 2])\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Function calls are not literals\")","output":"[1, 2, 3]\nFunction calls are not literals","explanation":["literal_eval supports Python literal structures such as lists, strings, and numbers, but rejects a function-call expression.","It is not unrestricted code execution, but very large or deeply nested inputs can still exhaust resources. Use size limits and format-specific parsers for external data."]}],"summary":"eval executes Python expressions, so do not use it to parse untrusted input. Choose a parser designed for the data format you actually expect.","minutes":3},{"id":26,"title":"What are weak references, and when would you use them in Python?","body":"### **What Are Weak References in Python?**\n\nA **weak reference** is a reference to an object that does not prevent the object from being garbage-collected. In other words, even if an object has a weak reference, it can still be destroyed when no strong references exist.\n\nThe `weakref` module in Python provides support for weak references.\n\n---\n\n### **Why Use Weak References?**\n\n1. **Avoid Memory Leaks:**\n    - When objects reference each other, it can prevent garbage collection. Weak references help by allowing the object to be garbage-collected when it’s no longer needed.\n2. **Cache Management:**\n    - Weak references are often used in **caching** where you want to store objects temporarily, but let them be automatically removed if they’re not used elsewhere.\n\n---\n\n### **How to Use Weak References?**\n\nThe `weakref.ref` function creates a weak reference to an object.\n\n### **Code Example**\n\n```python\n\nimport weakref\n\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n# Create an instance of MyClass\nobj = MyClass(\"My Object\")\n\n# Create a weak reference to the object\nweak_ref = weakref.ref(obj)\n\n# Access the object through the weak reference\nprint(weak_ref())  # Output: <__main__.MyClass object at 0x...>\nprint(weak_ref().name)  # Output: My Object\n\n# Delete the original strong reference\ndel obj\n\n# The object is now garbage-collected\nprint(weak_ref())  # Output: None\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **When to Use Weak References?**\n\n1. **Caching Systems:**\n    - Store objects temporarily without increasing memory usage unnecessarily.\n2. **Event Handlers/Callbacks:**\n    - Avoid objects being kept alive unnecessarily by event listeners or callbacks.\n3. **Prevent Circular References:**\n    - Use weak references when objects reference each other to prevent memory leaks.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Points to Remember**\n\n1. **Garbage Collection:**\n    \n    Weak references allow the object to be garbage-collected when no strong references exist.\n    \n2. **Accessing Weak References:**\n    \n    If the object is garbage-collected, the weak reference returns `None`.\n    \n3. **Performance:**\n    \n    Weak references avoid memory overhead for objects that are used only temporarily.\n    \n\n---\n\n### **Example: Cache with Weak References**\n\n```python\n\nimport weakref\n\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n# Create a dictionary to store weak references\ncache = weakref.WeakValueDictionary()\n\n# Add objects to the cache\nobj1 = MyClass(\"Object 1\")\ncache['obj1'] = obj1\n\nprint(cache['obj1'].name)  # Output: Object 1\n\n# Delete the strong reference\ndel obj1\n\n# Now the object is removed from the cache\nprint(cache.get('obj1'))  # Output: None\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Summary**\n\n- **Weak references** allow objects to be garbage-collected while still being accessible indirectly.\n- Use them for **caching**, **callbacks**, or **avoiding circular references**.\n- Weak references return `None` once the object is garbage-collected.\n\n```python\n# Import weakref module\nimport weakref\n\n# Define a simple class\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n# Create an instance of MyClass\nobj = MyClass(\"Example Object\")\n\n# Create a weak reference to the object\nweak_ref = weakref.ref(obj)\n\n# Access the object through the weak reference\nprint(\"Accessing weak reference:\", weak_ref())  # Output: Accessing weak reference: <__main__.MyClass object at 0x...>\nprint(\"Name of the object:\", weak_ref().name)   # Output: Name of the object: Example Object\n\n# Delete the original strong reference\ndel obj\n\n# After deletion, the object is garbage-collected, and weak reference returns None\nprint(\"After deletion, weak reference:\", weak_ref())  # Output: After deletion, weak reference: None\n\n# Example with WeakValueDictionary for caching\ncache = weakref.WeakValueDictionary()\n\n# Create another instance\nobj2 = MyClass(\"Cached Object\")\ncache['obj2'] = obj2\n\nprint(\"Cached object name:\", cache['obj2'].name)  # Output: Cached object name: Cached Object\n\n# Delete the strong reference\ndel obj2\n\n# After deletion, the object is removed from the cache automatically\nprint(\"After deletion from cache:\", cache.get('obj2'))  \n# Output: After deletion from cache: None\n\n```\n\n### **Explanation in the Code**\n\n1. **Weak Reference (`weakref.ref`)**:\n    - Created a weak reference to an object (`weak_ref`).\n    - After deleting the strong reference (`del obj`), the weak reference returns `None`.\n2. **`WeakValueDictionary`**:\n    - Cached an object (`cache['obj2']`).\n    - When the strong reference (`obj2`) is deleted, the object is automatically removed from the cache.\n\n```python\nimport weakref\n\n# Create a class\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n# Create an object\nobj = MyClass(\"My Object\")\n\n# Create a weak reference to the object\nweak_ref = weakref.ref(obj)\n\n# Access the object using the weak reference\nprint(weak_ref())  # Output: <__main__.MyClass object at 0x...>\n\n# Delete the object\ndel obj\n\n# Now the weak reference returns None\nprint(weak_ref())  # Output: None\n\n```\n\n### **Explanation**\n\n1. A weak reference to `obj` is created using `weakref.ref()`.\n2. After `obj` is deleted, the weak reference no longer points to the object and returns `None`.\n\n### **What is the Purpose of Weak References?**\n\n1. **Weak references** are used when you want to refer to an object **without stopping it from being deleted**.\n2. They let Python **garbage-collect** the object when no strong references to it exist, even if a weak reference is still pointing to it.\n\n---\n\n### **Why Use Weak References?**\n\n### Example Use Case 1: **Avoid Memory Waste**\n\n- Imagine you have a **cache** storing objects.\n- If the program forgets to delete these objects, they take up memory **forever**.\n- Weak references solve this problem by **automatically removing the object from the cache** when it’s no longer needed.\n\n---\n\n### **Simple Code Example**\n\n```python\n\nimport weakref\n\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n# Create an object\nobj = MyClass(\"My Object\")\n\n# Create a weak reference to the object\nweak_ref = weakref.ref(obj)\n\n# Access the object through the weak reference\nprint(weak_ref())  # Output: <__main__.MyClass object at 0x...>\n\n# Delete the strong reference\ndel obj\n\n# Now the weak reference is invalid because the object was deleted\nprint(weak_ref())  # Output: None\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **What Happens Here?**\n\n1. **`weak_ref` is like a \"soft pointer\":**\n    - It points to the object **without keeping it alive**.\n2. When **`del obj`** is called:\n    - The object is deleted.\n    - `weak_ref()` now returns `None` because the object is gone.\n\n---\n\n### **Why is This Useful?**\n\n### **Example: Automatic Cache Cleanup**\n\nIf you store objects in a cache and forget to remove them, they waste memory. Weak references let the cache clean itself:\n\n```python\n\nimport weakref\n\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n# Weak reference dictionary for caching\ncache = weakref.WeakValueDictionary()\n\n# Create an object and add it to the cache\nobj = MyClass(\"Cached Object\")\ncache['obj'] = obj\n\nprint(cache.get('obj'))  # Output: <__main__.MyClass object at 0x...>\n\n# Delete the strong reference\ndel obj\n\n# Now the cache is automatically cleaned\nprint(cache.get('obj'))  # Output: None\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **The Simplest Takeaway**\n\n- **Strong reference:** Keeps the object alive.\n- **Weak reference:** Points to the object but allows it to be deleted when no strong references exist.\n- **Use Case:** Avoid memory waste (like in caches) or prevent objects from being kept alive unnecessarily.","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Weak references","examples":[{"title":"Observe an object without owning it","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass User:\n    pass\n\nuser = User()\nref = weakref.ref(user)\nprint(ref() is user)\ndel user\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["Calling ref retrieves the referent while it exists. The weak reference does not prevent the object from being collected.","After its strong owner is removed, ref returns None. In real code, assign target = ref() and check target before accessing attributes."]},{"title":"Build a cache that does not retain values","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Product:\n    pass\n\ncache = weakref.WeakValueDictionary()\nproduct = Product()\ncache[\"item\"] = product\nprint(\"item\" in cache)\ndel product\ngc.collect()\nprint(\"item\" in cache)","output":"True\nFalse","explanation":["WeakValueDictionary holds values weakly, so the cache is not a strong owner of the product. While product exists, the entry can be retrieved normally.","Once that last strong reference disappears, the entry vanishes. Use an ordinary dictionary when the cache is supposed to retain values intentionally."]},{"title":"Track membership without owning members","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Listener:\n    pass\n\nlisteners = weakref.WeakSet()\nlistener = Listener()\nlisteners.add(listener)\nprint(len(listeners))\ndel listener\ngc.collect()\nprint(len(listeners))","output":"1\n0","explanation":["WeakSet is useful for registries of objects that should disappear when their owners release them. Adding a listener does not extend its lifetime.","Iteration results may change as objects are collected. Keep a strong reference during any operation that needs a listener to stay alive."]}],"summary":"A weak reference lets you observe an object without keeping it alive. Check whether the object still exists before using it.","minutes":7},{"id":27,"title":"How does the `asyncio` event loop work in Python?","body":"### **How Does the `asyncio` Event Loop Work in Python?**\n\nThe **`asyncio` event loop** is the core of asynchronous programming in Python. It runs asynchronous tasks (like functions defined with `async def`) and ensures that these tasks are executed in a non-blocking way.\n\n---\n\n### **How It Works (Simplified Explanation)**\n\n1. The **event loop** runs multiple tasks concurrently.\n2. It uses **coroutines** (special functions defined with `async def`) to perform non-blocking operations like waiting for I/O (e.g., reading from a file or making an API call).\n3. When a task needs to \"wait\" (like `await`), the loop switches to another task, ensuring the program continues running without getting stuck.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Points**\n\n- The event loop schedules tasks and decides which one runs next.\n- Tasks only \"pause\" when they encounter an `await` keyword, allowing the loop to run other tasks in the meantime.\n\n---\n\n### **Simple Code Example**\n\n```python\n\nimport asyncio\n\n# Define an asynchronous function\nasync def task_1():\n    print(\"Task 1 started\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # Simulates a non-blocking delay\n    print(\"Task 1 finished\")\n\nasync def task_2():\n    print(\"Task 2 started\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)  # Simulates a non-blocking delay\n    print(\"Task 2 finished\")\n\n# Main function to run the event loop\nasync def main():\n    # Run tasks concurrently\n    await asyncio.gather(task_1(), task_2())\n\n# Run the event loop\nasyncio.run(main())\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **What Happens in the Code?**\n\n1. **Task 1 and Task 2 Start Together:**\n    - Both tasks are scheduled to run concurrently.\n2. **First `await` in Task 1 (`await asyncio.sleep(2)`):**\n    - Task 1 \"pauses\" for 2 seconds.\n    - The event loop switches to Task 2.\n3. **First `await` in Task 2 (`await asyncio.sleep(1)`):**\n    - Task 2 \"pauses\" for 1 second.\n    - The event loop checks for other tasks to run.\n4. Task 2 finishes before Task 1 because its delay is shorter.\n\n---\n\n### **Output**\n\n```python\n\nTask 1 started\nTask 2 started\nTask 2 finished\nTask 1 finished\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Why Use an Event Loop?**\n\n- **Non-Blocking:** Instead of waiting for one task to finish (e.g., a file read), other tasks can run in the meantime.\n- **Efficient I/O Handling:** Ideal for tasks involving I/O operations like API requests, database queries, or file operations.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Functions in `asyncio`:**\n\n1. **`asyncio.run()`**\n    - Starts the event loop.\n    - Runs the main coroutine.\n2. **`await`**\n    - Pauses the coroutine, allowing the event loop to switch to another task.\n3. **`asyncio.gather()`**\n    - Runs multiple tasks concurrently.\n\n---\n\n### **Summary**\n\n- The **event loop** handles multiple tasks concurrently in an efficient, non-blocking way.\n- Tasks \"pause\" when they encounter `await`, and the loop switches to another task.\n- This is ideal for I/O-bound operations like network requests or database queries.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\n# Define an asynchronous task\nasync def task_1():\n    print(\"Task 1 started\")  # Task 1 begins execution\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # Non-blocking pause for 2 seconds\n    print(\"Task 1 finished\")  # Task 1 resumes and finishes after 2 seconds\n\nasync def task_2():\n    print(\"Task 2 started\")  # Task 2 begins execution\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)  # Non-blocking pause for 1 second\n    print(\"Task 2 finished\")  # Task 2 resumes and finishes after 1 second\n\n# Main function to run the event loop\nasync def main():\n    # Run both tasks concurrently\n    # Task 1 will start, pause, and allow Task 2 to run in the meantime\n    await asyncio.gather(task_1(), task_2())\n\n# Start the event loop and execute the main coroutine\nasyncio.run(main())\n\n\"\"\"\nOutput:\nTask 1 started  # Task 1 starts first\nTask 2 started  # Task 2 starts almost immediately after Task 1\nTask 2 finished # Task 2 finishes first because it waits only 1 second\nTask 1 finished # Task 1 finishes after waiting 2 seconds\n\"\"\"\n\n```\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\n# Define asynchronous tasks\nasync def download_file(file_name):\n    print(f\"Downloading {file_name} started\")  # Task starts\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # Simulates download time (non-blocking pause)\n    print(f\"Downloading {file_name} completed\")  # Task finishes\n\nasync def process_file(file_name):\n    print(f\"Processing {file_name} started\")  # Task starts\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)  # Simulates processing time (non-blocking pause)\n    print(f\"Processing {file_name} completed\")  # Task finishes\n\n# Main function\nasync def main():\n    # Run the download and process tasks concurrently\n    await asyncio.gather(\n        download_file(\"file1.txt\"),\n        process_file(\"file1.txt\"),\n    )\n\n# Run the event loop\nasyncio.run(main())\n\n\"\"\"\nOutput:\nDownloading file1.txt started  # Download starts first\nProcessing file1.txt started   # Processing starts while download is in progress\nProcessing file1.txt completed # Processing finishes after 1 second\nDownloading file1.txt completed# Download finishes after 2 seconds\n\"\"\"\n\n```\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\n# Define an asynchronous task for fetching data\nasync def fetch_data(api_name):\n    print(f\"Fetching data from {api_name} started\")  # Task starts\n    await asyncio.sleep(3)  # Simulates fetching time (non-blocking pause)\n    print(f\"Fetching data from {api_name} completed\")  # Task finishes\n\n# Define an asynchronous task for saving data\nasync def save_data(file_name):\n    print(f\"Saving data to {file_name} started\")  # Task starts\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # Simulates saving time (non-blocking pause)\n    print(f\"Saving data to {file_name} completed\")  # Task finishes\n\n# Main function\nasync def main():\n    # Run fetch and save tasks concurrently\n    await asyncio.gather(\n        fetch_data(\"API_1\"),\n        save_data(\"database.db\"),\n    )\n\n# Run the event loop\nasyncio.run(main())\n\n\"\"\"\nOutput:\nFetching data from API_1 started  # Fetch task starts first\nSaving data to database.db started # Save task starts while fetch is running\nSaving data to database.db completed # Save finishes after 2 seconds\nFetching data from API_1 completed  # Fetch finishes after 3 seconds\n\"\"\"\n\n```\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\n# Define an asynchronous task for downloading a file\nasync def download_file(file_name, delay):\n    print(f\"Starting download of {file_name}\")  # Task starts\n    await asyncio.sleep(delay)  # Simulate download time (non-blocking pause)\n    print(f\"Completed download of {file_name}\")  # Task finishes\n    return file_name\n\n# Define an asynchronous task for processing a file\nasync def process_file(file_name):\n    print(f\"Starting processing of {file_name}\")  # Task starts\n    await asyncio.sleep(3)  # Simulate processing time (non-blocking pause)\n    print(f\"Completed processing of {file_name}\")  # Task finishes\n\n# Define an asynchronous task for sending an email after processing\nasync def send_email(file_name):\n    print(f\"Starting to send email for {file_name}\")  # Task starts\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)  # Simulate email sending time (non-blocking pause)\n    print(f\"Email sent for {file_name}\")  # Task finishes\n\n# Main function orchestrating tasks\nasync def main():\n    # Step 1: Download multiple files concurrently\n    downloads = await asyncio.gather(\n        download_file(\"file1.txt\", 2),\n        download_file(\"file2.txt\", 4),\n        download_file(\"file3.txt\", 1),\n    )\n\n    # Step 2: Process each downloaded file one by one\n    for file_name in downloads:\n        await process_file(file_name)\n        # Step 3: Send an email after each file is processed\n        await send_email(file_name)\n\n# Run the event loop\nasyncio.run(main())\n\n\"\"\"\nOutput:\nStarting download of file1.txt\nStarting download of file2.txt\nStarting download of file3.txt\nCompleted download of file3.txt  # file3 completes first (1 second delay)\nCompleted download of file1.txt  # file1 completes second (2 second delay)\nCompleted download of file2.txt  # file2 completes last (4 second delay)\nStarting processing of file3.txt\nCompleted processing of file3.txt\nStarting to send email for file3.txt\nEmail sent for file3.txt\nStarting processing of file1.txt\nCompleted processing of file1.txt\nStarting to send email for file1.txt\nEmail sent for file1.txt\nStarting processing of file2.txt\nCompleted processing of file2.txt\nStarting to send email for file2.txt\nEmail sent for file2.txt\n\"\"\"\n\n```\n\n### **Explanation**\n\n- **`asyncio.gather`:** Downloads all files concurrently with different delays.\n- **Sequential Processing:** Each file is processed one by one after downloading.\n- **Chained Tasks:** After processing each file, an email is sent, maintaining a logical order.\n- The tasks use non-blocking delays, so downloading, processing, and email sending are efficiently handled by the event loop.\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\nThe **`asyncio` event loop** allows you to run multiple tasks **concurrently** without blocking the program. Here's what it does in the simplest terms:\n\n1. **Handles Multiple Tasks:** It switches between tasks whenever they \"pause\" using `await`, so no time is wasted.\n2. **Non-Blocking:** Tasks like downloading, processing, or waiting don’t stop the program; other tasks continue running in the meantime.\n3. **Efficient:** Ideal for I/O-bound operations like file downloads, API requests, or database queries.\n\n---\n\n### **How It Works**\n\n- Tasks **start together**, but their completion depends on how long each one takes.\n- The event loop keeps checking for tasks that are ready to resume.\n\n---\n\n### **Why Use It?**\n\nUse **`asyncio`** to:\n\n- Run **concurrent operations** efficiently.\n- Save time on tasks involving **delays or waiting**.\n- Write clean, asynchronous code for real-world problems like downloading files, handling APIs, or managing databases.\n\nIn short, the event loop is your manager, making sure everything runs smoothly without anyone waiting unnecessarily!","category":"Concurrency","label":"The asyncio event loop","examples":[{"title":"Create a coroutine, then run it","code":"import asyncio\nimport inspect\n\nasync def answer():\n    return 7\n\ncoroutine = answer()\nprint(inspect.iscoroutine(coroutine))\nprint(asyncio.run(coroutine))","output":"True\n7","explanation":["answer() returns a coroutine object, not 7 immediately. asyncio.run creates an event loop, runs the coroutine, and returns its final result.","In an environment that already has an event loop, such as some notebooks, await answer() instead of nesting asyncio.run."]},{"title":"Schedule concurrent waits and retain result order","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def task(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return name.upper()\n\nasync def main():\n    results = await asyncio.gather(task(\"a\"), task(\"b\"))\n    print(results)\n\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"['A', 'B']","explanation":["gather schedules both awaitables and waits for their results. The output list follows argument order rather than completion order.","The tasks yield at sleep(0). CPU-heavy loops with no suspension would instead monopolize the event-loop thread until they finish."]},{"title":"Cancel a task and still run cleanup","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def worker():\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(60)\n    finally:\n        print(\"cleanup\")\n\nasync def main():\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker())\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    task.cancel()\n    try:\n        await task\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        print(\"cancelled\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"cleanup\ncancelled","explanation":["The initial sleep(0) gives worker a chance to enter its try block. Cancellation then interrupts its suspended sleep, triggering finally.","Awaiting the task observes CancelledError. The example does not wait 60 seconds: cancellation requests termination and the awaited task handles the unwind."]}],"summary":"Calling async def creates a coroutine. Awaiting or scheduling it runs the work; await lets other tasks proceed when the operation actually suspends.","minutes":8},{"id":28,"title":"What is the difference between `__new__` and `__init__` in Python?","body":"### **Difference Between `__new__` and `__init__` in Python**\n\nBoth `__new__` and `__init__` are special methods in Python used during object creation, but they serve different purposes.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Differences**\n\n1. **`__new__`:**\n    - It is the method that **creates the object**.\n    - It is called **before** `__init__`.\n    - It returns a new instance of the class.\n    - It’s a **class method** and takes the class (`cls`) as its first argument.\n2. **`__init__`:**\n    - It **initializes the object** after it has been created.\n    - It is called **after** `__new__`.\n    - It sets up attributes for the object.\n    - It’s an **instance method** and takes the instance (`self`) as its first argument.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example**\n\n```python\n\nclass Example:\n    def __new__(cls, *args, **kwargs):\n        print(\"Creating the object in __new__\")\n        instance = super().__new__(cls)  # Create the object\n        return instance\n\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        print(\"Initializing the object in __init__\")\n        self.name = name\n\n# Creating an object\nobj = Example(\"My Object\")\n\nprint(f\"Object name: {obj.name}\")\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Output**\n\n```less\n\nCreating the object in __new__\nInitializing the object in __init__\nObject name: My Object\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Explanation**\n\n1. **`__new__` is called first:**\n    - It creates the object and allocates memory for it.\n    - You can customize object creation here.\n2. **`__init__` is called next:**\n    - It initializes the object with attributes or other setup logic.\n    - The object (`self`) already exists at this point.\n3. The result:\n    - `__new__` creates the object.\n    - `__init__` prepares the object for use.\n\n---\n\n### **When to Use `__new__`?**\n\n- Use `__new__` if you need to control the creation of objects, especially in scenarios like:\n    - Implementing **singleton pattern** (only one instance of a class).\n    - Subclassing **immutable types** (like `int`, `str`, or `tuple`).\n\n---\n\n### **Example with Immutable Type**\n\n```python\n\nclass MyInt(int):\n    def __new__(cls, value):\n        print(\"In __new__\")\n        return super().__new__(cls, value * 2)  # Modify object creation\n\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        print(\"In __init__\")\n        self.value = value\n\n# Create an instance of MyInt\nnum = MyInt(5)\nprint(f\"Value of num: {num}\")  # Output: 10\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```markdown\n\nIn __new__\nIn __init__\nValue of num: 10\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Simplest Takeaway**\n\n- **`__new__` creates the object.**\n- **`__init__` initializes the object.**\n- `__new__` is rarely overridden, but it’s powerful when you need fine control over object creation.\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\n1. **`__new__`:**\n    - Creates the object.\n    - Called first.\n    - Rarely overridden.\n2. **`__init__`:**\n    - Initializes the object.\n    - Called after `__new__`.\n    - Sets up attributes.\n\n**Takeaway:**\n\n- Use `__new__` to control **object creation**.\n- Use `__init__` to control **object initialization**.","category":"Classes & design","label":"__new__ vs __init__","examples":[{"title":"Trace creation before initialization","code":"class Item:\n    def __new__(cls, name):\n        print(\"new\")\n        return super().__new__(cls)\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        print(\"init\")\n        self.name = name\n\nprint(Item(\"book\").name)","output":"new\ninit\nbook","explanation":["__new__ receives the class and returns the instance. Since it returns an Item instance, Python proceeds to __init__ with that instance as self.","__init__ assigns state but must return None. Returning a new object from __init__ is not how instance construction works."]},{"title":"Customize an immutable built-in subclass","code":"class UpperText(str):\n    def __new__(cls, value):\n        return super().__new__(cls, value.upper())\n\ntext = UpperText(\"hello\")\nprint(text)\nprint(type(text).__name__)","output":"HELLO\nUpperText","explanation":["The string content must be chosen while the immutable object is created, so __new__ transforms the value before delegating to str.__new__.","An __init__ method cannot rewrite the already-created string content in place. Immutable subclasses are a legitimate reason to customize __new__."]},{"title":"See when initialization is skipped","code":"class Factory:\n    def __new__(cls):\n        return {\"value\": 7}\n    def __init__(self):\n        print(\"not called\")\n\nresult = Factory()\nprint(type(result).__name__)\nprint(result[\"value\"])","output":"dict\n7","explanation":["__new__ returns a dictionary rather than an instance of Factory. Python therefore does not call Factory.__init__ on that unrelated object.","This is legal but surprising in an ordinary class API. Prefer a plainly named factory function when returning an unrelated type would otherwise confuse callers."]}],"summary":"__new__ creates or selects the instance. __init__ then initializes it when appropriate. Most ordinary classes only need __init__.","minutes":3},{"id":29,"title":"What is the difference between `is` and `==` in Python?","body":"Both `is` and `==` are used for comparisons in Python, but they check for different things.\n\n---\n\n### **1. `is`**\n\n- **What it does:**The `is` operator checks if **two variables point to the same object** in memory.\n- **Comparison Type:**Checks **identity**, not value.\n- **Use Case:**Use `is` when you want to check if two variables reference the **exact same object**.\n\n---\n\n### **2. `==`**\n\n- **What it does:**The `==` operator checks if **two variables have the same value**.\n- **Comparison Type:**Checks **equality of values**.\n- **Use Case:**Use `==` when you want to check if two variables have **equal values**.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example**\n\n```python\n# Example 1: Using `is`\na = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a  # `b` points to the same object as `a`\nc = [1, 2, 3]  # `c` is a separate object with the same value\n\nprint(a is b)  # Output: True (a and b are the same object)\nprint(a is c)  # Output: False (a and c are different objects)\nprint(a == c)  # Output: True (a and c have the same value)\n\n# Example 2: Using `==`\nx = 5\ny = 5\nz = int(5)\n\nprint(x == y)  # Output: True (x and y have the same value)\nprint(x is y)  # Output: True (small integers are cached, so x and y point to the same object)\nprint(x is z)  # Output: True (small integers are cached in Python)\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Explanation of the Outputs**\n\n1. **`is` checks object identity:**\n    - `a is b`: True because `b` is a reference to the same object as `a`.\n    - `a is c`: False because `a` and `c` are different objects in memory.\n2. **`==` checks value equality:**\n    - `a == c`: True because the contents of `a` and `c` are the same.\n3. **Caching of small integers and strings:**\n    - Python caches small integers (`5 to 256`) and some strings for efficiency, so `x is z` is True.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Differences**\n\n| **Operator** | **Checks** | **Example** | **Output** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `is` | Object identity (same object in memory) | `[1, 2] is [1, 2]` | False |\n| `==` | Value equality (same content) | `[1, 2] == [1, 2]` | True |\n\n---\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\n- **`is`:** Checks if two variables point to the **same object**.\n- **`==`:** Checks if two variables have the **same value**.\n- Use `is` for identity checks, and `==` for value comparisons.\n\n```python\n# Example 1: Using `is` to check object identity\na = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a  # b points to the same object as a\nc = [1, 2, 3]  # c is a new object with the same value as a\n\nprint(a is b)  # Output: True (a and b are the same object)\nprint(a is c)  # Output: False (a and c are different objects in memory)\n\n# Example 2: Using `==` to check value equality\nprint(a == b)  # Output: True (a and b have the same value)\nprint(a == c)  # Output: True (a and c have the same value)\n\n# Example 3: Small integer caching\nx = 5\ny = 5\nz = int(5)\n\nprint(x == y)  # Output: True (x and y have the same value)\nprint(x is y)  # Output: True (x and y point to the same object due to caching)\nprint(x is z)  # Output: True (z is also cached as 5)\n\n# Example 4: Strings and caching\nstr1 = \"hello\"\nstr2 = \"hello\"\nstr3 = str(\"hello\")\n\nprint(str1 == str2)  # Output: True (same value)\nprint(str1 is str2)  # Output: True (same object, cached string)\nprint(str1 is str3)  # Output: True (cached string)\n\n```\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\n- **`is`:** Checks if two variables refer to the **same object** in memory.\n- **`==`:** Checks if two variables have the **same value**.\n\n**Use `is`** for identity checks.\n\n**Use `==`** for value comparisons.","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Identity vs equality","examples":[{"title":"Compare equal but distinct lists","code":"a = [1, 2]\nb = [1, 2]\nc = a\nprint(a == b)\nprint(a is b)\nprint(a is c)","output":"True\nFalse\nTrue","explanation":["a and b have equal contents but were created as different list objects. c is another name for the same object as a.","Identity is useful when shared ownership matters. It is not a replacement for comparing the logical values of records, strings, or numbers."]},{"title":"Compare a shallow copy at two levels","code":"a = [[1]]\nb = a.copy()\nprint(a == b)\nprint(a is b)\nprint(a[0] is b[0])","output":"True\nFalse\nTrue","explanation":["The outer containers are distinct but equal. Their first elements refer to the same nested list because the copy is shallow.","Identity comparisons at the relevant nesting level reveal sharing that a simple equality test cannot show."]},{"title":"Distinguish None from false-like values","code":"def describe(value):\n    if value is None:\n        return \"missing\"\n    return \"provided\"\n\nprint(describe(None))\nprint(describe(0))\nprint(describe([]))","output":"missing\nprovided\nprovided","explanation":["None is a specific singleton used here to mean absence. Zero and an empty list are false-like but are still supplied values.","Using if not value would combine all three cases. Choose the test that matches the actual data contract instead of treating truthiness and absence as interchangeable."]}],"summary":"== compares values according to equality rules. is compares whether two references point to the exact same object. Use is None for the missing-value sentinel.","minutes":5},{"id":30,"title":"How do you handle circular imports in Python?","body":"### **Handling Circular Imports in Python**\n\n**Circular imports** occur when two or more modules depend on each other, creating a cycle. This can cause errors because Python executes modules line by line, and circular references can lead to incomplete or broken imports.\n\n---\n\n### **How to Handle Circular Imports**\n\n1. **Reorganize Code:**\n    - Move shared functionality to a separate module to break the circular dependency.\n2. **Use `import` Inside Functions:**\n    - Import specific components where they are needed, instead of at the top of the file.\n3. **Use `if __name__ == \"__main__\"`:**\n    - Prevent certain code from being executed during import.\n\n---\n\n### **Example of Circular Import**\n\n### **Broken Circular Import**\n\n```python\n# File: module_a.py\nfrom module_b import func_b\n\ndef func_a():\n    print(\"Function A\")\n    func_b()\n\n# File: module_b.py\nfrom module_a import func_a\n\ndef func_b():\n    print(\"Function B\")\n    func_a()\n\n# Main file\nfrom module_a import func_a\nfunc_a()\n\n```\n\n**Error:**\n\n```jsx\njavascript\nCopy code\nImportError: cannot import name 'func_a' from partially initialized module 'module_a'\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Solution 1: Reorganize Code**\n\nMove the shared functionality into a third module to break the circular dependency.\n\n```python\n# File: shared.py\ndef shared_function():\n    print(\"Shared Function\")\n\n# File: module_a.py\nfrom shared import shared_function\n\ndef func_a():\n    print(\"Function A\")\n    shared_function()\n\n# File: module_b.py\nfrom shared import shared_function\n\ndef func_b():\n    print(\"Function B\")\n    shared_function()\n\n# Main file\nfrom module_a import func_a\nfrom module_b import func_b\n\nfunc_a()\nfunc_b()\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```vbnet\nvbnet\nCopy code\nFunction A\nShared Function\nFunction B\nShared Function\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Solution 2: Import Inside Functions**\n\nImport specific functions where they are needed.\n\n```python\n# File: module_a.py\ndef func_a():\n    print(\"Function A\")\n    from module_b import func_b  # Import inside the function\n    func_b()\n\n# File: module_b.py\ndef func_b():\n    print(\"Function B\")\n    from module_a import func_a  # Import inside the function\n    func_a()\n\n# Main file\nfrom module_a import func_a\n\nfunc_a()\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```css\ncss\nCopy code\nFunction A\nFunction B\nFunction A\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Solution 3: Use `if __name__ == \"__main__\"`**\n\nUse this construct to prevent the execution of import-sensitive code during the import process.\n\n```python\n# File: module_a.py\nfrom module_b import func_b\n\ndef func_a():\n    print(\"Function A\")\n    func_b()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    func_a()\n\n# File: module_b.py\nfrom module_a import func_a\n\ndef func_b():\n    print(\"Function B\")\n    func_a()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    func_b()\n\n# Main file\nfrom module_a import func_a\n\nfunc_a()\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```css\ncss\nCopy code\nFunction A\nFunction B\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\n1. **Reorganize Code:** Move shared functionality to a separate module.\n2. **Import Inside Functions:** Import where needed to delay the circular dependency.\n3. **Use `if __name__ == \"__main__\"`:** Prevent execution during imports.\n\nCircular imports can be avoided by thoughtful code organization and using delayed imports where necessary!\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\nTo handle circular imports in Python:\n\n1. **Reorganize code:** Move shared code to a separate module.\n2. **Import inside functions:** Import only when needed, not at the top of the file.\n3. **Use `if __name__ == \"__main__\"`:** Prevent code from running during imports.\n\n**Key Point:** Break the cycle by restructuring or delaying imports.","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Circular imports","examples":[{"title":"Share definitions through a neutral module","code":"# Single-file model of three modules; no files need to be created.\nfrom types import SimpleNamespace\n\ncommon = SimpleNamespace(prefix=\"user\")  # common.py\ndef module_a_label(name):                # module_a.py imports common\n    return common.prefix + \":\" + name\ndef module_b_label(name):                # module_b.py imports common\n    return common.prefix + \":\" + name.upper()\n\nprint(module_a_label(\"Ada\"))\nprint(module_b_label(\"Ada\"))","output":"user:Ada\nuser:ADA","explanation":["Both components depend on the shared definition rather than importing each other. The namespaces and comments model the dependency direction in one runnable file.","In a real project, put the shared definition in common.py and import it from both modules. This example demonstrates the refactor, not a real import cycle."]},{"title":"Perform an import only when an operation needs it","code":"def circle_area(radius):\n    from math import pi\n    return round(pi * radius * radius, 2)\n\nprint(circle_area(2))\nprint(circle_area(3))","output":"12.57\n28.27","explanation":["The import statement is inside the function, so it executes when the function is called rather than during the surrounding module’s initial top-level setup.","This illustrates deferred import timing with a safe standard-library dependency. Local imports can help a specific cycle, but do not repair a fundamentally tangled module design."]},{"title":"Use dependency injection to remove a reverse dependency","code":"def build_report(load_user):\n    user = load_user()\n    return \"Report for \" + user[\"name\"]\n\ndef load_user():\n    return {\"name\": \"Ada\"}\n\nprint(build_report(load_user))","output":"Report for Ada","explanation":["The report component receives its loader instead of importing the concrete data component. A composition layer wires the pieces together.","This removes the need for a reverse import and simplifies testing. It also avoids the separate bug of functions calling one another recursively without a stopping condition."]}],"summary":"Circular imports happen when modules depend on each other during initialization. Move shared definitions to a neutral module or defer a carefully chosen dependency.","minutes":4},{"id":31,"title":"Explain how `multiprocessing` differs from `threading` in Python.","body":"### **Difference Between Multiprocessing and Threading**\n\n1. **Multiprocessing:**\n    - Uses **multiple processes**.\n    - Each process has its **own memory space**.\n    - Suitable for **CPU-bound tasks** (e.g., mathematical computations).\n    - Can utilize multiple CPU cores.\n2. **Threading:**\n    - Uses **multiple threads** within the same process.\n    - Threads share the **same memory space**.\n    - Suitable for **I/O-bound tasks** (e.g., file operations, network requests).\n    - Limited by Python's **Global Interpreter Lock (GIL)**, so it doesn't fully utilize multiple CPU cores.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example: Multiprocessing vs Threading**\n\n### **Multiprocessing Example**\n\n```python\n\nfrom multiprocessing import Process\nimport os\n\ndef task(name):\n    print(f\"Task {name} running in process: {os.getpid()}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    processes = []\n    for i in range(3):\n        p = Process(target=task, args=(i,))\n        processes.append(p)\n        p.start()\n\n    for p in processes:\n        p.join()\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```arduino\n\nTask 0 running in process: 12345\nTask 1 running in process: 12346\nTask 2 running in process: 12347\n\n```\n\n- Each task runs in a **separate process** with its own process ID.\n\n---\n\n### **Threading Example**\n\n```python\n\nfrom threading import Thread\nimport threading\n\ndef task(name):\n    print(f\"Task {name} running in thread: {threading.current_thread().name}\")\n\nthreads = []\nfor i in range(3):\n    t = Thread(target=task, args=(i,))\n    threads.append(t)\n    t.start()\n\nfor t in threads:\n    t.join()\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```arduino\n\nTask 0 running in thread: Thread-1\nTask 1 running in thread: Thread-2\nTask 2 running in thread: Thread-3\n\n```\n\n- Each task runs in a **thread** within the same process.\n\n---\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\n| **Feature** | **Multiprocessing** | **Threading** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Memory | Separate for each process | Shared across threads |\n| Use Case | CPU-bound tasks | I/O-bound tasks |\n| CPU Core Utilization | Can use multiple cores | Limited by GIL |\n\nUse **multiprocessing** for heavy computations and **threading** for tasks waiting on external resources like files or networks.\n\n### **Detailed Conclusion**\n\n**Multiprocessing** and **Threading** are tools in Python to perform tasks concurrently, but they work differently and are suited for different purposes:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Multiprocessing**\n\n- **How it works:**\n    \n    Creates multiple **processes**, each with its own memory space. These processes run independently and can utilize multiple CPU cores.\n    \n- **Best for:**\n    \n    **CPU-bound tasks** like heavy computations or data processing because it bypasses Python's **Global Interpreter Lock (GIL)** and uses multiple cores.\n    \n- **Key Characteristics:**\n    - **Separate memory:** Processes don’t share memory, which avoids data corruption but requires inter-process communication.\n    - **Slower to start:** Creating processes has more overhead compared to threads.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Threading**\n\n- **How it works:**\n    \n    Creates multiple **threads** within the same process. Threads share the same memory space and run in parallel but are limited by the GIL.\n    \n- **Best for:**\n    \n    **I/O-bound tasks** like file reading/writing, API calls, or database queries where the program spends time waiting for external operations.\n    \n- **Key Characteristics:**\n    - **Shared memory:** Threads share data easily but need synchronization (like locks) to avoid data corruption.\n    - **Lighter:** Threads are faster to start and use less memory than processes.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Differences**\n\n| **Feature** | **Multiprocessing** | **Threading** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Memory Usage** | Each process has its own memory | Threads share memory |\n| **Best For** | CPU-bound tasks (heavy computation) | I/O-bound tasks (waiting tasks) |\n| **Parallelism** | True parallelism (uses multiple cores) | Limited by GIL (one thread runs at a time) |\n| **Overhead** | Higher (creating processes) | Lower (lighter threads) |\n| **Communication** | Requires inter-process communication (e.g., `Queue`) | Shared memory makes communication easier |\n\n---\n\n### **When to Use?**\n\n- Use **Multiprocessing** when:\n    - Your task is **CPU-intensive** and needs true parallelism.\n    - You need to utilize multiple CPU cores.\n    - Example: Large-scale matrix computations, video processing.\n- Use **Threading** when:\n    - Your task is **I/O-intensive** and spends time waiting for external resources.\n    - You want lightweight, faster-to-start concurrency.\n    - Example: File downloads, API requests, database queries.\n\n---\n\nBy understanding the nature of your task (CPU-bound vs I/O-bound), you can choose the right tool for efficient performance!\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\n- **Multiprocessing:**\n    \n    Use it for **CPU-heavy tasks** (e.g., calculations) because it uses multiple CPU cores and has separate memory for each process.\n    \n- **Threading:**\n    \n    Use it for **I/O-heavy tasks** (e.g., file or network operations) because threads are lightweight and share memory.\n    \n\n**Key Difference:**\n\n- Multiprocessing = **Multiple processes**, true parallelism.\n- Threading = **Multiple threads**, limited by Python's GIL.\n\nChoose based on your task: **CPU-bound → Multiprocessing**, **I/O-bound → Threading**.","category":"Concurrency","label":"Processes vs threads","examples":[{"title":"Share state deliberately between threads","code":"from threading import Thread, Lock\n\nvalues = []\nlock = Lock()\ndef work(n):\n    with lock:\n        values.append(n * n)\n\nthreads = [Thread(target=work, args=(n,)) for n in [2, 3]]\nfor thread in threads:\n    thread.start()\nfor thread in threads:\n    thread.join()\nprint(sorted(values))","output":"[4, 9]","explanation":["Both threads access the same values list and lock. join ensures work finishes before the main thread reads the completed data.","Sorting avoids making a promise about thread completion order. Shared memory is convenient but introduces synchronization requirements."]},{"title":"Return results from a process pool","code":"from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\n\ndef total(n):\n    return sum(range(n))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=2) as pool:\n        print(list(pool.map(total, [4, 5])))","output":"[6, 10]","explanation":["Each worker calculates its own result and sends it back through the pool. Save this as a script; the module-level function can be imported in spawned workers.","map preserves input order. These tiny jobs are too small to justify process overhead in practice, but show the structure for independent larger computations."]},{"title":"See that worker mutation does not edit the parent list","code":"from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\n\ndef extend(values):\n    values.append(3)\n    return values\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    original = [1, 2]\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=1) as pool:\n        result = pool.submit(extend, original).result()\n    print(original)\n    print(result)","output":"[1, 2]\n[1, 2, 3]","explanation":["The argument is serialized for the worker process, which changes its own received list. The parent’s original list stays unchanged.","The modified list must be explicitly returned or communicated. Shared-memory and manager facilities exist, but ordinary process arguments do not create shared mutable Python objects."]}],"summary":"Threads share a process and require coordination for shared state. Processes have separate memory and can execute CPU work independently, with communication and startup costs.","minutes":5},{"id":32,"title":"What are `namedtuples` in Python, and when would you use them?","body":"### **What Are `namedtuples` in Python?**\n\n`namedtuple` is a function from the `collections` module in Python. It creates a tuple-like object where fields can be accessed by **name** instead of index, making the code more readable and self-documenting.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Features**\n\n1. **Immutability:** Like tuples, `namedtuples` are immutable.\n2. **Field Names:** Fields can be accessed using **dot notation** (`obj.field_name`) or by index.\n3. **Readable Code:** Makes data handling more intuitive and readable compared to regular tuples.\n4. **Memory Efficient:** Uses less memory than a dictionary.\n\n---\n\n### **When to Use `Namedtuples`**\n\n- When you need a lightweight, immutable object to store data.\n- As a replacement for dictionaries or tuples when field names make code more readable.\n- For data that is not expected to change (e.g., representing a point in a coordinate system, or a record from a database).\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example**\n\n```python\n\nfrom collections import namedtuple\n\n# Define a namedtuple\nPoint = namedtuple('Point', ['x', 'y'])\n\n# Create an instance of the namedtuple\np1 = Point(10, 20)\n\n# Access fields by name\nprint(\"x-coordinate:\", p1.x)  # Output: x-coordinate: 10\nprint(\"y-coordinate:\", p1.y)  # Output: y-coordinate: 20\n\n# Access fields by index\nprint(\"x-coordinate (by index):\", p1[0])  # Output: x-coordinate (by index): 10\nprint(\"y-coordinate (by index):\", p1[1])  # Output: y-coordinate (by index): 20\n\n# Namedtuples are immutable\n# p1.x = 30  # Uncommenting this line will raise an AttributeError\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Output**\n\n```csharp\ncsharp\nCopy code\nx-coordinate: 10\ny-coordinate: 20\nx-coordinate (by index): 10\ny-coordinate (by index): 20\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Advantages of `Namedtuples` Over Tuples**\n\n### Using a Tuple\n\n```python\np = (10, 20)\nprint(\"x-coordinate:\", p[0])  # Output: x-coordinate: 10\nprint(\"y-coordinate:\", p[1])  # Output: y-coordinate: 20\n\n```\n\n**Problem:** Accessing by index (`p[0]`) is less readable and error-prone.\n\n### Using a `Namedtuple`\n\n```python\np = Point(10, 20)\nprint(\"x-coordinate:\", p.x)  # Output: x-coordinate: 10\n\n```\n\n**Advantage:** Accessing by name (`p.x`) is more intuitive and readable.\n\n---\n\n### **Example: Replacing a Dictionary**\n\n### Using a Dictionary\n\n```python\nperson = {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 30}\nprint(\"Name:\", person['name'])  # Output: Name: Alice\nprint(\"Age:\", person['age'])    # Output: Age: 30\n\n```\n\n**Problem:** Accessing by keys can be error-prone (`person['name']`).\n\n### Using a `Namedtuple`\n\n```python\nPerson = namedtuple('Person', ['name', 'age'])\nperson = Person('Alice', 30)\nprint(\"Name:\", person.name)  # Output: Name: Alice\nprint(\"Age:\", person.age)    # Output: Age: 30\n\n```\n\n**Advantage:** `person.name` is safer and more descriptive.\n\n---\n\n### **Simplest Takeaway**\n\n- `namedtuple` = Tuple with named fields for better readability.\n- Use it when you need lightweight, readable, and immutable objects.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import namedtuple\n\n# Define a namedtuple\nPoint = namedtuple('Point', ['x', 'y'])\n\n# Create an instance of the namedtuple\np1 = Point(10, 20)\n\n# Access fields by name\nprint(\"x-coordinate:\", p1.x)  # Output: x-coordinate: 10\nprint(\"y-coordinate:\", p1.y)  # Output: y-coordinate: 20\n\n# Access fields by index\nprint(\"x-coordinate (by index):\", p1[0])  # Output: x-coordinate (by index): 10\nprint(\"y-coordinate (by index):\", p1[1])  # Output: y-coordinate (by index): 20\n\n# Namedtuples are immutable\ntry:\n    p1.x = 30  # Attempt to change a value\nexcept AttributeError as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)  # Explanation: Namedtuples are immutable, so this raises an AttributeError\n    # Output: Error: can't set attribute\n\n# Another example: Person namedtuple\nPerson = namedtuple('Person', ['name', 'age'])\nperson = Person('Alice', 30)\n\nprint(\"Name:\", person.name)  # Output: Name: Alice (accessing by field name is easy and intuitive)\nprint(\"Age:\", person.age)    # Output: Age: 30\n\n```\n\n### Explanation Within the Code\n\n- The code uses a `namedtuple` to define structured data types with named fields.\n- Fields are accessed by name (`p1.x`) or index (`p1[0]`).\n- `Namedtuples` are immutable, so trying to change a value raises an error.\n- The `Person` `namedtuple` is another example to demonstrate real-world usage.\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion on `namedtuple`**\n\n- **`namedtuple`:** A tuple with named fields.\n- **Access:** Use field names instead of indexes.\n- **Immutable:** You can't change values after creation.","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Named tuples","examples":[{"title":"Read named and positional fields","code":"from collections import namedtuple\n\nPoint = namedtuple(\"Point\", \"x y\")\np = Point(2, 3)\nprint(p.x, p[1])\nprint(p)\nprint(tuple(p))","output":"2 3\nPoint(x=2, y=3)\n(2, 3)","explanation":["The field names improve readability while indexing and tuple conversion still work. namedtuple generates a tuple subclass for this record structure.","Use names when they make meaning clearer, especially when several same-typed fields would otherwise be easy to mix up."]},{"title":"Produce a modified record without mutating it","code":"from collections import namedtuple\n\nPoint = namedtuple(\"Point\", \"x y\")\np = Point(2, 3)\nq = p._replace(x=9)\nprint(p)\nprint(q)\ntry:\n    p.x = 5\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"Fields are immutable\")","output":"Point(x=2, y=3)\nPoint(x=9, y=3)\nFields are immutable","explanation":["_replace returns a new named tuple with selected fields changed. The original record retains its x value.","Direct assignment fails because tuple slots cannot be reassigned. The underscore-prefixed helper is part of the namedtuple API."]},{"title":"Use defaults and dictionary conversion","code":"from collections import namedtuple\n\nUser = namedtuple(\"User\", \"name active\", defaults=[True])\nuser = User(\"Ada\")\nprint(user.active)\nprint(dict(user._asdict()))","output":"True\n{'name': 'Ada', 'active': True}","explanation":["defaults supplies values for the rightmost fields, so active defaults to True when omitted. _asdict exposes the named fields as a mapping.","This is a shallow conversion: contained objects are not deep-copied. For richer validation or behavior, a dataclass or regular class may be more suitable."]}],"summary":"A named tuple is a tuple-like record whose fields also have names. The record is immutable, but its contained values may still be mutable.","minutes":5},{"id":33,"title":"What are coroutines, and how do they differ from regular functions?","body":"### Coroutines in Python\n\nCoroutines are a special type of function in Python that allow for asynchronous programming. Unlike regular functions, which execute from start to finish when called, coroutines can be paused and resumed, enabling them to handle asynchronous tasks like waiting for I/O operations or running tasks concurrently.\n\n### Key Differences Between Coroutines and Regular Functions:\n\n| **Feature** | **Regular Function** | **Coroutine** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Execution Flow | Runs from start to finish in one go. | Can be paused (`await`) and resumed later. |\n| Syntax | Uses `def` and returns values. | Uses `async def` and works with `await`. |\n| Context of Use | Synchronous programming. | Asynchronous programming (e.g., I/O tasks). |\n\n---\n\n### Example: Regular Function vs Coroutine\n\n### Regular Function Example\n\n```python\n\ndef greet():\n    print(\"Hello!\")\n    return \"Greetings\"\n\nresult = greet()\nprint(\"Returned:\", result)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```makefile\n\nHello!\nReturned: Greetings\n\n```\n\n- **Behavior**: The function `greet()` executes fully when called.\n\n---\n\n### Coroutine Example\n\n```python\n\nimport asyncio\n\n# Define a coroutine\nasync def greet():\n    print(\"Hello!\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)  # Simulate an asynchronous pause\n    print(\"How are you?\")\n    return \"Greetings from coroutine\"\n\n# Run the coroutine\nasync def main():\n    result = await greet()  # Await the coroutine to complete\n    print(\"Returned:\", result)\n\nasyncio.run(main())  # Run the main coroutine\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```sql\n\nHello!\n# (1-second pause)\nHow are you?\nReturned: Greetings from coroutine\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations:\n\n1. **`async def`**: Defines a coroutine.\n2. **`await`**: Pauses the coroutine to wait for the result of an asynchronous task (like `asyncio.sleep`).\n3. **`asyncio.run()`**: Executes the main coroutine, managing the event loop.\n\n---\n\n### Advantages of Coroutines\n\n1. **Concurrency**: Coroutines allow multiple tasks to run \"simultaneously\" without blocking each other.\n2. **Non-blocking**: Efficient for I/O-bound operations (e.g., reading files or making API calls).\n\n---\n\n### Advanced Example: Multiple Coroutines\n\n```python\n\nimport asyncio\n\n# Define two coroutines\nasync def task1():\n    print(\"Task 1 started\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # Simulate a 2-second delay\n    print(\"Task 1 completed\")\n\nasync def task2():\n    print(\"Task 2 started\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)  # Simulate a 1-second delay\n    print(\"Task 2 completed\")\n\n# Run multiple coroutines\nasync def main():\n    await asyncio.gather(task1(), task2())  # Run both tasks concurrently\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```arduino\narduino\nCopy code\nTask 1 started\nTask 2 started\nTask 2 completed\nTask 1 completed\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n- Regular functions execute completely when called.\n- Coroutines allow pausing and resuming, enabling asynchronous and concurrent programming.\n- Use `async def`, `await`, and `asyncio` for creating and running coroutines.\n\nThis approach is particularly useful in scenarios like web servers, real-time updates, or handling multiple I/O operations efficiently.\n\n### Conclusion\n\n- **Regular Functions**: Run from start to end without stopping.\n- **Coroutines**: Can pause (`await`) and resume later, useful for asynchronous tasks like waiting or multitasking.\n- **Key Difference**: Coroutines use `async def` and `await`, making them non-blocking and efficient for tasks like file reading, API calls, or running multiple tasks together.\n\n**Think of coroutines as \"smart functions\" that can take breaks and continue later.**","category":"Concurrency","label":"Coroutines","examples":[{"title":"Create a coroutine, then run it","code":"import asyncio\nimport inspect\n\nasync def answer():\n    return 7\n\ncoroutine = answer()\nprint(inspect.iscoroutine(coroutine))\nprint(asyncio.run(coroutine))","output":"True\n7","explanation":["answer() returns a coroutine object, not 7 immediately. asyncio.run creates an event loop, runs the coroutine, and returns its final result.","In an environment that already has an event loop, such as some notebooks, await answer() instead of nesting asyncio.run."]},{"title":"Schedule concurrent waits and retain result order","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def task(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return name.upper()\n\nasync def main():\n    results = await asyncio.gather(task(\"a\"), task(\"b\"))\n    print(results)\n\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"['A', 'B']","explanation":["gather schedules both awaitables and waits for their results. The output list follows argument order rather than completion order.","The tasks yield at sleep(0). CPU-heavy loops with no suspension would instead monopolize the event-loop thread until they finish."]},{"title":"Cancel a task and still run cleanup","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def worker():\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(60)\n    finally:\n        print(\"cleanup\")\n\nasync def main():\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker())\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    task.cancel()\n    try:\n        await task\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        print(\"cancelled\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"cleanup\ncancelled","explanation":["The initial sleep(0) gives worker a chance to enter its try block. Cancellation then interrupts its suspended sleep, triggering finally.","Awaiting the task observes CancelledError. The example does not wait 60 seconds: cancellation requests termination and the awaited task handles the unwind."]}],"summary":"Calling async def creates a coroutine. Awaiting or scheduling it runs the work; await lets other tasks proceed when the operation actually suspends.","minutes":4},{"id":34,"title":"What are descriptors, and how are they used in Python?","body":"### What are Descriptors?\n\nDescriptors are objects in Python that manage the behavior of attributes when they are accessed, modified, or deleted. They are a way to customize attribute access in a class.\n\nDescriptors are implemented by defining any of the following methods in a class:\n\n1. `__get__(self, instance, owner)` - Handles attribute access.\n2. `__set__(self, instance, value)` - Handles attribute modification.\n3. `__delete__(self, instance)` - Handles attribute deletion.\n\n### Why Use Descriptors?\n\n- To control how attributes are accessed and modified.\n- Useful for validation, type checking, or computed attributes.\n\n---\n\n### Example with Simple Explanation\n\nLet's build a descriptor that ensures an attribute is always positive.\n\n```python\n\n# Descriptor class\nclass PositiveNumber:\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner):\n        return instance.__dict__.get(self.name, 0)  # Retrieve the attribute value\n\n    def __set__(self, instance, value):\n        if value < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Value must be positive!\")  # Validation\n        instance.__dict__[self.name] = value  # Set the attribute value\n\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):\n        self.name = name  # Automatically set the attribute name\n\n# Class using the descriptor\nclass Product:\n    price = PositiveNumber()  # Use descriptor for 'price'\n\n# Example usage\nitem = Product()\n\n# Set a valid price\nitem.price = 100\nprint(\"Price:\", item.price)  # Accessing the price\n\n# Try setting a negative price (raises an error)\ntry:\n    item.price = -50\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```makefile\nmakefile\nCopy code\nPrice: 100\nError: Value must be positive!\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of Code\n\n1. **Descriptor (`PositiveNumber`)**:\n    - `__get__`: Retrieves the value of the attribute from the instance dictionary.\n    - `__set__`: Validates and sets the value in the instance dictionary.\n    - `__set_name__`: Automatically assigns the attribute name for use in the dictionary.\n2. **Using the Descriptor**:\n    - The `price` attribute of `Product` is managed by `PositiveNumber`.\n    - When you assign a value (`item.price = 100`), the `__set__` method is called.\n    - When you access the value (`item.price`), the `__get__` method is called.\n\n---\n\n### When to Use Descriptors?\n\n- When you need reusable attribute control logic (e.g., type checking, validation, computed properties).\n- When creating frameworks or libraries that require customization of attribute access.\n\n**Descriptors make attribute management more powerful and reusable compared to using regular methods or properties!**\n\n### **What is the Goal of the Code?**\n\nWe want to:\n\n1. Create a custom behavior for the `price` attribute in a `Product` class.\n2. Ensure that the `price` is always **positive**.\n3. Use Python's **descriptor protocol** to implement this behavior.\n\n---\n\n### **Code with Detailed Explanations**\n\n```python\n\n# Descriptor class\nclass PositiveNumber:\n    \"\"\"\n    This is a descriptor class that manages how an attribute behaves.\n    Specifically, it ensures that the attribute always has a positive value.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner):\n        \"\"\"\n        This method is called when the attribute (e.g., item.price) is accessed.\n\n        Parameters:\n        - instance: The object that owns the attribute (e.g., `item` in `item.price`).\n        - owner: The class to which the object belongs (e.g., `Product`).\n\n        Returns:\n        - The value of the attribute stored in the instance's dictionary.\n        \"\"\"\n        print(f\"Getting value of '{self.name}' from {instance}\")\n        # Use the instance's __dict__ to get the actual value.\n        return instance.__dict__.get(self.name, 0)\n\n    def __set__(self, instance, value):\n        \"\"\"\n        This method is called when the attribute (e.g., item.price) is assigned a value.\n\n        Parameters:\n        - instance: The object that owns the attribute (e.g., `item` in `item.price = 100`).\n        - value: The value being assigned to the attribute.\n\n        Behavior:\n        - If the value is negative, it raises a ValueError.\n        - If the value is valid, it stores it in the instance's dictionary.\n        \"\"\"\n        print(f\"Setting value of '{self.name}' to {value} in {instance}\")\n        if value < 0:\n            # Prevent negative values.\n            raise ValueError(\"Value must be positive!\")\n        # Store the value in the instance's dictionary under the attribute name.\n        instance.__dict__[self.name] = value\n\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):\n        \"\"\"\n        This method is automatically called when the descriptor is assigned to a class attribute.\n\n        Parameters:\n        - owner: The class in which the descriptor is used (e.g., `Product`).\n        - name: The name of the attribute being managed (e.g., 'price').\n\n        Behavior:\n        - Saves the attribute name for use in __get__ and __set__.\n        \"\"\"\n        print(f\"Setting name '{name}' in descriptor for class {owner}\")\n        self.name = name  # Save the name of the attribute (e.g., 'price').\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **How Does the Descriptor Work?**\n\n### 1. **`__set_name__` Method**:\n\n- When the `PositiveNumber` descriptor is assigned to the `price` attribute in the `Product` class:\n    \n    ```python\n    \n    price = PositiveNumber()\n    \n    ```\n    \n- Python automatically calls `__set_name__` with the class (`Product`) and the name of the attribute (`price`).\n- This allows the descriptor to remember the attribute’s name for later use in `__get__` and `__set__`.\n\n### 2. **`__set__` Method**:\n\n- When you assign a value to `price`:\n    \n    ```python\n    \n    item.price = 100\n    \n    ```\n    \n- The `__set__` method of the descriptor is called instead of directly setting the value in the object’s dictionary.\n- Inside `__set__`:\n    - It checks if the value is negative and raises an error if it is.\n    - If the value is valid, it stores it in the instance's `__dict__` with the attribute name (`price`) as the key.\n\n### 3. **`__get__` Method**:\n\n- When you access `price`:\n    \n    ```python\n    python\n    Copy code\n    print(item.price)\n    \n    ```\n    \n- The `__get__` method is called instead of directly retrieving the value from the object.\n- Inside `__get__`:\n    - It looks up the value in the instance's `__dict__` using the attribute name (`price`).\n\n---\n\n### **The Product Class Using the Descriptor**\n\n```python\n\nclass Product:\n    \"\"\"\n    A class that represents a product.\n    The price attribute is managed by the PositiveNumber descriptor.\n    \"\"\"\n    price = PositiveNumber()  # Assign the descriptor to the 'price' attribute.\n\n```\n\nHere’s what happens:\n\n1. When the `Product` class is created, the `PositiveNumber` descriptor is assigned to `price`.\n2. The `PositiveNumber` descriptor now controls how `price` behaves for all instances of `Product`.\n\n---\n\n### **Example Usage with Explanations**\n\n```python\n\n# Create an instance of Product\nitem = Product()\n\n# Set a valid price\nitem.price = 100  # __set__ is called\nprint(\"Price after setting valid value:\", item.price)  # __get__ is called\n\n```\n\n**What Happens:**\n\n1. `item.price = 100` calls `__set__` in `PositiveNumber`.\n    - It validates the value and sets it in `item.__dict__`.\n2. `print(item.price)` calls `__get__` in `PositiveNumber`.\n    - It retrieves the value from `item.__dict__`.\n\n**Output:**\n\n```csharp\n\nSetting value of 'price' to 100 in <__main__.Product object at 0x...>\nGetting value of 'price' from <__main__.Product object at 0x...>\nPrice after setting valid value: 100\n\n```\n\n---\n\n```python\n\n# Try setting a negative price\ntry:\n    item.price = -50  # __set__ is called and raises an error\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(\"Error when setting negative value:\", e)\n\n```\n\n**What Happens:**\n\n1. `item.price = -50` calls `__set__` in `PositiveNumber`.\n    - It detects that the value is negative and raises a `ValueError`.\n\n**Output:**\n\n```csharp\n\nSetting value of 'price' to -50 in <__main__.Product object at 0x...>\nError when setting negative value: Value must be positive!\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Summary of How It Works**\n\n1. **Descriptor Setup**:\n    - `PositiveNumber` is attached to the `price` attribute in `Product`.\n    - The descriptor manages how `price` is accessed or modified.\n2. **Accessing the Value**:\n    - `__get__` retrieves the value from the instance's `__dict__`.\n3. **Setting the Value**:\n    - `__set__` validates and then sets the value in the instance's `__dict__`.\n4. **Error Handling**:\n    - If a negative value is set, `__set__` raises an error.\n\nThis approach allows you to customize attribute behavior while keeping your class simple and clean.\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\n1. **What Are Descriptors?**\n    - Descriptors are objects in Python that control the behavior of attributes (e.g., accessing, modifying, or deleting them) in a class.\n2. **How Do They Work?**\n    - Descriptors implement one or more of these methods:\n        - `__get__(self, instance, owner)` → Handles attribute access.\n        - `__set__(self, instance, value)` → Handles attribute modification.\n        - `__delete__(self, instance)` → Handles attribute deletion.\n    - The `__set_name__` method is used to assign the attribute name during class creation.\n3. **Why Use Descriptors?**\n    - To add custom behavior to attributes (e.g., validation, type enforcement).\n    - To make reusable and clean code for managing attributes in multiple classes.\n4. **Key Benefits**:\n    - Centralized logic for attribute handling.\n    - Flexibility to add constraints or computed properties.\n5. **Real-World Usage**:\n    - Validation (e.g., ensuring positive values).\n    - Type checking (e.g., ensuring an attribute is an integer or string).\n    - Frameworks (e.g., Django uses descriptors for field validation in models).\n\nDescriptors are powerful tools for managing attributes dynamically and efficiently, especially in advanced use cases like validation and frameworks.","category":"Classes & design","label":"Descriptors","examples":[{"title":"Calculate a value through __get__","code":"class Double:\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner):\n        if instance is None:\n            return self\n        return instance.value * 2\n\nclass Number:\n    doubled = Double()\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n\nn = Number(4)\nprint(n.doubled)\nn.value = 7\nprint(n.doubled)","output":"8\n14","explanation":["Double is stored on the class, so reading n.doubled invokes its __get__ with n as the instance. The returned result uses the instance’s current state.","The instance-is-None branch handles access through Number.doubled. Descriptors should consider class access as well as instance access."]},{"title":"Reuse validation across attributes","code":"class Positive:\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):\n        self.storage = \"_\" + name\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner):\n        if instance is None:\n            return self\n        return getattr(instance, self.storage)\n    def __set__(self, instance, value):\n        if value <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Must be positive\")\n        setattr(instance, self.storage, value)\n\nclass Box:\n    width = Positive()\n    height = Positive()\n\nb = Box()\nb.width, b.height = 3, 4\nprint(b.width * b.height)\ntry:\n    b.width = 0\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)","output":"12\nMust be positive","explanation":["__set_name__ gives each descriptor its declared attribute name so it can store values separately. Assigning width invokes validation in __set__.","The actual values live on each Box instance under different backing names. The shared descriptor stores the rule and storage name, not one shared width for every box."]},{"title":"See data-descriptor precedence","code":"class Fixed:\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner):\n        return 10\n    def __set__(self, instance, value):\n        raise AttributeError(\"Read-only\")\n\nclass Item:\n    value = Fixed()\n\nitem = Item()\nitem.__dict__[\"value\"] = 99\nprint(item.value)\ntry:\n    item.value = 3\nexcept AttributeError as error:\n    print(error)","output":"10\nRead-only","explanation":["Defining __set__ makes Fixed a data descriptor. Its getter takes precedence over a same-named entry in the instance dictionary.","Normal assignment calls its setter and is rejected. A non-data descriptor that only defines __get__ has different precedence and can be shadowed by instance state."]}],"summary":"A descriptor is an object that customizes attribute access through __get__, __set__, or __delete__. Properties and bound methods use related descriptor machinery.","minutes":9},{"id":35,"title":"Explain `__slots__` and how they improve performance in Python.","body":"### **What is `__slots__` in Python?**\n\nIn Python, the `__slots__` attribute is used in a class to restrict the attributes that can be dynamically added to instances of the class. It prevents the creation of a default `__dict__` for storing instance attributes, thus:\n\n1. Reducing memory usage by avoiding the `__dict__` overhead.\n2. Improving performance by faster attribute access.\n\n---\n\n### **How Does `__slots__` Work?**\n\nWhen `__slots__` is defined, Python uses a static structure (like a tuple or array) to store the defined attributes instead of a dynamic dictionary. This saves memory and makes attribute access slightly faster.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example with Explanation**\n\nLet’s compare a class with and without `__slots__`.\n\n### **Without `__slots__`**\n\n```python\n\nclass WithoutSlots:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n# Create an instance\nperson1 = WithoutSlots(\"Alice\", 25)\n\n# Add a new attribute dynamically\nperson1.address = \"123 Street\"\nprint(\"Person1 Name:\", person1.name)\nprint(\"Person1 Age:\", person1.age)\nprint(\"Person1 Address:\", person1.address)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```yaml\n\nPerson1 Name: Alice\nPerson1 Age: 25\nPerson1 Address: 123 Street\n\n```\n\n### **With `__slots__`**\n\n```python\n\nclass WithSlots:\n    __slots__ = ['name', 'age']  # Only 'name' and 'age' are allowed as attributes\n\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n# Create an instance\nperson2 = WithSlots(\"Bob\", 30)\n\n# Try adding a new attribute dynamically\ntry:\n    person2.address = \"456 Avenue\"  # This will raise an error\nexcept AttributeError as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n\nprint(\"Person2 Name:\", person2.name)\nprint(\"Person2 Age:\", person2.age)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```tsx\n\nError: 'WithSlots' object has no attribute 'address'\nPerson2 Name: Bob\nPerson2 Age: 30\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Key Observations**\n\n1. **Dynamic Attribute Restriction**:\n    - In the `WithoutSlots` class, you can add attributes dynamically (e.g., `address`).\n    - In the `WithSlots` class, only attributes defined in `__slots__` can exist.\n2. **Memory Usage**:\n    - Classes with `__slots__` use less memory because they don’t create a `__dict__` for each instance.\n\n---\n\n### **Performance Comparison**\n\nLet’s measure memory usage:\n\n```python\n\nimport sys\n\nclass WithoutSlots:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\nclass WithSlots:\n    __slots__ = ['name', 'age']\n\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n# Create instances\nperson1 = WithoutSlots(\"Alice\", 25)\nperson2 = WithSlots(\"Bob\", 30)\n\n# Measure memory usage\nprint(\"Memory usage without __slots__:\", sys.getsizeof(person1.__dict__))\nprint(\"Memory usage with __slots__:\", sys.getsizeof(person2))\n\n```\n\n**Output (Approximate):**\n\n```markdown\n\nMemory usage without __slots__: 104\nMemory usage with __slots__: 48\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Advantages of `__slots__`**\n\n1. **Memory Optimization**: Saves memory for classes with many instances.\n2. **Faster Attribute Access**: No dictionary lookup required for attributes.\n3. **Prevents Attribute Misuse**: Restricts adding unintended or invalid attributes.\n\n---\n\n### **When to Use `__slots__`**\n\n- Use `__slots__` for classes where:\n    1. There are a large number of instances.\n    2. Attribute names are fixed and well-known in advance.\n    3. Memory usage is a concern.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\n`__slots__` is a powerful tool for memory and performance optimization in Python. It is especially useful in applications requiring many lightweight objects or where dynamic attribute addition is unnecessary. However, it reduces flexibility and should only be used when you need its benefits.\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\n- `__slots__` reduces memory usage by avoiding the creation of a `__dict__` for each object.\n- It makes attribute access faster and restricts adding new attributes not defined in `__slots__`.\n- Use it when you need lightweight objects with fixed attributes and want better performance.","category":"Memory & objects","label":"__slots__","examples":[{"title":"Restrict instance attributes","code":"class Point:\n    __slots__ = (\"x\", \"y\")\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x, self.y = x, y\n\np = Point(2, 3)\nprint(p.x, p.y)\nprint(hasattr(p, \"__dict__\"))\ntry:\n    p.z = 4\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"z is not a declared slot\")","output":"2 3\nFalse\nz is not a declared slot","explanation":["The class reserves storage for x and y without a normal per-instance attribute dictionary. Assigning an undeclared z therefore fails.","This can save memory across many small instances, but the exact saving depends on the Python implementation and class hierarchy."]},{"title":"Slots are still writable","code":"class Account:\n    __slots__ = (\"balance\",)\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self.balance = balance\n\na = Account(10)\na.balance += 5\nprint(a.balance)","output":"15","explanation":["A slot controls where an attribute can be stored; it is not a read-only declaration. Updating the declared balance attribute works normally.","Use properties, validation, or an appropriate immutable data design when you need restrictions on changing values."]},{"title":"Extend slots explicitly in subclasses","code":"class Base:\n    __slots__ = (\"x\",)\n\nclass Child(Base):\n    __slots__ = (\"y\",)\n\nc = Child()\nc.x, c.y = 1, 2\nprint(c.x + c.y)\nprint(hasattr(c, \"__dict__\"))","output":"3\nFalse","explanation":["Child inherits the x slot and adds its own y slot. Defining slots again avoids introducing an ordinary instance dictionary in this subclass.","A subclass that omits __slots__ normally gets a __dict__, so memory and attribute restrictions must be considered across the whole inheritance chain."]}],"summary":"__slots__ reserves named instance attributes and can reduce per-instance memory overhead. It does not automatically make instances immutable or faster in every workload.","minutes":4},{"id":36,"title":"What are the benefits and limitations of using Python’s `dataclasses`?","body":"### **What are Python's `dataclasses`?**\n\nPython’s `dataclasses` module (introduced in Python 3.7) provides a way to automatically generate common methods like `__init__`, `__repr__`, and `__eq__` for classes that primarily store data. This reduces boilerplate code and makes data modeling easier.\n\n---\n\n### **Benefits of Using `dataclasses`**\n\n1. **Less Boilerplate Code**:\n    - Automatically generates `__init__`, `__repr__`, and other methods.\n2. **Better Readability**:\n    - Cleaner and more concise class definitions.\n3. **Customizable**:\n    - Allows fine-grained control over field behavior (e.g., default values, immutability).\n4. **Type Annotations**:\n    - Encourages the use of type hints, making the code more robust.\n\n---\n\n### **Limitations of `dataclasses`**\n\n1. **Immutable Objects**:\n    - Immutability can be enforced with `frozen=True`, but it's not as strict or safe as `namedtuple`.\n2. **Not Suitable for All Classes**:\n    - Ideal for classes storing data, but less useful for behavior-heavy or logic-heavy classes.\n3. **Performance**:\n    - Slightly slower for initialization compared to manually optimized classes due to auto-generated methods.\n4. **Field Default Factory Complexity**:\n    - Using mutable defaults (like lists or dictionaries) requires `field(default_factory=...)`, which can be less intuitive.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example**\n\n### **Without `dataclasses`**\n\n```python\nclass Person:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f\"Person(name={self.name}, age={self.age})\"\n\n# Create an instance\nperson = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nprint(person)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```scss\nscss\nCopy code\nPerson(name=Alice, age=30)\n\n```\n\n### **With `dataclasses`**\n\n```python\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Person:\n    name: str\n    age: int\n\n# Create an instance\nperson = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nprint(person)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```scss\n\nPerson(name='Alice', age=30)\n\n```\n\n**Key Observations**:\n\n- `dataclasses` automatically generates the `__init__` and `__repr__` methods.\n- Less code, same functionality.\n\n---\n\n### **Advanced Features of `dataclasses`**\n\n1. **Default Values**:\n\n```python\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Product:\n    name: str\n    price: float = 0.0  # Default price\n\nitem = Product(\"Book\")\nprint(item)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```scss\nscss\nCopy code\nProduct(name='Book', price=0.0)\n\n```\n\n---\n\n1. **Immutability**:\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)  # Makes the class immutable\nclass Point:\n    x: int\n    y: int\n\npoint = Point(1, 2)\nprint(point)\n\n# Try modifying the point\ntry:\n    point.x = 3  # Raises an error\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```scss\nscss\nCopy code\nPoint(x=1, y=2)\nError: cannot assign to field 'x'\n\n```\n\n---\n\n1. **Default Factory for Mutable Fields**:\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Basket:\n    items: list = field(default_factory=list)  # Ensures a new list for each instance\n\nbasket1 = Basket()\nbasket2 = Basket()\nbasket1.items.append(\"Apple\")\nprint(\"Basket1:\", basket1.items)\nprint(\"Basket2:\", basket2.items)\n\n```\n\n**Output:**\n\n```vbnet\nvbnet\nCopy code\nBasket1: ['Apple']\nBasket2: []\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\n**Benefits**:\n\n- Simplifies code for data-centric classes.\n- Improves readability and reduces boilerplate.\n- Offers flexibility with default values, immutability, and type hints.\n\n**Limitations**:\n\n- Not ideal for logic-heavy classes.\n- Requires careful handling of mutable defaults.\n\n`dataclasses` are perfect for clean, efficient, and type-safe data modeling!\n\n### **Detailed Explanation with Code and Output**\n\nHere is a detailed explanation of Python's `dataclasses`, including how they simplify class creation, with inline code, outputs, and explanations.\n\n---\n\n### **Basic Example of `dataclass`**\n\n```python\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Person:\n    name: str  # Attribute with type hint\n    age: int   # Attribute with type hint\n\n# Create an instance of Person\nperson = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nprint(person)  # Output: Automatically calls the generated __repr__\n\n# Output:\n# Person(name='Alice', age=30)\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n1. The `@dataclass` decorator automatically generates:\n    - `__init__` → Initializes `name` and `age`.\n    - `__repr__` → Returns a string representation of the object.\n2. You don’t need to write the constructor or `__repr__` manually.\n\n---\n\n### **Default Values**\n\n```python\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Product:\n    name: str\n    price: float = 0.0  # Default value for price\n\n# Create instances with and without specifying price\nproduct1 = Product(\"Book\")\nproduct2 = Product(\"Laptop\", 1200.0)\n\nprint(product1)  # Output: Product(name='Book', price=0.0)\nprint(product2)  # Output: Product(name='Laptop', price=1200.0)\n\n# Output:\n# Product(name='Book', price=0.0)\n# Product(name='Laptop', price=1200.0)\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- Default values like `price=0.0` are automatically applied if the argument is not passed.\n- This makes defining default behaviors easier and cleaner.\n\n---\n\n### **Immutability with `frozen=True`**\n\n```python\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)  # Makes the class immutable\nclass Point:\n    x: int\n    y: int\n\n# Create an instance\npoint = Point(1, 2)\nprint(point)  # Output: Point(x=1, y=2)\n\n# Attempt to modify the instance\ntry:\n    point.x = 10  # This raises an AttributeError\nexcept AttributeError as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n\n# Output:\n# Point(x=1, y=2)\n# Error: cannot assign to field 'x'\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `frozen=True` makes instances immutable, preventing modifications to attributes after creation.\n- Useful for creating read-only or constant-like objects.\n\n---\n\n### **Mutable Defaults and `default_factory`**\n\n```python\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Basket:\n    items: list = field(default_factory=list)  # Ensures a unique list for each instance\n\n# Create two instances\nbasket1 = Basket()\nbasket2 = Basket()\n\n# Modify one instance\nbasket1.items.append(\"Apple\")\nprint(\"Basket1:\", basket1.items)  # Output: Basket1: ['Apple']\nprint(\"Basket2:\", basket2.items)  # Output: Basket2: []\n\n# Output:\n# Basket1: ['Apple']\n# Basket2: []\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- Using `default_factory` ensures that each instance gets its own unique list.\n- Without `default_factory`, all instances would share the same list (unintended behavior).\n\n---\n\n### **Comparisons and Equality**\n\n```python\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Person:\n    name: str\n    age: int\n\n# Create two instances with the same data\nperson1 = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nperson2 = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\n\n# Compare instances\nprint(person1 == person2)  # Output: True\n\n# Modify one instance\nperson2.age = 31\nprint(person1 == person2)  # Output: False\n\n# Output:\n# True\n# False\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `dataclasses` automatically generates the `__eq__` method.\n- Instances with the same data are considered equal.\n- Modifying one instance breaks the equality.\n\n---\n\n### **Limitations of `dataclasses`**\n\n1. **Not for Logic-Heavy Classes**:\n    - `dataclasses` are ideal for storing data but not for classes with significant business logic or behavior.\n2. **Cannot Dynamically Add Attributes** (with `frozen=True`):\n    \n    ```python\n    \n    @dataclass(frozen=True)\n    class Immutable:\n        value: int\n    obj = Immutable(10)\n    try:\n        obj.new_attr = \"Not allowed\"  # Raises AttributeError\n    except AttributeError as e:\n        print(\"Error:\", e)\n    # Output:\n    # Error: 'Immutable' object has no attribute 'new_attr'\n    \n    ```\n    \n\n---\n\n### **Summary**\n\n- **Benefits**:\n    - Reduces boilerplate code (`__init__`, `__repr__`, etc.).\n    - Encourages use of type hints for better readability and debugging.\n    - Supports features like immutability (`frozen=True`) and default values.\n- **Limitations**:\n    - Less suitable for logic-heavy classes.\n    - Mutable fields require careful handling (`default_factory`).\n\n`dataclasses` are best for simple data storage classes, ensuring code remains clean, efficient, and maintainable!","category":"Classes & design","label":"Dataclasses: benefits & limitations","examples":[{"title":"Generate an initializer, representation, and equality","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Point:\n    x: int\n    y: int = 0\n\nprint(Point(2))\nprint(Point(2, 3) == Point(2, 3))","output":"Point(x=2, y=0)\nTrue","explanation":["The decorator generates an initializer with y defaulting to zero, a readable repr, and field-based equality under normal defaults.","The annotations identify fields, but do not automatically enforce their types at runtime. Add validation if your object requires stricter guarantees."]},{"title":"Give every instance its own mutable default","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Basket:\n    items: list = field(default_factory=list)\n\na = Basket()\nb = Basket()\na.items.append(\"apple\")\nprint(a.items)\nprint(b.items)","output":"['apple']\n[]","explanation":["default_factory is called for each new Basket, producing separate lists. Mutating a’s list therefore leaves b’s list untouched.","A single shared mutable default would couple otherwise independent records. Factories make creation of fresh state explicit."]},{"title":"Make a frozen value object","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass, FrozenInstanceError\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Key:\n    code: str\n\nkey = Key(\"A\")\nprint({key: \"value\"}[Key(\"A\")])\ntry:\n    key.code = \"B\"\nexcept FrozenInstanceError:\n    print(\"Frozen field\")","output":"value\nFrozen field","explanation":["The frozen dataclass rejects ordinary field reassignment. With these hashable fields and normal equality settings, it also supports use as a dictionary key.","Freezing is not a recursive transformation of contained objects. A mutable field would still require care and could prevent hashing."]}],"summary":"A dataclass writes common data-object methods for you from annotated fields. You still decide validation, mutability, defaults, and application behavior.","minutes":6},{"id":37,"title":"What is the purpose of `functools.lru_cache`, and how does it work?","body":"### **What is `functools.lru_cache`?**\n\n`functools.lru_cache` is a decorator in Python used for **caching the results of function calls**. It stores the results of expensive or frequently used function calls, so subsequent calls with the same arguments return the cached result instead of re-computing.\n\n- **Purpose**:\n    - To improve performance by avoiding redundant computations.\n    - Especially useful for recursive or computationally expensive functions.\n\n---\n\n### **How Does `lru_cache` Work?**\n\n- **LRU** stands for **Least Recently Used**:\n    - The cache has a limited size (by default 128).\n    - If the cache exceeds its size, the least recently used entries are discarded.\n- **Cache Key**:\n    - Each unique function input forms a key, and the output is stored against this key.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example**\n\n### **Without `lru_cache`**\n\n```python\n\nimport time\n\ndef expensive_function(x):\n    print(f\"Computing {x}...\")\n    time.sleep(2)  # Simulate a time-consuming operation\n    return x * x\n\n# Call the function multiple times\nprint(expensive_function(2))\nprint(expensive_function(2))  # Recomputes\n\n# Output:\n# Computing 2...\n# 4\n# Computing 2...\n# 4\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- The function recalculates the result every time it’s called, even with the same input.\n\n---\n\n### **With `lru_cache`**\n\n```python\n\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nimport time\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=3)  # Cache up to 3 results\ndef expensive_function(x):\n    print(f\"Computing {x}...\")\n    time.sleep(2)  # Simulate a time-consuming operation\n    return x * x\n\n# Call the function multiple times\nprint(expensive_function(2))  # First call, computes and caches result\nprint(expensive_function(2))  # Second call, retrieves from cache\nprint(expensive_function(3))  # Computes and caches result\nprint(expensive_function(4))  # Computes and caches result\nprint(expensive_function(2))  # Still in cache, retrieves\nprint(expensive_function(5))  # Cache exceeds maxsize, least used (3) removed\nprint(expensive_function(3))  # Recomputes because it was removed from cache\n\n# Output:\n# Computing 2...\n# 4\n# 4\n# Computing 3...\n# 9\n# Computing 4...\n# 16\n# 4\n# Computing 5...\n# 25\n# Computing 3...\n# 9\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n1. **First Call**:\n    - `expensive_function(2)` is computed and stored in the cache.\n2. **Second Call**:\n    - `expensive_function(2)` retrieves the result from the cache (no re-computation).\n3. **Cache Eviction**:\n    - When the cache size exceeds 3, the least recently used result (for input `3`) is removed.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Features of `lru_cache`**\n\n1. **`maxsize` Parameter**:\n    - Limits the number of cached results.\n    - Default is 128; `None` means unlimited cache size.\n2. **Thread-Safe**:\n    - Can be used safely in multi-threaded environments.\n3. **Clear Cache**:\n    - Use `expensive_function.cache_clear()` to clear all cached results.\n\n---\n\n### **Advanced Example: Fibonacci with `lru_cache`**\n\nFibonacci is a classic example where caching improves performance significantly.\n\n```python\n\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)  # Unlimited cache size\ndef fibonacci(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return n\n    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2)\n\n# Compute Fibonacci numbers\nprint(fibonacci(10))  # Output: 55\nprint(fibonacci(20))  # Output: 6765\n\n# Check cache info\nprint(fibonacci.cache_info())  # Displays cache usage stats\n\n# Output:\n# 55\n# 6765\n# CacheInfo(hits=18, misses=21, maxsize=None, currsize=21)\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- Recursive calls with the same `n` use cached results, avoiding redundant computations.\n- `cache_info()` shows statistics like cache hits, misses, and current size.\n\n---\n\n### **Benefits of `lru_cache`**\n\n1. **Performance Improvement**:\n    - Reduces redundant computations for functions with repeated inputs.\n2. **Ease of Use**:\n    - Requires only one decorator (`@lru_cache`) to enable caching.\n3. **Customizable**:\n    - Cache size can be adjusted based on requirements.\n\n---\n\n### **Limitations of `lru_cache`**\n\n1. **Memory Usage**:\n    - Caching large results or using unlimited cache can consume a lot of memory.\n2. **Immutable Arguments**:\n    - Works only with `hashable` (immutable) arguments like numbers, strings, and tuples.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\n`functools.lru_cache` is a powerful tool for optimizing functions by caching their results. It’s especially useful for computationally expensive or recursive functions, making Python programs faster and more efficient with minimal effort.\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\n- `functools.lru_cache` is a decorator that **caches function results** to avoid redundant computations.\n- It improves performance by reusing results for the same inputs.\n- Cache size is configurable, and least recently used entries are removed when the cache is full.\n- Works best for **computationally expensive** or **recursive functions**.","category":"Functions","label":"The LRU cache","examples":[{"title":"Reuse an expensive result","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=2)\ndef square(n):\n    print(\"compute\", n)\n    return n * n\n\nprint(square(4))\nprint(square(4))\ninfo = square.cache_info()\nprint(info.hits, info.misses)","output":"compute 4\n16\n16\n1 1","explanation":["The first call misses the cache, executes square, and stores 16. The second call returns the stored result without executing the body again.","The compute message appears only once. cache_info reports one hit and one miss; those counts show reuse without relying on unreliable timing measurements."]},{"title":"Watch least-recently-used eviction","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=2)\ndef square(n):\n    print(\"compute\", n)\n    return n * n\n\nfor n in [1, 2, 1, 3, 2]:\n    square(n)\nprint(square.cache_info().hits, square.cache_info().misses)","output":"compute 1\ncompute 2\ncompute 3\ncompute 2\n1 4","explanation":["After caching 1 and 2, revisiting 1 makes it more recent. Inserting 3 therefore evicts 2, which must be computed again on the final request.","LRU tracks recency, not frequency or numerical order. A small cache can repeatedly recompute values when the working set exceeds its capacity."]},{"title":"Use hashable keys and clear stale results","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=4)\ndef total(values):\n    return sum(values)\n\nprint(total((2, 3)))\ntry:\n    total([2, 3])\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Use a hashable key\")\ntotal.cache_clear()\nprint(total.cache_info().currsize)","output":"5\nUse a hashable key\n0","explanation":["The tuple of integers is hashable and can form a cache key. A mutable list cannot, so the list call raises TypeError before entering the function body.","cache_clear removes existing entries and resets statistics. Prefer caching repeatable computations; mutable external state can otherwise make remembered answers stale."]}],"summary":"Memoization remembers answers to repeated calls. An LRU cache limits memory by discarding the answer that has gone unused the longest.","minutes":5},{"id":38,"title":"What are `magic methods` in Python, and why are they useful?","body":"### **What are Magic Methods in Python?**\n\nMagic methods (also called **dunder methods**, short for \"double underscore\") are special methods in Python with names surrounded by double underscores (e.g., `__init__`, `__str__`). They define how objects of a class behave with built-in operations like initialization, comparison, arithmetic, and more.\n\n---\n\n### **Why Are Magic Methods Useful?**\n\n1. **Custom Behavior**:\n    - Allow classes to define custom behavior for built-in operations.\n    - Example: Define how objects should be added (`__add__`) or compared (`__eq__`).\n2. **Readable and Intuitive Code**:\n    - Magic methods make object operations feel natural (e.g., `object1 + object2`).\n3. **Key for Python Features**:\n    - Essential for features like iteration (`__iter__`), string representation (`__str__`), and context managers (`__enter__`, `__exit__`).\n\n---\n\n### **Examples of Common Magic Methods**\n\n### 1. **`__init__` (Initialization)**\n\n```python\n\nclass Person:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\nperson = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nprint(person.name)  # Output: Alice\nprint(person.age)   # Output: 30\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `__init__` is called automatically when creating an instance of a class.\n- Used to initialize attributes of the object.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **`__str__` (String Representation)**\n\n```python\n\nclass Person:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f\"{self.name} is {self.age} years old.\"\n\nperson = Person(\"Bob\", 25)\nprint(person)  # Output: Bob is 25 years old.\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `__str__` defines the string representation of an object when used with `print()` or `str()`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **`__add__` (Addition)**\n\n```python\n\nclass Number:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        return self.value + other.value\n\nnum1 = Number(10)\nnum2 = Number(20)\nprint(num1 + num2)  # Output: 30\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `__add__` customizes the behavior of the `+` operator for class objects.\n- In this example, `num1 + num2` returns the sum of their `value` attributes.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **`__eq__` (Equality Check)**\n\n```python\n\nclass Person:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return self.name == other.name and self.age == other.age\n\nperson1 = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nperson2 = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nprint(person1 == person2)  # Output: True\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `__eq__` defines how equality (`==`) is checked between objects.\n- Here, two `Person` objects are equal if their `name` and `age` are the same.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **`__len__` (Length of Object)**\n\n```python\n\nclass Group:\n    def __init__(self, members):\n        self.members = members\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.members)\n\ngroup = Group([\"Alice\", \"Bob\", \"Charlie\"])\nprint(len(group))  # Output: 3\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `__len__` is used to define the behavior of `len()` for a class.\n- Here, it returns the number of members in the group.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **`__getitem__` (Indexing)**\n\n```python\n\nclass Team:\n    def __init__(self, members):\n        self.members = members\n\n    def __getitem__(self, index):\n        return self.members[index]\n\nteam = Team([\"Alice\", \"Bob\", \"Charlie\"])\nprint(team[1])  # Output: Bob\n\n```\n\n**Explanation**:\n\n- `__getitem__` allows objects to support indexing like lists or dictionaries.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\n- **Magic Methods**:\n    - Enable classes to integrate seamlessly with Python's built-in features.\n    - Examples: `__init__` (initialization), `__add__` (addition), `__str__` (string representation).\n- **Why Useful?**\n    - Make objects more intuitive and natural to use.\n    - Provide flexibility to customize behavior for operators and built-in functions.\n\nMagic methods make Python a highly expressive and readable language!\n\nHere’s a **detailed explanation with code, output, and inline explanations** for understanding Python's magic methods.\n\n---\n\n### **1. `__init__`: Initialization**\n\n```python\n\nclass Person:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        \"\"\"\n        Magic method called when a new object is created.\n        Initializes the object's attributes.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n# Create an instance of Person\nperson = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\n\n# Access attributes\nprint(person.name)  # Output: Alice\nprint(person.age)   # Output: 30\n\n# Output:\n# Alice\n# 30\n\n# Explanation:\n# `__init__` is called automatically when creating an object.\n# It sets the initial state of the object (e.g., name and age).\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. `__str__`: String Representation**\n\n```python\n\nclass Person:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Magic method to define what should be returned when the object\n        is converted to a string (e.g., by print()).\n        \"\"\"\n        return f\"{self.name} is {self.age} years old.\"\n\nperson = Person(\"Bob\", 25)\nprint(person)  # Output: Bob is 25 years old.\n\n# Output:\n# Bob is 25 years old.\n\n# Explanation:\n# `__str__` defines the string representation of an object.\n# It's automatically called when using `print()` or `str()` on the object.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **3. `__add__`: Addition**\n\n```python\n\nclass Number:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        \"\"\"\n        Magic method to define the behavior of the `+` operator.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.value + other.value\n\nnum1 = Number(10)\nnum2 = Number(20)\n\n# Use the + operator\nprint(num1 + num2)  # Output: 30\n\n# Output:\n# 30\n\n# Explanation:\n# `__add__` is called when using the `+` operator between two objects.\n# In this case, it adds the `value` attributes of the two `Number` objects.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **4. `__eq__`: Equality Check**\n\n```python\n\nclass Person:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        \"\"\"\n        Magic method to define the behavior of the `==` operator.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.name == other.name and self.age == other.age\n\nperson1 = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nperson2 = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nperson3 = Person(\"Bob\", 40)\n\n# Check equality\nprint(person1 == person2)  # Output: True\nprint(person1 == person3)  # Output: False\n\n# Output:\n# True\n# False\n\n# Explanation:\n# `__eq__` is called when using the `==` operator.\n# It compares the attributes of the objects to determine equality.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **5. `__len__`: Length**\n\n```python\n\nclass Group:\n    def __init__(self, members):\n        self.members = members\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Magic method to define the behavior of the `len()` function.\n        \"\"\"\n        return len(self.members)\n\ngroup = Group([\"Alice\", \"Bob\", \"Charlie\"])\n\n# Get the length of the group\nprint(len(group))  # Output: 3\n\n# Output:\n# 3\n\n# Explanation:\n# `__len__` is called when using `len()` on an object.\n# It returns the number of members in the group.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **6. `__getitem__`: Indexing**\n\n```python\n\nclass Team:\n    def __init__(self, members):\n        self.members = members\n\n    def __getitem__(self, index):\n        \"\"\"\n        Magic method to define the behavior of indexing (e.g., obj[index]).\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.members[index]\n\nteam = Team([\"Alice\", \"Bob\", \"Charlie\"])\n\n# Access members using indexing\nprint(team[0])  # Output: Alice\nprint(team[2])  # Output: Charlie\n\n# Output:\n# Alice\n# Charlie\n\n# Explanation:\n# `__getitem__` is called when using indexing on an object.\n# It retrieves the member at the specified index.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **7. `__repr__`: Developer-Friendly String Representation**\n\n```python\n\nclass Person:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Magic method for a developer-friendly string representation.\n        Called when using `repr(obj)` or in interactive shells.\n        \"\"\"\n        return f\"Person(name={self.name!r}, age={self.age!r})\"\n\nperson = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nprint(repr(person))  # Output: Person(name='Alice', age=30)\n\n# Output:\n# Person(name='Alice', age=30)\n\n# Explanation:\n# `__repr__` is called when using `repr()` or inspecting an object.\n# It provides a detailed string for debugging or logging.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **8. `__call__`: Callable Objects**\n\n```python\n\nclass Greeter:\n    def __call__(self, name):\n        \"\"\"\n        Magic method to make an object callable like a function.\n        \"\"\"\n        return f\"Hello, {name}!\"\n\ngreeter = Greeter()\n\n# Call the object like a function\nprint(greeter(\"Alice\"))  # Output: Hello, Alice!\n\n# Output:\n# Hello, Alice!\n\n# Explanation:\n# `__call__` is called when an object is used as a function.\n# It enables objects to behave like callable functions.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\nMagic methods make Python classes powerful and expressive by allowing custom behavior for built-in operations. Common examples include:\n\n- `__init__`: Initializes objects.\n- `__str__` and `__repr__`: String representations.\n- `__add__`: Custom addition.\n- `__eq__`: Equality checks.\n- `__len__` and `__getitem__`: Length and indexing.\n\nMagic methods allow you to design intuitive and Pythonic classes while keeping your code clean and readable.","category":"Classes & design","label":"Magic methods","examples":[{"title":"Support len and indexing","code":"class Team:\n    def __init__(self, names):\n        self.names = list(names)\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.names)\n    def __getitem__(self, index):\n        return self.names[index]\n\nteam = Team([\"Ada\", \"Grace\"])\nprint(len(team))\nprint(team[1])","output":"2\nGrace","explanation":["len(team) uses __len__, and team[1] uses __getitem__. The methods delegate to the internal list.","These hooks let your type fit ordinary Python expressions. Keep their behavior unsurprising: indexing should select an element and length should describe a non-negative size."]},{"title":"Define addition for a small value type","code":"class Score:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Score):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return Score(self.value + other.value)\n\nprint((Score(2) + Score(3)).value)\ntry:\n    Score(2) + 3\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Unsupported operands\")","output":"5\nUnsupported operands","explanation":["The + operator finds __add__. It returns a new Score for a supported pair rather than mutating either input.","NotImplemented tells Python that this operand combination is unsupported, allowing other operator protocol steps before a final TypeError."]},{"title":"Control truthiness through length","code":"class Basket:\n    def __init__(self, items):\n        self.items = items\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.items)\n\nprint(bool(Basket([])))\nprint(bool(Basket([\"apple\"])))","output":"False\nTrue","explanation":["When __bool__ is not defined, Python can use __len__ to determine truthiness: zero is false and a positive length is true.","This makes if basket behave like if list. A separate __bool__ can override the meaning when length is not the right signal."]}],"summary":"Dunder methods connect your class to Python syntax and built-in operations. Implement the protocol that matches the behavior your object naturally supports.","minutes":8},{"id":39,"title":"What are `frozen sets`, and when would you use them?","body":"### **What Are Frozen Sets?**\n\nIn Python, a **frozen set** is an immutable version of a regular set. Unlike a regular set, which can be modified (e.g., adding or removing elements), a frozen set is **immutable** and cannot be changed after creation.\n\nFrozen sets are part of Python’s **set data type family**, and they support operations like union, intersection, and difference.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Features of Frozen Sets**\n\n1. **Immutable**: Cannot be modified after creation.\n2. **`Hashable`**: Can be used as keys in dictionaries or elements in other sets.\n3. **Subset of Set Operations**: Supports operations like union, intersection, and difference but not modifying methods like `add` or `remove`.\n\n---\n\n### **Why Use Frozen Sets?**\n\n1. **Immutable Data**:\n    - When you want a set of items to remain constant.\n    - Useful for creating read-only sets.\n2. **`Hashable` Objects**:\n    - Can be used as keys in dictionaries or elements of another set (unlike mutable sets).\n3. **Ensuring Data Integrity**:\n    - Prevent accidental modification of data.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Examples**\n\n### **1. Creating a Frozen Set**\n\n```python\n\n# Create a frozen set\nfrozen_set = frozenset([1, 2, 3, 4])\n\n# Access elements or check membership\nprint(frozen_set)            # Output: frozenset({1, 2, 3, 4})\nprint(2 in frozen_set)       # Output: True\n\n# Output:\n# frozenset({1, 2, 3, 4})\n# True\n\n# Explanation:\n# `frozenset` is created from an iterable (e.g., list).\n# You can check membership like in a regular set.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. Frozen Sets Are Immutable**\n\n```python\n\n# Create a frozen set\nfrozen_set = frozenset([1, 2, 3, 4])\n\n# Try modifying the frozen set\ntry:\n    frozen_set.add(5)  # Attempt to add an element\nexcept AttributeError as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n\n# Output:\n# Error: 'frozenset' object has no attribute 'add'\n\n# Explanation:\n# Frozen sets do not support modifying methods like `add` or `remove`.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **3. Using Frozen Sets in a Dictionary**\n\n```python\n\n# Use a frozen set as a dictionary key\nfrozen_set = frozenset([1, 2, 3])\nmy_dict = {frozen_set: \"immutable set\"}\n\n# Access the value\nprint(my_dict[frozen_set])  # Output: immutable set\n\n# Output:\n# immutable set\n\n# Explanation:\n# A frozen set is hashable and can be used as a dictionary key, unlike a regular set.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **4. Set Operations with Frozen Sets**\n\n```python\n\n# Create frozen sets\nfrozen_set1 = frozenset([1, 2, 3])\nfrozen_set2 = frozenset([3, 4, 5])\n\n# Perform set operations\nunion_result = frozen_set1 | frozen_set2         # Union\nintersection_result = frozen_set1 & frozen_set2 # Intersection\ndifference_result = frozen_set1 - frozen_set2   # Difference\n\nprint(\"Union:\", union_result)                   # Output: frozenset({1, 2, 3, 4, 5})\nprint(\"Intersection:\", intersection_result)     # Output: frozenset({3})\nprint(\"Difference:\", difference_result)         # Output: frozenset({1, 2})\n\n# Output:\n# Union: frozenset({1, 2, 3, 4, 5})\n# Intersection: frozenset({3})\n# Difference: frozenset({1, 2})\n\n# Explanation:\n# Frozen sets support all set operations (union, intersection, difference) like regular sets.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **5. Nested Sets with Frozen Sets**\n\n```python\n\n# Create a set containing frozen sets\nnested_set = {frozenset([1, 2]), frozenset([3, 4])}\nprint(nested_set)  # Output: {frozenset({1, 2}), frozenset({3, 4})}\n\n# Output:\n# {frozenset({1, 2}), frozenset({3, 4})}\n\n# Explanation:\n# Frozen sets, being hashable, can be elements of another set, unlike regular sets.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **When to Use Frozen Sets**\n\n1. **Immutable Collections**:\n    - When you need to ensure the set's contents cannot change.\n2. **`Hashable` Requirements**:\n    - Use frozen sets as dictionary keys or elements of other sets.\n3. **Ensuring Consistency**:\n    - Prevent accidental modification of critical data.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\n- Frozen sets are **immutable** and **`hashable`** sets.\n- Use them when you need a **read-only set** or when working with **nested sets** or **dictionary keys**.\n- While frozen sets cannot be modified, they support common set operations like union and intersection, making them versatile for immutable collections.","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Frozen sets","examples":[{"title":"Remove duplicates without depending on display order","code":"values = frozenset([3, 1, 3, 2])\nprint(sorted(values))\nprint(2 in values)\nprint(len(values))","output":"[1, 2, 3]\nTrue\n3","explanation":["Like a set, frozenset retains unique elements and supports membership checks. It does not define an application-level sorted iteration order.","Sorting only for display makes the expected output stable. The frozenset itself remains unordered and immutable."]},{"title":"Use a set of tags as a dictionary key","code":"routes = {frozenset([\"read\", \"write\"]): \"editor\"}\nkey = frozenset([\"write\", \"read\"])\nprint(routes[key])\nprint(key == frozenset([\"read\", \"write\"]))","output":"editor\nTrue","explanation":["Set equality ignores input ordering. Both frozensets represent the same pair of permissions and compare equal as keys.","Every member must be hashable. A frozenset containing a mutable list cannot be created just by freezing the outer container."]},{"title":"Return new sets instead of mutating the old one","code":"a = frozenset([1, 2])\nb = frozenset([2, 3])\nprint(sorted(a | b))\nprint(sorted(a & b))\nprint(sorted(a - b))\ntry:\n    a.add(4)\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"No mutating add method\")","output":"[1, 2, 3]\n[2]\n[1]\nNo mutating add method","explanation":["Union, intersection, and difference calculate new results while leaving a and b unchanged. Sorting makes the displayed values deterministic.","The absent add method reflects immutability. Rebind a name to a newly computed frozenset when the logical value should change."]}],"summary":"frozenset is an immutable set of unique, hashable elements. It supports set operations and can itself be a dictionary key or an element of another set.","minutes":4},{"id":40,"title":"Explain the `@classmethod` and `@staticmethod` decorators and their differences.","body":"### **What are `@classmethod` and `@staticmethod` in Python?**\n\nBoth `@classmethod` and `@staticmethod` are decorators used to define methods in a class, but they work differently.\n\n---\n\n### **1. `@classmethod`**\n\n- A `@classmethod` is bound to the **class**, not an instance of the class.\n- It can access and modify class-level attributes but not instance attributes.\n- The first parameter is `cls`, representing the class.\n\n---\n\n### **2. `@staticmethod`**\n\n- A `@staticmethod` is not bound to the class or its instances.\n- It does not take `self` or `cls` as the first argument.\n- Used for utility functions that do not need access to class or instance attributes.\n\n---\n\n### **Differences Between `@classmethod` and `@staticmethod`**\n\n| Feature | `@classmethod` | `@staticmethod` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Bound To** | Class (`cls`) | Neither class nor instance |\n| **Access** | Can access/modify class attributes | Cannot access class or instance attributes |\n| **Usage** | Methods that work with class-level data | General utility functions |\n\n---\n\n### **Code Examples**\n\n### **1. `@classmethod` Example**\n\n```python\n\nclass Employee:\n    company_name = \"TechCorp\"  # Class attribute\n\n    @classmethod\n    def set_company_name(cls, name):\n        \"\"\"\n        A class method to modify the class attribute.\n        \"\"\"\n        cls.company_name = name\n\n# Access and modify the class attribute using a class method\nprint(\"Before:\", Employee.company_name)  # Output: Before: TechCorp\nEmployee.set_company_name(\"InnovateTech\")\nprint(\"After:\", Employee.company_name)   # Output: After: InnovateTech\n\n# Output:\n# Before: TechCorp\n# After: InnovateTech\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `set_company_name` is a class method.\n# - It modifies the class attribute `company_name` for all instances.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. `@staticmethod` Example**\n\n```python\n\nclass MathUtility:\n    @staticmethod\n    def add(a, b):\n        \"\"\"\n        A static method that adds two numbers.\n        \"\"\"\n        return a + b\n\n# Call the static method\nprint(\"Addition:\", MathUtility.add(5, 10))  # Output: Addition: 15\n\n# Output:\n# Addition: 15\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `add` is a static method.\n# - It performs a utility function unrelated to any specific class or instance data.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **3. `@classmethod` vs `@staticmethod`**\n\n```python\n\nclass Employee:\n    company_name = \"TechCorp\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def get_company_name(cls):\n        \"\"\"\n        Class method to access the class attribute.\n        \"\"\"\n        return cls.company_name\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def greet():\n        \"\"\"\n        Static method for general-purpose behavior.\n        \"\"\"\n        return \"Welcome to the company!\"\n\n# Call the class method\nprint(Employee.get_company_name())  # Output: TechCorp\n\n# Call the static method\nprint(Employee.greet())  # Output: Welcome to the company!\n\n# Output:\n# TechCorp\n# Welcome to the company!\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `get_company_name` accesses the class attribute using `cls`.\n# - `greet` is a standalone utility function that doesn't need class or instance data.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Key Points**\n\n1. **`@classmethod`**:\n    - Used when you need to work with class-level data (`cls`).\n    - Useful for factory methods or modifying class attributes.\n2. **`@staticmethod`**:\n    - Used for utility functions that don’t depend on the class or its instances.\n3. **Differences**:\n    - `@classmethod` takes `cls` as the first argument and works with class-level data.\n    - `@staticmethod` does not take `cls` or `self` and works independently of the class.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\n- Use **`@classmethod`** when you need access to the class itself (`cls`), such as modifying class attributes.\n- Use **`@staticmethod`** for utility or helper methods that don’t need to interact with the class or its instances.","category":"Classes & design","label":"Class & static method decorators","examples":[{"title":"Compare all three kinds of method","code":"class Demo:\n    label = \"class\"\n    def instance(self):\n        return self.label\n    @classmethod\n    def class_name(cls):\n        return cls.__name__\n    @staticmethod\n    def add(a, b):\n        return a + b\n\nd = Demo()\nd.label = \"instance\"\nprint(d.instance())\nprint(Demo.class_name())\nprint(Demo.add(2, 3))","output":"instance\nDemo\n5","explanation":["The instance method reads d’s own label. The class method receives Demo and reads its name. The static method just uses the explicitly supplied numbers.","A static method does not automatically know the instance or calling class. It groups a utility with the class without binding self or cls."]},{"title":"Make an inheritance-friendly alternate constructor","code":"class User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n    @classmethod\n    def from_text(cls, text):\n        return cls(text.strip())\n\nclass Admin(User):\n    pass\n\nuser = Admin.from_text(\" Ada \")\nprint(type(user).__name__)\nprint(user.name)","output":"Admin\nAda","explanation":["Calling the inherited class method through Admin binds cls to Admin. Constructing cls(...) therefore returns the appropriate subclass.","Hard-coding User(...) would lose this behavior. Alternate constructors are a common reason to choose classmethod over staticmethod."]},{"title":"Keep utilities independent from state","code":"class Temperature:\n    @staticmethod\n    def celsius_to_fahrenheit(celsius):\n        return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32\n\nprint(Temperature.celsius_to_fahrenheit(0))\nprint(Temperature().celsius_to_fahrenheit(100))","output":"32.0\n212.0","explanation":["Both calls pass only the explicit temperature value; no instance is injected when the static method is accessed through an instance.","The utility could also be a module-level function. Keeping it on the class is an organizational choice, not a requirement for accessing object state."]}],"summary":"An instance method receives self, a class method receives cls, and a static method receives neither automatically. Choose based on which state the method needs.","minutes":4},{"id":41,"title":"What is the `__del__` method in Python, and why is it discouraged in certain cases?","body":"### **What is the `__del__` Method in Python?**\n\n- The `__del__` method in Python is a special method called a **destructor**.\n- It is invoked automatically when an object is about to be destroyed (i.e., garbage collected).\n- Typically used to clean up resources (e.g., closing files or database connections).\n\n---\n\n### **Syntax**\n\n```python\n\ndef __del__(self):\n    # Clean up resources\n    print(f\"Object {self} is being destroyed.\")\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Why Is `__del__` Discouraged?**\n\n1. **Unpredictable Timing**:\n    - Python uses garbage collection, so the exact time when `__del__` is called is unpredictable.\n    - Example: When the program ends, some objects may not be garbage collected immediately.\n2. **Circular References**:\n    - Objects involved in circular references may not have their `__del__` method called, leading to potential resource leaks.\n3. **Error Handling Issues**:\n    - Exceptions raised in `__del__` are ignored, which can make debugging difficult.\n4. **Better Alternatives**:\n    - Use `with` statements and context managers (`__enter__` and `__exit__`) for resource management instead of relying on `__del__`.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Example**\n\n### **Example of `__del__`**\n\n```python\n\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        print(f\"Object {self.name} created.\")\n\n    def __del__(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Destructor to clean up resources.\n        \"\"\"\n        print(f\"Object {self.name} is being destroyed.\")\n\n# Create an object\nobj = MyClass(\"TestObject\")\nprint(\"Doing some work...\")\n\n# Delete the object\ndel obj\n\nprint(\"End of program.\")\n\n# Output:\n# Object TestObject created.\n# Doing some work...\n# Object TestObject is being destroyed.\n# End of program.\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `__init__` initializes the object.\n# - `__del__` is called automatically when the object is deleted using `del`.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Circular Reference Issue**\n\n```python\n\nclass Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\n    def __del__(self):\n        print(f\"Node with value {self.value} is being destroyed.\")\n\n# Create circular references\nnode1 = Node(1)\nnode2 = Node(2)\n\nnode1.next = node2\nnode2.next = node1\n\n# Delete the references\ndel node1\ndel node2\n\nprint(\"End of program.\")\n\n# Output:\n# End of program.\n\n# Explanation:\n# - Circular references prevent the `__del__` method from being called.\n# - The garbage collector cannot resolve circular references automatically.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Better Alternative: Context Managers**\n\nInstead of using `__del__`, use context managers for deterministic resource management.\n\n```python\n\nclass FileManager:\n    def __init__(self, file_name):\n        self.file = open(file_name, \"w\")\n        print(f\"File {file_name} opened.\")\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        return self.file\n\n    def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc_value, traceback):\n        self.file.close()\n        print(\"File closed.\")\n\n# Use with statement\nwith FileManager(\"example.txt\") as file:\n    file.write(\"Hello, World!\")\n\n# Output:\n# File example.txt opened.\n# File closed.\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `__enter__` opens the resource.\n# - `__exit__` ensures the resource is closed, even if an exception occurs.\n# - This is more predictable and safer than using `__del__`.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Key Points**\n\n1. **`__del__` Use Case**:\n    - Useful for cleanup tasks but should be avoided due to unpredictable behavior.\n2. **Issues with `__del__`**:\n    - Timing is not guaranteed (depends on garbage collection).\n    - Circular references can prevent `__del__` from being called.\n3. **Preferred Approach**:\n    - Use `with` statements and context managers for predictable and reliable resource management.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\nThe `__del__` method can be used for cleanup tasks but is discouraged due to its unpredictable timing and issues with circular references. Instead, use context managers (`with` statements) for resource management, which provide more control and reliability.","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Finalizers & __del__","examples":[{"title":"Deleting a name is not deleting every reference","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Item:\n    pass\n\na = Item()\nb = a\nref = weakref.ref(a)\ndel a\nprint(ref() is b)\ndel b\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["The first del removes only the a binding. Since b still owns the same instance, the object remains alive.","The example uses a weak reference to observe lifetime without relying on finalizer print timing, which is not a portable cleanup guarantee."]},{"title":"Use a context manager for predictable cleanup","code":"class Resource:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"open\")\n        return self\n    def __exit__(self, kind, value, traceback):\n        print(\"close\")\n        return False\n\ntry:\n    with Resource():\n        print(\"work\")\n        raise ValueError(\"problem\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"handled\")","output":"open\nwork\nclose\nhandled","explanation":["__exit__ runs as the with block exits, including during exception propagation. Returning False keeps the exception active for the outer handler.","This gives a defined cleanup boundary. Relying on __del__ would tie cleanup to object lifetime and interpreter behavior instead."]},{"title":"Register an explicit finalization callback","code":"import weakref\n\nclass Owner:\n    pass\n\nowner = Owner()\ncleanup = weakref.finalize(owner, print, \"released\")\nprint(cleanup.alive)\ncleanup()\nprint(cleanup.alive)\ncleanup()","output":"True\nreleased\nFalse","explanation":["weakref.finalize creates a callback that can also be invoked explicitly. Here we call it ourselves so the output does not depend on collection timing.","The callback runs at most once: afterward alive is False and another explicit call does nothing. For structured resource management, a context manager is usually clearer."]}],"summary":"del removes a reference, while __del__ is a finalizer with timing limitations. Use with and explicit cleanup when the timing of resource release matters.","minutes":4},{"id":42,"title":"How does the `yield from` statement work in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: `yield from` is used to delegate part of a generator’s operations to another generator or `iterable`, allowing it to yield all values from the sub-generator in sequence. This simplifies code by avoiding nested loops and enables direct interaction between the outer generator and the sub-generator. It is particularly useful in coroutine chaining and complex data processing pipelines.\n\n```python\n\ndef sub_generator():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\n\ndef main_generator():\n    yield from sub_generator()\n    yield 3\n\nfor value in main_generator():\n    print(value)  # Output: 1, 2, 3\n\n```","category":"Iteration","label":"Delegating with yield from","examples":[{"title":"Compare yielding a list with delegating to it","code":"def one_item():\n    yield [1, 2]\n\ndef many_items():\n    yield from [1, 2]\n\nprint(list(one_item()))\nprint(list(many_items()))","output":"[[1, 2]]\n[1, 2]","explanation":["yield returns the list object as one item. yield from repeatedly forwards the items produced by iterating that list.","This is one-level delegation, not automatic recursive flattening of every nested structure."]},{"title":"Compose smaller generators","code":"def letters():\n    yield \"A\"\n    yield \"B\"\n\ndef message():\n    yield \"start\"\n    yield from letters()\n    yield \"end\"\n\nprint(list(message()))","output":"['start', 'A', 'B', 'end']","explanation":["message begins its own sequence, delegates to letters until that generator is exhausted, and then resumes afterward.","Breaking a pipeline into small generators improves reuse without materializing intermediate lists."]},{"title":"Receive a delegated generator’s return value","code":"def child():\n    yield 1\n    return 99\n\ndef parent():\n    result = yield from child()\n    print(\"child returned\", result)\n    yield 2\n\nprint(list(parent()))","output":"child returned 99\n[1, 2]","explanation":["child yields 1 as an item but returns 99 as its completion value. yield from captures that completion value rather than yielding it to the caller.","The parent prints 99, then yields 2. This illustrates why delegation is richer than merely writing yield for each item in a loop."]}],"summary":"yield emits one value. yield from delegates iteration to another iterable and can also pass through generator communication and return values.","minutes":2},{"id":44,"title":"What is introspection, and how is it achieved in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: Introspection is the ability of a program to examine the type or attributes of objects at runtime. Python supports introspection with built-in functions and attributes like `type()`, `id()`, `dir()`, `hasattr()`, `getattr()`, and `isinstance()`. This allows Python to dynamically inspect and manipulate objects, making it useful for debugging, logging, or writing flexible code. For instance, `dir()` lists an object’s attributes, while `type()` reveals its type.\n\n```python\n\nobj = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(type(obj))          # Output: <class 'list'>\nprint(dir(obj))           # Lists all attributes and methods of `obj`\nprint(hasattr(obj, 'append'))  # Checks if `obj` has the `append` method\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Introspection","examples":[{"title":"Inspect the type and available names","code":"class User:\n    role = \"reader\"\n\nuser = User()\nprint(type(user).__name__)\nprint(\"role\" in dir(user))\nprint(hasattr(user, \"missing\"))","output":"User\nTrue\nFalse","explanation":["type identifies the class, dir provides discoverable attribute names, and hasattr checks whether an attribute lookup succeeds.","These tools answer different questions. Attribute lookup can execute properties or descriptors, so inspection is not always free of side effects."]},{"title":"Read attributes dynamically with a default","code":"class User:\n    name = \"Ada\"\n\nuser = User()\nfor field in [\"name\", \"city\"]:\n    print(getattr(user, field, \"unknown\"))","output":"Ada\nunknown","explanation":["getattr accepts the attribute name as data, useful for controlled field selection. Its third argument supplies a fallback for an absent attribute.","Validate field names when they come from outside your program. Dynamic lookup should not expose arbitrary internal attributes unintentionally."]},{"title":"Inspect stored instance state","code":"class Point:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.x = 2\n        self.y = 3\n\np = Point()\nprint(sorted(vars(p).items()))\nsetattr(p, \"x\", 9)\nprint(p.x)","output":"[('x', 2), ('y', 3)]\n9","explanation":["vars returns the instance dictionary for an object that has one. setattr performs a named assignment just like p.x = 9.","This only shows dictionary-backed instance state, not every inherited attribute or property. Objects using slots may not provide an instance dictionary."]}],"summary":"Introspection means asking a running object about its type, attributes, or behavior. It supports debugging, interactive exploration, and adaptable tooling.","minutes":2},{"id":45,"title":"Explain the difference between mutable and immutable types in Python.","body":"- **Answer**: In Python, mutable types are those whose values can be changed after creation (e.g., lists, dictionaries, sets), while immutable types cannot be modified after they are created (e.g., tuples, strings, integers). Immutability provides predictability in multi-threaded programs, as immutable objects cannot change state. Mutable objects, however, allow modifications without creating new objects, which is useful for efficient data manipulation.\n\n```python\n\n# Mutable\nmutable_list = [1, 2, 3]\nmutable_list[0] = 4  # Allowed\n\n# Immutable\nimmutable_tuple = (1, 2, 3)\n# immutable_tuple[0] = 4  # Raises TypeError\n\n```","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Mutable vs immutable objects","examples":[{"title":"Mutate a shared list","code":"a = [1, 2]\nb = a\nb.append(3)\nprint(a)\nprint(a is b)","output":"[1, 2, 3]\nTrue","explanation":["Both names refer to one list. append changes that list in place, so the update is visible through either name.","No new outer list was created. Use a deliberate copy if another part of the program must retain an independent container."]},{"title":"Rebind an immutable string","code":"a = \"cat\"\nb = a\na += \"s\"\nprint(a)\nprint(b)\nprint(a is b)","output":"cats\ncat\nFalse","explanation":["Strings cannot be extended in place. a += \"s\" produces the concatenated string and rebinds a to it; b still refers to the original value.","The old value remains unchanged. This differs from a mutable operation such as list.append, which changes an existing object."]},{"title":"Mutate an object inside an immutable tuple","code":"record = ([1, 2], \"fixed\")\nrecord[0].append(3)\nprint(record)\ntry:\n    record[1] = \"changed\"\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Tuple slots cannot be reassigned\")","output":"([1, 2, 3], 'fixed')\nTuple slots cannot be reassigned","explanation":["The tuple keeps the same two references throughout. Its first reference points to a list, and that list can still change.","Replacing a tuple slot fails. Tuple immutability is therefore different from deep immutability of every object reachable from the tuple."]}],"summary":"Mutable objects can change in place; immutable objects cannot. An immutable container can still refer to a mutable object inside it.","minutes":2},{"id":46,"title":"What is duck typing, and how is it used in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: Duck typing is a concept in Python where the type of an object is determined by its behavior (methods and properties) rather than its explicit class inheritance. This allows Python to achieve polymorphism without enforcing strict inheritance, as any object that provides the necessary methods or properties can be used interchangeably. This is expressed by the saying, \"If it looks like a duck and quacks like a duck, it's a duck.\"\n\n```python\n\nclass Duck:\n    def quack(self):\n        return \"Quack\"\n\nclass Dog:\n    def quack(self):\n        return \"Bark like a duck\"\n\ndef make_it_quack(duck_like):\n    return duck_like.quack()\n\nmake_it_quack(Duck())  # Output: \"Quack\"\nmake_it_quack(Dog())   # Output: \"Bark like a duck\"\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Duck typing in practice","examples":[{"title":"Accept different implementations of the same behavior","code":"class Dog:\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"woof\"\nclass Robot:\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"beep\"\n\ndef announce(thing):\n    print(thing.speak())\n\nannounce(Dog())\nannounce(Robot())","output":"woof\nbeep","explanation":["announce depends on a callable speak method with suitable behavior. Dog and Robot satisfy that contract without a common custom base class.","This reduces coupling, but the behavioral contract still matters: a method with the same name but incompatible arguments is not automatically suitable."]},{"title":"Read text from any suitable stream","code":"from io import StringIO\n\ndef first_line(source):\n    return source.readline().strip()\n\nprint(first_line(StringIO(\"hello\\nworld\")))","output":"hello","explanation":["The function needs readline, not an actual file on disk. StringIO supplies that behavior using an in-memory string.","A real text file can work with the same function. Programming to a small protocol makes testing easier and avoids unnecessary concrete type checks."]},{"title":"Report an unsupported operation clearly","code":"def area(shape):\n    return shape.area()\n\nclass Square:\n    def area(self):\n        return 9\n\nprint(area(Square()))\ntry:\n    area(object())\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"An area() method is required\")","output":"9\nAn area() method is required","explanation":["The generic function tries the required operation directly. A plain object lacks area and raises AttributeError.","This illustrates the protocol boundary. In a larger program, avoid broad exception handling that could hide an unrelated AttributeError raised inside a valid implementation."]}],"summary":"Duck typing asks whether an object supports the needed behavior. A useful object does not always need to inherit a specific class.","minutes":2},{"id":47,"title":"What is the purpose of `inspect` in Python?","body":"### **What is `inspect` in Python?**\n\nThe `inspect` module in Python provides several useful functions to retrieve information about live objects such as classes, functions, methods, modules, and frames. It is particularly useful for introspection, debugging, and understanding Python code.\n\n---\n\n### **Purpose of `inspect`**\n\n1. **Introspection**:\n    - Examine live objects like functions, methods, and classes.\n    - Retrieve their signature, source code, or documentation.\n2. **Debugging**:\n    - Understand the structure of objects and their attributes.\n    - Navigate stack frames during runtime.\n3. **Dynamic Analysis**:\n    - Analyze and work with objects at runtime dynamically.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Functions of `inspect`**\n\n### **1. `inspect.signature`**: Retrieve the signature of a function.\n\n```python\n\nimport inspect\n\ndef example_function(a, b=10, *args, **kwargs):\n    \"\"\"This is a sample function.\"\"\"\n    return a + b\n\n# Get the function signature\nsignature = inspect.signature(example_function)\nprint(\"Function Signature:\", signature)\n\n# Output:\n# Function Signature: (a, b=10, *args, **kwargs)\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `inspect.signature` retrieves the function's parameter list and default values.\n# - Useful for understanding how to call the function.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. `inspect.getsource`**: Get the source code of an object.\n\n```python\n\nimport inspect\n\ndef sample_function():\n    return \"Hello, World!\"\n\n# Get the source code\nsource_code = inspect.getsource(sample_function)\nprint(\"Source Code:\\n\", source_code)\n\n# Output:\n# Source Code:\n# def sample_function():\n#     return \"Hello, World!\"\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `inspect.getsource` retrieves the actual source code of a function or class.\n# - Useful for debugging or documentation purposes.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **3. `inspect.getdoc`**: Get the documentation string of an object.\n\n```python\n\nimport inspect\n\ndef documented_function():\n    \"\"\"This function has a docstring.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\n# Get the docstring\ndocstring = inspect.getdoc(documented_function)\nprint(\"Docstring:\", docstring)\n\n# Output:\n# Docstring: This function has a docstring.\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `inspect.getdoc` retrieves the docstring for an object.\n# - Helpful for analyzing documentation directly from code.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **4. `inspect.isfunction` and `inspect.isclass`**: Check the type of an object.\n\n```python\n\nimport inspect\n\nclass MyClass:\n    pass\n\ndef my_function():\n    pass\n\n# Check the type of objects\nprint(\"Is MyClass a class?\", inspect.isclass(MyClass))  # Output: True\nprint(\"Is my_function a function?\", inspect.isfunction(my_function))  # Output: True\n\n# Output:\n# Is MyClass a class? True\n# Is my_function a function? True\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `inspect.isclass` and `inspect.isfunction` help identify whether an object is a class or a function.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **5. `inspect.stack`**: Get the current call stack.\n\n```python\n\nimport inspect\n\ndef sample_function():\n    # Print the current stack\n    stack = inspect.stack()\n    for frame in stack:\n        print(f\"Function: {frame.function}, Line: {frame.lineno}\")\n\nsample_function()\n\n# Output:\n# Function: sample_function, Line: 5\n# Function: <module>, Line: 11\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `inspect.stack` retrieves the current stack frames.\n# - Useful for debugging or tracing execution flow.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **6. `inspect.getmembers`**: Get all attributes and methods of an object.\n\n```python\n\nimport inspect\n\nclass MyClass:\n    def method_one(self):\n        pass\n\n    def method_two(self):\n        pass\n\n# Get all members of the class\nmembers = inspect.getmembers(MyClass, inspect.isfunction)\nprint(\"Class Methods:\", members)\n\n# Output:\n# Class Methods: [('method_one', <function MyClass.method_one at 0x...>), ('method_two', <function MyClass.method_two at 0x...>)]\n\n# Explanation:\n# - `inspect.getmembers` lists all attributes and methods of an object.\n# - In this example, it lists all functions in `MyClass`.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **When to Use `inspect`**\n\n- **Debugging**: To examine the structure and behavior of objects during runtime.\n- **Dynamic Analysis**: To work with unknown objects dynamically.\n- **Documentation**: To extract docstrings, signatures, and source code.\n- **Frameworks and Tools**: Widely used in libraries like `unittest` and `doctest`.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\nThe `inspect` module is a powerful tool for introspection and debugging in Python. It allows you to retrieve detailed information about functions, classes, methods, and stack frames, making it essential for dynamic analysis, documentation generation, and understanding complex codebases.\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\nThe `inspect` module in Python helps you:\n\n1. Examine objects like functions, classes, and methods at runtime.\n2. Retrieve information like function signatures, source code, and docstrings.\n3. Debug by analyzing call stacks and object attributes.\n\nIt’s useful for debugging, introspection, and dynamic analysis.","category":"Python toolkit","label":"The inspect module","examples":[{"title":"Inspect a signature and bind arguments","code":"import inspect\n\ndef add(a, b=2):\n    return a + b\n\nsignature = inspect.signature(add)\nbound = signature.bind(3)\nbound.apply_defaults()\nprint(signature)\nprint(dict(bound.arguments))","output":"(a, b=2)\n{'a': 3, 'b': 2}","explanation":["signature describes the calling interface. bind checks a proposed argument arrangement and maps supplied values to parameter names.","apply_defaults fills in omitted defaulted parameters without executing add. This is useful for wrappers, validation tools, and command dispatchers."]},{"title":"Distinguish functions, classes, and bound methods","code":"import inspect\n\nclass User:\n    def greet(self):\n        return \"Hi\"\n\nprint(inspect.isclass(User))\nprint(inspect.isfunction(User.greet))\nprint(inspect.ismethod(User().greet))","output":"True\nTrue\nTrue","explanation":["Access through the class exposes the original function in this example. Access through an instance produces a bound method with the receiver attached.","These distinctions help tools decide how to inspect or invoke an object without assuming every callable has identical binding behavior."]},{"title":"Retrieve documentation and saved source","code":"import inspect\n\ndef square(n):\n    \"\"\"Return n squared.\"\"\"\n    return n * n\n\nprint(inspect.getdoc(square))\nsource = inspect.getsource(square)\nprint(\"def square\" in source)\nprint(\"return n * n\" in source)","output":"Return n squared.\nTrue\nTrue","explanation":["Run this example from a saved .py file so inspect can read its source. getdoc returns cleaned documentation; getsource retrieves the defining source text.","Built-in functions and some dynamically defined or interactive objects do not have retrievable source. Handle OSError or TypeError when building a general-purpose inspection tool."]}],"summary":"inspect provides structured information about functions, classes, signatures, documentation, and source. Some source information is unavailable for built-ins or interactive objects.","minutes":5},{"id":48,"title":"Explain `metaclasses` in Python and their use cases.","body":"- **Answer**: `Metaclasses` are \"classes of classes\" that define how classes are constructed. A `metaclass` can modify class attributes and methods at creation time, allowing customization of class behavior and enabling dynamic creation or alteration of classes. They are defined by setting the `__metaclass__` attribute in a class. Use cases for `metaclasses` include implementing design patterns, enforcing coding standards, and automatically registering classes.\n\n```python\n\nclass Meta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        print(f\"Creating class {name}\")\n        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, dct)\n\nclass MyClass(metaclass=Meta):\n    pass  # Output: \"Creating class MyClass\"\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Metaclass use cases","examples":[{"title":"See that classes are objects too","code":"class User:\n    pass\n\nuser = User()\nprint(type(user).__name__)\nprint(type(User).__name__)\nprint(isinstance(User, type))","output":"User\ntype\nTrue","explanation":["user is an instance of User, and User is itself an instance of type. type is the usual metaclass that constructs class objects.","This is the extra level metaprogramming works with: code can inspect or customize a class object, not just instances created from it."]},{"title":"Add an attribute during class creation","code":"class Tagged(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        namespace[\"tag\"] = name.lower()\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n\nclass Invoice(metaclass=Tagged):\n    pass\n\nprint(Invoice.tag)\nprint(Invoice().tag)","output":"invoice\ninvoice","explanation":["Tagged receives the new class name, base classes, and namespace before the class object is created. It inserts a class attribute into that namespace.","The attribute is available through both the class and its instances via normal attribute lookup. A simpler class decorator may be sufficient for many such transformations."]},{"title":"Reject an incomplete class definition","code":"class RequireRun(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        if \"run\" not in namespace:\n            raise TypeError(\"run is required\")\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n\ntry:\n    class Broken(metaclass=RequireRun):\n        pass\nexcept TypeError as error:\n    print(error)\n\nclass Job(metaclass=RequireRun):\n    def run(self):\n        return \"done\"\n\nprint(Job().run())","output":"run is required\ndone","explanation":["The metaclass validates the class namespace when the class statement runs, before any instance is created. Broken fails because it does not declare run.","This deliberately checks only the new namespace, not inherited methods. For ordinary interface requirements, abstract base classes often express the intent more clearly."]}],"summary":"A class makes instances; a metaclass makes classes. Use a metaclass only when class creation itself needs rules or customization.","minutes":2},{"id":49,"title":"How does Python handle exceptions in generators?","body":"- **Answer**: In Python, exceptions in generators can be handled using `try...except` blocks around `yield` statements, allowing specific exceptions to be caught and processed. Additionally, the `throw()` method can be used to raise an exception within the generator, which will be handled by the generator if it has appropriate exception handling. Unhandled exceptions terminate the generator, and calling `next()` on it will raise a `StopIteration` exception.\n\n```python\n\ndef generator():\n    try:\n        yield 1\n        yield 2\n    except ValueError:\n        yield \"Caught ValueError\"\n\ngen = generator()\nprint(next(gen))       # Output: 1\nprint(gen.throw(ValueError))  # Output: \"Caught ValueError\"\n\n```","category":"Iteration","label":"Generator exceptions","examples":[{"title":"Handle an injected exception and continue","code":"def worker():\n    try:\n        yield \"ready\"\n    except ValueError:\n        yield \"recovered\"\n\ng = worker()\nprint(next(g))\nprint(g.throw(ValueError(\"bad input\")))\nprint(next(g, \"done\"))","output":"ready\nrecovered\ndone","explanation":["The generator must first be advanced to its yield. throw then raises ValueError at that suspension point, where the surrounding handler can catch it.","The handler yields recovered. Once the handler finishes, the generator ends and next with a default reports done."]},{"title":"Run cleanup when a generator is closed","code":"def resource_values():\n    try:\n        yield 1\n        yield 2\n    finally:\n        print(\"cleanup\")\n\ng = resource_values()\nprint(next(g))\ng.close()\nprint(next(g, \"closed\"))","output":"1\ncleanup\nclosed","explanation":["close injects GeneratorExit at the suspension point. The finally block executes as the generator exits, so cleanup runs even though the second value was never requested.","Do not yield a new value while handling GeneratorExit. Resource ownership should remain explicit, especially when a consumer might abandon a generator early."]},{"title":"Distinguish return from raising StopIteration manually","code":"def broken():\n    yield 1\n    raise StopIteration(\"stop\")\n\ng = broken()\nprint(next(g))\ntry:\n    next(g)\nexcept RuntimeError:\n    print(\"Use return to finish a generator\")","output":"1\nUse return to finish a generator","explanation":["An unexpected StopIteration escaping a generator body is converted to RuntimeError in modern Python. This prevents accidental silent termination from masking bugs.","Use return or let execution reach the end to finish normally. The iterator protocol then reports completion to the consumer correctly."]}],"summary":"Generators can handle exceptions around yield points. throw injects an exception into a suspended generator, while close requests cleanup through GeneratorExit.","minutes":2},{"id":50,"title":"What is the `with` statement, and how does it work with context managers?","body":"- **Answer**: The `with` statement in Python is used to wrap the execution of a block with methods defined by a context manager. It ensures that setup and teardown actions are handled automatically, such as opening and closing files or managing database connections. The context manager must implement `__enter__` and `__exit__` methods. `__enter__` is called at the beginning of the `with` block, and `__exit__` is called when exiting it, handling exceptions if they occur.\n\n```python\n\nwith open('file.txt', 'w') as file:\n    file.write(\"Hello, world!\")\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"The with statement","examples":[{"title":"Implement the context-manager protocol","code":"class Session:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"enter\")\n        return \"ready\"\n    def __exit__(self, kind, value, traceback):\n        print(\"exit\")\n        return False\n\nwith Session() as status:\n    print(status)","output":"enter\nready\nexit","explanation":["The object’s __enter__ runs before the block, and its return value is bound to status. __exit__ runs when the block finishes.","Returning False means exceptions are not suppressed. It is normal for an exit method to return None with the same propagation behavior."]},{"title":"Use contextlib.contextmanager with finally","code":"from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef session():\n    print(\"open\")\n    try:\n        yield \"ready\"\n    finally:\n        print(\"close\")\n\ntry:\n    with session() as value:\n        print(value)\n        raise ValueError(\"failure\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"handled\")","output":"open\nready\nclose\nhandled","explanation":["The generator yields exactly once to enter the block. When the body raises, the exception is passed back at the yield point and finally still runs.","The exception then propagates to the outer handler. Putting cleanup after yield without finally would not provide the same guarantee when the body fails."]},{"title":"Suppress only a deliberate exception type","code":"from contextlib import suppress\n\nwith suppress(KeyError):\n    print({}[\"missing\"])\nprint(\"continued\")\n\ntry:\n    with suppress(KeyError):\n        int(\"bad\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"ValueError was not suppressed\")","output":"continued\nValueError was not suppressed","explanation":["suppress(KeyError) treats only KeyError as handled. The missing dictionary access is skipped and execution continues after the with block.","The unrelated ValueError still propagates. Narrow exception handling avoids concealing programming errors that were never intended to be ignored."]},{"title":"Manage a dynamic group of cleanup actions","code":"from contextlib import ExitStack\n\nwith ExitStack() as stack:\n    stack.callback(print, \"close first\")\n    stack.callback(print, \"close second\")\n    print(\"work\")","output":"work\nclose second\nclose first","explanation":["ExitStack records callbacks and runs them in reverse registration order when the stack exits. That mirrors nested with blocks.","This helps when the number of resources is discovered at runtime. Register each cleanup as soon as its corresponding resource is acquired."]}],"summary":"A context manager defines setup and cleanup around a with block. Cleanup happens on normal exit and while an exception unwinds the block.","minutes":2},{"id":51,"title":"What are ABCs (Abstract Base Classes) in Python, and how are they used?","body":"- **Answer**: Abstract Base Classes (ABCs) in Python are classes that cannot be instantiated and are designed to be inherited. They define methods that must be implemented in derived classes, enforcing an interface for subclasses. ABCs are implemented using the `abc` module, specifically by `subclassing` `ABC` and using the `@abstractmethod` decorator. They are commonly used to define standardized behaviors in large codebases, ensuring that all subclasses implement required methods.\n\n```python\n\nfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Circle(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, radius):\n        self.radius = radius\n\n    def area(self):\n        return 3.14159 * self.radius ** 2\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Abstract base classes","examples":[{"title":"Reject an incomplete implementation","code":"from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\ntry:\n    Shape()\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Implement area first\")","output":"Implement area first","explanation":["ABC and @abstractmethod mark area as a requirement. Python prevents constructing Shape while that requirement remains abstract.","The check concerns the class interface, not automatic correctness of an eventual area implementation. Tests must still verify the actual behavior."]},{"title":"Implement the contract in a concrete subclass","code":"from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Square(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, side):\n        self.side = side\n    def area(self):\n        return self.side ** 2\n\nsquare = Square(4)\nprint(square.area())\nprint(isinstance(square, Shape))","output":"16\nTrue","explanation":["Square supplies a concrete area method and can be instantiated. Its inheritance also makes isinstance(square, Shape) true.","The base provides a shared contract for callers. Other subclasses can compute area differently while exposing the same operation."]},{"title":"Combine reusable code with an abstract operation","code":"from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Report(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def rows(self):\n        pass\n    def count(self):\n        return len(self.rows())\n\nclass SmallReport(Report):\n    def rows(self):\n        return [\"A\", \"B\"]\n\nprint(SmallReport().count())","output":"2","explanation":["An abstract base class may contain concrete methods too. count is reusable code that calls the required rows operation implemented by the subclass.","This template-style design centralizes shared behavior while leaving one step customizable. It does not require every method on the base to be abstract."]}],"summary":"An abstract base class declares required behavior for subclasses. A subclass must implement remaining abstract methods before it can be instantiated.","minutes":2},{"id":52,"title":"How does the `yield from` expression work, and how is it different from `yield`?","body":"- **Answer**: The `yield from` expression is used to delegate part of a generator’s operations to another generator or iterable. It simplifies code by yielding all values from a sub-generator in sequence, eliminating the need for loops. `yield from` also enables the outer generator to receive values sent to the sub-generator, return values, and propagate exceptions, making it more powerful than `yield`.\n\n```python\n\ndef sub_generator():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\n\ndef main_generator():\n    yield from sub_generator()\n    yield 3\n\nfor value in main_generator():\n    print(value)  # Output: 1, 2, 3\n\n```","category":"Iteration","label":"yield vs yield from","examples":[{"title":"Compare yielding a list with delegating to it","code":"def one_item():\n    yield [1, 2]\n\ndef many_items():\n    yield from [1, 2]\n\nprint(list(one_item()))\nprint(list(many_items()))","output":"[[1, 2]]\n[1, 2]","explanation":["yield returns the list object as one item. yield from repeatedly forwards the items produced by iterating that list.","This is one-level delegation, not automatic recursive flattening of every nested structure."]},{"title":"Compose smaller generators","code":"def letters():\n    yield \"A\"\n    yield \"B\"\n\ndef message():\n    yield \"start\"\n    yield from letters()\n    yield \"end\"\n\nprint(list(message()))","output":"['start', 'A', 'B', 'end']","explanation":["message begins its own sequence, delegates to letters until that generator is exhausted, and then resumes afterward.","Breaking a pipeline into small generators improves reuse without materializing intermediate lists."]},{"title":"Receive a delegated generator’s return value","code":"def child():\n    yield 1\n    return 99\n\ndef parent():\n    result = yield from child()\n    print(\"child returned\", result)\n    yield 2\n\nprint(list(parent()))","output":"child returned 99\n[1, 2]","explanation":["child yields 1 as an item but returns 99 as its completion value. yield from captures that completion value rather than yielding it to the caller.","The parent prints 99, then yields 2. This illustrates why delegation is richer than merely writing yield for each item in a loop."]}],"summary":"yield emits one value. yield from delegates iteration to another iterable and can also pass through generator communication and return values.","minutes":2},{"id":53,"title":"What are weak references, and why are they useful?","body":"- **Answer**: Weak references allow an object to be referenced without preventing it from being garbage-collected. They are useful in caching scenarios where objects should not be retained longer than necessary. If an object has only weak references, it can be freed by garbage collection, preventing memory leaks. Weak references are created using the `weakref` module in Python.\n\n```python\nimport weakref\n\nclass MyClass:\n    pass\n\nobj = MyClass()\nweak_ref = weakref.ref(obj)\ndel obj\nprint(weak_ref())  # Output: None, as the object was garbage collected\n\n```","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Weak-reference use cases","examples":[{"title":"Observe an object without owning it","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass User:\n    pass\n\nuser = User()\nref = weakref.ref(user)\nprint(ref() is user)\ndel user\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["Calling ref retrieves the referent while it exists. The weak reference does not prevent the object from being collected.","After its strong owner is removed, ref returns None. In real code, assign target = ref() and check target before accessing attributes."]},{"title":"Build a cache that does not retain values","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Product:\n    pass\n\ncache = weakref.WeakValueDictionary()\nproduct = Product()\ncache[\"item\"] = product\nprint(\"item\" in cache)\ndel product\ngc.collect()\nprint(\"item\" in cache)","output":"True\nFalse","explanation":["WeakValueDictionary holds values weakly, so the cache is not a strong owner of the product. While product exists, the entry can be retrieved normally.","Once that last strong reference disappears, the entry vanishes. Use an ordinary dictionary when the cache is supposed to retain values intentionally."]},{"title":"Track membership without owning members","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Listener:\n    pass\n\nlisteners = weakref.WeakSet()\nlistener = Listener()\nlisteners.add(listener)\nprint(len(listeners))\ndel listener\ngc.collect()\nprint(len(listeners))","output":"1\n0","explanation":["WeakSet is useful for registries of objects that should disappear when their owners release them. Adding a listener does not extend its lifetime.","Iteration results may change as objects are collected. Keep a strong reference during any operation that needs a listener to stay alive."]}],"summary":"A weak reference lets you observe an object without keeping it alive. Check whether the object still exists before using it.","minutes":2},{"id":54,"title":"What is the purpose of `contextlib` in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: The `contextlib` module in Python provides utilities for working with context managers, allowing them to be created more easily and flexibly. It includes tools like `@contextmanager` for creating context managers with a single function using `yield` instead of defining a class with `__enter__` and `__exit__`. It simplifies resource management in Python by making context managers concise and readable.\n\n```python\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef managed_resource():\n    print(\"Resource acquired\")\n    yield\n    print(\"Resource released\")\n\nwith managed_resource():\n    print(\"Using the resource\")\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"The contextlib module","examples":[{"title":"Implement the context-manager protocol","code":"class Session:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"enter\")\n        return \"ready\"\n    def __exit__(self, kind, value, traceback):\n        print(\"exit\")\n        return False\n\nwith Session() as status:\n    print(status)","output":"enter\nready\nexit","explanation":["The object’s __enter__ runs before the block, and its return value is bound to status. __exit__ runs when the block finishes.","Returning False means exceptions are not suppressed. It is normal for an exit method to return None with the same propagation behavior."]},{"title":"Use contextlib.contextmanager with finally","code":"from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef session():\n    print(\"open\")\n    try:\n        yield \"ready\"\n    finally:\n        print(\"close\")\n\ntry:\n    with session() as value:\n        print(value)\n        raise ValueError(\"failure\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"handled\")","output":"open\nready\nclose\nhandled","explanation":["The generator yields exactly once to enter the block. When the body raises, the exception is passed back at the yield point and finally still runs.","The exception then propagates to the outer handler. Putting cleanup after yield without finally would not provide the same guarantee when the body fails."]},{"title":"Suppress only a deliberate exception type","code":"from contextlib import suppress\n\nwith suppress(KeyError):\n    print({}[\"missing\"])\nprint(\"continued\")\n\ntry:\n    with suppress(KeyError):\n        int(\"bad\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"ValueError was not suppressed\")","output":"continued\nValueError was not suppressed","explanation":["suppress(KeyError) treats only KeyError as handled. The missing dictionary access is skipped and execution continues after the with block.","The unrelated ValueError still propagates. Narrow exception handling avoids concealing programming errors that were never intended to be ignored."]},{"title":"Manage a dynamic group of cleanup actions","code":"from contextlib import ExitStack\n\nwith ExitStack() as stack:\n    stack.callback(print, \"close first\")\n    stack.callback(print, \"close second\")\n    print(\"work\")","output":"work\nclose second\nclose first","explanation":["ExitStack records callbacks and runs them in reverse registration order when the stack exits. That mirrors nested with blocks.","This helps when the number of resources is discovered at runtime. Register each cleanup as soon as its corresponding resource is acquired."]}],"summary":"A context manager defines setup and cleanup around a with block. Cleanup happens on normal exit and while an exception unwinds the block.","minutes":2},{"id":55,"title":"How do you handle circular imports in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: Circular imports occur when two modules import each other, causing a dependency loop that Python cannot resolve. Circular imports can be avoided by:\n    - Moving imports to local scopes (e.g., inside functions).\n    - Refactoring code to eliminate dependencies or move shared code to a third module.\n    - Using lazy imports or importing only what’s necessary.\n\n```python\n# Module A\ndef func_a():\n    from module_b import func_b\n    func_b()\n\n# Module B\ndef func_b():\n    from module_a import func_a\n    func_a()\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Resolving circular imports","examples":[{"title":"Share definitions through a neutral module","code":"# Single-file model of three modules; no files need to be created.\nfrom types import SimpleNamespace\n\ncommon = SimpleNamespace(prefix=\"user\")  # common.py\ndef module_a_label(name):                # module_a.py imports common\n    return common.prefix + \":\" + name\ndef module_b_label(name):                # module_b.py imports common\n    return common.prefix + \":\" + name.upper()\n\nprint(module_a_label(\"Ada\"))\nprint(module_b_label(\"Ada\"))","output":"user:Ada\nuser:ADA","explanation":["Both components depend on the shared definition rather than importing each other. The namespaces and comments model the dependency direction in one runnable file.","In a real project, put the shared definition in common.py and import it from both modules. This example demonstrates the refactor, not a real import cycle."]},{"title":"Perform an import only when an operation needs it","code":"def circle_area(radius):\n    from math import pi\n    return round(pi * radius * radius, 2)\n\nprint(circle_area(2))\nprint(circle_area(3))","output":"12.57\n28.27","explanation":["The import statement is inside the function, so it executes when the function is called rather than during the surrounding module’s initial top-level setup.","This illustrates deferred import timing with a safe standard-library dependency. Local imports can help a specific cycle, but do not repair a fundamentally tangled module design."]},{"title":"Use dependency injection to remove a reverse dependency","code":"def build_report(load_user):\n    user = load_user()\n    return \"Report for \" + user[\"name\"]\n\ndef load_user():\n    return {\"name\": \"Ada\"}\n\nprint(build_report(load_user))","output":"Report for Ada","explanation":["The report component receives its loader instead of importing the concrete data component. A composition layer wires the pieces together.","This removes the need for a reverse import and simplifies testing. It also avoids the separate bug of functions calling one another recursively without a stopping condition."]}],"summary":"Circular imports happen when modules depend on each other during initialization. Move shared definitions to a neutral module or defer a carefully chosen dependency.","minutes":2},{"id":56,"title":"How does Python’s garbage collection work, and what is the role of reference counting and cyclic garbage collection?","body":"- **Answer**: Python’s garbage collection manages memory by automatically deleting objects that are no longer needed. It primarily relies on **reference counting**, where each object keeps track of the number of references pointing to it. When an object’s reference count reaches zero, it’s immediately deallocated. However, reference counting alone cannot detect cyclic references (e.g., two objects referencing each other). For this, Python also has a **cyclic garbage collector** that identifies and collects these cycles. The `gc` module allows manual interaction with garbage collection, such as triggering collection and inspecting tracked objects.\n\n```python\n\nimport gc\ngc.collect()  # Manually trigger garbage collection\n\n```","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Reference counting & cycle collection","examples":[{"title":"Separate names from the object they reference","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Item:\n    pass\n\na = Item()\nb = a\nref = weakref.ref(a)\ndel a\nprint(ref() is b)\ndel b\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["a and b initially point to the same instance. Deleting a removes one binding, but b still keeps the object alive.","After deleting b and collecting garbage, the weak reference shows that the object is gone. The weak reference itself does not own the instance."]},{"title":"Collect an unreachable cycle","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Node:\n    pass\n\nnode = Node()\nnode.link = node\nref = weakref.ref(node)\ndel node\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True","explanation":["The node references itself, so its reference count alone cannot fall to zero when the external name is removed.","gc.collect asks the cyclic collector to examine unreachable tracked objects. It can reclaim this isolated self-cycle; the weak reference then returns None."]},{"title":"Release temporary references deliberately","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Record:\n    pass\n\nrecord = Record()\nref = weakref.ref(record)\ncache = [record]\ndel record\nprint(ref() is not None)\ncache.clear()\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["Containers also hold references. The list keeps the instance alive even after its original variable disappears.","Clearing the list removes that ownership. This demonstrates why a growing cache can retain memory even when the objects are no longer needed elsewhere."]}],"summary":"Variables refer to objects. CPython uses reference counting plus a cycle collector to reclaim unreachable objects; resource cleanup should still be explicit.","minutes":2},{"id":57,"title":"Explain the difference between `map`, `filter`, and `reduce`.","body":"- **Answer**: `map`, `filter`, and `reduce` are functional programming tools in Python for applying functions across sequences:\n    - **`map(function, iterable)`**: Applies `function` to each item in `iterable` and returns a map object (an iterator of results).\n    - **`filter(function, iterable)`**: Filters items in `iterable` based on `function`, returning only items where `function(item)` is `True`.\n    - **`reduce(function, iterable)`**: Applies `function` cumulatively to reduce `iterable` to a single value, typically used for aggregation (e.g., summing a list). Unlike `map` and `filter`, `reduce` is part of the `functools` module in Python 3.\n\n```python\nfrom functools import reduce\n\nnumbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(list(map(lambda x: x * 2, numbers)))  # Output: [2, 4, 6, 8]\nprint(list(filter(lambda x: x % 2 == 0, numbers)))  # Output: [2, 4]\nprint(reduce(lambda x, y: x + y, numbers))  # Output: 10\n\n```","category":"Iteration","label":"map, filter & reduce","examples":[{"title":"Transform and filter a sequence","code":"values = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nsquares = map(lambda n: n * n, values)\neven = filter(lambda n: n % 2 == 0, values)\nprint(list(squares))\nprint(list(even))","output":"[1, 4, 9, 16]\n[2, 4]","explanation":["map calls the transformation for each consumed input. filter calls a predicate and yields only the original values whose test is truthy.","The list calls materialize the iterators for display. A comprehension may be more readable when the transformation or filtering rule grows complex."]},{"title":"Reduce with an explicit initial accumulator","code":"from functools import reduce\n\nprint(reduce(lambda total, n: total + n, [2, 3, 4], 10))\nprint(reduce(lambda total, n: total + n, [], 10))","output":"19\n10","explanation":["The accumulator begins at 10, then incorporates 2, 3, and 4. With no items, the initial accumulator is returned unchanged.","An explicit initializer also defines empty-input behavior. Prefer sum for simple addition; reduce is useful for genuinely custom accumulation."]},{"title":"Observe lazy execution","code":"def show(n):\n    print(\"compute\", n)\n    return n * 2\n\nresult = map(show, [1, 2])\nprint(\"created\")\nprint(next(result))\nprint(list(result))","output":"created\ncompute 1\n2\ncompute 2\n[4]","explanation":["Constructing map does not immediately call show. Requesting the first item performs just the first transformation.","The final list drains the remaining work. If a transformation has side effects or raises, that behavior happens during consumption rather than necessarily at construction."]}],"summary":"map transforms items, filter keeps matching items, and reduce combines items into one result. map and filter are lazy iterators in Python 3.","minutes":2},{"id":58,"title":"What is the difference between `args` and `*kwargs` in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: `args` and `*kwargs` allow functions to accept a variable number of arguments. `args` collects additional positional arguments into a tuple, while `*kwargs` collects additional keyword arguments into a dictionary. They’re useful for creating flexible functions that accept any number of inputs and are commonly used in wrapper functions or for passing arguments to other functions.\n\n```python\n\ndef example(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(args)   # Tuple of positional arguments\n    print(kwargs) # Dictionary of keyword arguments\n\nexample(1, 2, 3, a=4, b=5)\n# Output:\n# (1, 2, 3)\n# {'a': 4, 'b': 5}\n\n```","category":"Functions","label":"*args & **kwargs","examples":[{"title":"See the collected argument containers","code":"def show(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(args)\n    print(sorted(kwargs.items()))\n\nshow(10, 20, color=\"blue\", size=3)","output":"(10, 20)\n[('color', 'blue'), ('size', 3)]","explanation":["The two unnamed values enter args in their original order. The named values enter kwargs under their keyword names.","Sorting keyword items makes this display independent of the order in which callers supply them. The names args and kwargs are conventions; the star syntax gives them their behavior."]},{"title":"Unpack a list and dictionary into a call","code":"def greet(name, punctuation=\"!\"):\n    return \"Hi \" + name + punctuation\n\nvalues = [\"Ada\"]\nsettings = {\"punctuation\": \"?\"}\nprint(greet(*values, **settings))","output":"Hi Ada?","explanation":["At the call site, *values expands the list into positional arguments and **settings expands the dictionary into keyword arguments.","The resulting call is equivalent to greet(\"Ada\", punctuation=\"?\"). Dictionary keys used for keyword unpacking must be strings accepted by the callable."]},{"title":"Detect duplicate argument assignment","code":"def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\ntry:\n    print(add(1, **{\"a\": 2, \"b\": 3}))\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Argument a was supplied twice\")","output":"Argument a was supplied twice","explanation":["The positional 1 already binds a. Expanding the dictionary tries to bind a again, so Python rejects the call before the function body executes.","Argument unpacking does not override a value already supplied through another argument path. Remove the duplicate before calling the function."]}],"summary":"*args gathers extra positional values into a tuple. **kwargs gathers extra named values into a dictionary. The stars also unpack arguments when calling functions.","minutes":2},{"id":59,"title":"What is a singleton pattern, and how can it be implemented in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: The Singleton pattern restricts a class to a single instance, ensuring consistent state across the application. In Python, this can be achieved by:\n    - Overriding the `__new__` method to return the same instance.\n    - Using module-level variables (since modules are singletons by nature).\n    - Using metaclasses, where the metaclass controls instance creation.\n\n```python\n\nclass Singleton:\n    _instance = None\n    def __new__(cls):\n        if not cls._instance:\n            cls._instance = super().__new__(cls)\n        return cls._instance\n\nobj1 = Singleton()\nobj2 = Singleton()\nprint(obj1 is obj2)  # Output: True\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"The singleton pattern","examples":[{"title":"Reuse one instance with __new__","code":"class Settings:\n    _instance = None\n    def __new__(cls):\n        if cls._instance is None:\n            cls._instance = super().__new__(cls)\n        return cls._instance\n\na = Settings()\nb = Settings()\nprint(a is b)\na.theme = \"dark\"\nprint(b.theme)","output":"True\ndark","explanation":["__new__ stores the first instance and returns it for later construction calls. Both variables therefore observe the same attributes.","This minimal demonstration is not a thread-safe singleton implementation and does not solve subclass lifetime rules. Shared state needs an explicit concurrency and ownership design."]},{"title":"Notice repeated initialization","code":"class Shared:\n    _instance = None\n    def __new__(cls):\n        if cls._instance is None:\n            cls._instance = super().__new__(cls)\n        return cls._instance\n    def __init__(self):\n        print(\"init\")\n\na = Shared()\nb = Shared()\nprint(a is b)","output":"init\ninit\nTrue","explanation":["Returning the same suitable instance from __new__ does not automatically prevent Python from calling __init__ again.","Repeated initialization can reset shared state unexpectedly. An initialization guard or a different factory design is needed when one-time setup is required."]},{"title":"Share an explicitly created dependency instead","code":"class Settings:\n    def __init__(self, mode):\n        self.mode = mode\n\ndef render(settings):\n    return \"mode=\" + settings.mode\n\nshared = Settings(\"dark\")\nprint(render(shared))\nprint(render(Settings(\"test\")))","output":"mode=dark\nmode=test","explanation":["The caller chooses when to share an instance instead of hiding that choice in construction. Production code can reuse shared while tests provide isolated settings.","This retains the useful shared-configuration behavior without enforcing a global singleton across every call site."]}],"summary":"A singleton tries to share one instance. Use it only when shared lifetime and state are intentional; a simple explicitly passed object is often easier to test.","minutes":2},{"id":60,"title":"What is monkey patching in Python, and when is it used?","body":"- **Answer**: Monkey patching is the practice of modifying or extending classes or modules at runtime, typically by replacing or adding attributes or methods. While it provides flexibility, it can make code harder to understand and maintain, as modifications aren’t visible directly in the source. Monkey patching is often used in testing or to quickly fix issues without modifying the original codebase. However, it’s discouraged in production unless absolutely necessary due to its potential for introducing unexpected behavior.\n\n```python\nimport some_module\n\ndef new_function():\n    print(\"Monkey-patched function\")\n\nsome_module.original_function = new_function\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Runtime monkey patching","examples":[{"title":"Replace a method at runtime","code":"class Greeter:\n    def hello(self):\n        return \"Hello\"\n\ng = Greeter()\nprint(g.hello())\nGreeter.hello = lambda self: \"Hi\"\nprint(g.hello())","output":"Hello\nHi","explanation":["The method is looked up on Greeter when g.hello is accessed. Replacing the class attribute changes what the existing instance calls.","This affects other instances using that class method too. It is a runtime modification, whereas a subclass override creates a separate specialization."]},{"title":"Scope a temporary test patch","code":"from unittest.mock import patch\n\nclass Clock:\n    def now(self):\n        return 100\n\nclock = Clock()\nwith patch.object(Clock, \"now\", return_value=7):\n    print(clock.now())\nprint(clock.now())","output":"7\n100","explanation":["patch.object temporarily substitutes a mock for the method. The with block establishes exactly where the replacement applies.","On exit, the original method is restored even if the block raises. Scoped patches reduce the risk of one test changing another test’s behavior."]},{"title":"Prefer explicit dependencies when possible","code":"def report(now):\n    return \"time=\" + str(now())\n\nprint(report(lambda: 7))\nprint(report(lambda: 12))","output":"time=7\ntime=12","explanation":["Instead of replacing a global clock, report accepts the behavior it depends on as an argument. Each caller can supply a deterministic clock.","Dependency injection makes the substitution visible in the function signature and avoids modifying a shared class or module. Use patching when that seam is not available."]}],"summary":"Monkey patching replaces behavior on an existing object, class, or module while the program runs. Keep patches narrow and restore the original behavior afterward.","minutes":2},{"id":61,"title":"Explain the use of the `@property` decorator and its benefits in Python.","body":"- **Answer**: The `@property` decorator allows you to define methods that behave like attributes, providing controlled access to instance variables. This enables encapsulation, where the internal representation of data can change without affecting the class interface. It also allows read-only properties and data validation. `@property` can be paired with `@<property>.setter` to define setter logic.\n\n```python\n\nclass Rectangle:\n    def __init__(self, width, height):\n        self._width = width\n        self._height = height\n\n    @property\n    def area(self):\n        return self._width * self._height\n\nrect = Rectangle(5, 10)\nprint(rect.area)  # Output: 50\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Properties & encapsulation","examples":[{"title":"Expose a computed attribute","code":"class Rectangle:\n    def __init__(self, width, height):\n        self.width, self.height = width, height\n    @property\n    def area(self):\n        return self.width * self.height\n\nr = Rectangle(3, 4)\nprint(r.area)\nr.width = 5\nprint(r.area)","output":"12\n20","explanation":["Accessing r.area calls the getter without parentheses. The result is recomputed from current attributes, so changing width changes the next result.","@property does not automatically cache values. Use it for a cheap computed attribute or for controlled access with a familiar interface."]},{"title":"Validate assignments with a setter","code":"class Account:\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self.balance = balance\n    @property\n    def balance(self):\n        return self._balance\n    @balance.setter\n    def balance(self, value):\n        if value < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Negative balance\")\n        self._balance = value\n\na = Account(10)\nprint(a.balance)\ntry:\n    a.balance = -1\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)\nprint(a.balance)","output":"10\nNegative balance\n10","explanation":["Both initialization and later assignment go through the setter. The rejected value is never stored because validation runs before updating _balance.","The backing name _balance avoids recursively assigning the property itself. The leading underscore signals an internal implementation detail, not enforced privacy."]},{"title":"Create a read-only public attribute","code":"class User:\n    def __init__(self, user_id):\n        self._user_id = user_id\n    @property\n    def user_id(self):\n        return self._user_id\n\nuser = User(7)\nprint(user.user_id)\ntry:\n    user.user_id = 8\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"Read-only property\")","output":"7\nRead-only property","explanation":["A property with no setter rejects ordinary assignment through that attribute name. The getter still permits reading.","This is API-level control, not deep immutability or a security boundary: internal code can still change the backing attribute if the design permits it."]}],"summary":"A property lets ordinary attribute access run controlled getter or setter logic. Callers keep using obj.value while the class manages how that value is read or changed.","minutes":2},{"id":62,"title":"How does Python’s `hash()` function work, and when is it used?","body":"- **Answer**: The `hash()` function in Python returns a unique integer for an object based on its contents, making it useful for quick comparisons and hash table lookups (e.g., in dictionaries and sets). `Hashable` objects must implement `__hash__` and `__eq__` consistently. Immutable objects like strings, numbers, and tuples are `hashable`, while mutable objects (e.g., lists, dictionaries) are not, as changes to mutable objects would alter the hash value, causing issues in data structures relying on stable hashes.\n\n```python\n\nprint(hash(\"Hello\"))  # Unique integer based on content\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Hashing objects","examples":[{"title":"Use equal immutable values as keys","code":"a = (\"A\", 1)\nb = tuple([\"A\", 1])\nprint(a == b)\nprint(hash(a) == hash(b))\nprint({a: \"found\"}[b])","output":"True\nTrue\nfound","explanation":["The two tuples compare equal and obey the same-hash requirement, so a dictionary entry inserted with one can be retrieved with the other.","The actual hash number is deliberately not printed: string-containing hashes can vary between Python processes. Equality of the hashes is the relevant guarantee."]},{"title":"Implement equality and hashing together","code":"class Key:\n    def __init__(self, code):\n        self.code = code\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Key):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.code == other.code\n    def __hash__(self):\n        return hash(self.code)\n\na, b = Key(\"A\"), Key(\"A\")\nprint(a == b)\nprint(hash(a) == hash(b))\nprint(len({a, b}))","output":"True\nTrue\n1","explanation":["Both methods derive from the same code value, so equal instances produce equal hashes and a set treats them as one value.","Do not mutate code while this object is in a set or used as a dictionary key. A frozen value object is safer when the key should never change."]},{"title":"Understand why defining equality can disable hashing","code":"class Value:\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return isinstance(other, Value)\n\nprint(Value.__hash__ is None)\ntry:\n    hash(Value())\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Unhashable without a compatible hash\")","output":"True\nUnhashable without a compatible hash","explanation":["When a class defines equality without a compatible hash, Python normally makes its instances unhashable rather than retaining identity-based hashing.","That prevents equal objects from accidentally having incompatible inherited hashes. Implement both consistently only when stable hashability suits the type."]}],"summary":"A hash helps dictionaries and sets find objects. Equal hashable objects must have equal hashes, and their hash must stay stable while they are used as keys.","minutes":2},{"id":63,"title":"What is method overloading, and how is it achieved in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: Python does not support traditional method overloading as seen in other languages (e.g., Java). Instead, you can achieve similar functionality by using default arguments, `args`, and `*kwargs`, or by using type checks within the function to differentiate based on the arguments. For a more structured approach, the `functools.singledispatch` decorator allows type-based dispatch for function overloading based on the first argument’s type.\n\n```python\n\nfrom functools import singledispatch\n\n@singledispatch\ndef process(data):\n    print(\"Default processing\")\n\n@process.register(int)\ndef _(data):\n    print(\"Processing integer:\", data)\n\nprocess(10)  # Output: \"Processing integer: 10\"\n\n```","category":"Functions","label":"Overloading alternatives","examples":[{"title":"Support several call shapes with defaults","code":"def area(width, height=None):\n    if height is None:\n        height = width\n    return width * height\n\nprint(area(4))\nprint(area(4, 3))","output":"16\n12","explanation":["The same function handles both calls. A missing height selects square behavior, while an explicit height selects rectangle behavior.","Using None as the missing-value marker avoids treating a legitimate zero height as though it were omitted. This is conditional behavior, not signature-based overload resolution."]},{"title":"Collect a variable number of inputs","code":"def add(*values):\n    return sum(values)\n\nprint(add())\nprint(add(2, 3))\nprint(add(2, 3, 4))","output":"0\n5\n9","explanation":["*values collects additional positional arguments into a tuple. sum works for an empty tuple and for tuples of any length containing compatible numeric values.","One definition covers all these arities. Document the accepted element types because *args itself does not validate their meaning."]},{"title":"See why redefining a method is not overloading","code":"class Greeter:\n    def hello(self, name):\n        return \"Hi \" + name\n\n    def hello(self):\n        return \"Hi everyone\"\n\ng = Greeter()\nprint(g.hello())\ntry:\n    g.hello(\"Ada\")\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Earlier definition was replaced\")","output":"Hi everyone\nEarlier definition was replaced","explanation":["A class body binds names in a namespace. The second def binds hello again, replacing the first function rather than adding another signature.","The surviving method accepts only self. Passing Ada gives it an unexpected positional argument and raises TypeError."]}],"summary":"Python does not automatically keep several same-named methods based on their signatures. Use defaults, variable arguments, or explicit type dispatch.","minutes":2},{"id":64,"title":"Explain how Python’s `copy` module handles shallow and deep copies.","body":"- **Answer**: Python’s `copy` module provides `copy()` for shallow copies and `deepcopy()` for deep copies. A **shallow copy** creates a new object but inserts references to the objects within it (e.g., elements in a list), so nested mutable objects remain linked. A **deep copy** recursively copies all objects, creating entirely independent copies of nested objects. Shallow copies are suitable for simple structures, while deep copies are used for complex, nested data structures where independence is required.\n\n```python\n\nimport copy\n\noriginal = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\nshallow = copy.copy(original)\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\n\noriginal[0][0] = 'Changed'\nprint(shallow)  # Output: [['Changed', 2], [3, 4]]\nprint(deep)     # Output: [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\n\n```","category":"Memory & objects","label":"The copy module","examples":[{"title":"Compare assignment, shallow copy, and deep copy","code":"import copy\n\noriginal = [[1], [2]]\nalias = original\nshallow = copy.copy(original)\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\nprint(alias is original)\nprint(shallow is original, shallow[0] is original[0])\nprint(deep[0] is original[0])","output":"True\nFalse True\nFalse","explanation":["Assignment creates another name for the same outer list. copy.copy creates a different outer list but retains references to the same inner lists.","deepcopy creates independent inner lists here. Test identity at both levels to understand which mutations will be shared."]},{"title":"Mutate a nested value","code":"import copy\n\na = {\"scores\": [10, 20]}\nb = copy.copy(a)\nc = copy.deepcopy(a)\nb[\"scores\"].append(30)\nprint(a[\"scores\"])\nprint(b[\"scores\"])\nprint(c[\"scores\"])","output":"[10, 20, 30]\n[10, 20, 30]\n[10, 20]","explanation":["a and b are separate dictionaries containing the same scores list. Appending through b changes that shared list, so a observes it too.","c contains a different list from the deep copy and retains its original values. Replacing a top-level dictionary entry would behave differently from mutating the referenced list."]},{"title":"Distinguish replacing an item from mutating it","code":"import copy\n\na = [[1], [2]]\nb = copy.copy(a)\nb[0] = [99]\nprint(a)\nprint(b)\nb[1].append(8)\nprint(a)\nprint(b)","output":"[[1], [2]]\n[[99], [2]]\n[[1], [2, 8]]\n[[99], [2, 8]]","explanation":["Assigning b[0] changes one slot in b’s outer list; it does not modify a’s slot or the old inner list.","The second inner list remains shared. Appending to b[1] mutates that object and is visible through a[1] as well."]}],"summary":"A shallow copy duplicates the outer container and shares nested objects. A deep copy also copies nested objects in this example, so later nested changes stay separate.","minutes":2},{"id":65,"title":"What are frozen sets, and when are they used in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: Frozen sets are immutable versions of regular sets in Python, created using `frozenset()`. Since they cannot be modified after creation, they are hashable and can be used as dictionary keys or set elements, which regular sets cannot. Frozen sets are useful for ensuring that collections of unique elements remain unchanged throughout the program’s lifetime.\n\n```python\n\nfs = frozenset([1, 2, 3])\nprint(fs)  # Output: frozenset({1, 2, 3})\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Using frozen sets","examples":[{"title":"Remove duplicates without depending on display order","code":"values = frozenset([3, 1, 3, 2])\nprint(sorted(values))\nprint(2 in values)\nprint(len(values))","output":"[1, 2, 3]\nTrue\n3","explanation":["Like a set, frozenset retains unique elements and supports membership checks. It does not define an application-level sorted iteration order.","Sorting only for display makes the expected output stable. The frozenset itself remains unordered and immutable."]},{"title":"Use a set of tags as a dictionary key","code":"routes = {frozenset([\"read\", \"write\"]): \"editor\"}\nkey = frozenset([\"write\", \"read\"])\nprint(routes[key])\nprint(key == frozenset([\"read\", \"write\"]))","output":"editor\nTrue","explanation":["Set equality ignores input ordering. Both frozensets represent the same pair of permissions and compare equal as keys.","Every member must be hashable. A frozenset containing a mutable list cannot be created just by freezing the outer container."]},{"title":"Return new sets instead of mutating the old one","code":"a = frozenset([1, 2])\nb = frozenset([2, 3])\nprint(sorted(a | b))\nprint(sorted(a & b))\nprint(sorted(a - b))\ntry:\n    a.add(4)\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"No mutating add method\")","output":"[1, 2, 3]\n[2]\n[1]\nNo mutating add method","explanation":["Union, intersection, and difference calculate new results while leaving a and b unchanged. Sorting makes the displayed values deterministic.","The absent add method reflects immutability. Rebind a name to a newly computed frozenset when the logical value should change."]}],"summary":"frozenset is an immutable set of unique, hashable elements. It supports set operations and can itself be a dictionary key or an element of another set.","minutes":2},{"id":66,"title":"How do you implement method chaining in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: Method chaining is a technique where multiple methods are called in a single line by having each method return `self`. This is often seen in libraries like pandas, where multiple transformations are applied in a single chain. Method chaining improves readability and can make code more concise, particularly for classes with methods that perform incremental transformations.\n\n```python\n\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n\n    def increment(self):\n        self.value += 1\n        return self\n\n    def double(self):\n        self.value *= 2\n        return self\n\nobj = MyClass(5)\nresult = obj.increment().double().value\nprint(result)  # Output: 12\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Method chaining","examples":[{"title":"Return self from mutating builder methods","code":"class Query:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.parts = []\n    def add(self, text):\n        self.parts.append(text)\n        return self\n    def build(self):\n        return \" \".join(self.parts)\n\nprint(Query().add(\"SELECT *\").add(\"FROM users\").build())","output":"SELECT * FROM users","explanation":["Each add updates the same Query and returns it, allowing the next add to find the same method again. build returns the final string.","A terminal operation can return a different type. This example assembles text only; it is not a safe parameterized SQL execution API."]},{"title":"Check whether chaining preserves identity","code":"class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 0\n    def increment(self):\n        self.value += 1\n        return self\n\nc = Counter()\nresult = c.increment().increment()\nprint(c.value)\nprint(result is c)","output":"2\nTrue","explanation":["Both calls mutate c. The final result is the same instance because increment returns self.","Callers holding any alias to c will see these changes. Document whether a fluent API mutates the receiver or returns a new value."]},{"title":"Design a chain that returns new values","code":"class Text:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n    def upper(self):\n        return Text(self.value.upper())\n    def surround(self):\n        return Text(\"[\" + self.value + \"]\")\n\noriginal = Text(\"hello\")\nchanged = original.upper().surround()\nprint(original.value)\nprint(changed.value)","output":"hello\n[HELLO]","explanation":["Each transformation constructs a new Text instead of modifying the receiver. The original remains unchanged while the returned objects form the chain.","Chaining is about compatible return values, not necessarily returning self. Immutable-style pipelines are another valid fluent design."]}],"summary":"Method chaining works when each call returns an object that supports the next call. Returning self creates a fluent API that keeps modifying the same object.","minutes":2},{"id":67,"title":"What is the `assert` statement, and how is it used?","body":"- **Answer**: The `assert` statement is used for debugging purposes to test conditions within code. It raises an `AssertionError` if the condition is `False`, optionally with a custom error message. Assertions are used to validate assumptions during development and are generally disabled in production, as they can be bypassed with the `O` (optimize) flag when running Python.\n\n```python\n\ndef divide(a, b):\n    assert b != 0, \"Division by zero\"\n    return a / b\n\ndivide(4, 2)  # Output: 2.0\ndivide(4, 0)  # Raises AssertionError: Division by zero\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Assertions","examples":[{"title":"Check an internal invariant","code":"def average(values):\n    assert values, \"Internal input must not be empty\"\n    return sum(values) / len(values)\n\nprint(average([2, 4]))\ntry:\n    average([])\nexcept AssertionError as error:\n    print(error)","output":"3.0\nInternal input must not be empty","explanation":["With normal execution, the assertion raises when its condition is false. This example illustrates the mechanism, not a recommendation to validate public input with assert.","Optimized execution can remove the assertion. A public average API should use an explicit ValueError check if empty input must always be rejected."]},{"title":"Use a permanent validation check","code":"def positive(value):\n    if value <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Must be positive\")\n    return value\n\nprint(positive(2))\ntry:\n    positive(0)\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)","output":"2\nMust be positive","explanation":["An ordinary if statement and raise remain part of the function under Python optimization. The check therefore enforces the contract in all normal execution modes.","Choose exception types that communicate the problem. Assertions are for assumptions about your own program, not mandatory external-input validation."]},{"title":"Avoid a tuple that is always truthy","code":"condition = False\nassert (condition, \"message\")\nprint(\"A nonempty tuple is truthy\")\ntry:\n    assert condition, \"Actual condition failed\"\nexcept AssertionError as error:\n    print(error)","output":"A nonempty tuple is truthy\nActual condition failed","explanation":["Parenthesizing both values constructs a two-item tuple, which is truthy regardless of the first element. Python may also emit a SyntaxWarning for this mistake.","The correct assertion syntax separates the condition and message with a comma outside that tuple. This output assumes assertions have not been disabled with -O."]}],"summary":"assert checks an internal assumption during development. Use explicit conditions and exceptions for checks that must remain active in production.","minutes":2},{"id":68,"title":"What is lazy evaluation, and how does Python use it?","body":"- **Answer**: Lazy evaluation is a technique where evaluation of an expression is deferred until its value is actually needed. Python uses lazy evaluation in generators, iterators, and with functions like `range()`, `map()`, and `filter()`. This approach optimizes memory usage and performance by not creating all items at once, especially useful when dealing with large datasets. The `itertools` module provides functions for lazy evaluation of infinite sequences as well.\n\n```python\ndef lazy_generator():\n    for i in range(3):\n        yield i * i\n\ngen = lazy_generator()\nprint(next(gen))  # Output: 0\nprint(next(gen))  # Output: 1\n\n```","category":"Iteration","label":"Lazy evaluation","examples":[{"title":"Delay a calculation until next","code":"def values():\n    print(\"working\")\n    yield 5\n\ng = values()\nprint(\"not yet\")\nprint(next(g))","output":"not yet\nworking\n5","explanation":["The generator function body does not run when g is created. next starts the body and requests the first value.","This can avoid unused work, but it also delays side effects and exceptions. Understand where the pipeline is actually consumed."]},{"title":"Read a small prefix of an infinite sequence","code":"from itertools import count, islice\n\nnumbers = count(10)\nprint(list(islice(numbers, 3)))\nprint(next(numbers))","output":"[10, 11, 12]\n13","explanation":["count can produce values indefinitely, but islice requests only three. The source iterator then remains positioned at 13.","Trying list(count(10)) would never finish and would keep growing memory. Always put a bound on materialization of an unbounded source."]},{"title":"Short-circuit a lazy search","code":"def checks():\n    for n in [0, 2, 3]:\n        print(\"check\", n)\n        yield n > 1\n\nprint(any(checks()))","output":"check 0\ncheck 2\nTrue","explanation":["any consumes until it finds a truthy result. It never asks for the final item after the test for 2 succeeds.","A lazy source makes that early stop avoid the remaining computation. With an already-built list of results, the work would have happened before any could short-circuit."]}],"summary":"Lazy evaluation delays work until values are requested. It can reduce intermediate storage and unnecessary computation, but the resulting iterator is usually consumed once.","minutes":2},{"id":69,"title":"What is a memory view in Python, and why would you use it?","body":"- **Answer**: A `memoryview` object in Python provides a way to access the memory of other binary objects like `bytes`, `bytearray`, or `array.array` without copying the underlying data. This is efficient for handling large binary data, as it allows direct manipulation of data segments. `memoryview` objects are particularly useful for slicing and reading binary data in memory-intensive applications like image processing.\n\n```python\ndata = bytearray(b\"Hello World\")\nview = memoryview(data)\nprint(view[0:5])  # Output: <memory at 0x...>\nprint(view[0:5].tobytes())  # Output: b'Hello'\n\n```","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Memory views","examples":[{"title":"Edit a bytearray through a view","code":"data = bytearray(b\"cat\")\nview = memoryview(data)\nview[0] = ord(\"b\")\nprint(data.decode())\nprint(view.readonly)\nview.release()","output":"bat\nFalse","explanation":["memoryview refers to the existing bytearray storage. Replacing its first byte changes the same storage read through data.","The view is writable because bytearray is mutable. release explicitly ends access through this view when it is no longer needed."]},{"title":"Slice a view and mutate shared storage","code":"data = bytearray(b\"abcd\")\nview = memoryview(data)\npart = view[1:3]\npart[0] = ord(\"Z\")\nprint(data.decode())\nprint(part.tobytes())\npart.release()\nview.release()","output":"aZcd\nb'Zc'","explanation":["The slice is another view into the same buffer, so part[0] corresponds to data[1]. Editing it updates the original bytearray.","tobytes creates a bytes copy of the selected data for display. It is the conversion, not the view slice, that materializes a new bytes object."]},{"title":"Respect read-only buffers","code":"view = memoryview(b\"abc\")\nprint(view.readonly)\ntry:\n    view[0] = 90\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Buffer is read-only\")\nview.release()","output":"True\nBuffer is read-only","explanation":["bytes supplies a read-only buffer, so the memoryview cannot be used to mutate it. Creating a view does not override the owner’s mutability rules.","Read-only views still allow efficient inspection. Use a bytearray when your operation intentionally requires writable byte storage."]}],"summary":"memoryview provides access to buffer data without first copying the entire buffer. A writable view can change its underlying mutable buffer.","minutes":2},{"id":70,"title":"Explain how Python’s `itertools` module works and give examples of its key functions.","body":"- **Answer**: The `itertools` module provides functions that create iterators for efficient looping, making it ideal for memory-efficient operations on large data. Key functions include:\n    - `count(start, step)`: Creates an infinite iterator, starting from `start`, incrementing by `step`.\n    - `cycle(iterable)`: Repeats elements of an iterable indefinitely.\n    - `accumulate(iterable)`: Returns cumulative sums (or other binary operations).\n    - `combinations(iterable, r)`: Returns `r` length tuples with unique combinations.\n    - `permutations(iterable, r)`: Returns `r` length tuples of permutations.\n\n```python\nfrom itertools import accumulate, permutations\n\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(list(accumulate(nums)))  # Output: [1, 3, 6]\nprint(list(permutations(nums, 2)))  # Output: [(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 1), (2, 3), (3, 1), (3, 2)]\n\n```","category":"Iteration","label":"The itertools module","examples":[{"title":"Join and group neighboring pairs","code":"from itertools import chain, pairwise\n\nprint(list(chain([1, 2], [3, 4])))\nprint(list(pairwise([10, 20, 30])))","output":"[1, 2, 3, 4]\n[(10, 20), (20, 30)]","explanation":["chain visits inputs one after another. pairwise produces overlapping adjacent pairs, useful for comparing consecutive observations.","pairwise requires Python 3.10 or newer. Neither tool recursively processes arbitrary nested objects."]},{"title":"Generate combinations and Cartesian products","code":"from itertools import combinations, product\n\nprint(list(combinations(\"ABC\", 2)))\nprint(list(product([1, 2], [\"x\", \"y\"])))","output":"[('A', 'B'), ('A', 'C'), ('B', 'C')]\n[(1, 'x'), (1, 'y'), (2, 'x'), (2, 'y')]","explanation":["combinations chooses groups without repeating positions or counting different orders separately. product pairs each value in the first input with each in the second.","The number of results can grow rapidly. Laziness avoids storing every result at once, but cannot remove the cost of consuming a huge output space."]},{"title":"Group consecutive runs, not all matching keys globally","code":"from itertools import groupby\n\nvalues = [\"A\", \"A\", \"B\", \"A\"]\ngroups = [(key, len(list(items))) for key, items in groupby(values)]\nprint(groups)","output":"[('A', 2), ('B', 1), ('A', 1)]","explanation":["groupby starts a new group whenever the key changes. The last A is separate because a B appears between the A runs.","Sort first if the goal is grouping every equal key together, and consume each group before advancing the shared outer iterator."]}],"summary":"itertools supplies small iterator tools that combine into efficient pipelines. Most produce values on demand rather than building full lists.","minutes":2},{"id":71,"title":"What is a metaclass, and how do you create one in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: A metaclass in Python is a class of a class that defines how classes behave. Metaclasses allow customization of class creation, enabling modification of attributes or methods at the time a class is created. To create a metaclass, define a class that inherits from `type` and overrides `__new__` or `__init__` methods. Then, set `metaclass` in the class definition to your custom metaclass.\n\n```python\nclass CustomMeta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        print(f\"Creating class {name}\")\n        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, dct)\n\nclass MyClass(metaclass=CustomMeta):\n    pass  # Output: Creating class MyClass\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Creating metaclasses","examples":[{"title":"See that classes are objects too","code":"class User:\n    pass\n\nuser = User()\nprint(type(user).__name__)\nprint(type(User).__name__)\nprint(isinstance(User, type))","output":"User\ntype\nTrue","explanation":["user is an instance of User, and User is itself an instance of type. type is the usual metaclass that constructs class objects.","This is the extra level metaprogramming works with: code can inspect or customize a class object, not just instances created from it."]},{"title":"Add an attribute during class creation","code":"class Tagged(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        namespace[\"tag\"] = name.lower()\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n\nclass Invoice(metaclass=Tagged):\n    pass\n\nprint(Invoice.tag)\nprint(Invoice().tag)","output":"invoice\ninvoice","explanation":["Tagged receives the new class name, base classes, and namespace before the class object is created. It inserts a class attribute into that namespace.","The attribute is available through both the class and its instances via normal attribute lookup. A simpler class decorator may be sufficient for many such transformations."]},{"title":"Reject an incomplete class definition","code":"class RequireRun(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        if \"run\" not in namespace:\n            raise TypeError(\"run is required\")\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n\ntry:\n    class Broken(metaclass=RequireRun):\n        pass\nexcept TypeError as error:\n    print(error)\n\nclass Job(metaclass=RequireRun):\n    def run(self):\n        return \"done\"\n\nprint(Job().run())","output":"run is required\ndone","explanation":["The metaclass validates the class namespace when the class statement runs, before any instance is created. Broken fails because it does not declare run.","This deliberately checks only the new namespace, not inherited methods. For ordinary interface requirements, abstract base classes often express the intent more clearly."]}],"summary":"A class makes instances; a metaclass makes classes. Use a metaclass only when class creation itself needs rules or customization.","minutes":2},{"id":72,"title":"How does `@wraps` work in Python, and why is it useful when creating decorators?","body":"### **What is `@wraps` in Python?**\n\nThe `@wraps` decorator is part of the `functools` module. It is used in Python to preserve the metadata (like name, docstring, and other attributes) of the original function when creating decorators.\n\nWithout `@wraps`, the metadata of the original function can be lost, making it harder to debug and understand decorated functions.\n\n---\n\n### **Why is `@wraps` Useful?**\n\n1. **Preserves Metadata**:\n    - Ensures the decorated function retains its original name, docstring, and other attributes.\n2. **Improves Debugging**:\n    - Helps identify the actual function being decorated when inspecting or debugging.\n3. **Simplifies Introspection**:\n    - Functions like `help()` or `__name__` will display correct information about the original function, even after decoration.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Examples**\n\n### **1. Without `@wraps`**\n\n```python\nfrom functools import wraps\n\n# A simple decorator\ndef my_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        \"\"\"Wrapper docstring\"\"\"\n        print(\"Decorating function...\")\n        return func(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\n@my_decorator\ndef greet(name):\n    \"\"\"This function greets a person.\"\"\"\n    return f\"Hello, {name}!\"\n\n# Call the decorated function\nprint(greet(\"Alice\"))  # Output: Hello, Alice!\n\n# Check metadata\nprint(\"Function name:\", greet.__name__)      # Output: wrapper\nprint(\"Function docstring:\", greet.__doc__) # Output: Wrapper docstring\n\n# Output:\n# Decorating function...\n# Hello, Alice!\n# Function name: wrapper\n# Function docstring: Wrapper docstring\n\n# Explanation:\n# - The metadata (`__name__` and `__doc__`) of the original `greet` function\n#   is replaced by the metadata of `wrapper`.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. With `@wraps`**\n\n```python\nfrom functools import wraps\n\n# A simple decorator with @wraps\ndef my_decorator(func):\n    @wraps(func)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        \"\"\"Wrapper docstring\"\"\"\n        print(\"Decorating function...\")\n        return func(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\n@my_decorator\ndef greet(name):\n    \"\"\"This function greets a person.\"\"\"\n    return f\"Hello, {name}!\"\n\n# Call the decorated function\nprint(greet(\"Alice\"))  # Output: Hello, Alice!\n\n# Check metadata\nprint(\"Function name:\", greet.__name__)      # Output: greet\nprint(\"Function docstring:\", greet.__doc__) # Output: This function greets a person.\n\n# Output:\n# Decorating function...\n# Hello, Alice!\n# Function name: greet\n# Function docstring: This function greets a person.\n\n# Explanation:\n# - The `@wraps(func)` decorator ensures that the metadata of the original\n#   `greet` function is retained after decoration.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **How `@wraps` Works**\n\nThe `@wraps` decorator internally uses `functools.update_wrapper` to copy the metadata of the original function (`func`) to the wrapper function. This includes:\n\n- `__name__`\n- `__doc__`\n- `__module__`\n- `__annotations__`\n- And other attributes.\n\n---\n\n### **When to Use `@wraps`**\n\n- **Always** use `@wraps` when creating decorators to ensure the original function’s metadata is preserved.\n- Especially useful in large projects or debugging to correctly identify the decorated function.\n\n---\n\n### **Simplest Conclusion**\n\n`@wraps` preserves the name, docstring, and metadata of the original function when creating decorators. It ensures that the decorated function behaves and appears like the original, making debugging and introspection easier.","category":"Functions","label":"Preserving metadata with wraps","examples":[{"title":"Preserve a function’s name and documentation","code":"from functools import wraps\n\ndef log(fn):\n    @wraps(fn)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        return fn(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\n@log\ndef greet():\n    \"\"\"Return a greeting.\"\"\"\n    return \"Hi\"\n\nprint(greet.__name__)\nprint(greet.__doc__)","output":"greet\nReturn a greeting.","explanation":["Without wraps, callers would inspect the wrapper’s name and documentation instead. wraps copies selected metadata from the wrapped function.","This matters for help output, debugging messages, documentation tools, and decorators that other tools inspect."]},{"title":"Inspect the original signature","code":"import inspect\nfrom functools import wraps\n\ndef decorate(fn):\n    @wraps(fn)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        return fn(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\n@decorate\ndef add(a, b=2):\n    return a + b\n\nprint(inspect.signature(add))\nprint(add(3))","output":"(a, b=2)\n5","explanation":["inspect.signature follows the __wrapped__ link installed by wraps and can report the original parameters. The physical wrapper still receives *args and **kwargs.","The call works because the wrapper forwards those arguments to the original function; preserving metadata does not forward arguments by itself."]},{"title":"Access the undecorated function","code":"from functools import wraps\n\ndef double(fn):\n    @wraps(fn)\n    def wrapper():\n        return fn() * 2\n    return wrapper\n\n@double\ndef answer():\n    return 7\n\nprint(answer())\nprint(answer.__wrapped__())","output":"14\n7","explanation":["Calling answer runs the wrapper and doubles the result. Calling its __wrapped__ attribute directly invokes the original function and bypasses that added behavior.","This escape hatch is useful for testing and introspection. It also means a decorator should not assume that its wrapper is an impenetrable security boundary."]}],"summary":"wraps keeps a decorated function recognizable by preserving metadata and a link to the original function. It does not change the wrapper’s actual runtime behavior.","minutes":4},{"id":73,"title":"Explain the purpose of the `weakref` module in Python.","body":"- **Answer**: The `weakref` module in Python allows for the creation of weak references to objects. A weak reference does not prevent an object from being garbage-collected, making it useful for caching applications where objects should be removed once they are no longer in use. When an object with only weak references is collected, the weak reference automatically becomes `None`.\n\n```python\nimport weakref\n\nclass MyClass:\n    pass\n\nobj = MyClass()\nweak_ref = weakref.ref(obj)\nprint(weak_ref())  # Output: <__main__.MyClass object at ...>\ndel obj\nprint(weak_ref())  # Output: None, as the object was garbage collected\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"The weakref module","examples":[{"title":"Observe an object without owning it","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass User:\n    pass\n\nuser = User()\nref = weakref.ref(user)\nprint(ref() is user)\ndel user\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["Calling ref retrieves the referent while it exists. The weak reference does not prevent the object from being collected.","After its strong owner is removed, ref returns None. In real code, assign target = ref() and check target before accessing attributes."]},{"title":"Build a cache that does not retain values","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Product:\n    pass\n\ncache = weakref.WeakValueDictionary()\nproduct = Product()\ncache[\"item\"] = product\nprint(\"item\" in cache)\ndel product\ngc.collect()\nprint(\"item\" in cache)","output":"True\nFalse","explanation":["WeakValueDictionary holds values weakly, so the cache is not a strong owner of the product. While product exists, the entry can be retrieved normally.","Once that last strong reference disappears, the entry vanishes. Use an ordinary dictionary when the cache is supposed to retain values intentionally."]},{"title":"Track membership without owning members","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Listener:\n    pass\n\nlisteners = weakref.WeakSet()\nlistener = Listener()\nlisteners.add(listener)\nprint(len(listeners))\ndel listener\ngc.collect()\nprint(len(listeners))","output":"1\n0","explanation":["WeakSet is useful for registries of objects that should disappear when their owners release them. Adding a listener does not extend its lifetime.","Iteration results may change as objects are collected. Keep a strong reference during any operation that needs a listener to stay alive."]}],"summary":"A weak reference lets you observe an object without keeping it alive. Check whether the object still exists before using it.","minutes":2},{"id":74,"title":"How do `@staticmethod` and `@classmethod` differ in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: `@staticmethod` and `@classmethod` are decorators for methods that do not operate on instance data:\n    - **`@staticmethod`** defines a method that does not take `self` or `cls` as an argument and behaves like a regular function within a class.\n    - **`@classmethod`** takes `cls` as its first argument, allowing access to class-level data and methods. It’s often used for factory methods that return instances of the class.\n\n```python\nclass Example:\n    @staticmethod\n    def static_method():\n        return \"This is a static method\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def class_method(cls):\n        return f\"This is a class method of {cls}\"\n\nprint(Example.static_method())  # Output: This is a static method\nprint(Example.class_method())   # Output: This is a class method of <class '__main__.Example'>\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Static vs class methods: revision","examples":[{"title":"Compare all three kinds of method","code":"class Demo:\n    label = \"class\"\n    def instance(self):\n        return self.label\n    @classmethod\n    def class_name(cls):\n        return cls.__name__\n    @staticmethod\n    def add(a, b):\n        return a + b\n\nd = Demo()\nd.label = \"instance\"\nprint(d.instance())\nprint(Demo.class_name())\nprint(Demo.add(2, 3))","output":"instance\nDemo\n5","explanation":["The instance method reads d’s own label. The class method receives Demo and reads its name. The static method just uses the explicitly supplied numbers.","A static method does not automatically know the instance or calling class. It groups a utility with the class without binding self or cls."]},{"title":"Make an inheritance-friendly alternate constructor","code":"class User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n    @classmethod\n    def from_text(cls, text):\n        return cls(text.strip())\n\nclass Admin(User):\n    pass\n\nuser = Admin.from_text(\" Ada \")\nprint(type(user).__name__)\nprint(user.name)","output":"Admin\nAda","explanation":["Calling the inherited class method through Admin binds cls to Admin. Constructing cls(...) therefore returns the appropriate subclass.","Hard-coding User(...) would lose this behavior. Alternate constructors are a common reason to choose classmethod over staticmethod."]},{"title":"Keep utilities independent from state","code":"class Temperature:\n    @staticmethod\n    def celsius_to_fahrenheit(celsius):\n        return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32\n\nprint(Temperature.celsius_to_fahrenheit(0))\nprint(Temperature().celsius_to_fahrenheit(100))","output":"32.0\n212.0","explanation":["Both calls pass only the explicit temperature value; no instance is injected when the static method is accessed through an instance.","The utility could also be a module-level function. Keeping it on the class is an organizational choice, not a requirement for accessing object state."]}],"summary":"An instance method receives self, a class method receives cls, and a static method receives neither automatically. Choose based on which state the method needs.","minutes":2},{"id":75,"title":"What is a closure in Python, and how does it work?","body":"- **Answer**: A closure in Python is a nested function that \"remembers\" the environment in which it was created, even after that environment has gone out of scope. Closures are useful for creating functions with specific, encapsulated behavior and state. They capture variables from their enclosing scope, allowing the inner function to access them later.\n\n```python\ndef make_multiplier(factor):\n    def multiply(x):\n        return x * factor\n    return multiply\n\ndoubler = make_multiplier(2)\nprint(doubler(5))  # Output: 10\n\n```","category":"Functions","label":"Closures in practice","examples":[{"title":"Build a configurable function","code":"def multiplier(factor):\n    def apply(value):\n        return value * factor\n    return apply\n\ndouble = multiplier(2)\ntriple = multiplier(3)\nprint(double(5))\nprint(triple(5))","output":"10\n15","explanation":["Each call to multiplier creates its own enclosing factor binding. The returned functions retain access to those separate bindings after multiplier finishes.","double and triple run the same inner function logic with different remembered configuration. This is useful for callbacks and small function factories."]},{"title":"Update captured state using nonlocal","code":"def make_counter():\n    count = 0\n    def next_value():\n        nonlocal count\n        count += 1\n        return count\n    return next_value\n\na = make_counter()\nb = make_counter()\nprint(a(), a(), b())","output":"1 2 1","explanation":["nonlocal directs assignment to the count binding in the enclosing function instead of creating a local count inside next_value.","a and b come from separate factory calls and have independent state. Two calls to a advance only its counter; b still starts at 1."]},{"title":"Avoid late-binding surprises in loops","code":"late = [lambda: n for n in range(3)]\nfixed = [lambda n=n: n for n in range(3)]\n\nprint([fn() for fn in late])\nprint([fn() for fn in fixed])","output":"[2, 2, 2]\n[0, 1, 2]","explanation":["The first set of lambdas looks up the shared loop binding when called, after the loop has finished with n equal to 2. A closure is not automatically a snapshot.","The default argument n=n is evaluated separately when each lambda is created. It preserves the value for that function call without relying on the later loop binding."]}],"summary":"A closure is an inner function that remembers variables from its surrounding function. It lets you keep small pieces of private state without a class.","minutes":2},{"id":76,"title":"Explain the purpose and use of the `__del__` method in Python.","body":"- **Answer**: The `__del__` method, also known as a destructor, is called when an object is about to be destroyed. It allows for cleanup actions (like closing files or releasing resources). However, its use is generally discouraged, as it can complicate garbage collection, especially with reference cycles. For resource management, context managers (using `with` statements) are often preferred.\n\n```python\nclass MyClass:\n    def __del__(self):\n        print(\"Object is being deleted\")\n\nobj = MyClass()\ndel obj  # Output: Object is being deleted\n\n```","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Object finalization","examples":[{"title":"Deleting a name is not deleting every reference","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Item:\n    pass\n\na = Item()\nb = a\nref = weakref.ref(a)\ndel a\nprint(ref() is b)\ndel b\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["The first del removes only the a binding. Since b still owns the same instance, the object remains alive.","The example uses a weak reference to observe lifetime without relying on finalizer print timing, which is not a portable cleanup guarantee."]},{"title":"Use a context manager for predictable cleanup","code":"class Resource:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"open\")\n        return self\n    def __exit__(self, kind, value, traceback):\n        print(\"close\")\n        return False\n\ntry:\n    with Resource():\n        print(\"work\")\n        raise ValueError(\"problem\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"handled\")","output":"open\nwork\nclose\nhandled","explanation":["__exit__ runs as the with block exits, including during exception propagation. Returning False keeps the exception active for the outer handler.","This gives a defined cleanup boundary. Relying on __del__ would tie cleanup to object lifetime and interpreter behavior instead."]},{"title":"Register an explicit finalization callback","code":"import weakref\n\nclass Owner:\n    pass\n\nowner = Owner()\ncleanup = weakref.finalize(owner, print, \"released\")\nprint(cleanup.alive)\ncleanup()\nprint(cleanup.alive)\ncleanup()","output":"True\nreleased\nFalse","explanation":["weakref.finalize creates a callback that can also be invoked explicitly. Here we call it ourselves so the output does not depend on collection timing.","The callback runs at most once: afterward alive is False and another explicit call does nothing. For structured resource management, a context manager is usually clearer."]}],"summary":"del removes a reference, while __del__ is a finalizer with timing limitations. Use with and explicit cleanup when the timing of resource release matters.","minutes":2},{"id":77,"title":"What are docstrings, and how are they used in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: Docstrings are string literals that appear at the beginning of a module, class, or function to describe its purpose. They are used by the `help()` function and IDEs for documentation and can be accessed via the `__doc__` attribute. Proper use of docstrings improves code readability and maintainability, providing in-line documentation for users and developers.\n\n```python\n\ndef example_function():\n    \"\"\"This function demonstrates the use of docstrings.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\nprint(example_function.__doc__)  # Output: This function demonstrates the use of docstrings\n\n```","category":"Functions","label":"Docstrings","examples":[{"title":"Retrieve a function’s documentation","code":"def add(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Return the sum of a and b.\"\"\"\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add.__doc__)\nprint(add(2, 3))","output":"Return the sum of a and b.\n5","explanation":["The string is the first statement in add, so Python exposes it through __doc__. The return statement provides the function’s result separately.","A docstring describes behavior; it is not a comment and is not automatically printed whenever the function runs."]},{"title":"Clean indentation in multiline documentation","code":"import inspect\n\ndef greet(name):\n    \"\"\"Greet a person.\n\n    Return a friendly message.\n    \"\"\"\n    return \"Hi \" + name\n\nprint(inspect.getdoc(greet))","output":"Greet a person.\n\nReturn a friendly message.","explanation":["inspect.getdoc removes common indentation from a multiline docstring, producing readable documentation outside the source file.","The blank line separates the short summary from the extra detail. This structure helps both humans and documentation generators."]},{"title":"Distinguish a docstring from a later string","code":"def correct():\n    \"\"\"I am documentation.\"\"\"\n    return 1\n\ndef later():\n    value = 1\n    \"This is not the function docstring.\"\n    return value\n\nprint(correct.__doc__ is not None)\nprint(later.__doc__ is None)","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["Only a qualifying first-statement string supplies the function docstring. A later standalone string does not become __doc__.","Keep the docstring immediately after def, then put implementation statements below it. Comments above def also do not populate __doc__."]}],"summary":"A docstring is a string placed first inside a module, class, or function. Python stores it as documentation that tools and readers can inspect.","minutes":2},{"id":78,"title":"How can you make an object callable in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: To make an object callable, you can define the `__call__` method within a class. When the object instance is called as if it were a function, the `__call__` method is invoked. This is useful for objects that need to behave like functions while maintaining their state or additional attributes.\n\n```python\nclass CallableObject:\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        return x * 2\n\nobj = CallableObject()\nprint(obj(5))  # Output: 10\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Implementing __call__","examples":[{"title":"Keep state across calls","code":"class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 0\n    def __call__(self):\n        self.value += 1\n        return self.value\n\ncounter = Counter()\nprint(callable(counter))\nprint(counter(), counter())","output":"True\n1 2","explanation":["counter() invokes Counter.__call__ on that instance. The value attribute persists between calls, so each invocation increments the same counter.","callable reports that the object supports calling; it does not guarantee that every possible argument list will be accepted."]},{"title":"Configure a callable object","code":"class Multiply:\n    def __init__(self, factor):\n        self.factor = factor\n    def __call__(self, value):\n        return value * self.factor\n\ndouble = Multiply(2)\nprint(double(7))\ndouble.factor = 3\nprint(double(7))","output":"14\n21","explanation":["The instance stores configuration in factor and applies it each time it is called. Updating the attribute changes future behavior.","This resembles a closure-based function factory, but a class makes state explicit through attributes and can expose additional methods."]},{"title":"Use callable instances in higher-order functions","code":"class LongerThan:\n    def __init__(self, size):\n        self.size = size\n    def __call__(self, text):\n        return len(text) > self.size\n\nwords = [\"a\", \"cat\", \"python\"]\nprint(list(filter(LongerThan(2), words)))","output":"['cat', 'python']","explanation":["filter calls the supplied object for each item just as it would call a function. The stored size determines which words pass.","This is useful when a callback needs reusable configuration. The consuming API only needs callable behavior, not a particular concrete type."]}],"summary":"Define __call__ on a class to make its instances callable. This gives you function-like syntax with object state.","minutes":2},{"id":79,"title":"What is a `frozenset`, and when would you use it?","body":"- **Answer**: A `frozenset` is an immutable version of a set in Python. Once created, it cannot be modified, making it suitable for use as dictionary keys or set elements, where immutability is required for consistent hashing. `frozenset` supports all operations of a set except those that modify it (like `add` or `remove`).\n\n```python\nfs = frozenset([1, 2, 3])\nprint(fs)  # Output: frozenset({1, 2, 3})\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"frozenset use cases","examples":[{"title":"Remove duplicates without depending on display order","code":"values = frozenset([3, 1, 3, 2])\nprint(sorted(values))\nprint(2 in values)\nprint(len(values))","output":"[1, 2, 3]\nTrue\n3","explanation":["Like a set, frozenset retains unique elements and supports membership checks. It does not define an application-level sorted iteration order.","Sorting only for display makes the expected output stable. The frozenset itself remains unordered and immutable."]},{"title":"Use a set of tags as a dictionary key","code":"routes = {frozenset([\"read\", \"write\"]): \"editor\"}\nkey = frozenset([\"write\", \"read\"])\nprint(routes[key])\nprint(key == frozenset([\"read\", \"write\"]))","output":"editor\nTrue","explanation":["Set equality ignores input ordering. Both frozensets represent the same pair of permissions and compare equal as keys.","Every member must be hashable. A frozenset containing a mutable list cannot be created just by freezing the outer container."]},{"title":"Return new sets instead of mutating the old one","code":"a = frozenset([1, 2])\nb = frozenset([2, 3])\nprint(sorted(a | b))\nprint(sorted(a & b))\nprint(sorted(a - b))\ntry:\n    a.add(4)\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"No mutating add method\")","output":"[1, 2, 3]\n[2]\n[1]\nNo mutating add method","explanation":["Union, intersection, and difference calculate new results while leaving a and b unchanged. Sorting makes the displayed values deterministic.","The absent add method reflects immutability. Rebind a name to a newly computed frozenset when the logical value should change."]}],"summary":"frozenset is an immutable set of unique, hashable elements. It supports set operations and can itself be a dictionary key or an element of another set.","minutes":2},{"id":80,"title":"Explain the difference between `deepcopy` and `copy` in Python.","body":"- **Answer**: `copy.copy()` creates a shallow copy of an object, meaning only the top-level structure is copied, and references to nested objects remain the same. This can lead to unexpected modifications if nested objects are mutable. `copy.deepcopy()` creates a deep copy, meaning it recursively duplicates the object and all nested objects, creating a fully independent copy. This is useful when you need to copy complex, nested data structures.\n\n```python\n\nimport copy\n\noriginal = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\nshallow_copy = copy.copy(original)\ndeep_copy = copy.deepcopy(original)\n\noriginal[0][0] = 'Changed'\nprint(shallow_copy)  # Output: [['Changed', 2], [3, 4]]\nprint(deep_copy)     # Output: [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\n\n```","category":"Memory & objects","label":"copy vs deepcopy: revision","examples":[{"title":"Compare assignment, shallow copy, and deep copy","code":"import copy\n\noriginal = [[1], [2]]\nalias = original\nshallow = copy.copy(original)\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\nprint(alias is original)\nprint(shallow is original, shallow[0] is original[0])\nprint(deep[0] is original[0])","output":"True\nFalse True\nFalse","explanation":["Assignment creates another name for the same outer list. copy.copy creates a different outer list but retains references to the same inner lists.","deepcopy creates independent inner lists here. Test identity at both levels to understand which mutations will be shared."]},{"title":"Mutate a nested value","code":"import copy\n\na = {\"scores\": [10, 20]}\nb = copy.copy(a)\nc = copy.deepcopy(a)\nb[\"scores\"].append(30)\nprint(a[\"scores\"])\nprint(b[\"scores\"])\nprint(c[\"scores\"])","output":"[10, 20, 30]\n[10, 20, 30]\n[10, 20]","explanation":["a and b are separate dictionaries containing the same scores list. Appending through b changes that shared list, so a observes it too.","c contains a different list from the deep copy and retains its original values. Replacing a top-level dictionary entry would behave differently from mutating the referenced list."]},{"title":"Distinguish replacing an item from mutating it","code":"import copy\n\na = [[1], [2]]\nb = copy.copy(a)\nb[0] = [99]\nprint(a)\nprint(b)\nb[1].append(8)\nprint(a)\nprint(b)","output":"[[1], [2]]\n[[99], [2]]\n[[1], [2, 8]]\n[[99], [2, 8]]","explanation":["Assigning b[0] changes one slot in b’s outer list; it does not modify a’s slot or the old inner list.","The second inner list remains shared. Appending to b[1] mutates that object and is visible through a[1] as well."]}],"summary":"A shallow copy duplicates the outer container and shares nested objects. A deep copy also copies nested objects in this example, so later nested changes stay separate.","minutes":2},{"id":81,"title":"How does the `super()` function work in Python, and when is it used?","body":"- **Answer**: `super()` is used to call methods from a parent or superclass. It allows derived classes to access and extend the functionality of the base class methods. This is especially useful in multiple inheritance scenarios to ensure that all superclass initializations are called. `super()` automatically resolves the method resolution order (MRO), which determines the order in which classes are initialized.\n\n```python\n\nclass A:\n    def __init__(self):\n        print(\"A initialized\")\n\nclass B(A):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        print(\"B initialized\")\n\nobj = B()\n# Output:\n# A initialized\n# B initialized\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Cooperative calls with super","examples":[{"title":"Extend a base initializer","code":"class Person:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\nclass Employee(Person):\n    def __init__(self, name, role):\n        super().__init__(name)\n        self.role = role\n\ne = Employee(\"Ada\", \"engineer\")\nprint(e.name, e.role)","output":"Ada engineer","explanation":["Employee delegates shared name initialization through super and then adds role-specific state. The instance is the same object throughout the calls.","The subclass does not have to duplicate Person’s initialization logic. Compatible arguments are still the programmer’s responsibility."]},{"title":"Trace a cooperative diamond","code":"class A:\n    def visit(self):\n        print(\"A\")\nclass B(A):\n    def visit(self):\n        print(\"B\")\n        super().visit()\nclass C(A):\n    def visit(self):\n        print(\"C\")\n        super().visit()\nclass D(B, C):\n    pass\n\nD().visit()","output":"B\nC\nA","explanation":["D’s MRO is D, B, C, A, object. The super call inside B proceeds to C for this instance, even though B’s declared base is A.","C then continues to A. Calling A.visit directly from B would bypass C and break this cooperative chain."]},{"title":"Add behavior around the inherited result","code":"class Formatter:\n    def format(self, text):\n        return text.strip()\n\nclass LoudFormatter(Formatter):\n    def format(self, text):\n        return super().format(text).upper()\n\nprint(LoudFormatter().format(\" hello \"))","output":"HELLO","explanation":["The base implementation removes surrounding spaces, then the subclass uppercases that returned string.","super returns a lookup helper, not the base instance itself. The inherited method still operates with the same receiver."]}],"summary":"super() continues method lookup after the current class in the MRO. It enables cooperative inheritance and is not simply a fixed direct-parent reference.","minutes":2},{"id":82,"title":"Explain `staticmethod`, `classmethod`, and `property`. When do you use each?","body":"- **Answer**: These are decorators that modify how methods work within a class:\n    - **`@staticmethod`**: Defines a method that does not access instance (`self`) or class (`cls`) data, behaving like a regular function within the class. Useful for utility functions that are logically related to the class but don’t require access to its data.\n    - **`@classmethod`**: Uses `cls` as its first parameter instead of `self`, giving it access to class variables and other class methods. It’s often used for factory methods.\n    - **`@property`**: Allows you to define getters and setters for attributes, enabling controlled access to instance variables.\n\n```python\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self._value = value\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def utility():\n        return \"Utility function\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_value(cls, value):\n        return cls(value)\n\n    @property\n    def value(self):\n        return self._value\n\n    @value.setter\n    def value(self, new_value):\n        self._value = new_value\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Methods & properties","examples":[{"title":"Expose a property alongside an action","code":"class Wallet:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._balance = 0\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        self._balance += amount\n    @property\n    def balance(self):\n        return self._balance\n\nw = Wallet()\nw.deposit(10)\nprint(w.balance)","output":"10","explanation":["deposit is an action and is called with parentheses. balance is exposed as a property and read with attribute syntax.","Keeping this distinction makes a class interface easier to understand. Real financial code would also need appropriate numeric types and validation."]},{"title":"Use a class method to construct an instance","code":"class Distance:\n    def __init__(self, meters):\n        self.meters = meters\n    @classmethod\n    def from_km(cls, kilometers):\n        return cls(kilometers * 1000)\n    @property\n    def km(self):\n        return self.meters / 1000\n\nd = Distance.from_km(2)\nprint(d.meters)\nprint(d.km)","output":"2000\n2.0","explanation":["from_km receives the calling class and constructs an instance. km computes a readable view of stored meters.","The constructor and property play different roles: one creates an object, and the other provides an attribute-like conversion of current state."]},{"title":"Keep a static validator independent of state","code":"class User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        if not self.valid_name(name):\n            raise ValueError(\"Empty name\")\n        self.name = name\n    @staticmethod\n    def valid_name(name):\n        return bool(name.strip())\n\nprint(User.valid_name(\"Ada\"))\nprint(User(\"Grace\").name)","output":"True\nGrace","explanation":["valid_name needs only its argument, so it does not receive self or cls. It can be used before constructing a User as well as from initialization.","Static methods are a useful way to group class-related helpers, but a module function is also reasonable when the operation has no meaningful class association."]}],"summary":"Use methods for actions and properties for controlled attribute access. Instance methods know the object, class methods know the calling class, and static methods need only explicit inputs.","minutes":2},{"id":83,"title":"What is a descriptor in Python, and how do you implement one?","body":"- **Answer**: Descriptors are objects that control the behavior of attribute access by defining `__get__`, `__set__`, and `__delete__` methods. They are commonly used in managing access to data (such as validating input or implementing computed properties). Descriptors enable greater control over how class attributes are accessed and modified.\n\n```python\nclass Descriptor:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner):\n        return instance.__dict__[self.name]\n\n    def __set__(self, instance, value):\n        if value < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Value must be positive\")\n        instance.__dict__[self.name] = value\n\nclass MyClass:\n    value = Descriptor(\"value\")\n\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n\nobj = MyClass(5)\nprint(obj.value)  # Output: 5\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Implementing descriptors","examples":[{"title":"Calculate a value through __get__","code":"class Double:\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner):\n        if instance is None:\n            return self\n        return instance.value * 2\n\nclass Number:\n    doubled = Double()\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n\nn = Number(4)\nprint(n.doubled)\nn.value = 7\nprint(n.doubled)","output":"8\n14","explanation":["Double is stored on the class, so reading n.doubled invokes its __get__ with n as the instance. The returned result uses the instance’s current state.","The instance-is-None branch handles access through Number.doubled. Descriptors should consider class access as well as instance access."]},{"title":"Reuse validation across attributes","code":"class Positive:\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):\n        self.storage = \"_\" + name\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner):\n        if instance is None:\n            return self\n        return getattr(instance, self.storage)\n    def __set__(self, instance, value):\n        if value <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Must be positive\")\n        setattr(instance, self.storage, value)\n\nclass Box:\n    width = Positive()\n    height = Positive()\n\nb = Box()\nb.width, b.height = 3, 4\nprint(b.width * b.height)\ntry:\n    b.width = 0\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)","output":"12\nMust be positive","explanation":["__set_name__ gives each descriptor its declared attribute name so it can store values separately. Assigning width invokes validation in __set__.","The actual values live on each Box instance under different backing names. The shared descriptor stores the rule and storage name, not one shared width for every box."]},{"title":"See data-descriptor precedence","code":"class Fixed:\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner):\n        return 10\n    def __set__(self, instance, value):\n        raise AttributeError(\"Read-only\")\n\nclass Item:\n    value = Fixed()\n\nitem = Item()\nitem.__dict__[\"value\"] = 99\nprint(item.value)\ntry:\n    item.value = 3\nexcept AttributeError as error:\n    print(error)","output":"10\nRead-only","explanation":["Defining __set__ makes Fixed a data descriptor. Its getter takes precedence over a same-named entry in the instance dictionary.","Normal assignment calls its setter and is rejected. A non-data descriptor that only defines __get__ has different precedence and can be shadowed by instance state."]}],"summary":"A descriptor is an object that customizes attribute access through __get__, __set__, or __delete__. Properties and bound methods use related descriptor machinery.","minutes":2},{"id":84,"title":"What is memoization, and how is it implemented in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: Memoization is an optimization technique used to speed up function calls by storing the results of expensive function calls and returning the cached result when the same inputs occur again. In Python, memoization can be implemented using a dictionary or with the `@functools.lru_cache` decorator for automatic caching with a least-recently-used eviction policy.\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=128)\ndef fibonacci(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return n\n    return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2)\n\nprint(fibonacci(10))  # Output: 55\n\n```","category":"Functions","label":"Implementing memoization","examples":[{"title":"Reuse an expensive result","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=2)\ndef square(n):\n    print(\"compute\", n)\n    return n * n\n\nprint(square(4))\nprint(square(4))\ninfo = square.cache_info()\nprint(info.hits, info.misses)","output":"compute 4\n16\n16\n1 1","explanation":["The first call misses the cache, executes square, and stores 16. The second call returns the stored result without executing the body again.","The compute message appears only once. cache_info reports one hit and one miss; those counts show reuse without relying on unreliable timing measurements."]},{"title":"Watch least-recently-used eviction","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=2)\ndef square(n):\n    print(\"compute\", n)\n    return n * n\n\nfor n in [1, 2, 1, 3, 2]:\n    square(n)\nprint(square.cache_info().hits, square.cache_info().misses)","output":"compute 1\ncompute 2\ncompute 3\ncompute 2\n1 4","explanation":["After caching 1 and 2, revisiting 1 makes it more recent. Inserting 3 therefore evicts 2, which must be computed again on the final request.","LRU tracks recency, not frequency or numerical order. A small cache can repeatedly recompute values when the working set exceeds its capacity."]},{"title":"Use hashable keys and clear stale results","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=4)\ndef total(values):\n    return sum(values)\n\nprint(total((2, 3)))\ntry:\n    total([2, 3])\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Use a hashable key\")\ntotal.cache_clear()\nprint(total.cache_info().currsize)","output":"5\nUse a hashable key\n0","explanation":["The tuple of integers is hashable and can form a cache key. A mutable list cannot, so the list call raises TypeError before entering the function body.","cache_clear removes existing entries and resets statistics. Prefer caching repeatable computations; mutable external state can otherwise make remembered answers stale."]}],"summary":"Memoization remembers answers to repeated calls. An LRU cache limits memory by discarding the answer that has gone unused the longest.","minutes":2},{"id":85,"title":"How do context managers work, and what is the `@contextmanager` decorator?","body":"- **Answer**: Context managers manage resources by setting up and cleaning up resources automatically. The most common context manager is the `with` statement (e.g., with file handling). The `@contextmanager` decorator in the `contextlib` module allows for creating context managers using functions rather than classes by splitting setup and teardown around a `yield` statement.\n\n```python\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef my_context():\n    print(\"Enter\")\n    yield\n    print(\"Exit\")\n\nwith my_context():\n    print(\"Within context\")\n\n```","category":"Functions","label":"The contextmanager decorator","examples":[{"title":"Implement the context-manager protocol","code":"class Session:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"enter\")\n        return \"ready\"\n    def __exit__(self, kind, value, traceback):\n        print(\"exit\")\n        return False\n\nwith Session() as status:\n    print(status)","output":"enter\nready\nexit","explanation":["The object’s __enter__ runs before the block, and its return value is bound to status. __exit__ runs when the block finishes.","Returning False means exceptions are not suppressed. It is normal for an exit method to return None with the same propagation behavior."]},{"title":"Use contextlib.contextmanager with finally","code":"from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef session():\n    print(\"open\")\n    try:\n        yield \"ready\"\n    finally:\n        print(\"close\")\n\ntry:\n    with session() as value:\n        print(value)\n        raise ValueError(\"failure\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"handled\")","output":"open\nready\nclose\nhandled","explanation":["The generator yields exactly once to enter the block. When the body raises, the exception is passed back at the yield point and finally still runs.","The exception then propagates to the outer handler. Putting cleanup after yield without finally would not provide the same guarantee when the body fails."]},{"title":"Suppress only a deliberate exception type","code":"from contextlib import suppress\n\nwith suppress(KeyError):\n    print({}[\"missing\"])\nprint(\"continued\")\n\ntry:\n    with suppress(KeyError):\n        int(\"bad\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"ValueError was not suppressed\")","output":"continued\nValueError was not suppressed","explanation":["suppress(KeyError) treats only KeyError as handled. The missing dictionary access is skipped and execution continues after the with block.","The unrelated ValueError still propagates. Narrow exception handling avoids concealing programming errors that were never intended to be ignored."]},{"title":"Manage a dynamic group of cleanup actions","code":"from contextlib import ExitStack\n\nwith ExitStack() as stack:\n    stack.callback(print, \"close first\")\n    stack.callback(print, \"close second\")\n    print(\"work\")","output":"work\nclose second\nclose first","explanation":["ExitStack records callbacks and runs them in reverse registration order when the stack exits. That mirrors nested with blocks.","This helps when the number of resources is discovered at runtime. Register each cleanup as soon as its corresponding resource is acquired."]}],"summary":"A context manager defines setup and cleanup around a with block. Cleanup happens on normal exit and while an exception unwinds the block.","minutes":2},{"id":86,"title":"How does the `__new__` method differ from `__init__`, and when would you use it?","body":"- **Answer**: `__new__` is responsible for creating a new instance of a class, while `__init__` initializes the instance after it is created. `__new__` is called before `__init__` and can return an instance of a different class. `__new__` is often used in singleton patterns, immutable types, or when customizing instance creation.\n\n```python\nclass MyClass:\n    def __new__(cls):\n        instance = super().__new__(cls)\n        print(\"Creating instance\")\n        return instance\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        print(\"Initializing instance\")\n\nobj = MyClass()\n# Output:\n# Creating instance\n# Initializing instance\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Instance creation & initialization","examples":[{"title":"Trace creation before initialization","code":"class Item:\n    def __new__(cls, name):\n        print(\"new\")\n        return super().__new__(cls)\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        print(\"init\")\n        self.name = name\n\nprint(Item(\"book\").name)","output":"new\ninit\nbook","explanation":["__new__ receives the class and returns the instance. Since it returns an Item instance, Python proceeds to __init__ with that instance as self.","__init__ assigns state but must return None. Returning a new object from __init__ is not how instance construction works."]},{"title":"Customize an immutable built-in subclass","code":"class UpperText(str):\n    def __new__(cls, value):\n        return super().__new__(cls, value.upper())\n\ntext = UpperText(\"hello\")\nprint(text)\nprint(type(text).__name__)","output":"HELLO\nUpperText","explanation":["The string content must be chosen while the immutable object is created, so __new__ transforms the value before delegating to str.__new__.","An __init__ method cannot rewrite the already-created string content in place. Immutable subclasses are a legitimate reason to customize __new__."]},{"title":"See when initialization is skipped","code":"class Factory:\n    def __new__(cls):\n        return {\"value\": 7}\n    def __init__(self):\n        print(\"not called\")\n\nresult = Factory()\nprint(type(result).__name__)\nprint(result[\"value\"])","output":"dict\n7","explanation":["__new__ returns a dictionary rather than an instance of Factory. Python therefore does not call Factory.__init__ on that unrelated object.","This is legal but surprising in an ordinary class API. Prefer a plainly named factory function when returning an unrelated type would otherwise confuse callers."]}],"summary":"__new__ creates or selects the instance. __init__ then initializes it when appropriate. Most ordinary classes only need __init__.","minutes":2},{"id":87,"title":"What are the `dataclasses` in Python, and when are they useful?","body":"- **Answer**: `dataclasses`, introduced in Python 3.7, are a way to automatically generate special methods (like `__init__`, `__repr__`, and `__eq__`) for classes that are primarily used to store data. They simplify the creation of classes by reducing boilerplate code, and are especially useful for classes with multiple attributes where immutability is desired (achieved using `frozen=True`).\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Person:\n    name: str\n    age: int\n\nperson = Person(\"Alice\", 30)\nprint(person)  # Output: Person(name='Alice', age=30)\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Using dataclasses","examples":[{"title":"Generate an initializer, representation, and equality","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Point:\n    x: int\n    y: int = 0\n\nprint(Point(2))\nprint(Point(2, 3) == Point(2, 3))","output":"Point(x=2, y=0)\nTrue","explanation":["The decorator generates an initializer with y defaulting to zero, a readable repr, and field-based equality under normal defaults.","The annotations identify fields, but do not automatically enforce their types at runtime. Add validation if your object requires stricter guarantees."]},{"title":"Give every instance its own mutable default","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Basket:\n    items: list = field(default_factory=list)\n\na = Basket()\nb = Basket()\na.items.append(\"apple\")\nprint(a.items)\nprint(b.items)","output":"['apple']\n[]","explanation":["default_factory is called for each new Basket, producing separate lists. Mutating a’s list therefore leaves b’s list untouched.","A single shared mutable default would couple otherwise independent records. Factories make creation of fresh state explicit."]},{"title":"Make a frozen value object","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass, FrozenInstanceError\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Key:\n    code: str\n\nkey = Key(\"A\")\nprint({key: \"value\"}[Key(\"A\")])\ntry:\n    key.code = \"B\"\nexcept FrozenInstanceError:\n    print(\"Frozen field\")","output":"value\nFrozen field","explanation":["The frozen dataclass rejects ordinary field reassignment. With these hashable fields and normal equality settings, it also supports use as a dictionary key.","Freezing is not a recursive transformation of contained objects. A mutable field would still require care and could prevent hashing."]}],"summary":"A dataclass writes common data-object methods for you from annotated fields. You still decide validation, mutability, defaults, and application behavior.","minutes":2},{"id":88,"title":"What is the `@singledispatch` decorator, and how is it used?","body":"- **Answer**: The `@singledispatch` decorator from the `functools` module allows creating a single-dispatch generic function. This means you can define one main function and then register additional implementations for different argument types, making it a form of function overloading based on the type of the first argument.","category":"Functions","label":"Single dispatch","examples":[{"title":"Register specialized behavior","code":"from functools import singledispatch\n\n@singledispatch\ndef describe(value):\n    return \"other\"\n\n@describe.register(int)\ndef _(value):\n    return \"integer\"\n\nprint(describe(4))\nprint(describe(\"four\"))","output":"integer\nother","explanation":["The int implementation handles 4. No string implementation is registered, so the original generic function handles \"four\".","The registry selects code by the first argument’s type, not its value, name, or the desired return type."]},{"title":"Dispatch through inheritance","code":"from functools import singledispatch\n\n@singledispatch\ndef label(value):\n    return \"object\"\n\n@label.register(int)\ndef _(value):\n    return \"integer family\"\n\nprint(label(True))\nprint(label(3.5))","output":"integer family\nobject","explanation":["bool is a subclass of int, so the registered int implementation also applies to True when no more-specific bool registration exists.","A float does not inherit int and falls back to the generic implementation. Register bool separately if it needs distinct treatment."]},{"title":"Notice that only the first argument dispatches","code":"from functools import singledispatch\n\n@singledispatch\ndef combine(first, second):\n    return \"generic\"\n\n@combine.register(str)\ndef _(first, second):\n    return first + \":\" + str(second)\n\nprint(combine(\"score\", 5))\nprint(combine(5, \"score\"))","output":"score:5\ngeneric","explanation":["A string in the first position selects the specialized implementation. Moving that string to the second position does not influence singledispatch selection.","Use explicit branching or another dispatch design if the combination of multiple argument types must determine behavior."]}],"summary":"singledispatch chooses a function implementation from the first argument’s type. It is useful when one operation needs different behavior for different kinds of values.","minutes":2},{"id":89,"title":"How does the `__slots__` attribute work, and how does it improve performance?","body":"- **Answer**: The `__slots__` attribute defines a fixed set of attributes for instances, preventing the creation of `__dict__` and `__weakref__` attributes, which store instance data by default. By avoiding these dynamic structures, `__slots__` reduces memory usage and can improve attribute access speed. However, it limits flexibility since new attributes cannot be added dynamically.\n\n```python\n\nclass Point:\n    __slots__ = ['x', 'y']\n\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n\np = Point(1, 2)\n# p.z = 3  # This would raise an AttributeError\n\n```","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Slots & instance attributes","examples":[{"title":"Restrict instance attributes","code":"class Point:\n    __slots__ = (\"x\", \"y\")\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x, self.y = x, y\n\np = Point(2, 3)\nprint(p.x, p.y)\nprint(hasattr(p, \"__dict__\"))\ntry:\n    p.z = 4\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"z is not a declared slot\")","output":"2 3\nFalse\nz is not a declared slot","explanation":["The class reserves storage for x and y without a normal per-instance attribute dictionary. Assigning an undeclared z therefore fails.","This can save memory across many small instances, but the exact saving depends on the Python implementation and class hierarchy."]},{"title":"Slots are still writable","code":"class Account:\n    __slots__ = (\"balance\",)\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self.balance = balance\n\na = Account(10)\na.balance += 5\nprint(a.balance)","output":"15","explanation":["A slot controls where an attribute can be stored; it is not a read-only declaration. Updating the declared balance attribute works normally.","Use properties, validation, or an appropriate immutable data design when you need restrictions on changing values."]},{"title":"Extend slots explicitly in subclasses","code":"class Base:\n    __slots__ = (\"x\",)\n\nclass Child(Base):\n    __slots__ = (\"y\",)\n\nc = Child()\nc.x, c.y = 1, 2\nprint(c.x + c.y)\nprint(hasattr(c, \"__dict__\"))","output":"3\nFalse","explanation":["Child inherits the x slot and adds its own y slot. Defining slots again avoids introducing an ordinary instance dictionary in this subclass.","A subclass that omits __slots__ normally gets a __dict__, so memory and attribute restrictions must be considered across the whole inheritance chain."]}],"summary":"__slots__ reserves named instance attributes and can reduce per-instance memory overhead. It does not automatically make instances immutable or faster in every workload.","minutes":2},{"id":90,"title":"What is a metaclass, and how is it different from a regular class?","body":"- **Answer**: A metaclass is a class of a class that defines how a class behaves. Metaclasses allow customization of class creation by modifying the attributes or behavior of classes. They are different from regular classes in that they control the creation and initialization of classes rather than instances. A class specifies `metaclass=Meta` to use a metaclass.\n\n```python\nclass Meta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        print(f\"Creating class {name}\")\n        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, dct)\n\nclass MyClass(metaclass=Meta):\n    pass  # Output: Creating class MyClass\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Metaclasses vs regular classes","examples":[{"title":"See that classes are objects too","code":"class User:\n    pass\n\nuser = User()\nprint(type(user).__name__)\nprint(type(User).__name__)\nprint(isinstance(User, type))","output":"User\ntype\nTrue","explanation":["user is an instance of User, and User is itself an instance of type. type is the usual metaclass that constructs class objects.","This is the extra level metaprogramming works with: code can inspect or customize a class object, not just instances created from it."]},{"title":"Add an attribute during class creation","code":"class Tagged(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        namespace[\"tag\"] = name.lower()\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n\nclass Invoice(metaclass=Tagged):\n    pass\n\nprint(Invoice.tag)\nprint(Invoice().tag)","output":"invoice\ninvoice","explanation":["Tagged receives the new class name, base classes, and namespace before the class object is created. It inserts a class attribute into that namespace.","The attribute is available through both the class and its instances via normal attribute lookup. A simpler class decorator may be sufficient for many such transformations."]},{"title":"Reject an incomplete class definition","code":"class RequireRun(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        if \"run\" not in namespace:\n            raise TypeError(\"run is required\")\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n\ntry:\n    class Broken(metaclass=RequireRun):\n        pass\nexcept TypeError as error:\n    print(error)\n\nclass Job(metaclass=RequireRun):\n    def run(self):\n        return \"done\"\n\nprint(Job().run())","output":"run is required\ndone","explanation":["The metaclass validates the class namespace when the class statement runs, before any instance is created. Broken fails because it does not declare run.","This deliberately checks only the new namespace, not inherited methods. For ordinary interface requirements, abstract base classes often express the intent more clearly."]}],"summary":"A class makes instances; a metaclass makes classes. Use a metaclass only when class creation itself needs rules or customization.","minutes":2},{"id":91,"title":"What is a frozen set, and when would you use it?","body":"- **Answer**: A frozen set is an immutable version of a set. Once created, it cannot be modified (no adding or removing elements). Frozen sets are useful as dictionary keys or set elements, where immutability is required to ensure consistent hashing.\n\n```python\nfs = frozenset([1, 2, 3])\nprint(fs)  # Output: frozenset({1, 2, 3})\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Immutable sets: revision","examples":[{"title":"Remove duplicates without depending on display order","code":"values = frozenset([3, 1, 3, 2])\nprint(sorted(values))\nprint(2 in values)\nprint(len(values))","output":"[1, 2, 3]\nTrue\n3","explanation":["Like a set, frozenset retains unique elements and supports membership checks. It does not define an application-level sorted iteration order.","Sorting only for display makes the expected output stable. The frozenset itself remains unordered and immutable."]},{"title":"Use a set of tags as a dictionary key","code":"routes = {frozenset([\"read\", \"write\"]): \"editor\"}\nkey = frozenset([\"write\", \"read\"])\nprint(routes[key])\nprint(key == frozenset([\"read\", \"write\"]))","output":"editor\nTrue","explanation":["Set equality ignores input ordering. Both frozensets represent the same pair of permissions and compare equal as keys.","Every member must be hashable. A frozenset containing a mutable list cannot be created just by freezing the outer container."]},{"title":"Return new sets instead of mutating the old one","code":"a = frozenset([1, 2])\nb = frozenset([2, 3])\nprint(sorted(a | b))\nprint(sorted(a & b))\nprint(sorted(a - b))\ntry:\n    a.add(4)\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"No mutating add method\")","output":"[1, 2, 3]\n[2]\n[1]\nNo mutating add method","explanation":["Union, intersection, and difference calculate new results while leaving a and b unchanged. Sorting makes the displayed values deterministic.","The absent add method reflects immutability. Rebind a name to a newly computed frozenset when the logical value should change."]}],"summary":"frozenset is an immutable set of unique, hashable elements. It supports set operations and can itself be a dictionary key or an element of another set.","minutes":2},{"id":92,"title":"Explain method chaining and how to implement it.","body":"- **Answer**: Method chaining allows multiple method calls in a single statement by having each method return `self`. This pattern is commonly used in libraries like pandas for chaining multiple transformations in a concise and readable way.\n\n```python\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n\n    def increment(self):\n        self.value += 1\n        return self\n\n    def double(self):\n        self.value *= 2\n        return self\n\nobj = MyClass(5)\nresult = obj.increment().double().value\nprint(result)  # Output: 12\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Fluent method chaining","examples":[{"title":"Return self from mutating builder methods","code":"class Query:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.parts = []\n    def add(self, text):\n        self.parts.append(text)\n        return self\n    def build(self):\n        return \" \".join(self.parts)\n\nprint(Query().add(\"SELECT *\").add(\"FROM users\").build())","output":"SELECT * FROM users","explanation":["Each add updates the same Query and returns it, allowing the next add to find the same method again. build returns the final string.","A terminal operation can return a different type. This example assembles text only; it is not a safe parameterized SQL execution API."]},{"title":"Check whether chaining preserves identity","code":"class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 0\n    def increment(self):\n        self.value += 1\n        return self\n\nc = Counter()\nresult = c.increment().increment()\nprint(c.value)\nprint(result is c)","output":"2\nTrue","explanation":["Both calls mutate c. The final result is the same instance because increment returns self.","Callers holding any alias to c will see these changes. Document whether a fluent API mutates the receiver or returns a new value."]},{"title":"Design a chain that returns new values","code":"class Text:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n    def upper(self):\n        return Text(self.value.upper())\n    def surround(self):\n        return Text(\"[\" + self.value + \"]\")\n\noriginal = Text(\"hello\")\nchanged = original.upper().surround()\nprint(original.value)\nprint(changed.value)","output":"hello\n[HELLO]","explanation":["Each transformation constructs a new Text instead of modifying the receiver. The original remains unchanged while the returned objects form the chain.","Chaining is about compatible return values, not necessarily returning self. Immutable-style pipelines are another valid fluent design."]}],"summary":"Method chaining works when each call returns an object that supports the next call. Returning self creates a fluent API that keeps modifying the same object.","minutes":2},{"id":93,"title":"What are coroutines, and how do they differ from regular functions?","body":"- **Answer**: Coroutines are a type of function that can pause and resume execution with the `await` keyword, enabling asynchronous programming. They are defined using `async def`. Unlike regular functions that complete upon execution, coroutines can yield control back to an event loop, making them ideal for non-blocking, I/O-bound tasks.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def my_coroutine():\n    print(\"Starting\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    print(\"Ending\")\n\nasyncio.run(my_coroutine())\n\n```","category":"Concurrency","label":"Coroutines vs functions","examples":[{"title":"Create a coroutine, then run it","code":"import asyncio\nimport inspect\n\nasync def answer():\n    return 7\n\ncoroutine = answer()\nprint(inspect.iscoroutine(coroutine))\nprint(asyncio.run(coroutine))","output":"True\n7","explanation":["answer() returns a coroutine object, not 7 immediately. asyncio.run creates an event loop, runs the coroutine, and returns its final result.","In an environment that already has an event loop, such as some notebooks, await answer() instead of nesting asyncio.run."]},{"title":"Schedule concurrent waits and retain result order","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def task(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return name.upper()\n\nasync def main():\n    results = await asyncio.gather(task(\"a\"), task(\"b\"))\n    print(results)\n\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"['A', 'B']","explanation":["gather schedules both awaitables and waits for their results. The output list follows argument order rather than completion order.","The tasks yield at sleep(0). CPU-heavy loops with no suspension would instead monopolize the event-loop thread until they finish."]},{"title":"Cancel a task and still run cleanup","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def worker():\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(60)\n    finally:\n        print(\"cleanup\")\n\nasync def main():\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker())\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    task.cancel()\n    try:\n        await task\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        print(\"cancelled\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"cleanup\ncancelled","explanation":["The initial sleep(0) gives worker a chance to enter its try block. Cancellation then interrupts its suspended sleep, triggering finally.","Awaiting the task observes CancelledError. The example does not wait 60 seconds: cancellation requests termination and the awaited task handles the unwind."]}],"summary":"Calling async def creates a coroutine. Awaiting or scheduling it runs the work; await lets other tasks proceed when the operation actually suspends.","minutes":2},{"id":94,"title":"What is a closure in Python, and why are closures useful?","body":"- **Answer**: A closure is a function that captures variables from its enclosing lexical scope. Closures are created when a nested function references values in its outer scope and retains those values even after the outer function has finished executing. Closures are useful for creating function factories and encapsulating behavior with state.\n\n```python\n\ndef make_multiplier(factor):\n    def multiply(x):\n        return x * factor\n    return multiply\n\ndoubler = make_multiplier(2)\nprint(doubler(5))  # Output: 10\n\n```","category":"Functions","label":"Closure use cases","examples":[{"title":"Build a configurable function","code":"def multiplier(factor):\n    def apply(value):\n        return value * factor\n    return apply\n\ndouble = multiplier(2)\ntriple = multiplier(3)\nprint(double(5))\nprint(triple(5))","output":"10\n15","explanation":["Each call to multiplier creates its own enclosing factor binding. The returned functions retain access to those separate bindings after multiplier finishes.","double and triple run the same inner function logic with different remembered configuration. This is useful for callbacks and small function factories."]},{"title":"Update captured state using nonlocal","code":"def make_counter():\n    count = 0\n    def next_value():\n        nonlocal count\n        count += 1\n        return count\n    return next_value\n\na = make_counter()\nb = make_counter()\nprint(a(), a(), b())","output":"1 2 1","explanation":["nonlocal directs assignment to the count binding in the enclosing function instead of creating a local count inside next_value.","a and b come from separate factory calls and have independent state. Two calls to a advance only its counter; b still starts at 1."]},{"title":"Avoid late-binding surprises in loops","code":"late = [lambda: n for n in range(3)]\nfixed = [lambda n=n: n for n in range(3)]\n\nprint([fn() for fn in late])\nprint([fn() for fn in fixed])","output":"[2, 2, 2]\n[0, 1, 2]","explanation":["The first set of lambdas looks up the shared loop binding when called, after the loop has finished with n equal to 2. A closure is not automatically a snapshot.","The default argument n=n is evaluated separately when each lambda is created. It preserves the value for that function call without relying on the later loop binding."]}],"summary":"A closure is an inner function that remembers variables from its surrounding function. It lets you keep small pieces of private state without a class.","minutes":2},{"id":95,"title":"How do you create a custom iterator in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: To create a custom iterator in Python, you need to implement the `__iter__()` and `__next__()` methods. The `__iter__()` method should return the iterator object itself, and `__next__()` should return the next item in the sequence, raising `StopIteration` when there are no more items.\n\n```python\n\nclass MyIterator:\n    def __init__(self, limit):\n        self.limit = limit\n        self.counter = 0\n\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return self\n\n    def __next__(self):\n        if self.counter < self.limit:\n            self.counter += 1\n            return self.counter\n        else:\n            raise StopIteration\n\nfor item in MyIterator(3):\n    print(item)  # Output: 1, 2, 3\n\n```","category":"Iteration","label":"Building custom iterators","examples":[{"title":"Separate the iterable from its iterator","code":"values = [10, 20]\niterator = iter(values)\nprint(next(iterator))\nprint(next(iterator))\nprint(next(iterator, \"finished\"))\nprint(list(values))","output":"10\n20\nfinished\n[10, 20]","explanation":["iter creates a cursor over the list. Consuming that cursor does not remove values from the list itself.","next with a default returns finished when this iterator is exhausted. Without the default, the same situation raises StopIteration."]},{"title":"Implement a custom iterator","code":"class Countdown:\n    def __init__(self, start):\n        self.current = start\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return self\n    def __next__(self):\n        if self.current <= 0:\n            raise StopIteration\n        value = self.current\n        self.current -= 1\n        return value\n\nc = Countdown(3)\nprint(list(c))\nprint(list(c))","output":"[3, 2, 1]\n[]","explanation":["__next__ updates the instance’s cursor after returning each value. __iter__ returns self because the object is its own iterator.","The cursor remains exhausted after the first list call. This single-pass behavior is also characteristic of generators."]},{"title":"Create a reusable iterable with fresh iterators","code":"class Countdown:\n    def __init__(self, start):\n        self.start = start\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return iter(range(self.start, 0, -1))\n\nc = Countdown(3)\nprint(list(c))\nprint(list(c))","output":"[3, 2, 1]\n[3, 2, 1]","explanation":["Here the object stores configuration, not a shared mutable cursor. Each __iter__ call creates a new iterator over the range.","Repeated loops therefore get independent traversal state. Choose between an iterable and an iterator deliberately when designing a collection-like API."]}],"summary":"An iterable can supply an iterator. An iterator keeps a current position and returns items through next until it raises StopIteration.","minutes":2},{"id":96,"title":"What are mixins, and how are they used in Python?","body":"- **Answer**: Mixins are a form of multiple inheritance in Python that allow you to add reusable functionality to classes. A mixin typically does not stand alone and is intended to be used as a superclass that adds specific methods or attributes to other classes. This promotes code reuse and can be useful in adding specific capabilities to unrelated classes.\n\n```python\n\nclass LogMixin:\n    def log(self, message):\n        print(f\"[LOG] {message}\")\n\nclass DatabaseConnection(LogMixin):\n    def connect(self):\n        self.log(\"Connecting to database...\")\n\ndb = DatabaseConnection()\ndb.connect()  # Output: [LOG] Connecting to database...\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Mixins","examples":[{"title":"Reuse a serialization capability","code":"class DictMixin:\n    def to_dict(self):\n        return dict(self.__dict__)\n\nclass User(DictMixin):\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\nprint(User(\"Ada\").to_dict())","output":"{'name': 'Ada'}","explanation":["The mixin contributes to_dict while User defines the actual data. Other ordinary classes with an instance dictionary can reuse the same helper.","This mixin assumes __dict__ exists and does not filter private or sensitive attributes. State its limitations rather than treating it as universal serialization."]},{"title":"Combine independent capabilities","code":"class GreetingMixin:\n    def greet(self):\n        return \"Hi \" + self.name\n\nclass LabelMixin:\n    def label(self):\n        return self.name.upper()\n\nclass User(GreetingMixin, LabelMixin):\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\nu = User(\"Ada\")\nprint(u.greet())\nprint(u.label())","output":"Hi Ada\nADA","explanation":["Each mixin supplies a separate behavior that relies on the documented name attribute. Neither has to own initialization here.","Avoid mixing many overlapping responsibilities or conflicting method names. Small capabilities are easier to combine and reason about."]},{"title":"Make a mixin cooperative with super","code":"class PrefixMixin:\n    def render(self):\n        return \"prefix:\" + super().render()\n\nclass Content:\n    def render(self):\n        return \"body\"\n\nclass Page(PrefixMixin, Content):\n    pass\n\nprint(Page().render())","output":"prefix:body","explanation":["Page’s MRO places PrefixMixin before Content. The mixin adds a prefix and delegates to the next render implementation through super.","The ordering of bases matters, and the mixin requires a later compatible render method. Cooperative interfaces make that dependency predictable."]}],"summary":"A mixin adds a focused capability through inheritance. Keep it small and make its requirements on the receiving class clear.","minutes":2},{"id":97,"title":"Explain the Observer pattern and how you would implement it in Python.","body":"- **Answer**: The Observer pattern is a behavioral design pattern in which an object (the subject) maintains a list of dependents (observers) that are notified of any state changes. This pattern is useful for implementing event-driven programming. In Python, it can be implemented by defining a subject class that registers and notifies observers.\n\n```python\nclass Subject:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._observers = []\n\n    def register(self, observer):\n        self._observers.append(observer)\n\n    def notify(self, message):\n        for observer in self._observers:\n            observer.update(message)\n\nclass Observer:\n    def update(self, message):\n        print(f\"Observer received: {message}\")\n\nsubject = Subject()\nobserver = Observer()\nsubject.register(observer)\nsubject.notify(\"State changed\")  # Output: Observer received: State changed\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"The Observer pattern","examples":[{"title":"Notify a subscribed callback","code":"class Subject:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.listeners = []\n    def subscribe(self, callback):\n        self.listeners.append(callback)\n    def emit(self, value):\n        for callback in tuple(self.listeners):\n            callback(value)\n\ns = Subject()\ns.subscribe(lambda value: print(\"received\", value))\ns.emit(7)","output":"received 7","explanation":["The subject stores a callback and calls it when an event occurs. It does not need to know how that callback handles the value.","Iterating a tuple snapshot prevents simple subscription-list edits from changing the current loop’s membership mid-notification. This is synchronous notification, not an event loop."]},{"title":"Send the same event to several observers","code":"class Subject:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.listeners = []\n    def subscribe(self, callback):\n        self.listeners.append(callback)\n    def emit(self, value):\n        for callback in self.listeners:\n            callback(value)\n\ns = Subject()\ns.subscribe(lambda n: print(\"double\", n * 2))\ns.subscribe(lambda n: print(\"square\", n * n))\ns.emit(3)","output":"double 6\nsquare 9","explanation":["Both observers receive the same value and perform independent work. Registration order determines notification order in this list-based implementation.","An exception from one callback would interrupt this simple loop. A production design should explicitly decide error isolation, ordering, and whether asynchronous delivery is needed."]},{"title":"Unsubscribe when a listener is no longer needed","code":"class Subject:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.listeners = []\n    def subscribe(self, callback):\n        self.listeners.append(callback)\n    def unsubscribe(self, callback):\n        self.listeners.remove(callback)\n    def emit(self, value):\n        for callback in tuple(self.listeners):\n            callback(value)\n\ndef listener(value):\n    print(value)\n\ns = Subject()\ns.subscribe(listener)\ns.emit(\"first\")\ns.unsubscribe(listener)\ns.emit(\"second\")\nprint(len(s.listeners))","output":"first\n0","explanation":["Removing the callback stops future notifications. The second event has no listener and produces no message.","Explicit unsubscription also releases the subject’s strong reference to that callback. This matters when callbacks are bound methods that otherwise keep their owner instances alive."]}],"summary":"The Observer pattern lets listeners subscribe to changes. A subject publishes events, and registered listeners decide how to react.","minutes":2},{"id":98,"title":"How do `weakref` proxies work, and when would you use them?","body":"- **Answer**: `weakref` proxies allow you to reference an object without preventing it from being garbage-collected, unlike strong references. This is helpful in caching and situations where you don’t want an object to persist solely due to your reference to it. A `weakref.proxy` provides a transparent reference that behaves like the original object but will raise a `ReferenceError` if accessed after the object is garbage-collected.\n\n```python\nimport weakref\n\nclass MyClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\nobj = MyClass(\"example\")\nproxy = weakref.proxy(obj)\nprint(proxy.name)  # Output: example\ndel obj\n# Accessing proxy.name now would raise ReferenceError since obj is deleted\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Weak-reference proxies","examples":[{"title":"Read attributes through a proxy","code":"import weakref\n\nclass User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\nuser = User(\"Ada\")\nproxy = weakref.proxy(user)\nprint(proxy.name)\nproxy.name = \"Grace\"\nprint(user.name)","output":"Ada\nGrace","explanation":["Attribute operations on the proxy are forwarded to its referent. Setting proxy.name updates the original User instance.","The proxy is not a copied object and does not own the instance’s lifetime. The user variable supplies the strong ownership in this example."]},{"title":"Handle access after the target disappears","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass User:\n    name = \"Ada\"\n\nuser = User()\nproxy = weakref.proxy(user)\ndel user\ngc.collect()\ntry:\n    print(proxy.name)\nexcept ReferenceError:\n    print(\"Target no longer exists\")","output":"Target no longer exists","explanation":["Removing the last strong owner allows collection even though the proxy is still bound. A proxy operation then has no live target to forward to.","Catch ReferenceError where disappearance is a normal possibility, or redesign ownership if the operation requires the object to stay alive."]},{"title":"Compare weakref.ref with weakref.proxy","code":"import gc\nimport weakref\n\nclass Item:\n    value = 7\n\nitem = Item()\nref = weakref.ref(item)\nproxy = weakref.proxy(item)\nprint(ref().value, proxy.value)\ndel item\ngc.collect()\nprint(ref() is None)\ntry:\n    proxy.value\nexcept ReferenceError:\n    print(\"Proxy raises ReferenceError\")","output":"7 7\nTrue\nProxy raises ReferenceError","explanation":["A weak reference must be called to retrieve its target, while a proxy forwards supported operations directly. Neither keeps the target alive.","After collection, the reference returns None; the proxy raises when used. Choose the interface that makes disappearance easiest to handle safely."]}],"summary":"A weak proxy acts like its live target but does not keep it alive. Access after collection raises ReferenceError instead of returning None.","minutes":2},{"id":99,"title":"What are ABCs (Abstract Base Classes) in Python, and why are they useful?","body":"- **Answer**: Abstract Base Classes (ABCs) are classes that cannot be instantiated and define a common interface for subclasses. By using ABCs, you enforce that derived classes implement specific methods, which is beneficial for creating a structured, predictable API. Python’s `abc` module provides tools for defining and working with ABCs.\n\n```python\nfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Circle(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, radius):\n        self.radius = radius\n\n    def area(self):\n        return 3.14159 * self.radius ** 2\n\nshape = Circle(5)\nprint(shape.area())  # Output: 78.53975\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Abstract class contracts","examples":[{"title":"Reject an incomplete implementation","code":"from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\ntry:\n    Shape()\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Implement area first\")","output":"Implement area first","explanation":["ABC and @abstractmethod mark area as a requirement. Python prevents constructing Shape while that requirement remains abstract.","The check concerns the class interface, not automatic correctness of an eventual area implementation. Tests must still verify the actual behavior."]},{"title":"Implement the contract in a concrete subclass","code":"from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Square(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, side):\n        self.side = side\n    def area(self):\n        return self.side ** 2\n\nsquare = Square(4)\nprint(square.area())\nprint(isinstance(square, Shape))","output":"16\nTrue","explanation":["Square supplies a concrete area method and can be instantiated. Its inheritance also makes isinstance(square, Shape) true.","The base provides a shared contract for callers. Other subclasses can compute area differently while exposing the same operation."]},{"title":"Combine reusable code with an abstract operation","code":"from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Report(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def rows(self):\n        pass\n    def count(self):\n        return len(self.rows())\n\nclass SmallReport(Report):\n    def rows(self):\n        return [\"A\", \"B\"]\n\nprint(SmallReport().count())","output":"2","explanation":["An abstract base class may contain concrete methods too. count is reusable code that calls the required rows operation implemented by the subclass.","This template-style design centralizes shared behavior while leaving one step customizable. It does not require every method on the base to be abstract."]}],"summary":"An abstract base class declares required behavior for subclasses. A subclass must implement remaining abstract methods before it can be instantiated.","minutes":2},{"id":100,"title":"How does Python’s `__hash__` method work, and why is it important?","body":"- **Answer**: The `__hash__` method returns an integer, the hash value, which allows objects to be used in hash-based collections like sets and dictionaries. Objects need a consistent `__hash__` implementation that respects the `__eq__` method: if two objects are equal (`__eq__` returns `True`), their hash values must be the same. For mutable objects, it’s best to avoid defining `__hash__`, as changes to attributes can invalidate the hash.\n\n```python\nclass Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return self.x == other.x and self.y == other.y\n\n    def __hash__(self):\n        return hash((self.x, self.y))\n\npoint1 = Point(1, 2)\npoint2 = Point(1, 2)\nprint(hash(point1) == hash(point2))  # Output: True\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"The __hash__ contract","examples":[{"title":"Use equal immutable values as keys","code":"a = (\"A\", 1)\nb = tuple([\"A\", 1])\nprint(a == b)\nprint(hash(a) == hash(b))\nprint({a: \"found\"}[b])","output":"True\nTrue\nfound","explanation":["The two tuples compare equal and obey the same-hash requirement, so a dictionary entry inserted with one can be retrieved with the other.","The actual hash number is deliberately not printed: string-containing hashes can vary between Python processes. Equality of the hashes is the relevant guarantee."]},{"title":"Implement equality and hashing together","code":"class Key:\n    def __init__(self, code):\n        self.code = code\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Key):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.code == other.code\n    def __hash__(self):\n        return hash(self.code)\n\na, b = Key(\"A\"), Key(\"A\")\nprint(a == b)\nprint(hash(a) == hash(b))\nprint(len({a, b}))","output":"True\nTrue\n1","explanation":["Both methods derive from the same code value, so equal instances produce equal hashes and a set treats them as one value.","Do not mutate code while this object is in a set or used as a dictionary key. A frozen value object is safer when the key should never change."]},{"title":"Understand why defining equality can disable hashing","code":"class Value:\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return isinstance(other, Value)\n\nprint(Value.__hash__ is None)\ntry:\n    hash(Value())\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Unhashable without a compatible hash\")","output":"True\nUnhashable without a compatible hash","explanation":["When a class defines equality without a compatible hash, Python normally makes its instances unhashable rather than retaining identity-based hashing.","That prevents equal objects from accidentally having incompatible inherited hashes. Implement both consistently only when stable hashability suits the type."]}],"summary":"A hash helps dictionaries and sets find objects. Equal hashable objects must have equal hashes, and their hash must stay stable while they are used as keys.","minutes":2},{"id":101,"title":"What is method resolution order (MRO) in Python, and why is it important?","body":"- **Answer**: MRO determines the order in which methods are inherited in the presence of multiple inheritance. Python uses the C3 linearization algorithm to calculate MRO, ensuring that each class appears in the hierarchy only once, respecting a consistent and predictable order. This order is accessible via the `__mro__` attribute or `mro()` method.\n\n```python\nclass A: pass\nclass B(A): pass\nclass C(A): pass\nclass D(B, C): pass\n\nprint(D.__mro__)\n# Output: (<class '__main__.D'>, <class '__main__.B'>, <class '__main__.C'>, <class '__main__.A'>, <class 'object'>)\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Method resolution: revision","examples":[{"title":"Inspect a diamond hierarchy","code":"class A: pass\nclass B(A): pass\nclass C(A): pass\nclass D(B, C): pass\n\nprint([cls.__name__ for cls in D.__mro__])","output":"['D', 'B', 'C', 'A', 'object']","explanation":["D lists B before C, and both bases depend on A. C3 places the shared ancestor after both intermediate classes.","The order is neither a simple depth-first traversal nor a repeated visit to A. Read __mro__ when multiple inheritance behavior is unclear."]},{"title":"Resolve the first matching method","code":"class A:\n    def label(self):\n        return \"A\"\nclass B(A):\n    pass\nclass C(A):\n    def label(self):\n        return \"C\"\nclass D(B, C):\n    pass\n\nprint(D().label())","output":"C","explanation":["Lookup checks D, then B, then C, then A. B does not define label, so C’s version is found before A’s.","Even though B inherits A, Python does not complete B’s entire ancestry before considering C. The single combined MRO controls lookup."]},{"title":"Reject contradictory base orders","code":"class X: pass\nclass Y: pass\nclass A(X, Y): pass\nclass B(Y, X): pass\n\ntry:\n    class Broken(A, B):\n        pass\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"No consistent MRO\")","output":"No consistent MRO","explanation":["A requires X before Y, while B requires Y before X. A subclass combining both cannot satisfy those constraints at once.","Python raises at class creation rather than picking an arbitrary order. Redesign conflicting base relationships instead of relying on a guessed lookup path."]}],"summary":"MRO is the order Python searches through classes for attributes and methods. In multiple inheritance, it prevents inconsistent parent ordering.","minutes":2},{"id":102,"title":"Explain monkey patching and when it should be avoided.","body":"- **Answer**: Monkey patching is the practice of modifying or extending code at runtime by altering classes or modules after they have been loaded. This technique can be useful in testing or quick fixes but should generally be avoided in production as it can make code unpredictable and hard to debug. It may lead to compatibility issues or unintended behavior.\n\n```python\nimport some_module\n\ndef new_function():\n    return \"Monkey patched!\"\n\nsome_module.original_function = new_function\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Monkey patching trade-offs","examples":[{"title":"Replace a method at runtime","code":"class Greeter:\n    def hello(self):\n        return \"Hello\"\n\ng = Greeter()\nprint(g.hello())\nGreeter.hello = lambda self: \"Hi\"\nprint(g.hello())","output":"Hello\nHi","explanation":["The method is looked up on Greeter when g.hello is accessed. Replacing the class attribute changes what the existing instance calls.","This affects other instances using that class method too. It is a runtime modification, whereas a subclass override creates a separate specialization."]},{"title":"Scope a temporary test patch","code":"from unittest.mock import patch\n\nclass Clock:\n    def now(self):\n        return 100\n\nclock = Clock()\nwith patch.object(Clock, \"now\", return_value=7):\n    print(clock.now())\nprint(clock.now())","output":"7\n100","explanation":["patch.object temporarily substitutes a mock for the method. The with block establishes exactly where the replacement applies.","On exit, the original method is restored even if the block raises. Scoped patches reduce the risk of one test changing another test’s behavior."]},{"title":"Prefer explicit dependencies when possible","code":"def report(now):\n    return \"time=\" + str(now())\n\nprint(report(lambda: 7))\nprint(report(lambda: 12))","output":"time=7\ntime=12","explanation":["Instead of replacing a global clock, report accepts the behavior it depends on as an argument. Each caller can supply a deterministic clock.","Dependency injection makes the substitution visible in the function signature and avoids modifying a shared class or module. Use patching when that seam is not available."]}],"summary":"Monkey patching replaces behavior on an existing object, class, or module while the program runs. Keep patches narrow and restore the original behavior afterward.","minutes":2},{"id":103,"title":"What is the difference between deep copy and shallow copy?","body":"- **Answer**: A **shallow copy** duplicates the top-level structure of an object but not nested objects. This means that nested mutable objects still refer to the original objects. A **deep copy** recursively copies all objects, creating a complete independent copy, which is useful for complex data structures.\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\noriginal = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\nshallow = copy.copy(original)\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\n\noriginal[0][0] = 'Changed'\nprint(shallow)  # Output: [['Changed', 2], [3, 4]]\nprint(deep)     # Output: [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\n\n```","category":"Memory & objects","label":"Nested copies: revision","examples":[{"title":"Compare assignment, shallow copy, and deep copy","code":"import copy\n\noriginal = [[1], [2]]\nalias = original\nshallow = copy.copy(original)\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\nprint(alias is original)\nprint(shallow is original, shallow[0] is original[0])\nprint(deep[0] is original[0])","output":"True\nFalse True\nFalse","explanation":["Assignment creates another name for the same outer list. copy.copy creates a different outer list but retains references to the same inner lists.","deepcopy creates independent inner lists here. Test identity at both levels to understand which mutations will be shared."]},{"title":"Mutate a nested value","code":"import copy\n\na = {\"scores\": [10, 20]}\nb = copy.copy(a)\nc = copy.deepcopy(a)\nb[\"scores\"].append(30)\nprint(a[\"scores\"])\nprint(b[\"scores\"])\nprint(c[\"scores\"])","output":"[10, 20, 30]\n[10, 20, 30]\n[10, 20]","explanation":["a and b are separate dictionaries containing the same scores list. Appending through b changes that shared list, so a observes it too.","c contains a different list from the deep copy and retains its original values. Replacing a top-level dictionary entry would behave differently from mutating the referenced list."]},{"title":"Distinguish replacing an item from mutating it","code":"import copy\n\na = [[1], [2]]\nb = copy.copy(a)\nb[0] = [99]\nprint(a)\nprint(b)\nb[1].append(8)\nprint(a)\nprint(b)","output":"[[1], [2]]\n[[99], [2]]\n[[1], [2, 8]]\n[[99], [2, 8]]","explanation":["Assigning b[0] changes one slot in b’s outer list; it does not modify a’s slot or the old inner list.","The second inner list remains shared. Appending to b[1] mutates that object and is visible through a[1] as well."]}],"summary":"A shallow copy duplicates the outer container and shares nested objects. A deep copy also copies nested objects in this example, so later nested changes stay separate.","minutes":2},{"id":104,"title":"How does Python handle circular imports, and what are the best practices to avoid them?","body":"- **Answer**: Circular imports occur when two modules depend on each other, creating a loop that prevents Python from completing the imports. To avoid circular imports, use:\n    - **Local imports** within functions or methods instead of at the module level.\n    - **Refactoring** to place shared code into a third module.\n    - **Conditional imports** or importing only what is necessary in each module.\n\n```python\n# Module A\ndef func_a():\n    from module_b import func_b\n    func_b()\n\n# Module B\ndef func_b():\n    from module_a import func_a\n    func_a()\n\n```","category":"Python toolkit","label":"Circular-import best practices","examples":[{"title":"Share definitions through a neutral module","code":"# Single-file model of three modules; no files need to be created.\nfrom types import SimpleNamespace\n\ncommon = SimpleNamespace(prefix=\"user\")  # common.py\ndef module_a_label(name):                # module_a.py imports common\n    return common.prefix + \":\" + name\ndef module_b_label(name):                # module_b.py imports common\n    return common.prefix + \":\" + name.upper()\n\nprint(module_a_label(\"Ada\"))\nprint(module_b_label(\"Ada\"))","output":"user:Ada\nuser:ADA","explanation":["Both components depend on the shared definition rather than importing each other. The namespaces and comments model the dependency direction in one runnable file.","In a real project, put the shared definition in common.py and import it from both modules. This example demonstrates the refactor, not a real import cycle."]},{"title":"Perform an import only when an operation needs it","code":"def circle_area(radius):\n    from math import pi\n    return round(pi * radius * radius, 2)\n\nprint(circle_area(2))\nprint(circle_area(3))","output":"12.57\n28.27","explanation":["The import statement is inside the function, so it executes when the function is called rather than during the surrounding module’s initial top-level setup.","This illustrates deferred import timing with a safe standard-library dependency. Local imports can help a specific cycle, but do not repair a fundamentally tangled module design."]},{"title":"Use dependency injection to remove a reverse dependency","code":"def build_report(load_user):\n    user = load_user()\n    return \"Report for \" + user[\"name\"]\n\ndef load_user():\n    return {\"name\": \"Ada\"}\n\nprint(build_report(load_user))","output":"Report for Ada","explanation":["The report component receives its loader instead of importing the concrete data component. A composition layer wires the pieces together.","This removes the need for a reverse import and simplifies testing. It also avoids the separate bug of functions calling one another recursively without a stopping condition."]}],"summary":"Circular imports happen when modules depend on each other during initialization. Move shared definitions to a neutral module or defer a carefully chosen dependency.","minutes":2},{"id":105,"title":"What is the difference between `@staticmethod`, `@classmethod`, and instance methods in Python?","body":"- **Answer**:\n    - **Instance methods**: Regular methods that take `self` as the first parameter and operate on instance data.\n    - **Class methods (`@classmethod`)**: Methods that take `cls` as the first parameter, allowing access to class-level data and other class methods.\n    - **Static methods (`@staticmethod`)**: Behave like regular functions within the class but do not access `self` or `cls`.\n\n```python\n\nclass MyClass:\n    def instance_method(self):\n        return \"Instance method\", self\n\n    @classmethod\n    def class_method(cls):\n        return \"Class method\", cls\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def static_method():\n        return \"Static method\"\n\n```","category":"Classes & design","label":"Instance, static & class methods","examples":[{"title":"Compare all three kinds of method","code":"class Demo:\n    label = \"class\"\n    def instance(self):\n        return self.label\n    @classmethod\n    def class_name(cls):\n        return cls.__name__\n    @staticmethod\n    def add(a, b):\n        return a + b\n\nd = Demo()\nd.label = \"instance\"\nprint(d.instance())\nprint(Demo.class_name())\nprint(Demo.add(2, 3))","output":"instance\nDemo\n5","explanation":["The instance method reads d’s own label. The class method receives Demo and reads its name. The static method just uses the explicitly supplied numbers.","A static method does not automatically know the instance or calling class. It groups a utility with the class without binding self or cls."]},{"title":"Make an inheritance-friendly alternate constructor","code":"class User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n    @classmethod\n    def from_text(cls, text):\n        return cls(text.strip())\n\nclass Admin(User):\n    pass\n\nuser = Admin.from_text(\" Ada \")\nprint(type(user).__name__)\nprint(user.name)","output":"Admin\nAda","explanation":["Calling the inherited class method through Admin binds cls to Admin. Constructing cls(...) therefore returns the appropriate subclass.","Hard-coding User(...) would lose this behavior. Alternate constructors are a common reason to choose classmethod over staticmethod."]},{"title":"Keep utilities independent from state","code":"class Temperature:\n    @staticmethod\n    def celsius_to_fahrenheit(celsius):\n        return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32\n\nprint(Temperature.celsius_to_fahrenheit(0))\nprint(Temperature().celsius_to_fahrenheit(100))","output":"32.0\n212.0","explanation":["Both calls pass only the explicit temperature value; no instance is injected when the static method is accessed through an instance.","The utility could also be a module-level function. Keeping it on the class is an organizational choice, not a requirement for accessing object state."]}],"summary":"An instance method receives self, a class method receives cls, and a static method receives neither automatically. Choose based on which state the method needs.","minutes":2},{"id":106,"title":"How does LEGB name resolution work, and when should you use global or nonlocal?","label":"LEGB & scope declarations","category":"Functions","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nPython normally resolves a name through Local, Enclosing function scopes, Global module scope, and Builtins. Assignment inside a function normally creates a local binding. global redirects assignment to the module binding; nonlocal redirects it to an existing enclosing function binding. Reading a value and assigning a value are different operations.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse enclosing scope for small stateful callbacks. Use explicit parameters and return values when shared mutable module state would make dependencies difficult to follow.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nReading a variable before a local assignment can raise UnboundLocalError, even if a global with that name exists. Scope is determined by the function’s statements, not only execution order.","summary":"Names are searched from nearby scopes outward. Assignment is local unless global or nonlocal explicitly chooses a different binding.","examples":[{"title":"Read local, enclosing, and global bindings","code":"name = \"global\"\ndef outer():\n    name = \"enclosing\"\n    def inner():\n        print(name)\n    inner()\n    print(len([1, 2]))\nouter()\nprint(name)","output":"enclosing\n2\nglobal","explanation":["inner finds name in its enclosing function because it has no local binding of that name. The module-level name remains separate.","len is found among builtins when no nearer binding shadows it. The example deliberately uses different names rather than modifying the module value."]},{"title":"Choose assignment targets explicitly","code":"count = 0\ndef outer():\n    value = 1\n    def inner():\n        nonlocal value\n        global count\n        value += 2\n        count += 1\n    inner()\n    return value\nprint(outer())\nprint(count)","output":"3\n1","explanation":["nonlocal refers to value in outer, while global refers to count at module scope. The two statements control assignment targets independently.","Use these declarations to express intended ownership, not to hide dependencies that could more clearly be passed as arguments."]},{"title":"Identify an unbound local","code":"value = 10\ndef broken():\n    print(value)\n    value = 20\ntry:\n    broken()\nexcept UnboundLocalError:\n    print(\"Local value was read before assignment\")\nprint(value)","output":"Local value was read before assignment\n10","explanation":["The later assignment marks value as local throughout broken. The earlier print therefore attempts to read an uninitialized local binding.","The global binding was never changed. Move the assignment, pass an argument, or deliberately declare global depending on the desired design."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":107,"title":"Why are mutable default arguments dangerous, and how can you avoid shared state?","label":"Mutable default arguments","category":"Functions","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nDefault argument expressions are evaluated when the function is defined, rather than on each call. A list used as a default can therefore be shared across many calls. A None sentinel followed by per-call construction gives each omitted argument a fresh container, while still permitting callers to pass an explicit container.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse this pattern for optional lists, dictionaries, and sets in ordinary functions. A deliberately shared default is possible, but should be rare and documented.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nChanging [] to {} does not solve the problem: both are mutable. Also distinguish an omitted value from a supplied empty container rather than replacing every false-like input.","summary":"A mutable default is created once. Use None and create a fresh container inside the function when each call needs independent state.","examples":[{"title":"Observe the shared list","code":"def add(value, items=[]):\n    items.append(value)\n    return items\nprint(add(\"A\"))\nprint(add(\"B\"))","output":"['A']\n['A', 'B']","explanation":["The first omitted argument uses the default list and mutates it. The second omitted argument uses that exact same object again.","Printing after each call shows the accumulation. The effect is caused by the default’s lifetime, not by the local parameter name."]},{"title":"Create a fresh list for omitted input","code":"def add(value, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(value)\n    return items\nprint(add(\"A\"))\nprint(add(\"B\"))","output":"['A']\n['B']","explanation":["Each omitted argument becomes None, then a new list is constructed inside that particular call. The calls no longer share their default data.","The identity test is important: an explicitly supplied empty list is a real container and should not be mistaken for an omitted argument."]},{"title":"Respect an explicitly passed container","code":"def add(value, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(value)\n    return items\nshared = []\nprint(add(1, shared) is shared)\nprint(add(2, shared))","output":"True\n[1, 2]","explanation":["The caller intentionally passes the same list, so both calls modify shared. The safe-default pattern does not silently copy explicit input.","Decide whether mutation is part of the API. Make a copy inside the function instead if caller-owned containers must remain untouched."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":108,"title":"What are positional-only and keyword-only parameters, and why design an API with them?","label":"Positional-only & keyword-only","category":"Functions","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nParameters before / are positional-only. Parameters after a bare * are keyword-only, while ordinary parameters between those markers accept either style. These markers define allowed calling conventions without changing the function’s main computation. Keyword-only options make calls self-describing; positional-only parameters can avoid exposing internal argument names as part of an API.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse keyword-only flags for easily confused options. Positional-only inputs are helpful for compact conventional operations or a parameter whose name should not constrain future versions.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA default does not make a parameter keyword-only. The placement of / and * determines that rule, and Python raises TypeError for invalid call styles.","summary":"A slash means “pass earlier parameters by position.” A star means “name later parameters explicitly.”","examples":[{"title":"Require an explicit keyword for a flag","code":"def format_name(name, *, upper=False):\n    return name.upper() if upper else name\nprint(format_name(\"Ada\", upper=True))\ntry:\n    format_name(\"Ada\", True)\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"upper must be named\")","output":"ADA\nupper must be named","explanation":["The bare star makes upper keyword-only, so the successful call documents what True means at the call site.","Supplying the flag as a second positional argument is rejected before the body runs. This prevents accidental positional interpretation."]},{"title":"Hide an internal parameter name from callers","code":"def double(value, /):\n    return value * 2\nprint(double(4))\ntry:\n    double(value=4)\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Use a positional argument\")","output":"8\nUse a positional argument","explanation":["value appears before the slash and cannot be supplied by keyword. Its name is available inside the implementation only for this calling convention.","The restriction can make renaming the implementation parameter less disruptive to callers, since they cannot depend on that keyword spelling."]},{"title":"Combine all three parameter styles","code":"def report(code, /, name, *, active=True):\n    return code, name, active\nprint(report(7, name=\"Ada\", active=False))\nprint(report(8, \"Grace\"))","output":"(7, 'Ada', False)\n(8, 'Grace', True)","explanation":["code is positional-only, name accepts position or keyword, and active is keyword-only with a default. Each portion of the signature has a distinct role.","Default values control omission, while the separators control how supplied arguments are written. These two concepts are independent."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":109,"title":"How do sequence unpacking and starred assignment work?","label":"Unpacking & starred assignment","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nUnpacking assigns items from an iterable to multiple targets. Without a star, the number of produced items must match the number of targets. A single starred target collects the remaining items into a new list, possibly empty. Nested target patterns unpack nested structures, which can make record processing readable when the shape is well defined.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse unpacking for small records, head-and-tail operations, swapping values, and loops over structured data. It communicates the expected shape directly.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA starred assignment still consumes the input iterable; it is not a lazy view. Unexpected input lengths can raise ValueError rather than silently discard extra items.","summary":"Unpacking assigns several values at once. A starred target gathers leftover items into a list.","examples":[{"title":"Capture a head and remaining values","code":"first, *rest = [10, 20, 30]\nprint(first)\nprint(rest)\nfirst, *rest = [10]\nprint(rest)","output":"10\n[20, 30]\n[]","explanation":["first receives one item, and rest collects everything after it. With only one input item, rest is an empty list rather than an error.","The list is newly collected data, not a live view of the original container. Avoid unpacking enormous or unbounded iterators."]},{"title":"Unpack nested records","code":"record = (\"Ada\", (7, 9))\nname, (low, high) = record\nprint(name, low, high)\na, b = 1, 2\na, b = b, a\nprint(a, b)","output":"Ada 7 9\n2 1","explanation":["The target structure mirrors the nested tuple structure. Python assigns the inner pair to low and high after unpacking the outer record.","During the swap, the right-hand values are evaluated before assignment to the left-hand targets, preserving both original values."]},{"title":"Reject an unexpected record length","code":"try:\n    a, b = [1, 2, 3]\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Expected exactly two items\")\na, *middle, b = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(a, middle, b)","output":"Expected exactly two items\n1 [2, 3] 4","explanation":["The first assignment has no starred target, so three input items cannot fit two targets. A shape mismatch raises ValueError.","The second pattern assigns the ends explicitly and collects the middle. It accepts varying middle lengths while still requiring enough items for both ends."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":110,"title":"How do comprehensions work, and what scope do their variables have?","label":"Comprehensions & their scope","category":"Iteration","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nComprehensions construct lists, sets, or dictionaries from an iterable, optionally filtering or nesting loops. In Python 3 their iteration variables have a separate comprehension scope rather than leaking into the surrounding function or module. A generator expression uses similar syntax but produces values lazily. Clear comprehensions express a transformation more directly than repeated append calls.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a comprehension for short, readable mapping and filtering. Split a complex expression into named steps when nested conditions make it hard to understand.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA list comprehension eagerly creates all results. Avoid using comprehensions only for side effects, and do not assume a mutable input becomes independent merely because the outer list is new.","summary":"A comprehension builds a collection from a clear rule. Its loop variable stays inside the comprehension in Python 3.","examples":[{"title":"Transform and filter in one expression","code":"values = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint([n * n for n in values if n % 2 == 0])\nprint({n: n * n for n in values[:2]})","output":"[4, 16]\n{1: 1, 2: 4}","explanation":["The if clause selects inputs before the result expression is evaluated. Only 2 and 4 contribute square values to the list.","The dictionary comprehension creates key/value pairs instead of list items. Its insertion order follows the traversal order in this example."]},{"title":"Keep the outer binding unchanged","code":"n = \"outside\"\nvalues = [n for n in range(3)]\nprint(values)\nprint(n)","output":"[0, 1, 2]\noutside","explanation":["The comprehension’s iteration variable is local to its own scope in Python 3. It does not overwrite the outer n binding.","This scope rule differs from an ordinary for statement at module scope. Do not generalize the behavior of one construct to the other."]},{"title":"Read nested comprehension order","code":"pairs = [(x, y) for x in [1, 2] for y in [\"a\", \"b\"]]\nprint(pairs)\nprint(sorted({letter for letter in \"banana\"}))","output":"[(1, 'a'), (1, 'b'), (2, 'a'), (2, 'b')]\n['a', 'b', 'n']","explanation":["The clauses read like nested loops from left to right: each x visits both y values. The displayed order follows that traversal.","A set comprehension removes duplicates. Sorting the resulting set for display avoids claiming that the set itself maintains a sorted iteration order."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":111,"title":"What does the walrus operator := do, and when is it useful?","label":"Assignment expressions with :=","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nAn assignment expression both binds a name and produces a value inside a larger expression. The := syntax is useful when an intermediate result is needed for both a condition and subsequent work. It is not a replacement for ordinary assignment statements, and some contexts require parentheses to make grouping explicit.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse it to avoid a repeated parse or function call in a condition. Prefer a regular statement when embedding the assignment would obscure the sequence of operations.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDo not confuse =, which assigns in a statement, with :=, which also returns the assigned value. Be careful with operator precedence and names that escape a comprehension.","summary":"The walrus operator saves a value while using it in an expression. Use it when that makes a repeated calculation easier to read.","examples":[{"title":"Reuse a length in a condition","code":"values = [1, 2, 3]\nif (size := len(values)) > 2:\n    print(\"size\", size)\nprint(size)","output":"size 3\n3","explanation":["The parenthesized expression binds size to the length, then compares that same value with 2. The name remains available afterward.","This avoids computing the length twice, although len on a list is already cheap. The main benefit here is showing the data flow clearly."]},{"title":"Parse once while filtering values","code":"texts = [\"0\", \"2\", \"3\"]\npositive = [value for text in texts if (value := int(text)) > 0]\nprint(positive)","output":"[2, 3]","explanation":["int(text) runs once per input and its result is reused in the output expression for matching items. Zero is filtered out.","Invalid integer text would still raise ValueError. Assignment expressions do not automatically provide error handling or input validation."]},{"title":"Stop an iterator loop using a sentinel","code":"source = iter([\"A\", \"B\", \"\"])\nwhile (item := next(source)) != \"\":\n    print(item)\nprint(\"done\")","output":"A\nB\ndone","explanation":["Each condition reads the next item and stores it for the loop body. The empty string is an explicit end marker in this bounded input.","Use a carefully chosen sentinel or catch StopIteration if exhaustion is also possible. This example’s sentinel is part of its input contract."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":112,"title":"How does structural pattern matching differ from a sequence of equality checks?","label":"Structural pattern matching","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nmatch/case, introduced in Python 3.10, selects cases by an object’s structure and can bind components to names. Sequence, mapping, and class patterns express expected shapes; guards add conditions after a pattern matches. The first successful case runs, and there is no fall-through. A bare name is usually a capture pattern, not a comparison with an existing variable.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse pattern matching for tagged records, command structures, and well-defined nested data. It can replace repetitive shape checks and manual indexing.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nWriting case expected with a bare name captures the value rather than comparing it to a prior expected binding. Use literal values, qualified constants, or guards for comparisons.","summary":"Pattern matching recognizes a value’s shape and extracts pieces. The first matching case with a satisfied guard wins.","examples":[{"title":"Match command shapes","code":"def command(value):\n    match value:\n        case [\"move\", x, y]:\n            return x + y\n        case _:\n            return \"unknown\"\nprint(command([\"move\", 2, 3]))\nprint(command([\"stop\"]))","output":"5\nunknown","explanation":["The first case requires three sequence items and a literal move tag. It then captures the remaining items as x and y.","The wildcard case provides a fallback. No later case runs after the successful branch returns."]},{"title":"Use a mapping pattern and a guard","code":"def status(record):\n    match record:\n        case {\"score\": score} if score >= 10:\n            return \"high\"\n        case {\"score\": _}:\n            return \"low\"\n        case _:\n            return \"missing\"\nprint(status({\"score\": 12, \"name\": \"Ada\"}))\nprint(status({\"score\": 3}))\nprint(status({}))","output":"high\nlow\nmissing","explanation":["The mapping pattern requires score but accepts additional keys. The guard runs only after score has been extracted.","The second case handles a present score that fails the first guard. The final wildcard covers records without the required shape."]},{"title":"Recognize that a bare name captures","code":"expected = \"A\"\nmatch \"B\":\n    case expected:\n        print(\"captured\", expected)\nprint(expected)","output":"captured B\nB","explanation":["expected is a capture pattern here, so it binds to B instead of comparing against the old A value. This case therefore succeeds.","Use a quoted literal, an enum member such as Kind.A, or an explicit guard when the intent is equality against a particular value."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":113,"title":"How do truthiness and short-circuit boolean operators work?","label":"Truthiness & short-circuiting","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nConditions use an object’s truth value, which may come from __bool__, __len__, or ordinary defaults. and and or evaluate left to right and stop as soon as the result is determined. They return one of their operands, not necessarily True or False. not converts the truth result to a boolean. Empty containers, zero, and None are all false-like but are not the same value.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse short-circuit conditions for guarded operations and any/all for collections of tests. Treat missing data separately when empty or zero values are legitimate.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nvalue or default replaces every false-like value, including zero. Use an explicit None check when only absence should trigger a default.","summary":"Boolean operators can skip work and return actual operands. False-like values are not automatically missing values.","examples":[{"title":"Inspect operand return values","code":"print(\"\" or \"fallback\")\nprint(\"ready\" and 7)\nprint(not [])","output":"fallback\n7\nTrue","explanation":["or returns the first truthy operand, while and returns the second operand when the first is truthy. The results therefore need not be booleans.","not always returns a boolean. An empty list is false-like, so negating its truth value produces True."]},{"title":"Show skipped evaluation","code":"def check():\n    print(\"called\")\n    return True\nprint(False and check())\nprint(True or check())","output":"False\nTrue","explanation":["The first expression already has a false left operand; the second already has a true left operand. Neither needs the function call.","The absence of called in the output makes short-circuiting visible. Avoid depending on skipped side effects for essential application work."]},{"title":"Keep zero when it is a supplied value","code":"def default_if_missing(value):\n    return 10 if value is None else value\nprint(0 or 10)\nprint(default_if_missing(0))\nprint(default_if_missing(None))","output":"10\n0\n10","explanation":["or replaces zero because zero is false-like. The explicit None test preserves zero and replaces only the chosen missing-value marker.","Choosing between these forms is a data-model decision. Similar care is needed for empty strings, empty lists, and custom false-like objects."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":114,"title":"How do chained comparisons work, and are middle expressions evaluated more than once?","label":"Chained comparisons","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nA comparison chain such as low < value <= high means that adjacent comparisons must all hold. Evaluation short-circuits on failure, and a middle expression is evaluated once for that chain. This differs from explicitly writing two comparisons that repeat a function call. Mixed comparison operators are allowed, but readability matters more than brevity.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse natural range checks for numbers and explicit comparisons when checking sorted relationships. The notation mirrors familiar mathematical intervals.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\na != b != c does not assert that a and c differ. It checks only adjacent pairs, so use an appropriate distinctness test when all values must be unique.","summary":"A comparison chain checks neighboring values, stops on failure, and evaluates each participating expression at most once.","examples":[{"title":"Express inclusive and exclusive bounds","code":"def allowed(value):\n    return 0 <= value < 10\nprint(allowed(0))\nprint(allowed(10))\nprint(allowed(7))","output":"True\nFalse\nTrue","explanation":["Zero satisfies the inclusive lower bound, while 10 fails the exclusive upper bound. The chain reads as one interval condition.","The chosen operators define the boundary contract. Tests should include endpoints, not just values comfortably inside the range."]},{"title":"Evaluate a middle function once","code":"def middle():\n    print(\"middle\")\n    return 5\nprint(1 < middle() < 9)","output":"middle\nTrue","explanation":["middle is evaluated once and its result participates in both adjacent comparisons. The printed message occurs once.","Writing 1 < middle() and middle() < 9 could call the function twice, which matters if the computation is expensive or has side effects."]},{"title":"Avoid mistaking adjacent inequality for uniqueness","code":"a, b, c = 1, 2, 1\nprint(a != b != c)\nprint(len({a, b, c}) == 3)","output":"True\nFalse","explanation":["The chain checks 1 != 2 and 2 != 1, both true. It does not also check the first 1 against the last 1.","A set-based uniqueness test works here because the values are hashable and their equality semantics match the requirement. Choose another approach for unhashable values."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":115,"title":"What does a stable sort guarantee, and how should you use key functions?","label":"Stable sorting & key functions","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nPython sorting is stable: items with equal sort keys retain their previous relative order. sorted produces a new list, while list.sort changes an existing list and returns None. A key function supplies the comparison value for each input item; a tuple key expresses primary and secondary ordering. Stability also permits deliberate multi-pass sorting.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse key functions to sort records by fields without building custom comparison methods. Stable ordering is valuable when preserving an earlier user-selected order within tied groups.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDo not assign result = items.sort() expecting a new sorted list. Also distinguish reverse ordering from reversing the input before a stable sort.","summary":"Sort by a key, and tied items keep their earlier order. sorted returns a new list; list.sort changes the original.","examples":[{"title":"Preserve relative order among equal keys","code":"records = [(\"Ada\", 2), (\"Grace\", 1), (\"Lin\", 2)]\nprint(sorted(records, key=lambda row: row[1]))","output":"[('Grace', 1), ('Ada', 2), ('Lin', 2)]","explanation":["Grace moves first because her key is 1. Ada and Lin both have key 2, so their original relative order is retained.","Stability describes tie behavior. It does not imply that records are also sorted alphabetically unless a secondary rule requests that."]},{"title":"Sort with a primary and secondary key","code":"records = [(\"Bob\", 2), (\"Ada\", 2), (\"Grace\", 1)]\nprint(sorted(records, key=lambda row: (row[1], row[0])))","output":"[('Grace', 1), ('Ada', 2), ('Bob', 2)]","explanation":["Tuple comparison uses the first element first and the second only when the first ties. The output orders by number, then name.","This makes both sorting rules explicit. The key function returns comparable values rather than mutating the original record."]},{"title":"Separate mutation from the return value","code":"values = [3, 1, 2]\nnew = sorted(values)\nprint(values, new)\nresult = values.sort()\nprint(values, result)","output":"[3, 1, 2] [1, 2, 3]\n[1, 2, 3] None","explanation":["sorted leaves values unchanged and returns another list. list.sort modifies values in place and intentionally returns None.","Choose based on ownership: preserve caller-owned order with sorted, or sort an owned mutable list in place when that is the intended operation."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":116,"title":"How does exception chaining preserve the cause of a failure?","label":"Exception chaining","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nWhen translating a low-level error into a domain-specific error, raise NewError(...) from original records an explicit cause. The new exception remains what callers catch, while its __cause__ points to the original failure. Python also records implicit exception context when an exception arises during handling. raise ... from None suppresses context display but is not a method of erasing all diagnostic state.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse chaining at abstraction boundaries, such as translating parsing failures into a configuration error. It gives callers a meaningful error while retaining debugging evidence.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nReplacing an exception with an unrelated generic message can hide the information needed to diagnose it. Avoid catching every exception merely to return a success-like default.","summary":"Raise a meaningful new error from the original one so the public message and underlying cause both remain available.","examples":[{"title":"Translate a parsing error","code":"def parse(text):\n    try:\n        return int(text)\n    except ValueError as error:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"Invalid configuration\") from error\ntry:\n    parse(\"bad\")\nexcept RuntimeError as error:\n    print(error)\n    print(type(error.__cause__).__name__)","output":"Invalid configuration\nValueError","explanation":["The caller catches RuntimeError at the intended abstraction level. The original ValueError is retained as the explicit cause.","Printing the cause type verifies preservation without making the example depend on a version-specific full traceback layout."]},{"title":"Observe implicit exception context","code":"try:\n    try:\n        int(\"bad\")\n    except ValueError:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"conversion failed\")\nexcept RuntimeError as error:\n    print(error.__cause__ is None)\n    print(type(error.__context__).__name__)","output":"True\nValueError","explanation":["Raising without from does not set an explicit cause, but Python records the error being handled as the context.","Explicit chaining communicates deliberate translation more clearly. Both mechanisms can preserve useful debugging relationships."]},{"title":"Suppress context display deliberately","code":"try:\n    try:\n        int(\"bad\")\n    except ValueError:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"Invalid input\") from None\nexcept RuntimeError as error:\n    print(error.__suppress_context__)\n    print(type(error.__context__).__name__)","output":"True\nValueError","explanation":["from None tells normal traceback presentation to suppress the implicit context. It does not remove the __context__ reference itself.","Use suppression when the underlying implementation detail is not useful to the caller, while retaining appropriate internal diagnostics."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":117,"title":"How do try, except, else, and finally divide error-handling responsibilities?","label":"try / except / else / finally","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nThe try suite contains the operation whose failures are being handled. except handles matching exceptions, else runs only when try completes without an exception or control-flow exit, and finally provides cleanup as the construct exits. Keeping successful follow-up work in else reduces the amount of code accidentally covered by a handler. A return in finally can override another return or suppress a pending exception.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse else for follow-up work that should not be mistaken for failure of the guarded operation. Use finally for cleanup that must accompany exit.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nReturning from finally can hide the real result or error. Keep cleanup straightforward and use context managers for resources with a clear ownership protocol.","summary":"try attempts, except handles failure, else handles success, and finally cleans up on exit.","examples":[{"title":"Trace a successful operation","code":"try:\n    value = int(\"7\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"failed\")\nelse:\n    print(\"success\", value)\nfinally:\n    print(\"cleanup\")","output":"success 7\ncleanup","explanation":["The conversion succeeds, so the handler is skipped and else runs. finally runs after the successful branch.","Follow-up code in else is outside the conversion’s ValueError handler, helping prevent unrelated errors from being misclassified."]},{"title":"Trace a handled failure","code":"try:\n    value = int(\"bad\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"failed\")\nelse:\n    print(\"success\")\nfinally:\n    print(\"cleanup\")","output":"failed\ncleanup","explanation":["The matching handler runs and else does not. finally still executes as the statement finishes.","A handler for another exception type would not handle this failure. Cleanup does not imply that all errors have been suppressed."]},{"title":"See why returning from finally is surprising","code":"def value():\n    try:\n        return \"original\"\n    finally:\n        return \"replacement\"\nprint(value())","output":"replacement","explanation":["The finally suite executes during the pending return and introduces a different return, replacing the original result.","This is a demonstration of behavior to avoid. Ordinary cleanup should not unexpectedly change the function’s success or failure contract."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":118,"title":"How should you design and raise custom exception classes?","label":"Custom exception design","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nCustom exception classes usually inherit from Exception or a more specific application exception. They give callers a meaningful type to catch without parsing message strings. An exception can retain structured attributes such as a field name or rejected value, while its message gives a readable description. Small hierarchies let callers catch one specific problem or a related family of problems.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a custom type for a meaningful domain failure that callers should distinguish from ordinary implementation errors. Keep data safe and avoid embedding secrets in messages.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDo not inherit ordinary application exceptions directly from BaseException, which also covers control-flow signals such as KeyboardInterrupt. Avoid creating a new type for every wording variation.","summary":"A custom exception gives an application failure a useful name and structured details that callers can handle reliably.","examples":[{"title":"Catch a domain-specific failure","code":"class InvalidAge(ValueError):\n    pass\ndef register(age):\n    if age < 0:\n        raise InvalidAge(\"Age cannot be negative\")\n    return \"ok\"\ntry:\n    register(-1)\nexcept InvalidAge as error:\n    print(error)","output":"Age cannot be negative","explanation":["InvalidAge remains a ValueError while providing a more specific category. Callers can handle just this domain failure if necessary.","The function explicitly raises the type at the validation boundary, rather than forcing consumers to inspect a generic error message."]},{"title":"Attach structured context","code":"class MissingField(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, field):\n        self.field = field\n        super().__init__(f\"Missing field: {field}\")\ntry:\n    raise MissingField(\"email\")\nexcept MissingField as error:\n    print(error.field)\n    print(str(error))","output":"email\nMissing field: email","explanation":["The field attribute lets programmatic handling use structured information. The base initializer also receives a useful display message.","Callers do not need to split or parse the string to recover the missing field name, so message wording can evolve independently."]},{"title":"Catch a related family of failures","code":"class AppError(Exception):\n    pass\nclass ValidationError(AppError):\n    pass\nclass StorageError(AppError):\n    pass\nfor error in [ValidationError(\"bad\"), StorageError(\"offline\")]:\n    try:\n        raise error\n    except AppError as caught:\n        print(type(caught).__name__)","output":"ValidationError\nStorageError","explanation":["Both concrete exceptions inherit AppError, so the shared handler can cover the application’s intended error family.","Unrelated bugs remain outside that family. A focused hierarchy avoids an overly broad except Exception that hides programming mistakes."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":119,"title":"What is the difference between EAFP and LBYL error-handling styles?","label":"EAFP vs LBYL","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nEAFP means attempting an operation and handling an expected failure. LBYL means checking a condition before attempting the operation. Both are useful: direct validation is clear for known input constraints, while EAFP can avoid repeated checks or stale assumptions about an object. A check followed by an action is not automatically atomic when another participant can change the state.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a narrow try block when a failure is an ordinary possible result of one operation. Use explicit validation when it communicates the contract clearly.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nCatching a broad exception around too much code can hide unrelated bugs. A preliminary existence check can become invalid before the action in concurrent or external systems.","summary":"EAFP tries and handles an expected failure. LBYL checks first. Keep either approach narrow and appropriate to the state being checked.","examples":[{"title":"Look before accessing a dictionary key","code":"data = {\"name\": \"Ada\"}\nif \"city\" in data:\n    print(data[\"city\"])\nelse:\n    print(\"unknown\")","output":"unknown","explanation":["The condition explicitly checks the expected case before the lookup. For this local stable dictionary, that is easy to read.","The code performs a check and an access separately. It should not be generalized into an atomic guarantee for externally changing resources."]},{"title":"Handle the specific failed operation","code":"data = {\"name\": \"Ada\"}\ntry:\n    city = data[\"city\"]\nexcept KeyError:\n    city = \"unknown\"\nprint(city)","output":"unknown","explanation":["The lookup is attempted directly, and only its expected KeyError is handled. The rest of the program proceeds with an explicit fallback.","Keeping the try suite small avoids confusing a KeyError from unrelated downstream code with a missing city key."]},{"title":"Use a direct API instead of either verbose pattern","code":"data = {\"name\": \"Ada\", \"city\": None}\nprint(data.get(\"country\", \"unknown\"))\nprint(data.get(\"city\", \"unknown\"))","output":"unknown\nNone","explanation":["dict.get expresses an optional lookup directly. Its fallback applies only when the key is absent, not when a present value is None.","Choose the API that matches the semantics. If absence and a stored None must differ, use a dedicated sentinel instead of merging them accidentally."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":120,"title":"What are ExceptionGroup and except*, and when do they help?","label":"Exception groups & except*","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nPython 3.11 introduced ExceptionGroup to represent several independent failures together. except* selects matching subgroups by exception type and permits separate handlers to process different parts of the group. This is important for structured concurrent operations where several tasks may fail. Handling one subgroup does not automatically handle all remaining failures.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse exception groups when an operation genuinely produces multiple failures, particularly with TaskGroup. Preserve group structure when callers need to inspect nested failures.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nAn ordinary except ValueError does not select ValueError members inside a group. except* has different restrictions, and unhandled members continue propagating.","summary":"ExceptionGroup carries several failures together. except* handles the matching parts without pretending the other failures disappeared.","examples":[{"title":"Inspect a group’s members","code":"group = ExceptionGroup(\"batch\", [ValueError(\"bad\"), TypeError(\"wrong\")])\nprint(group.message)\nprint([type(error).__name__ for error in group.exceptions])","output":"batch\n['ValueError', 'TypeError']","explanation":["The group retains both exceptions as structured members rather than flattening them into one message. Its message labels the combined operation.","This example requires Python 3.11 or newer. The exceptions are constructed here to make the structure visible without a traceback."]},{"title":"Handle different subgroups by type","code":"try:\n    raise ExceptionGroup(\"batch\", [ValueError(\"bad\"), TypeError(\"wrong\")])\nexcept* ValueError as group:\n    print(\"values\", len(group.exceptions))\nexcept* TypeError as group:\n    print(\"types\", len(group.exceptions))","output":"values 1\ntypes 1","explanation":["Each handler receives the matching subgroup. Both run because the original group contains failures of both selected types.","This is not ordinary first-matching-handler behavior. Any members not covered by handlers would remain pending for propagation."]},{"title":"Split a group for explicit processing","code":"group = ExceptionGroup(\"batch\", [ValueError(\"bad\"), KeyError(\"key\")])\nmatching, rest = group.split(ValueError)\nprint(type(matching.exceptions[0]).__name__)\nprint(type(rest.exceptions[0]).__name__)","output":"ValueError\nKeyError","explanation":["split divides matching members from the rest while preserving useful group structure. The result supports programmatic classification outside an except* statement.","A side can be None when it has no members. General-purpose code must check before accessing its exceptions attribute."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":121,"title":"Why use pathlib for filesystem paths instead of building strings manually?","label":"Pathlib & filesystem paths","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\npathlib represents paths as objects with operations for joining, inspecting, and accessing files. Path performs filesystem operations, while PurePath variants can model path syntax without touching the filesystem. The / operator joins components according to path rules. Reading and writing text should specify an encoding so behavior is not dependent on a machine’s default.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse Path for ordinary local file access and temporary directories for experiments. PurePosixPath or PureWindowsPath is useful when reasoning about another platform’s path syntax.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nJoining an absolute component can replace the earlier path. Also, resolving or joining a path alone does not establish that an untrusted target stays inside an allowed directory.","summary":"Path objects keep path handling readable and platform-aware. Choose explicit encodings and validate untrusted file locations.","examples":[{"title":"Inspect path components without creating files","code":"from pathlib import PurePosixPath\np = PurePosixPath(\"notes\") / \"chapter.txt\"\nprint(str(p))\nprint(p.stem, p.suffix)\nprint(p.with_suffix(\".md\"))","output":"notes/chapter.txt\nchapter .txt\nnotes/chapter.md","explanation":["PurePosixPath fixes the syntax for a portable, deterministic example. These operations describe names and do not access disk.","Changing a suffix returns another path object; it does not rename a real file. Filesystem operations require a concrete Path."]},{"title":"Read and write a temporary text file","code":"from pathlib import Path\nfrom tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nwith TemporaryDirectory() as folder:\n    file = Path(folder) / \"note.txt\"\n    file.write_text(\"hello\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    print(file.exists())\n    print(file.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))","output":"True\nhello","explanation":["The temporary directory owns the experiment’s files and cleans them up on exit. Path handles joining and text access.","An explicit encoding makes this text round-trip predictable. Real applications should still handle permission errors and other I/O failures."]},{"title":"Understand absolute path replacement","code":"from pathlib import PurePosixPath\nbase = PurePosixPath(\"/safe/root\")\nprint(base / \"child.txt\")\nprint(base / \"/elsewhere/file.txt\")","output":"/safe/root/child.txt\n/elsewhere/file.txt","explanation":["The relative child extends base, but an absolute right-hand component starts a new root path and replaces the previous base.","Do not accept untrusted components and assume joining guarantees containment. Validate the resolved destination before sensitive filesystem operations."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":122,"title":"How are str, bytes, encoding, and decoding related?","label":"Text encodings & bytes","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nstr holds Unicode text, while bytes holds byte values. Encoding converts text into bytes using a named encoding; decoding reverses that interpretation when the bytes are valid for that encoding. Character counts and byte counts can differ. File, network, and binary boundaries require a clear encoding contract. Error handlers such as replace deliberately lose information and should be chosen intentionally.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse explicit UTF-8 when that is the agreed format. Keep data as bytes for binary protocols and decode only where the protocol specifies text.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nCalling str on bytes does not decode the bytes into their text contents. It produces a representation of the bytes object; use decode with the correct encoding instead.","summary":"Encode text into bytes; decode bytes into text. The encoding is the shared rule that makes that conversion meaningful.","examples":[{"title":"Compare character count and byte count","code":"text = \"café\"\ndata = text.encode(\"utf-8\")\nprint(len(text), len(data))\nprint(data.hex())\nprint(data.decode(\"utf-8\") == text)","output":"4 5\n636166c3a9\nTrue","explanation":["The accented character occupies one Python string code point here but requires two UTF-8 bytes. The complete string therefore has different character and byte lengths.","Decoding with the same agreed encoding reconstructs the original text. The hexadecimal display avoids relying on terminal font or locale settings."]},{"title":"Handle invalid input deliberately","code":"data = bytes([255])\ntry:\n    data.decode(\"utf-8\")\nexcept UnicodeDecodeError:\n    print(\"Invalid UTF-8\")\nprint(ascii(data.decode(\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")))","output":"Invalid UTF-8\n'\\ufffd'","explanation":["The single byte 255 is not a valid standalone UTF-8 sequence, so strict decoding raises. The replacement handler substitutes the Unicode replacement character.","Replacement can be useful for best-effort display, but loses the original text meaning. Keep raw bytes if diagnostics or lossless recovery matter."]},{"title":"Distinguish representation from decoding","code":"data = b\"hello\"\nprint(str(data))\nprint(data.decode(\"ascii\"))\nprint(list(data[:2]))","output":"b'hello'\nhello\n[104, 101]","explanation":["str(data) describes a bytes object, including the b prefix and quotes. decode interprets its bytes as text using the specified encoding.","Indexing bytes yields integer byte values. A byte sequence is not a sequence of one-character Unicode strings."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":123,"title":"What does JSON serialization preserve, and which Python values need special handling?","label":"JSON serialization boundaries","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\njson.dumps converts supported Python data into JSON text, and json.loads parses JSON text back into Python values. JSON has objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, and null, not arbitrary Python classes or tuple types. A round-trip can therefore change certain Python details, such as converting a tuple into a list. Custom conversion must define a deliberate, interoperable representation.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse JSON for structured interchange and validate parsed data before using it. Stable key ordering is useful for readable snapshots but is not a replacement for a data schema.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA valid JSON document can still have the wrong shape for your application. Also, do not load untrusted serialized Python objects with pickle as if it were equivalent to JSON.","summary":"JSON exchanges a limited set of data shapes, not every Python object. Parse the text, then validate the resulting data.","examples":[{"title":"Round-trip ordinary data","code":"import json\ndata = {\"name\": \"Ada\", \"active\": True}\ntext = json.dumps(data, sort_keys=True)\nprint(text)\nprint(json.loads(text)[\"active\"])","output":"{\"active\": true, \"name\": \"Ada\"}\nTrue","explanation":["The serializer maps Python True to JSON true, and the parser maps it back. sort_keys makes this displayed object ordering explicit.","The parsed result is data, not a validated User object. Application rules must still check required fields and accepted value types."]},{"title":"Notice changes in container type","code":"import json\noriginal = (1, 2)\nrestored = json.loads(json.dumps(original))\nprint(restored)\nprint(type(restored).__name__)","output":"[1, 2]\nlist","explanation":["JSON arrays do not distinguish Python tuples from lists. Parsing the serialized array produces a list.","A successful round-trip of values does not guarantee preservation of every Python type or object identity. Use an explicit format when that distinction matters."]},{"title":"Define a representation for a date","code":"import json\nfrom datetime import date\nvalue = date(2026, 1, 2)\ntry:\n    json.dumps(value)\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Date needs conversion\")\nprint(json.dumps({\"day\": value.isoformat()}))","output":"Date needs conversion\n{\"day\": \"2026-01-02\"}","explanation":["A date is not a built-in JSON type, so ordinary serialization fails. Converting it to an agreed ISO-formatted string gives a clear representation.","The receiver must know whether that string represents a date. JSON alone does not attach a date type or timezone policy to strings."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":124,"title":"Why should CSV data be parsed with the csv module rather than split on commas?","label":"CSV parsing & quoting","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nCSV fields may contain commas, quotes, or line breaks when escaped according to a dialect. Splitting a line on commas ignores those rules and can corrupt record boundaries. csv.reader and csv.writer handle quoting, while DictReader and DictWriter associate columns with names. Parsed fields are normally strings, so numeric and domain conversion are separate steps.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse the csv module for tabular text exchange. When opening real CSV files, specify newline=\"\" and an explicit encoding to let the CSV layer handle line endings consistently.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA field that looks numeric is not automatically an integer. Validate headers and convert values intentionally rather than assuming every record has the expected shape.","summary":"CSV is a quoted data format, not just comma-separated substrings. Let csv parse fields, then convert their meanings.","examples":[{"title":"Parse a quoted comma correctly","code":"import csv\nfrom io import StringIO\ntext = 'name,city\\n\"Ada, A.\",London\\n'\nrows = list(csv.reader(StringIO(text)))\nprint(rows[1])","output":"['Ada, A.', 'London']","explanation":["The comma inside the quoted name belongs to one field, not a new column. csv.reader understands that quoting rule.","A plain split would create too many pieces and damage the record. The in-memory stream keeps this example self-contained."]},{"title":"Read by column name and convert a value","code":"import csv\nfrom io import StringIO\nrows = csv.DictReader(StringIO(\"name,score\\nAda,7\\n\"))\nrow = next(rows)\nprint(row[\"name\"])\nprint(type(row[\"score\"]).__name__)\nprint(int(row[\"score\"]) + 1)","output":"Ada\nstr\n8","explanation":["DictReader uses the first record as headers and provides named string fields. The score remains a string until explicitly converted.","Handle missing or invalid columns in real data. Parsing a syntactically valid CSV file does not establish that its contents meet your business rules."]},{"title":"Write the required quoting automatically","code":"import csv\nfrom io import StringIO\noutput = StringIO()\nwriter = csv.writer(output, lineterminator=\"\\n\")\nwriter.writerow([\"Ada, A.\", 7])\nprint(output.getvalue().strip())","output":"\"Ada, A.\",7","explanation":["The writer detects the comma in the name and adds quotes automatically. A fixed line terminator makes the demonstrated output deterministic.","Use the writer rather than hand-building quotes, which becomes error-prone when values themselves contain quotes or newlines."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":125,"title":"What is the difference between naive and timezone-aware datetime values?","label":"Naive & aware datetimes","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nAn aware datetime has timezone information that defines its relation to a time standard. A naive datetime has no such interpretation attached. UTC-aware values are useful for recording instants, while a separate local-zone conversion is needed for presentation. Attaching a timezone to wall-clock fields and converting an already-aware instant are different operations. Civil time can have ambiguous or nonexistent local times around clock changes.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse aware timestamps for events exchanged across systems. Keep a clear policy for user-local dates, scheduled wall-clock times, and elapsed durations.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nreplace(tzinfo=...) labels existing fields without converting them. Use astimezone for conversion of an aware instant, and do not silently mix naive and aware timestamps.","summary":"A naive datetime has no timezone meaning attached. An aware datetime identifies how its clock fields relate to an instant.","examples":[{"title":"Represent an explicit UTC instant","code":"from datetime import datetime, timezone\nstamp = datetime(2026, 1, 2, 12, 30, tzinfo=timezone.utc)\nprint(stamp.isoformat())\nprint(stamp.utcoffset().total_seconds())","output":"2026-01-02T12:30:00+00:00\n0.0","explanation":["The +00:00 offset states the UTC interpretation explicitly. No machine-local timezone is consulted to construct this timestamp.","Use fixed dates in reproducible examples instead of now. Applications can capture the current UTC time with an explicit aware-time API."]},{"title":"Convert the same instant to another offset","code":"from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone\nutc = datetime(2026, 1, 2, 12, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc)\noffset = timezone(timedelta(hours=5, minutes=30))\nlocal = utc.astimezone(offset)\nprint(local.isoformat())\nprint(local == utc)","output":"2026-01-02T17:30:00+05:30\nTrue","explanation":["astimezone changes displayed clock fields and offset while preserving the represented instant. Equality confirms that both aware values identify the same time.","This fixed-offset timezone has no daylight-saving rules. Use an appropriate ZoneInfo zone when historical or seasonal civil-time changes matter."]},{"title":"Reject ordering across missing timezone meaning","code":"from datetime import datetime, timezone\nnaive = datetime(2026, 1, 2)\naware = datetime(2026, 1, 2, tzinfo=timezone.utc)\ntry:\n    print(naive < aware)\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Choose a consistent timezone policy\")","output":"Choose a consistent timezone policy","explanation":["Python rejects this ordering comparison because the naive value lacks the information needed to relate it to the aware instant.","Do not patch over the problem by arbitrarily labeling the naive value. First establish what timezone its original clock fields actually meant."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":126,"title":"Why can floating-point arithmetic be surprising, and when should you use Decimal?","label":"Floating point & Decimal","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nA binary float represents numbers using a finite binary fraction, so many decimal fractions are approximated. The representation is excellent for many scientific and general computations, but exact decimal rounding rules may require Decimal. Constructing Decimal from a string preserves that decimal spelling, while constructing it from a float preserves the float’s already-approximated value. Arithmetic context and rounding mode remain part of the contract.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse approximate comparisons for numerical floats and Decimal when the application requires deliberate decimal precision and rounding rules. Choose the numeric model before performing calculations.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nConverting an imprecise float to Decimal afterward cannot recover an original exact decimal value. Also, formatting a value to two places does not change its underlying arithmetic type.","summary":"Floats approximate many decimal fractions. Use Decimal from strings when exact decimal input and explicit rounding rules matter.","examples":[{"title":"See binary rounding and a tolerant comparison","code":"import math\nvalue = 0.1 + 0.2\nprint(value)\nprint(value == 0.3)\nprint(math.isclose(value, 0.3))","output":"0.30000000000000004\nFalse\nTrue","explanation":["The finite binary approximations produce a result slightly different from the float representing 0.3. Exact equality therefore fails.","isclose uses tolerances rather than a blanket equality rule. Set tolerances appropriate to the scale and accuracy requirements of the computation."]},{"title":"Build exact decimal values from strings","code":"from decimal import Decimal\nvalue = Decimal(\"0.1\") + Decimal(\"0.2\")\nprint(value)\nprint(value == Decimal(\"0.3\"))\nprint(Decimal(0.1) == Decimal(\"0.1\"))","output":"0.3\nTrue\nFalse","explanation":["The string inputs specify exact decimal fractions within this simple calculation. Their sum is exactly the intended decimal 0.3.","Decimal(0.1) instead imports the already-approximated float value. The source of a numeric value matters as much as the final class used to store it."]},{"title":"Specify a rounding rule","code":"from decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP\nvalue = Decimal(\"2.345\")\nrounded = value.quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)\nprint(rounded)\nprint(value)","output":"2.35\n2.345","explanation":["quantize requests two decimal places with an explicit half-up tie rule. The original immutable value is unchanged.","Different domains can require different rounding policies. State the rule instead of assuming a display format or default context matches it."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":127,"title":"When is fractions.Fraction more useful than float?","label":"Exact rational arithmetic","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nFraction represents a rational number as an exact numerator and denominator in reduced form. Arithmetic between rational values remains exact unless you deliberately convert into a different numeric model. Construction from an integer pair or decimal string differs from construction from a float: a float input represents its binary value exactly, including any prior approximation. limit_denominator can find a simpler nearby rational.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse fractions for ratios, exact combinatorial calculations, and teaching arithmetic. Consider growth in numerator and denominator sizes during long computations.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nFraction(0.1) is not the same as Fraction(\"0.1\"). An exact representation of an approximate input does not make the original decimal interpretation exact.","summary":"Fraction keeps ratios exact as integers over integers. Choose inputs carefully, especially when converting existing floats.","examples":[{"title":"Add exact rational values","code":"from fractions import Fraction\nvalue = Fraction(1, 3) + Fraction(1, 6)\nprint(value)\nprint(value.numerator, value.denominator)","output":"1/2\n1 2","explanation":["Fraction combines the ratios exactly and reduces the result to its simplest form. No floating-point intermediate is required.","The numerator and denominator remain integers, which makes exact equality and later symbolic ratio calculations straightforward."]},{"title":"Compare decimal-text and float construction","code":"from fractions import Fraction\nprint(Fraction(\"0.1\"))\nprint(Fraction(0.1) == Fraction(1, 10))\nprint(Fraction(\"0.1\") == Fraction(1, 10))","output":"1/10\nFalse\nTrue","explanation":["The string specifies the exact decimal tenth, while the float already contains a binary approximation. Fraction faithfully represents whichever value it receives.","Decide whether you need the exact stored float or the decimal value a user intended. Those are different conversion tasks."]},{"title":"Approximate a ratio with a bounded denominator","code":"from fractions import Fraction\nestimate = Fraction(\"3.14159\").limit_denominator(100)\nprint(estimate)\nprint(float(Fraction(1, 4)))","output":"311/99\n0.25","explanation":["limit_denominator chooses a nearby rational whose denominator is at most 100. This is a deliberate approximation of the original exact decimal fraction.","Converting a quarter to float is exact here because its denominator is a power of two. Not every rational converts to an exact binary float."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":128,"title":"How are bool, int, float, NaN, and numeric comparisons related?","label":"Numeric types & NaN","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nPython numeric types have compatible operations in many common cases, but they do not all represent the same values or semantics. bool is a subclass of int, so True and False participate numerically as 1 and 0. Floats include non-finite values such as infinity and NaN. NaN has unusual comparison rules, including not being equal to itself, so explicit predicates are needed when checking data quality.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse isinstance when accepting numeric families and exact type checks when excluding booleans is intentional. Handle non-finite values explicitly in computations that require finite measurements.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA check value == float(\"nan\") never identifies NaN. Also, int conversion truncates toward zero; it is not a general rounding operation.","summary":"Numeric types overlap, but their edge cases differ. Check for NaN and finiteness explicitly, and decide whether booleans count as integers in your API.","examples":[{"title":"See the bool/int relationship","code":"print(isinstance(True, int))\nprint(type(True) is int)\nprint(True + True)\nprint(sum([True, False, True]))","output":"True\nFalse\n2\n2","explanation":["bool inherits from int but is still its own exact type. Arithmetic treats True as 1 and False as 0.","Counting predicate results with sum can be useful. Validation that requires an ordinary integer may deliberately reject bool despite isinstance returning True."]},{"title":"Check NaN with the correct predicate","code":"import math\nvalue = float(\"nan\")\nprint(value == value)\nprint(math.isnan(value))\nprint(math.isfinite(value))\nprint(math.isfinite(float(\"inf\")))","output":"False\nTrue\nFalse\nFalse","explanation":["NaN represents a not-a-number result and compares unequal even to itself. math.isnan detects that category directly.","math.isfinite excludes both NaN and infinity. Use it when downstream calculations require ordinary finite measurements."]},{"title":"Separate truncation, rounding, and floor","code":"import math\nprint(int(-2.9))\nprint(math.floor(-2.9))\nprint(round(2.5), round(3.5))","output":"-2\n-3\n2 4","explanation":["int truncates toward zero, while floor selects the greatest integer not greater than the input. Negative values make the distinction visible.","The exact-half examples use round’s ties-to-even behavior. Choose explicit rounding policies when a domain requires a different treatment."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":129,"title":"Why can visually identical Unicode strings compare unequal?","label":"Unicode normalization & casefold","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nUnicode text can encode a visually similar character as one code point or as a base character plus combining marks. Python string equality compares code points rather than rendered appearance. Normalization provides named transformations such as NFC to produce a consistent representation for many equivalent sequences. casefold supports more comprehensive caseless matching than lower, but neither operation is a universal policy for every language or identifier.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nNormalize according to a defined application policy before comparisons, storage deduplication, or search. Retain original user-facing text where appropriate.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nLength in code points is not necessarily the number of user-perceived characters. Normalization and case folding do not solve every confusable-character or language-specific issue.","summary":"Text that looks the same can have different code points. Normalize and casefold only according to a deliberate comparison policy.","examples":[{"title":"Compare composed and decomposed text","code":"import unicodedata\na = \"\\u00e9\"\nb = \"e\\u0301\"\nprint(len(a), len(b))\nprint(a == b)\nprint(unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", a) == unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", b))","output":"1 2\nFalse\nTrue","explanation":["The two strings use different code-point sequences, so direct equality is false. NFC combines the decomposed sequence where a composed equivalent exists.","The normalized values compare equal. This example does not assume that every apparent visual match is canonically equivalent."]},{"title":"Use casefold for a caseless comparison","code":"a = \"Stra\\u00dfe\"\nb = \"STRASSE\"\nprint(a.lower() == b.lower())\nprint(a.casefold() == b.casefold())","output":"False\nTrue","explanation":["lower leaves the special German letter distinct from ss, while casefold applies a stronger caseless matching transformation.","Use the transformation for comparison keys rather than silently replacing the user’s original display text. Locale-specific requirements may need additional handling."]},{"title":"Inspect code points explicitly","code":"text = \"e\\u0301\"\nprint([f\"U+{ord(char):04X}\" for char in text])\nprint(len(text.encode(\"utf-8\")))","output":"['U+0065', 'U+0301']\n3","explanation":["The code-point list reveals the base e and combining accent separately. Their UTF-8 encoding occupies three bytes.","Code points, encoded bytes, and rendered graphemes are different units. Choose the unit that matches the operation instead of treating every length as a character count."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":130,"title":"How do regex search, match, fullmatch, groups, and raw strings differ?","label":"Regular expression matching modes","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nre.search looks for a match anywhere, re.match starts at the beginning, and re.fullmatch requires the whole string to satisfy the pattern. Groups extract structured portions of a match. Raw string literals reduce Python-level backslash escaping, while the regex engine still interprets regex escapes. A pattern matching a prefix is not automatically a full-input validator.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse fullmatch for simple whole-field format checks, search for finding embedded text, and captured groups when a match needs named components. Keep patterns understandable and bounded for untrusted input.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nAn unescaped dot means any suitable character, not a literal period. Raw strings do not turn regex metacharacters into literal text; re.escape handles literal fragments.","summary":"Choose where a regex may match, then extract the needed groups. Raw strings preserve backslashes for the regex parser rather than disabling regex syntax.","examples":[{"title":"Compare matching positions","code":"import re\ntext = \"item 123\"\nprint(bool(re.search(r\"\\d+\", text)))\nprint(bool(re.match(r\"\\d+\", text)))\nprint(bool(re.fullmatch(r\"\\d+\", \"123x\")))","output":"True\nFalse\nFalse","explanation":["search finds digits after the prefix. match rejects the same input because digits do not begin at position zero.","fullmatch rejects the trailing x even though the string starts with digits. Whole-field validation needs that complete coverage."]},{"title":"Extract named groups","code":"import re\nmatch = re.fullmatch(r\"(?P<name>[A-Za-z]+):(?P<score>\\d+)\", \"Ada:7\")\nprint(match.group(\"name\"))\nprint(int(match.group(\"score\")) + 1)","output":"Ada\n8","explanation":["Named groups identify the two meaningful fields of this simple format. The digit group is still text until converted to an integer.","Check for a None match in real input handling. Accessing group on a failed match is a separate error from a valid match with an unexpected value."]},{"title":"Escape a literal fragment","code":"import re\nfragment = \"a.b\"\npattern = re.escape(fragment)\nprint(bool(re.fullmatch(pattern, \"a.b\")))\nprint(bool(re.fullmatch(pattern, \"axb\")))","output":"True\nFalse","explanation":["re.escape protects the period so it matches itself. Without escaping, a dot would match the x in the second string too.","Escape data fragments, not an entire deliberately authored regex. Pattern syntax and literal input serve different roles."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":131,"title":"When should you use Counter and defaultdict instead of an ordinary dictionary?","label":"Counter & defaultdict","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nCounter counts hashable values and supplies zero for missing counts. defaultdict calls a configured factory when an absent key is accessed through indexing, then stores the new value. These tools express common accumulation patterns without repeated initialization branches. They remain dictionaries with additional behavior, so methods such as get do not necessarily trigger the same missing-key machinery as indexing.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse Counter for frequencies and defaultdict(list) or defaultdict(set) for grouping. A factory should create fresh mutable state for each new key.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\ndefaultdict(list) passes the factory itself, not list(). Also, reading a missing key through [] mutates the mapping by inserting a value, which may be surprising during inspection.","summary":"Counter counts things; defaultdict creates a starting value for new keys. Their convenience depends on understanding when missing-key access changes state.","examples":[{"title":"Count repeated values","code":"from collections import Counter\ncounts = Counter(\"banana\")\nprint(counts[\"a\"], counts[\"b\"], counts[\"x\"])\nprint(counts.most_common(1))","output":"3 1 0\n[('a', 3)]","explanation":["Counter accumulates occurrences and reports zero for an unseen key. most_common selects the highest-frequency item in this input.","Values must be suitable dictionary keys. Counter does not group mutable lists as keys without converting them into a hashable representation."]},{"title":"Group records without manual initialization","code":"from collections import defaultdict\ngroups = defaultdict(list)\nfor city, name in [(\"A\", \"Ada\"), (\"B\", \"Grace\"), (\"A\", \"Lin\")]:\n    groups[city].append(name)\nprint(dict(groups))","output":"{'A': ['Ada', 'Lin'], 'B': ['Grace']}","explanation":["The first indexed access for a city calls list to create its independent group. Later accesses reuse that city’s stored list.","Passing the callable list lets the mapping create separate containers. A single pre-created shared list would couple unrelated groups."]},{"title":"Distinguish get from indexed missing access","code":"from collections import defaultdict\ndata = defaultdict(list)\nprint(data.get(\"x\"))\nprint(\"x\" in data)\nprint(data[\"x\"])\nprint(\"x\" in data)","output":"None\nFalse\n[]\nTrue","explanation":["get does not invoke defaultdict’s factory, so the first read leaves the mapping unchanged. Indexed access creates and stores the default list.","This matters when diagnostics should not mutate data. Use the access form whose state changes match the intended operation."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":132,"title":"Why is collections.deque useful for queues and bounded history?","label":"Deque & bounded history","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ndeque supports efficient additions and removals at both ends. It is suitable for FIFO queues, stacks, and bounded history buffers. A maxlen limit discards items from the opposite end when appending to a full deque. It is not a replacement for a list when frequent middle indexing is the main operation, and a series of operations still needs an appropriate concurrency design.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse append plus popleft for a local FIFO queue, or a bounded deque for the latest observations. Use queue.Queue when blocking producer/consumer coordination is required.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\npopleft on an empty deque raises IndexError. Also, a bounded deque’s automatic eviction may discard important work if it is mistakenly used as a lossless task queue.","summary":"A deque is efficient at both ends. It makes a good local queue or rolling history, but bounds can intentionally discard old items.","examples":[{"title":"Process items in first-in, first-out order","code":"from collections import deque\nqueue = deque()\nqueue.append(\"first\")\nqueue.append(\"second\")\nprint(queue.popleft())\nprint(queue.popleft())","output":"first\nsecond","explanation":["Items enter on the right and leave on the left, preserving arrival order. This avoids repeatedly shifting the remainder of a list.","The example assumes work exists before removal. Handle empty state explicitly if producers and consumers do not run in a fixed sequence."]},{"title":"Keep only recent history","code":"from collections import deque\nhistory = deque(maxlen=3)\nfor value in [1, 2, 3, 4]:\n    history.append(value)\nprint(list(history))","output":"[2, 3, 4]","explanation":["Adding 4 to the full deque evicts 1 from the opposite end. The buffer therefore retains exactly the most recent three entries.","This is intentionally lossy storage. Use a different design for pending work that must never disappear without being processed."]},{"title":"Rotate and extend on either side","code":"from collections import deque\nvalues = deque([1, 2, 3])\nvalues.rotate(1)\nprint(list(values))\nvalues.appendleft(0)\nprint(list(values))","output":"[3, 1, 2]\n[0, 3, 1, 2]","explanation":["A positive rotation moves items from the right end to the left. appendleft then inserts a new leading item.","These operations are useful for cyclic scheduling and endpoint-oriented algorithms. They do not imply that arbitrary middle operations have list-like performance."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":133,"title":"What invariant does heapq maintain, and how do priority queues handle ties?","label":"Heaps & priority queues","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nheapq maintains a min-heap in a list: the smallest item is at index zero, and each parent is no greater than its children. The list is not globally sorted. heappop removes the minimum while restoring the invariant. Tuple entries allow a priority followed by tie-breaker fields. A monotonically increasing counter prevents accidental comparison of payload objects when priorities tie.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a heap when repeatedly selecting the next smallest priority without sorting the full set each time. It suits schedulers and top-k selection.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nReading the raw heap list as sorted output is incorrect. Equal priorities can trigger comparisons of later tuple fields, so ensure those fields have a defined ordering.","summary":"A heap quickly exposes the smallest priority, but its backing list is not a sorted list. Add an explicit tie-breaker for equal priorities.","examples":[{"title":"Extract values in increasing order","code":"import heapq\nheap = [5, 1, 3]\nheapq.heapify(heap)\nprint(heap[0])\nprint([heapq.heappop(heap) for _ in range(3)])","output":"1\n[1, 3, 5]","explanation":["heapify arranges the existing list into a valid heap. Repeated heappop calls produce increasing values even though the raw layout need not be fully sorted.","Each pop changes the heap. Do not expect to retain the original list or iterate it afterward as if nothing were consumed."]},{"title":"Use a counter to preserve order among tied priorities","code":"import heapq\nfrom itertools import count\nserial = count()\nheap = []\nfor name in [\"first\", \"second\"]:\n    heapq.heappush(heap, (1, next(serial), {\"name\": name}))\nprint(heapq.heappop(heap)[2][\"name\"])\nprint(heapq.heappop(heap)[2][\"name\"])","output":"first\nsecond","explanation":["The equal priority is followed by a unique increasing number, so tuple comparison never reaches the unorderable dictionaries.","The counter also gives first-in ordering for this tied priority. Other applications may deliberately choose a different tie-breaking policy."]},{"title":"Select a small number of extreme values","code":"import heapq\nvalues = [9, 2, 7, 1, 8]\nprint(heapq.nsmallest(2, values))\nprint(heapq.nlargest(2, values))","output":"[1, 2]\n[9, 8]","explanation":["These helpers return the requested extremes in sorted order without requiring the caller to manage a heap explicitly.","Choose based on workload: a full sort may be simpler or preferable when most of the collection is needed. Performance claims depend on input size and k."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":134,"title":"How do bisect_left, bisect_right, and insort work on sorted data?","label":"Binary insertion with bisect","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nbisect finds insertion positions in an already-sorted sequence using binary search. bisect_left chooses a position before equal values, while bisect_right chooses after them. Neither function by itself guarantees that the requested value exists. insort also inserts into a mutable list, but list shifting still makes that insertion linear in the worst case. The ordering assumptions must remain consistent.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse bisect for repeated boundary queries and moderate sorted lists. It is valuable for counts within numeric intervals and maintaining small ordered collections.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nCalling bisect on unsorted input produces a position with no useful sorted-search guarantee. Do not mistake logarithmic search for logarithmic list insertion.","summary":"Bisect locates boundaries in sorted data. Left goes before ties, right goes after ties, and insertion still has list-shifting costs.","examples":[{"title":"Locate both boundaries of equal values","code":"from bisect import bisect_left, bisect_right\nvalues = [1, 2, 2, 4]\nleft = bisect_left(values, 2)\nright = bisect_right(values, 2)\nprint(left, right)\nprint(right - left)","output":"1 3\n2","explanation":["The half-open interval from left to right contains all values equal to 2. Their difference gives the count of equal items.","This relies on the sequence already being sorted under the same comparison rules. No scan of all ties is needed to locate the boundaries."]},{"title":"Check membership after finding a position","code":"from bisect import bisect_left\nvalues = [1, 3, 5]\ndef contains(target):\n    index = bisect_left(values, target)\n    return index < len(values) and values[index] == target\nprint(contains(3))\nprint(contains(4))","output":"True\nFalse","explanation":["bisect_left returns where the target could be inserted, including positions for absent targets. The equality check establishes actual membership.","The bounds test prevents indexing beyond the end. Short-circuiting keeps the second check from running when the position is invalid."]},{"title":"Maintain sorted order on insertion","code":"from bisect import insort\nvalues = [1, 3, 5]\ninsort(values, 4)\ninsort(values, 3)\nprint(values)","output":"[1, 3, 3, 4, 5]","explanation":["insort locates a valid boundary and inserts the new value into the list. Existing values shift to make room.","For high-volume updates, choose a data structure suited to that workload rather than assuming binary position search eliminates insertion costs."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":135,"title":"How do Enum members improve the representation of fixed choices?","label":"Enums & fixed choices","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nEnum defines a named set of members representing a fixed domain, such as workflow states. Members have names and associated values, and ordinary Enum members remain distinct from their underlying values. Aliases can occur when values repeat unless uniqueness is enforced. auto can generate values, but the generated storage value should not automatically become an external protocol contract.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse an enum for meaningful closed choices and convert external inputs at a clear boundary. A recognizable member is more expressive than an unexplained numeric constant.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDo not assume Color.RED equals the string or integer stored in its value. Also, adding an enum does not automatically validate every assignment to an ordinary object attribute.","summary":"Enums name the allowed choices in a domain. Convert raw values into members explicitly, and treat their names and stored values as different things.","examples":[{"title":"Read a member’s name and value","code":"from enum import Enum\nclass Status(Enum):\n    NEW = \"new\"\n    DONE = \"done\"\nprint(Status.NEW.name)\nprint(Status.NEW.value)\nprint(Status.NEW == \"new\")","output":"NEW\nnew\nFalse","explanation":["The member has a symbolic name and a stored value, but remains an Enum member rather than an ordinary string.","That separation prevents accidental mixing of unrelated domains that happen to share the same raw value."]},{"title":"Validate a raw value at a boundary","code":"from enum import Enum\nclass Status(Enum):\n    NEW = \"new\"\n    DONE = \"done\"\nprint(Status(\"done\") is Status.DONE)\ntry:\n    Status(\"missing\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Unknown status\")","output":"True\nUnknown status","explanation":["Calling the enum class with a known value retrieves the corresponding member. An unknown value raises instead of silently creating a new choice.","Handle that conversion failure where outside data enters the application. An existing plain string variable does not validate itself merely because an enum exists."]},{"title":"Prevent accidental aliases","code":"from enum import Enum, unique\ntry:\n    @unique\n    class Code(Enum):\n        FIRST = 1\n        SECOND = 1\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Duplicate enum values\")","output":"Duplicate enum values","explanation":["Without an explicit uniqueness rule, equal values can create aliases. @unique rejects the duplicate during class construction.","Choose aliases deliberately when they are meaningful; use uniqueness enforcement when each named choice must have its own value."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":136,"title":"How do typing.Protocol and structural subtyping differ from explicit inheritance?","label":"Protocols & structural typing","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nA Protocol describes the attributes and methods a type must provide to satisfy a static interface. An ordinary class can satisfy it structurally without inheriting from the protocol. This brings a checkable contract to duck-typed APIs. @runtime_checkable enables limited isinstance checks for the presence of members, but does not validate method signatures, return types, or actual behavior.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse protocols to describe small capabilities consumed by a function, especially when accepting third-party objects that should not inherit your base class.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA runtime protocol match does not prove full correctness or compatible types. Static checking and behavior tests serve different purposes from a shallow runtime presence check.","summary":"A protocol describes required behavior without requiring shared inheritance. Runtime checks are limited; they do not replace type checking or tests.","examples":[{"title":"Accept an implementation without protocol inheritance","code":"from typing import Protocol\nclass Named(Protocol):\n    name: str\nclass User:\n    name = \"Ada\"\ndef greet(value: Named) -> str:\n    return \"Hi \" + value.name\nprint(greet(User()))","output":"Hi Ada","explanation":["User provides the attribute the protocol describes without explicitly inheriting Named. A static checker can recognize this structural match.","At runtime, greet simply accesses name. The annotation documents the intended contract but does not insert validation into the call."]},{"title":"Enable a limited runtime member check","code":"from typing import Protocol, runtime_checkable\n@runtime_checkable\nclass Sized(Protocol):\n    def __len__(self) -> int: ...\nprint(isinstance([1, 2], Sized))\nprint(isinstance(7, Sized))","output":"True\nFalse","explanation":["The list supplies the required special method while an integer does not. runtime_checkable permits this isinstance use.","The check concerns member availability, not a guarantee that every supported object will return a meaningful non-negative length under all conditions."]},{"title":"Do not mistake presence for a verified signature","code":"from typing import Protocol, runtime_checkable\n@runtime_checkable\nclass Runner(Protocol):\n    def run(self) -> str: ...\nclass Wrong:\n    def run(self, required):\n        return required\nvalue = Wrong()\nprint(isinstance(value, Runner))\ntry:\n    value.run()\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Signature is incompatible\")","output":"True\nSignature is incompatible","explanation":["The method exists, so the shallow runtime protocol check succeeds. Calling it with the promised zero-extra-argument interface still fails.","A static checker can catch the incompatible signature before execution. This is why runtime_checkable should not be described as complete interface validation."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":137,"title":"How do TypeVar and generics express relationships between types?","label":"Type variables & generics","category":"Functions","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nA type variable represents a type relationship across annotations, such as connecting a container’s element type with a function’s return type. A generic function can support many types while still preserving those relationships for a static checker. Bounds and constraints limit the intended possibilities. Runtime function calls still follow ordinary Python behavior unless validation is explicitly added.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse generics for reusable containers, identity-like transformations, and functions where a fixed annotation would discard useful type information. Keep contracts as narrow as practical.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA TypeVar is not a runtime cast, constructor, or automatic validator. Do not assume that annotations can prevent a caller from supplying an incompatible value during execution.","summary":"Generics let one implementation preserve useful type relationships for many inputs. They guide checking rather than enforcing types at runtime.","examples":[{"title":"Connect input and output element types","code":"from typing import TypeVar\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")\ndef first(values: list[T]) -> T:\n    return values[0]\nprint(first([1, 2]))\nprint(first([\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]))","output":"1\nAda","explanation":["A static checker can infer int for the first result and str for the second from the same generic function.","The implementation still assumes a nonempty list. Type relationships alone do not guarantee valid lengths or every other value-level condition."]},{"title":"Write a small generic container","code":"from typing import Generic, TypeVar\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")\nclass Box(Generic[T]):\n    def __init__(self, value: T):\n        self.value = value\nprint(Box[int](7).value)\nprint(Box[str](\"hello\").value)","output":"7\nhello","explanation":["The type parameter describes the relationship between the container and its stored value. It helps checking tools retain information at call sites.","Generic specialization does not create separate optimized storage implementations here. The normal class stores the supplied Python object."]},{"title":"Observe that a bound is a checking contract","code":"from typing import TypeVar\nT = TypeVar(\"T\", bound=int)\ndef echo(value: T) -> T:\n    return value\nprint(echo(3))\nprint(echo(\"runtime still accepts this\"))","output":"3\nruntime still accepts this","explanation":["A checker should reject the string call because it violates the bound. The runtime body simply returns its input and performs no type test.","This intentionally invalid typed call illustrates the distinction between static diagnostics and execution. Add an explicit check if rejection is a required runtime behavior."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":138,"title":"What does TypedDict describe, and does it validate dictionaries at runtime?","label":"TypedDict & data shapes","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nTypedDict describes the expected keys and value types of a dictionary for static type checking. Required and NotRequired distinguish key presence requirements. Instances remain ordinary dict objects at runtime, without automatic schema validation. This makes TypedDict useful for documenting structured records while retaining dictionary operations, but parsed external data still needs explicit checks.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse TypedDict for internal record-shaped dictionaries and API boundaries whose validated data can then be given a useful static shape.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nConstructing or annotating a TypedDict does not automatically reject missing keys or invalid value types during execution. It is not an isinstance schema validator.","summary":"TypedDict documents a dictionary’s expected shape for checking tools. Runtime data is still a dict and needs its own validation.","examples":[{"title":"Describe required and optional fields","code":"from typing import TypedDict, NotRequired\nclass User(TypedDict):\n    name: str\n    city: NotRequired[str]\nuser: User = {\"name\": \"Ada\"}\nprint(user[\"name\"])\nprint(user.get(\"city\", \"unknown\"))\nprint(type(user).__name__)","output":"Ada\nunknown\ndict","explanation":["name is required by the declared static shape, while city may be absent. Ordinary dictionary access rules still apply.","NotRequired requires Python 3.11 or a compatible typing backport. It describes presence, not a value that must be None."]},{"title":"See that runtime construction does not enforce fields","code":"from typing import TypedDict\nclass User(TypedDict):\n    name: str\nvalue = User()\nprint(value)\ntry:\n    print(value[\"name\"])\nexcept KeyError:\n    print(\"Required field was not validated\")","output":"{}\nRequired field was not validated","explanation":["The runtime constructor creates a normal empty dictionary. Accessing a missing field raises the normal KeyError later.","A static checker can flag the incomplete construction. External data should be validated before callers rely on required-field annotations."]},{"title":"Validate a record before consuming it","code":"from typing import TypedDict\nclass User(TypedDict):\n    name: str\ndef parse(data) -> User:\n    if not isinstance(data, dict) or not isinstance(data.get(\"name\"), str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a name string\")\n    return {\"name\": data[\"name\"]}\nprint(parse({\"name\": \"Ada\"}))\ntry:\n    parse({\"name\": 7})\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)","output":"{'name': 'Ada'}\nExpected a name string","explanation":["The explicit conditions enforce the value-level requirement, and the function creates a record with the validated shape.","The return annotation documents the result for later checking. It complements the validation rather than replacing the code that performs it."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":139,"title":"How should loggers, handlers, levels, and formatting work together?","label":"Structured application logging","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nA logger creates records at levels such as DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, and ERROR. Handlers route eligible records to destinations, and formatters control their presentation. The effective logger level and each handler’s level can both filter output. Module-named loggers let applications configure reporting centrally. Passing format arguments separately delays message interpolation until logging needs the message.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse logging for operational diagnostics with useful context. Let the application configure destinations instead of every library repeatedly configuring global logging.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nAdding handlers repeatedly can duplicate output. Avoid logging secrets, and do not treat a log message as a substitute for actually handling or propagating an error.","summary":"Loggers produce records, handlers send them somewhere, and formatters display them. Levels decide which records are kept.","examples":[{"title":"Filter and format log records deterministically","code":"import io\nimport logging\nstream = io.StringIO()\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"lesson\")\nlogger.setLevel(logging.INFO)\nlogger.propagate = False\nhandler = logging.StreamHandler(stream)\nhandler.setFormatter(logging.Formatter(\"%(levelname)s:%(message)s\"))\nlogger.addHandler(handler)\nlogger.debug(\"hidden\")\nlogger.info(\"hello %s\", \"Ada\")\nprint(stream.getvalue().strip())","output":"INFO:hello Ada","explanation":["The INFO threshold drops the DEBUG record. The formatter emits a stable level-and-message string into an in-memory stream.","Passing Ada as a separate logging argument avoids eager string interpolation when a record is filtered. The example uses an isolated named logger."]},{"title":"Apply a stricter handler threshold","code":"import io\nimport logging\nstream = io.StringIO()\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"threshold\")\nlogger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\nlogger.propagate = False\nhandler = logging.StreamHandler(stream)\nhandler.setLevel(logging.WARNING)\nlogger.addHandler(handler)\nlogger.info(\"not routed\")\nlogger.warning(\"attention\")\nprint(stream.getvalue().strip())","output":"attention","explanation":["The logger permits both records, but the handler accepts only WARNING and above. Filtering can therefore occur at more than one layer.","When a message appears to be missing, inspect both logger configuration and handler configuration rather than checking a single level setting."]},{"title":"Add contextual fields to a record","code":"import io\nimport logging\nstream = io.StringIO()\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"context\")\nlogger.setLevel(logging.INFO)\nlogger.propagate = False\nhandler = logging.StreamHandler(stream)\nhandler.setFormatter(logging.Formatter(\"%(request_id)s:%(message)s\"))\nlogger.addHandler(handler)\nlogger.info(\"complete\", extra={\"request_id\": \"R7\"})\nprint(stream.getvalue().strip())","output":"R7:complete","explanation":["extra adds contextual record fields for a formatter or downstream structured logging handler. The request identifier makes the event easier to correlate.","If the formatter requires a field, ensure every routed record supplies it or provide defaults. Contextual fields should not include confidential values accidentally."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":140,"title":"How do mocks, specs, and call assertions help isolate a unit test?","label":"Mocks & interaction testing","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nunittest.mock supplies replaceable callables and objects that record interactions. return_value defines a simple response, side_effect can raise or compute responses, and call assertions inspect how the dependency was used. A spec or autospec limits accidental access to unsupported interfaces. Mocking is useful at a chosen dependency boundary, but tests should still exercise meaningful behavior rather than merely repeat the implementation.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse mocks for controlled failures, expensive boundaries, or external effects that should not occur in a unit test. Prefer simple real values or fakes when they communicate behavior more clearly.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nPatching the wrong lookup location leaves the code using its original dependency. Overly permissive mocks can also hide misspelled methods and unrealistic interactions.","summary":"Mocks replace a dependency and record its use. Give them a realistic interface and assert behavior that matters to the caller.","examples":[{"title":"Check a dependency call and its result","code":"from unittest.mock import Mock\nloader = Mock(return_value={\"name\": \"Ada\"})\ndef greeting(load):\n    return \"Hi \" + load(7)[\"name\"]\nprint(greeting(loader))\nloader.assert_called_once_with(7)\nprint(\"call verified\")","output":"Hi Ada\ncall verified","explanation":["The fake loader returns a known record, making the greeting deterministic. The assertion verifies the important request identifier passed across the boundary.","The test checks a visible result as well as the interaction. It avoids requiring a real database or network request."]},{"title":"Model a failing dependency","code":"from unittest.mock import Mock\nload = Mock(side_effect=TimeoutError(\"slow\"))\ntry:\n    load()\nexcept TimeoutError:\n    print(\"timeout handled\")\nprint(load.call_count)","output":"timeout handled\n1","explanation":["side_effect raises when the mock is called, allowing a repeatable failure path without sleeping or relying on an external system.","The attempted call is still recorded. A mock failure scenario is useful only if the surrounding code’s handling is also asserted appropriately."]},{"title":"Catch an unsupported mocked attribute","code":"from unittest.mock import Mock\nclass Service:\n    def fetch(self):\n        return \"real\"\nfake = Mock(spec_set=Service)\nfake.fetch.return_value = \"fake\"\nprint(fake.fetch())\ntry:\n    fake.fecth()\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"Unknown method rejected\")","output":"fake\nUnknown method rejected","explanation":["spec_set restricts the mock to attributes on the specified interface, so a misspelled method is not silently fabricated.","This is more realistic than an unrestricted mock, though it does not replace integration tests proving that the real service contract actually works."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":141,"title":"Why use if __name__ == \"__main__\", and what happens on import?","label":"Script entry points & imports","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nA module executed as the top-level script normally has __name__ equal to __main__. An imported module uses its module name instead. Top-level statements run during module initialization, so separating definitions from a guarded main function prevents ordinary imports from starting command-line work. This is particularly important when spawned worker processes import the main module.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a main function plus the guard for scripts that should also expose reusable functions. Keep import-time side effects small and deliberate.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nThe guard does not stop all top-level code from running on import; only its guarded body is skipped. Code placed above or outside it still executes during initialization.","summary":"The main guard runs script-only work while allowing functions to be imported without automatically starting that work.","examples":[{"title":"Check the name in a saved script","code":"def main():\n    print(\"script work\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()","output":"script work","explanation":["Running this saved file directly gives it the top-level script name and executes main. The function definition itself does not call the function.","When importing the same file as a module, the guard would be false and main would remain available for explicit use."]},{"title":"Model imported and direct execution names","code":"code = 'print(\"top level\");\\nif __name__ == \"__main__\": print(\"guarded\")'\nprint(\"import model\")\nexec(code, {\"__name__\": \"example_module\"})\nprint(\"script model\")\nexec(code, {\"__name__\": \"__main__\"})","output":"import model\ntop level\nscript model\ntop level\nguarded","explanation":["This fixed, trusted exec input models two module namespaces; it is not loading external text. The top-level print executes in both namespaces.","Only the guarded print depends on the special name. The example makes clear that the guard is a conditional statement, not an automatic import sandbox."]},{"title":"Keep reusable computation separate from presentation","code":"def square(value):\n    return value * value\ndef main():\n    print(square(7))\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()","output":"49","explanation":["The computational function returns a result without printing. The script entry function decides how to present it.","This separation makes importing and testing square straightforward. It also keeps command-line behavior from being mixed into the function’s core result."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":142,"title":"How do packages and explicit relative imports organize modules?","label":"Packages & relative imports","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nA package organizes a module hierarchy and normally provides a package path for locating submodules. A regular package uses __init__.py, while namespace packages can span locations without that same file requirement. Explicit relative imports use a known package context, such as from .helpers import parse. They are not ordinary filesystem-relative lookups based only on the current working directory.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse package structure to express dependency boundaries and run a package entry point with python -m when package-relative imports are needed.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nRunning an inner file directly can remove the package context required for relative imports. Changing working directory is not the same as defining a correct package hierarchy.","summary":"Packages give modules a hierarchy. Relative imports refer to that hierarchy, so they need package context rather than just a matching directory.","examples":[{"title":"Inspect a standard-library module’s package context","code":"import xml.etree.ElementTree as element_tree\nprint(element_tree.__package__)\nprint(element_tree.Element(\"root\").tag)","output":"xml.etree\nroot","explanation":["ElementTree is a module inside the xml.etree package hierarchy. Its __package__ gives the context used for package-relative resolution.","The example imports a real standard-library module, avoiding a simulated claim about a nonexistent multi-file project."]},{"title":"Resolve a relative module with an explicit package","code":"from importlib import import_module\nmodule = import_module(\".ElementTree\", package=\"xml.etree\")\nprint(module.Element(\"item\").tag)\nprint(module.__name__)","output":"item\nxml.etree.ElementTree","explanation":["The leading dot means relative resolution, and package supplies the required base. The result is the same fully qualified module.","Use this mechanism only when dynamic importing is actually needed. Ordinary explicit import statements are clearer for fixed dependencies."]},{"title":"Distinguish a package from an ordinary module","code":"import math\nimport xml\nprint(hasattr(xml, \"__path__\"))\nprint(hasattr(math, \"__path__\"))\nfrom importlib import import_module\ntry:\n    import_module(\".helpers\")\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Relative import needs package context\")","output":"True\nFalse\nRelative import needs package context","explanation":["xml is a package with a package path; math is an ordinary extension module. Both can be imported, but they have different hierarchy roles.","The relative import request without a package is rejected. A leading dot alone does not tell the import machinery which package should contain helpers."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":143,"title":"How do __getattribute__ and __getattr__ differ during attribute lookup?","label":"Attribute lookup hooks","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\n__getattribute__ participates in ordinary attribute access and must usually delegate to the base implementation to preserve descriptors and normal lookup rules. __getattr__ is a fallback used after normal lookup fails with AttributeError. This makes __getattr__ suitable for selected virtual attributes, while __getattribute__ is a broader and more delicate interception point. These hooks do not universally intercept every implicit special-method lookup.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse __getattr__ for narrow fallback behavior or lazy wrappers. Override __getattribute__ only when broad interception is a deliberate part of the object’s design.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nReading self.name from inside an unrestricted __getattribute__ override can recurse indefinitely. Use object.__getattribute__ or carefully designed delegation for internal access.","summary":"__getattribute__ handles normal lookup; __getattr__ is the missing-attribute fallback. Delegate carefully to avoid recursion and preserve normal behavior.","examples":[{"title":"Add a missing-attribute fallback","code":"class Settings:\n    theme = \"dark\"\n    def __getattr__(self, name):\n        if name == \"color\":\n            return \"blue\"\n        raise AttributeError(name)\nsettings = Settings()\nprint(settings.theme)\nprint(settings.color)\nprint(hasattr(settings, \"missing\"))","output":"dark\nblue\nFalse","explanation":["theme is found normally, so the fallback is unnecessary. color is missing and is supplied by __getattr__.","Raising AttributeError for unsupported names preserves hasattr and ordinary missing-attribute behavior. Returning a default for every typo can hide mistakes."]},{"title":"Intercept one normal attribute safely","code":"class User:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.name = \"Ada\"\n    def __getattribute__(self, name):\n        value = object.__getattribute__(self, name)\n        return value.upper() if name == \"name\" else value\nprint(User().name)","output":"ADA","explanation":["The override delegates to object.__getattribute__ to obtain the real value, then transforms only the intended attribute.","Using self.name while handling the same lookup would recurse. Narrow interception is easier to reason about than changing every attribute access."]},{"title":"Observe the fallback after normal failure","code":"class Example:\n    def __getattribute__(self, name):\n        print(\"lookup\", name)\n        return object.__getattribute__(self, name)\n    def __getattr__(self, name):\n        return \"fallback\"\nprint(Example().unknown)","output":"lookup unknown\nfallback","explanation":["The normal lookup hook runs first and delegates, which raises AttributeError for unknown. Python then invokes __getattr__ as the fallback.","The two hooks are not competing aliases. Their order and failure signaling determine whether fallback behavior is reached."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":144,"title":"What does double-underscore name mangling do, and is it privacy?","label":"Name mangling & conventions","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nAn attribute with a leading double underscore and no special-method-style trailing double underscore is transformed inside a class definition to include the class name. This reduces accidental collisions when subclasses use the same internal spelling. It is not encryption or an access-control boundary. A single leading underscore instead communicates a non-public convention without the same transformation.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse mangling sparingly when a base class needs an internal attribute unlikely to collide with subclass state. Prefer clear public contracts and ordinary internal naming for most classes.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDo not confuse __value with __len__. Special protocol names ending in double underscores are not ordinary private fields, and mangled names can still be accessed explicitly.","summary":"Name mangling prevents accidental subclass name collisions. It is a naming transformation, not a security or privacy guarantee.","examples":[{"title":"Inspect the transformed storage name","code":"class Secret:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__value = 7\n    def reveal(self):\n        return self.__value\nitem = Secret()\nprint(item.reveal())\nprint(sorted(vars(item)))","output":"7\n['_Secret__value']","explanation":["The class body’s __value references are transformed to a class-qualified name, which is visible in the instance dictionary.","The method reads the same transformed name. This is why a plain item.__value lookup would not find that stored field."]},{"title":"Avoid a base/subclass field collision","code":"class Base:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__value = \"base\"\n    def base_value(self):\n        return self.__value\nclass Child(Base):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.__value = \"child\"\nitem = Child()\nprint(item.base_value())\nprint(sorted(vars(item)))","output":"base\n['_Base__value', '_Child__value']","explanation":["The identical source spelling is mangled using the class where it appears. Base and Child therefore store different attributes.","The base method still reads its own base field, which prevents an accidental override by the subclass’s similarly named implementation detail."]},{"title":"See that mangling is not access control","code":"class Example:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__value = 7\nitem = Example()\nprint(item._Example__value)\nitem._Example__value = 9\nprint(item._Example__value)","output":"7\n9","explanation":["A caller who knows the transformed name can still read and modify the attribute. Python does not enforce secrecy through this convention.","Respect non-public APIs even when technically accessible. Confidential data needs an actual security design rather than a naming prefix."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":145,"title":"How do class attributes, instance attributes, and shared mutable defaults differ?","label":"Class vs instance state","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nClass attributes are shared through the class namespace, while ordinary instance attributes are stored per instance. Reading an absent instance name can fall back to the class, but assigning the name on an instance usually creates an instance binding instead of changing the class. Mutating a shared class-level list is different: every instance still referring to that list sees the mutation.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse class attributes for genuinely shared constants or configuration, and initialize per-object mutable data inside __init__. Document deliberate shared registries explicitly.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nPutting items = [] on a class does not give each instance a fresh list. Also, item.count += 1 can create an instance shadow rather than update a common class counter.","summary":"Instance state belongs to one object; class state is shared. Create mutable per-instance containers during initialization.","examples":[{"title":"Observe accidental shared mutable state","code":"class Basket:\n    items = []\na, b = Basket(), Basket()\na.items.append(\"apple\")\nprint(b.items)\nprint(a.items is b.items)","output":"['apple']\nTrue","explanation":["Neither instance owns a separate items attribute, so both look up the same class-level list. append mutates that shared object.","This is an ownership mistake when baskets should be independent. The class declaration creates the list once, not once per instance."]},{"title":"Give each instance an independent container","code":"class Basket:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\na, b = Basket(), Basket()\na.items.append(\"apple\")\nprint(a.items)\nprint(b.items)","output":"['apple']\n[]","explanation":["Each constructor call creates and stores a fresh list on its receiver. The two baskets therefore maintain separate state.","This is the ordinary pattern for mutable per-object fields. A class-level constant can still be appropriate for immutable shared defaults."]},{"title":"Distinguish shadowing from a class update","code":"class Counter:\n    count = 0\na, b = Counter(), Counter()\na.count += 1\nprint(a.count, b.count, Counter.count)\nCounter.count = 5\nprint(a.count, b.count, Counter.count)","output":"1 0 0\n1 5 5","explanation":["The augmented assignment reads a’s inherited zero, computes a new integer, and writes an instance attribute. It does not update Counter.count.","Later changing the class affects b, which still falls back to the class, but a keeps its own shadowing value."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":146,"title":"How do enumerate and zip support parallel iteration, and what does strict=True add?","label":"Enumerate & strict zip","category":"Iteration","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nenumerate pairs each item with an incrementing index, optionally starting from a chosen number. zip pairs items from several iterables and is lazy; by default it stops at the shortest input. strict=True, introduced in Python 3.10, raises when input lengths differ rather than silently truncating. These tools help express relationships between data streams without manual index bookkeeping.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse enumerate when both a position and value are needed. Use strict zip when equal-length columns are part of the data contract rather than a convenient coincidence.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA zip iterator is consumed once, and mismatched lengths are detected during consumption, not necessarily when zip is constructed. Truncation can silently lose unmatched records.","summary":"Enumerate adds positions; zip pairs streams. Use strict zip when unequal lengths should be an error.","examples":[{"title":"Number records from a chosen start","code":"for number, name in enumerate([\"Ada\", \"Grace\"], start=1):\n    print(number, name)","output":"1 Ada\n2 Grace","explanation":["enumerate maintains the index and yields it alongside each original item. start changes the displayed numbering without changing the source collection.","This avoids manually incrementing a counter that could become inconsistent after a continue statement or later loop edit."]},{"title":"Compare truncating and strict pairing","code":"print(list(zip([1, 2], [\"A\"])))\ntry:\n    list(zip([1, 2], [\"A\"], strict=True))\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Lengths differ\")","output":"[(1, 'A')]\nLengths differ","explanation":["Ordinary zip stops when the shorter sequence ends, leaving 2 unmatched. strict=True treats that mismatch as a failure.","The list call consumes the iterator and exposes the error. Creating a lazy zip alone does not force all inputs to be checked immediately."]},{"title":"Transpose rectangular rows","code":"rows = [(1, \"A\"), (2, \"B\")]\ncolumns = list(zip(*rows, strict=True))\nprint(columns)","output":"[(1, 2), ('A', 'B')]","explanation":["The star expands the rows into separate zip arguments. Items at the same column position become tuples of column values.","Strict mode verifies that the row iterables have matching lengths during consumption. Handle the empty-input case separately if your application requires a known column shape."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":147,"title":"How does functools.partial differ from a closure or a lambda?","label":"Partial function application","category":"Functions","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\npartial creates a callable with some positional or keyword arguments pre-filled for an existing callable. It reuses the original function rather than defining a new function body. Remaining arguments are supplied later, and later keyword arguments can override pre-filled keyword values. A closure or lambda is more flexible when actual computation or control flow must change, rather than only argument binding.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse partial to configure callbacks or specialize a reusable operation with a clear named dependency. It can be easier to inspect than a one-off anonymous forwarding lambda.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nBinding an object does not copy it: mutable bound arguments remain shared. Also, binding a positional argument can make a later duplicate keyword invalid.","summary":"partial saves some arguments for a later function call. It configures an existing operation rather than rewriting its behavior.","examples":[{"title":"Pre-fill one argument","code":"from functools import partial\ndef multiply(factor, value):\n    return factor * value\ndouble = partial(multiply, 2)\nprint(double(7))\nprint(double.func is multiply)\nprint(double.args)","output":"14\nTrue\n(2,)","explanation":["double stores multiply and the initial factor argument. Calling it appends the later value and invokes the original function.","The func and args attributes make that configuration inspectable. The partial object is a callable, not a newly defined function with a custom body."]},{"title":"Override a pre-filled keyword","code":"from functools import partial\ndef greet(name, punctuation=\"!\"):\n    return \"Hi \" + name + punctuation\nfriendly = partial(greet, punctuation=\"!\")\nprint(friendly(\"Ada\"))\nprint(friendly(\"Ada\", punctuation=\"?\"))","output":"Hi Ada!\nHi Ada?","explanation":["The stored keyword supplies a default for the partial call. An explicitly supplied later keyword replaces the stored value.","This differs from supplying a positional value and then assigning the same parameter by keyword. Ordinary duplicate positional/keyword binding rules still apply."]},{"title":"Observe shared mutable bound state","code":"from functools import partial\ndef append_to(items, value):\n    items.append(value)\n    return items\nitems = []\nadd = partial(append_to, items)\nprint(add(1))\nprint(add(2))\nprint(items)","output":"[1]\n[1, 2]\n[1, 2]","explanation":["The partial retains the same list object rather than a copy. Each call therefore changes the shared bound list.","This may be intentional for a collector callback, but it is not an isolation mechanism. Create or copy state explicitly when independent calls are required."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":148,"title":"What limits recursion in Python, and when is an iterative approach better?","label":"Recursion & iterative alternatives","category":"Functions","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nRecursive functions solve a problem by invoking the same operation on a smaller subproblem, requiring a base case and progress toward it. Python maintains call frames and has a recursion limit intended to prevent uncontrolled depth. Ordinary Python does not promise tail-call elimination. An iterative loop or explicit stack avoids relying on deep call nesting and can make resource usage easier to control.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse recursion for naturally recursive structures with manageable depth. Use iteration or an explicit stack for deep inputs or algorithms where the call stack would become a limitation.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nIncreasing the recursion limit does not make unlimited recursion safe. Missing a base case or failing to reduce the problem still causes unbounded work or a RecursionError.","summary":"Recursion needs a stopping case and a smaller next problem. For deep inputs, use a loop or an explicit stack instead of hoping calls are optimized away.","examples":[{"title":"Use a clear base case","code":"def factorial(n):\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Use a nonnegative integer\")\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)\nprint(factorial(0))\nprint(factorial(5))","output":"1\n120","explanation":["Zero is the stopping case, and each recursive call reduces a nonnegative input by one. The return values combine while the calls unwind.","The example is for small integer inputs. A complete public API would also define and validate accepted types and practical resource limits."]},{"title":"Compute the same result iteratively","code":"def factorial(n):\n    result = 1\n    for value in range(2, n + 1):\n        result *= value\n    return result\nprint(factorial(5))\nprint(factorial(0))","output":"120\n1","explanation":["The loop stores the accumulating result in one local variable instead of building a chain of Python calls. Empty iteration naturally preserves the zero-factorial result.","This implementation illustrates the iterative structure. Add the same input validation contract used by the recursive version when exposing it to callers."]},{"title":"Traverse nested data with an explicit stack","code":"values = [1, [2, [3]], 4]\nstack = list(reversed(values))\nresult = []\nwhile stack:\n    value = stack.pop()\n    if isinstance(value, list):\n        stack.extend(reversed(value))\n    else:\n        result.append(value)\nprint(result)","output":"[1, 2, 3, 4]","explanation":["The explicit stack holds pending traversal work. Reversing before pushing preserves left-to-right output despite last-in-first-out removal.","This avoids one Python call frame per nesting level. Cyclic structures still require a separate detection policy to prevent infinite traversal."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":149,"title":"What problem do contextvars solve in asynchronous applications?","label":"Context-local state with contextvars","category":"Concurrency","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nContextVar stores a value associated with the current execution context rather than one global mutable binding. Asyncio tasks normally receive a copy of the current context when created, allowing request metadata to vary independently between tasks. set returns a token that can restore the previous binding with reset. Context isolation does not deep-copy arbitrary mutable values stored inside a variable.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse context variables for request identifiers, tracing metadata, or other scoped information that would be awkward to pass through every helper. Keep ownership and reset boundaries clear.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nStoring a shared mutable dictionary in a context variable does not magically isolate mutations. Context-local bindings and deep object isolation are different guarantees.","summary":"A ContextVar gives each execution context its own binding. Set it for a scope and reset it afterward; do not assume mutable contents are copied.","examples":[{"title":"Restore an earlier binding with a token","code":"from contextvars import ContextVar\nrequest = ContextVar(\"request\", default=\"none\")\nprint(request.get())\ntoken = request.set(\"R7\")\nprint(request.get())\nrequest.reset(token)\nprint(request.get())","output":"none\nR7\nnone","explanation":["set changes the current context’s binding and returns the information needed to restore its previous state. reset returns the example to its default lookup.","Use try/finally around temporary changes in real code so a failure does not leave a later operation with the wrong contextual value."]},{"title":"Keep task bindings independent","code":"import asyncio\nfrom contextvars import ContextVar\nname = ContextVar(\"name\", default=\"parent\")\nasync def worker(value):\n    name.set(value)\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return name.get()\nasync def main():\n    print(await asyncio.gather(worker(\"A\"), worker(\"B\")))\n    print(name.get())\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"['A', 'B']\nparent","explanation":["Each task updates its own contextual binding and retains it across suspension. One task does not overwrite the other task’s string binding.","The parent context remains unchanged. This is useful for per-request identifiers in concurrent asynchronous handlers."]},{"title":"Run an operation in a copied context","code":"from contextvars import ContextVar, copy_context\nvalue = ContextVar(\"value\", default=0)\ncontext = copy_context()\ncontext.run(value.set, 7)\nprint(context.run(value.get))\nprint(value.get())","output":"7\n0","explanation":["The copied context can be entered to set and read its own binding. The outer current context keeps the default.","The context copy contains bindings, not a recursive clone of every stored object. Mutable shared values still need deliberate ownership."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":150,"title":"How does asyncio.TaskGroup provide structured concurrency?","label":"Structured concurrency with TaskGroup","category":"Concurrency","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nTaskGroup, available in Python 3.11+, owns tasks created inside its asynchronous context. Exiting waits for those tasks. If one task fails with an ordinary exception, the group cancels remaining tasks and reports failures through an exception group after cleanup. This ties task lifetime to a lexical scope instead of leaving background work unowned. Cancellation remains cooperative, so tasks must not block the event loop indefinitely.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse TaskGroup for related concurrent operations that should complete as one unit. Keep unrelated long-lived services under an explicit lifecycle owner instead.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDo not discard cleanup by swallowing CancelledError indiscriminately. TaskGroup is a lifecycle mechanism, not automatic isolation from blocking code or arbitrary shared-state races.","summary":"TaskGroup owns a set of related tasks, waits for them, and coordinates cancellation when one fails.","examples":[{"title":"Read results after all owned tasks finish","code":"import asyncio\nasync def square(value):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return value * value\nasync def main():\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n        tasks = [group.create_task(square(n)) for n in [2, 3]]\n    print([task.result() for task in tasks])\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"[4, 9]","explanation":["Leaving the async with block waits until the group’s tasks finish. Their results can then be retrieved without pending-work ambiguity.","The list follows the stored task order, not a claim about completion order. The scope gives each task a clear owner."]},{"title":"Handle the grouped failure","code":"import asyncio\nasync def fail():\n    raise ValueError(\"bad task\")\nasync def main():\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n            group.create_task(fail())\n    except* ValueError as errors:\n        print(\"failures\", len(errors.exceptions))\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"failures 1","explanation":["The failing child is reported as part of an exception group when the TaskGroup exits. except* selects the ValueError subgroup.","A single failing child can still be wrapped in a group because the abstraction supports multiple independent failures."]},{"title":"Clean up a sibling that gets cancelled","code":"import asyncio\nevents = []\nasync def wait():\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(60)\n    finally:\n        events.append(\"cleanup\")\nasync def fail():\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    raise ValueError(\"bad\")\nasync def main():\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n            group.create_task(wait())\n            group.create_task(fail())\n    except* ValueError:\n        events.append(\"failure\")\n    print(sorted(events))\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"['cleanup', 'failure']","explanation":["The waiting task enters its try before the other task fails. Group cancellation unwinds the pending sleep and runs finally without waiting sixty seconds.","The parent observes failure only after group cleanup. Sorting the event names avoids making the final display depend on incidental logging order."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":151,"title":"How do async iterators and async generators differ from ordinary iterators?","label":"Async iterators & generators","category":"Iteration","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nAn asynchronous iterator provides __aiter__ and an asynchronous __anext__ operation that can suspend before returning the next item. async for awaits those items and stops on StopAsyncIteration. An async generator uses async def together with yield to implement this pattern concisely. It cannot be consumed by ordinary list or next in the same way as a synchronous iterator.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse async iteration for data arriving through asynchronous I/O or pipelines where requesting the next item may require waiting. Use explicit closing when early exit must trigger resource cleanup promptly.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nAn async generator is not a coroutine returning a ready list. async for and async comprehensions require an asynchronous context, and abandoned generators still need a clear cleanup policy.","summary":"Async iteration lets getting the next item involve await. Use async for to consume it and close resource-owning generators explicitly when stopping early.","examples":[{"title":"Yield values from an asynchronous generator","code":"import asyncio\nasync def values():\n    for n in range(3):\n        await asyncio.sleep(0)\n        yield n\nasync def main():\n    async for value in values():\n        print(value)\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"0\n1\n2","explanation":["The async generator can suspend before yielding each item. async for handles the awaitable next-item protocol automatically.","sleep(0) is a controlled suspension point rather than a real I/O request. The example demonstrates consumption without external dependencies."]},{"title":"Build a filtered async comprehension","code":"import asyncio\nasync def values():\n    for n in range(4):\n        yield n\nasync def main():\n    print([n * n async for n in values() if n % 2 == 0])\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"[0, 4]","explanation":["The comprehension consumes an asynchronous iterator and materializes the matching transformed items into a normal list.","The list result is eager even though the source is asynchronous. Bound the stream before collecting it if the source could be unbounded."]},{"title":"Close an async generator when breaking early","code":"import asyncio\nfrom contextlib import aclosing\nasync def values():\n    try:\n        yield 1\n        yield 2\n    finally:\n        print(\"cleanup\")\nasync def main():\n    async with aclosing(values()) as stream:\n        async for value in stream:\n            print(value)\n            break\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"1\ncleanup","explanation":["aclosing awaits the generator’s close operation when leaving the context. The finally block runs even though the consumer stops after one item.","This gives cleanup a deliberate boundary rather than relying on later garbage collection or event-loop shutdown."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":152,"title":"How does queue.Queue coordinate producer and consumer threads?","label":"Thread-safe work queues","category":"Concurrency","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nqueue.Queue provides synchronized operations for passing work between threads. get can wait for an item, put can wait for capacity, and each task_done marks one retrieved item complete for join’s accounting. A sentinel value can request worker termination. Queue safety does not automatically make processing side effects safe or define how every worker failure should be handled.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a queue to separate producers from worker threads and introduce bounded buffering where needed. Give worker shutdown and error reporting explicit designs.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nQueue.join waits for unfinished-task accounting, not directly for thread termination. Missing task_done calls can block it indefinitely; an empty queue does not necessarily mean all work is complete.","summary":"Queue passes work safely between threads. Pair each retrieved task with task_done, and separately manage worker shutdown and joining.","examples":[{"title":"Run a worker with explicit shutdown","code":"from queue import Queue\nfrom threading import Thread\nqueue = Queue()\nresults = []\ndef worker():\n    while True:\n        value = queue.get()\n        try:\n            if value is None:\n                return\n            results.append(value * value)\n        finally:\n            queue.task_done()\nthread = Thread(target=worker)\nthread.start()\nfor value in [2, 3, None]:\n    queue.put(value)\nqueue.join()\nthread.join()\nprint(results)","output":"[4, 9]","explanation":["One worker processes FIFO inputs and exits on the sentinel. finally balances each get with task_done, including the shutdown item.","Queue.join waits for the accounting to reach zero, while thread.join confirms the worker has actually exited. They solve different lifecycle concerns."]},{"title":"Handle nonblocking empty and full states","code":"from queue import Queue, Empty, Full\nqueue = Queue(maxsize=1)\nqueue.put_nowait(\"A\")\ntry:\n    queue.put_nowait(\"B\")\nexcept Full:\n    print(\"full\")\nprint(queue.get_nowait())\nqueue.task_done()\ntry:\n    queue.get_nowait()\nexcept Empty:\n    print(\"empty\")","output":"full\nA\nempty","explanation":["The bounded queue rejects an immediate second item instead of blocking. After retrieving the existing item, another immediate get reports Empty.","Use these explicit exceptions instead of assuming a preliminary empty or full check remains valid while other threads act."]},{"title":"Choose a priority-based work queue","code":"from queue import PriorityQueue\nqueue = PriorityQueue()\nqueue.put((2, \"later\"))\nqueue.put((1, \"first\"))\nwhile not queue.empty():\n    print(queue.get()[1])\n    queue.task_done()\nqueue.join()","output":"first\nlater","explanation":["PriorityQueue selects smaller tuple priorities before larger ones. The loop is safe as written because this example has no concurrent producers.","In a live multi-threaded system, avoid relying on empty as a stable promise. Use blocking gets, sentinels, or another explicit coordination policy."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":153,"title":"How does threading.Event coordinate signals without a busy-wait loop?","label":"Thread signaling with Event","category":"Concurrency","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nEvent maintains a shared set/clear flag. wait blocks until the flag is set or an optional timeout expires; set wakes waiting threads, and clear resets the signal. It communicates a condition such as readiness or shutdown rather than carrying a stream of distinct messages. Several rapid set calls do not accumulate a count of events.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse Event for one shared state transition or a simple stop request. Use Queue when every individual item must be delivered, or Condition when a richer predicate must be protected with state.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nAn Event is not a mutual-exclusion lock, and wait returning does not automatically make a group of state changes atomic. A cleared flag also does not erase data written elsewhere.","summary":"Event is a shared readiness flag that threads can wait on efficiently. It is a signal, not a queue of messages or a lock.","examples":[{"title":"Inspect set, wait, and clear behavior","code":"from threading import Event\nevent = Event()\nprint(event.wait(timeout=0))\nevent.set()\nprint(event.wait(timeout=0))\nevent.clear()\nprint(event.is_set())","output":"False\nTrue\nFalse","explanation":["A zero timeout checks the current condition without waiting. set makes waits succeed until clear resets the flag.","Repeated set calls do not create several independently consumable signals. Choose a queue or semaphore if counting separate work matters."]},{"title":"Release a worker after initialization","code":"from threading import Event, Thread\nready = Event()\nvalues = []\ndef worker():\n    ready.wait()\n    values.append(\"started\")\nthread = Thread(target=worker)\nthread.start()\nready.set()\nthread.join()\nprint(values)","output":"['started']","explanation":["The worker waits without polling repeatedly. Setting ready permits it to continue, and joining ensures the output is observed after completion.","The flag remains set after waking this worker. Other later waiters would also proceed until the event is cleared."]},{"title":"Use separate readiness and release signals","code":"from threading import Event, Thread\nready, release = Event(), Event()\nresults = []\ndef worker():\n    ready.set()\n    release.wait()\n    results.append(\"complete\")\nthread = Thread(target=worker)\nthread.start()\nready.wait()\nprint(\"worker ready\")\nrelease.set()\nthread.join()\nprint(results)","output":"worker ready\n['complete']","explanation":["The first event reports that the worker reached its waiting stage. The second event explicitly grants permission to finish.","Separating directions makes the handshake understandable. More complicated repeating protocols generally need a carefully defined state machine rather than ad hoc signal reuse."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":154,"title":"How do asyncio.Semaphore and BoundedSemaphore limit concurrent work?","label":"Async concurrency limits","category":"Concurrency","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nAn asyncio semaphore tracks available permits. A task acquires a permit before entering a constrained region and releases it afterward; async with manages that lifetime safely. The limit controls concurrently admitted tasks, not a requests-per-second rate. BoundedSemaphore additionally rejects releases that would increase the count beyond its initial value. These primitives coordinate tasks on an event loop, not arbitrary operating-system threads.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a semaphore around a limited resource such as a connection-dependent operation. Keep the permit held only for the operation whose concurrency should be constrained.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nLaunching many tasks and placing the semaphore only after the expensive resource has already been acquired does not limit that acquisition. Rate limiting requires a separate time-based policy.","summary":"A semaphore limits how many tasks enter a region at once. Use async with so permits are released even when work fails.","examples":[{"title":"Measure the maximum admitted concurrency","code":"import asyncio\nactive = 0\nmaximum = 0\nasync def main():\n    semaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(2)\n    async def work():\n        global active, maximum\n        async with semaphore:\n            active += 1\n            maximum = max(maximum, active)\n            try:\n                await asyncio.sleep(0)\n            finally:\n                active -= 1\n    await asyncio.gather(*(work() for _ in range(5)))\n    print(maximum, active)\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"2 0","explanation":["At most two tasks hold permits. The explicit suspension lets overlap occur, and the counter shows that all tasks release their active state before completion.","This single-event-loop counter has no suspension inside its update steps. Shared counters used across threads require an appropriate synchronization design."]},{"title":"Reject an extra release with a bounded semaphore","code":"import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    semaphore = asyncio.BoundedSemaphore(1)\n    await semaphore.acquire()\n    semaphore.release()\n    try:\n        semaphore.release()\n    except ValueError:\n        print(\"Too many releases\")\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"Too many releases","explanation":["The first release returns the acquired permit. The second would exceed the configured starting capacity and is rejected.","BoundedSemaphore helps expose accounting bugs. Prefer async with for ordinary scoped use so successful acquisition and release remain paired."]},{"title":"Release permits during exception handling","code":"import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    semaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(1)\n    try:\n        async with semaphore:\n            raise ValueError(\"failed\")\n    except ValueError:\n        print(\"handled\")\n    async with semaphore:\n        print(\"permit available\")\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"handled\npermit available","explanation":["The context manager releases the permit as the first block exits with an error. The later acquisition can therefore proceed.","Manual acquire without a reliable finally release can strand permits and stall unrelated work. Scope the resource ownership explicitly."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":155,"title":"How do unittest fixtures, subTest, and cleanup callbacks make tests reliable?","label":"Test fixtures & subtests","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nunittest.TestCase supplies assertions, fixtures, and result reporting. setUp runs before each test method, helping avoid accidental shared per-test state. subTest identifies cases inside one test so several inputs can be checked with useful failure context. addCleanup registers cleanup functions that run after the test, including important failure paths. Tests should observe meaningful behavior and keep external dependencies controlled.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse fixtures for small repeatable setup, subtests for related input cases, and cleanup callbacks for resources whose release should not depend on reaching the end of a test body.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDo not depend on test execution order or put mutable per-test state on the class unintentionally. A passing test that merely duplicates implementation expressions offers little independent evidence.","summary":"Reliable tests start from controlled state, check visible behavior, and release resources even when a check fails.","examples":[{"title":"Run a focused behavior test","code":"import unittest\ndef double(value):\n    return value * 2\nclass Tests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_double(self):\n        self.assertEqual(double(3), 6)\nresult = unittest.TestResult()\nunittest.defaultTestLoader.loadTestsFromTestCase(Tests).run(result)\nprint(result.testsRun, result.wasSuccessful())","output":"1 True","explanation":["The test verifies the function’s public result through a standard assertion. A TestResult captures outcome without version-dependent runner formatting.","A test failure is recorded rather than presented as the example’s expected success output. Real suites should include meaningful boundary and failure cases as well."]},{"title":"Give each test fresh fixture state","code":"import unittest\nclass Tests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp(self):\n        self.items = []\n    def test_one(self):\n        self.items.append(1)\n        self.assertEqual(self.items, [1])\n    def test_two(self):\n        self.assertEqual(self.items, [])\nresult = unittest.TestResult()\nunittest.defaultTestLoader.loadTestsFromTestCase(Tests).run(result)\nprint(result.testsRun, result.wasSuccessful())","output":"2 True","explanation":["setUp constructs an independent list before each test method. Mutating the first test’s list does not leak into the second fixture.","This makes the test contract independent of incidental ordering. Avoid shared mutable class attributes when the intended lifetime is one test."]},{"title":"Use subtests and guaranteed cleanup","code":"import unittest\nevents = []\nclass Tests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_cases(self):\n        self.addCleanup(events.append, \"cleaned\")\n        for value, expected in [(0, 0), (2, 4), (-1, -2)]:\n            with self.subTest(value=value):\n                self.assertEqual(value * 2, expected)\nresult = unittest.TestResult()\nunittest.defaultTestLoader.loadTestsFromTestCase(Tests).run(result)\nprint(result.wasSuccessful())\nprint(events)","output":"True\n['cleaned']","explanation":["Each subtest labels a related input case, improving failure diagnostics while preserving one shared test procedure. The example covers zero, positive, and negative inputs.","The registered cleanup runs after the test. Real resources should be acquired and registered for cleanup promptly so failures do not leak them."]}],"minutes":2},{"id":156,"title":"How do slicing, negative indices, and extended slice assignment work?","label":"Slicing & extended assignment","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nA slice selects positions using start, stop, and step. The stop is excluded, omitted boundaries depend on the step direction, and negative indices count from the end. Slicing a list creates a new outer list, while slice assignment changes an existing list. Extended slice assignment with a non-unit step requires the replacement to contain exactly as many elements as the selected positions.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse slices for subsequences, reversing a sequence, and replacing contiguous or regularly spaced positions. Keep complex index arithmetic in named variables so the intended range is easy to inspect.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA list slice is shallow: nested objects can remain shared. A negative step also changes the default boundaries; a zero step raises ValueError.","summary":"A slice chooses positions with an excluded stop. Reading copies a list’s outer container; assigning to a slice updates the existing list.","examples":[{"title":"Select and reverse positions","code":"values = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\nprint(values[1:4:2])\nprint(values[-2:])\nprint(values[::-1])","output":"[20, 40]\n[40, 50]\n[50, 40, 30, 20, 10]","explanation":["The first slice visits indices 1 and 3, stopping before index 4. Negative indices locate the final two elements.","A step of -1 traverses from the final element toward the beginning because both explicit boundaries are omitted."]},{"title":"Replace a contiguous slice","code":"values = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nalias = values\nvalues[1:3] = [8, 9, 10]\nprint(values)\nprint(alias is values)","output":"[1, 8, 9, 10, 4]\nTrue","explanation":["A contiguous replacement can change the length: two selected positions are replaced with three new elements.","Slice assignment mutates the existing list. The alias still references that same list and observes its updated contents."]},{"title":"Match an extended slice length","code":"values = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nvalues[::2] = [10, 20, 30]\nprint(values)\ntry:\n    values[::2] = [99]\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"length mismatch\")","output":"[10, 1, 20, 3, 30, 5]\nlength mismatch","explanation":["The extended slice selects indices 0, 2, and 4. The three replacements occupy exactly those existing positions.","A single replacement cannot fill the three selected positions, so the second extended assignment raises ValueError."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":157,"title":"What makes range a compact reusable sequence rather than a generator?","label":"Range objects & arithmetic sequences","category":"Iteration","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nrange represents an arithmetic sequence with a start, excluded stop, and nonzero step. It stores the sequence parameters instead of materializing every integer. A range supports iteration, indexing, slicing, and membership tests, and can be iterated again because it is a sequence rather than a one-shot iterator.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse range for integer-indexed loops and compact arithmetic progressions. Materialize a list only when you need a mutable container of all the values.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nThe stop is excluded, and a step pointing away from the stop produces an empty range. range arguments describe integers, not arbitrary floating-point intervals.","summary":"range stores the rule for an integer sequence. It is compact, reusable, and excludes the stop value.","examples":[{"title":"Include a descending step","code":"numbers = range(8, 1, -2)\nprint(list(numbers))\nprint(numbers[1])\nprint(2 in numbers)","output":"[8, 6, 4, 2]\n6\nTrue","explanation":["The negative step subtracts two at each position, and 2 is included because the stop is 1 rather than 2.","Indexing and membership operate on the range sequence directly; creating a list is only needed here to display all elements."]},{"title":"Iterate a range twice","code":"numbers = range(1, 4)\nprint(sum(numbers))\nprint(list(numbers))\nprint(list(numbers[1:]))","output":"6\n[1, 2, 3]\n[2, 3]","explanation":["sum consumes an iterator obtained from the range, not the range object itself. Another iteration still starts at its first element.","Slicing a range returns another range that describes the selected arithmetic progression without requiring an intermediate list."]},{"title":"Describe a very large sequence","code":"numbers = range(0, 1_000_000, 5)\nprint(len(numbers))\nprint(numbers[-1])\nprint(list(range(5, 1)))","output":"200000\n999995\n[]","explanation":["There are 200,000 multiples of five from zero up to but excluding one million. The last is five below that stop.","The second range has a positive default step but a smaller stop, so it describes an empty sequence rather than counting backward."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":158,"title":"How do dictionary views and insertion order behave during updates?","label":"Dictionary views & insertion order","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nDictionary keys, values, and items return view objects that reflect later changes to the dictionary. Dictionaries preserve insertion order. Updating an existing key keeps its position; deleting it and inserting it again places it at the end. A list constructed from a view is a separate snapshot of its elements at that time.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse views to inspect current mappings without copying all entries. Use a snapshot when you need stable traversal while adding or deleting keys.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nAdding or deleting keys while directly iterating a dictionary can raise RuntimeError. A view is not an immutable snapshot, even when stored in another variable.","summary":"Dictionary views stay connected to the mapping. Existing-key updates preserve order; a deleted and reinserted key moves to the end.","examples":[{"title":"Observe a live keys view","code":"data = {\"red\": 1}\nview = data.keys()\nsnapshot = list(view)\ndata[\"blue\"] = 2\nprint(list(view))\nprint(snapshot)","output":"['red', 'blue']\n['red']","explanation":["The view reads current keys, so it includes blue after insertion. The earlier list keeps only the keys copied into it.","Both variables are valid ways to inspect keys, but choose a view for current state and a list when a stable snapshot is intended."]},{"title":"Update and reinsert keys","code":"data = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2, \"c\": 3}\ndata[\"b\"] = 20\nprint(list(data))\ndel data[\"a\"]\ndata[\"a\"] = 10\nprint(list(data))","output":"['a', 'b', 'c']\n['b', 'c', 'a']","explanation":["Changing the value for b leaves its insertion position intact. Dictionary ordering is not a sort by key or by value.","Deleting a removes that entry. Inserting a new entry for a afterward places it at the end of the remaining insertion order."]},{"title":"Delete safely using a snapshot","code":"data = {\"a\": 0, \"b\": 2, \"c\": 0}\nfor key in list(data):\n    if data[key] == 0:\n        del data[key]\nprint(data)","output":"{'b': 2}","explanation":["list(data) copies the keys before the loop begins, so deleting dictionary entries does not resize the object being iterated.","The loop still reads each current value from data. Snapshotting the keys is sufficient here because the goal is controlled deletion."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":159,"title":"How do set union, intersection, difference, and symmetric difference compare?","label":"Set algebra & membership","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nA set stores unique hashable elements. Union keeps elements from either operand, intersection keeps those shared by both, difference removes members of the right operand, and symmetric difference keeps elements found in exactly one operand. Set comparisons can express subset and superset relationships.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse set algebra to compare permissions, remove duplicate identifiers, and find missing or overlapping records. Sort a set when presentation needs deterministic order.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA set is unordered and cannot preserve duplicate counts. Difference is directional: a - b is generally different from b - a.","summary":"Sets answer membership questions. Union combines, intersection overlaps, difference subtracts, and symmetric difference keeps non-overlapping members.","examples":[{"title":"Compare two collections","code":"a = {1, 2, 3}\nb = {3, 4}\nprint(sorted(a | b))\nprint(sorted(a & b))\nprint(sorted(a ^ b))","output":"[1, 2, 3, 4]\n[3]\n[1, 2, 4]","explanation":["Union includes each distinct member once, while intersection includes only 3 because it occurs in both sets.","Symmetric difference excludes shared elements. Sorting is used solely to make the displayed list order predictable."]},{"title":"Find missing permissions","code":"required = {\"read\", \"write\"}\ngranted = {\"read\", \"audit\"}\nprint(sorted(required - granted))\nprint(required <= granted)\nprint({\"read\"} < required)","output":"['write']\nFalse\nTrue","explanation":["Subtracting granted from required identifies required permissions that are missing. The direction of subtraction matters.","The <= test asks whether all requirements are granted. The strict subset test is true for read because required contains an additional member."]},{"title":"Compare mutation with a new set","code":"a = {1, 2}\nb = a | {3}\nprint(sorted(a))\na.update({4})\nprint(sorted(a))\nprint(sorted(b))","output":"[1, 2]\n[1, 2, 4]\n[1, 2, 3]","explanation":["The union operator returns a new set and leaves a unchanged. The new set b does not track later mutations to a.","update changes a in place. Choosing a mutating method versus a new result should match who owns and shares the original set."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":160,"title":"Why is str.join useful for building text from many pieces?","label":"Joining text & iterable boundaries","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nseparator.join(parts) combines string elements with a separator between adjacent elements. It accepts an iterable of strings, including a generator, and returns one new string. The separator is not added before the first element or after the last. Non-string elements must be explicitly converted when that conversion is intended.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse join for readable reports, generated lines, and delimited display text. For CSV with quoting rules, use the csv module rather than constructing records by joining fields.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\njoin does not automatically stringify integers, and joining a dictionary iterates its keys. An empty iterable produces an empty string.","summary":"join puts a separator between string pieces. Convert non-strings deliberately and use a format-aware writer when quoting rules matter.","examples":[{"title":"Join ordinary pieces","code":"parts = [\"learn\", \"practice\", \"repeat\"]\nprint(\" -> \".join(parts))\nprint(repr(\",\".join([])))","output":"learn -> practice -> repeat\n''","explanation":["The separator appears only between neighboring pieces, so three strings produce two arrows and no trailing separator.","With no pieces there is nothing to concatenate. repr makes the resulting empty string visible in the displayed output."]},{"title":"Convert generated numbers","code":"numbers = [2, 4, 6]\nprint(\", \".join(str(n) for n in numbers))\nprint(\"\".join([\"Py\", \"thon\"]))","output":"2, 4, 6\nPython","explanation":["The generator explicitly converts every integer to a string before join consumes it. join itself does not make that conversion.","An empty separator concatenates the pieces directly. The original list and its integer elements are not modified by formatting."]},{"title":"Handle an invalid element","code":"try:\n    print(\"-\".join([\"item\", 3]))\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"all pieces must be strings\")\nprint(\"/\".join({\"home\": 1, \"docs\": 2}))","output":"all pieces must be strings\nhome/docs","explanation":["An integer element violates join’s input contract, so the operation raises TypeError instead of silently formatting that value.","Iterating a dictionary yields its keys in insertion order. Joining a mapping therefore combines its keys unless another iterable is explicitly supplied."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":161,"title":"When should you use split, rsplit, or partition to separate text?","label":"Splitting & partitioning strings","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nsplit returns pieces separated by a delimiter, optionally limiting the number of splits. Without an explicit delimiter, it collapses whitespace runs and drops leading and trailing whitespace. partition always returns three strings: text before the first delimiter, the delimiter, and text after it. rsplit and rpartition start from the right.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse partition for a single structured boundary such as a key and value. Use split for multiple fields when the format has no escaping or quoting rules.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nsplit() and split(\" \") have different empty-field behavior. A missing partition delimiter is represented by empty separator and tail fields, not an exception.","summary":"split creates a list of pieces. partition always gives a before/separator/after triple, making one optional boundary easy to handle.","examples":[{"title":"Compare whitespace handling","code":"text = \"  a   b  \"\nprint(text.split())\nprint(\"a,,b,\".split(\",\"))","output":"['a', 'b']\n['a', '', 'b', '']","explanation":["The default whitespace mode treats adjacent spaces as one separator and discards empty pieces at the boundaries.","An explicit comma separator preserves empty fields, including the trailing empty field after the final comma."]},{"title":"Partition a configuration value","code":"key, sep, value = \"mode=fast=extra\".partition(\"=\")\nprint(key, sep, value)\nprint(\"missing\".partition(\"=\"))","output":"mode = fast=extra\n('missing', '', '')","explanation":["partition stops at the first equals sign, leaving the remainder intact as the value even when another equals sign follows.","When the separator is absent, the original text occupies the first result field. The empty separator lets the caller detect that case."]},{"title":"Split from the right","code":"path = \"folder/archive.tar.gz\"\nprint(path.rsplit(\".\", 1))\nprint(\"a:b:c\".split(\":\", 1))\nprint(\"a:b:c\".rsplit(\":\", 1))","output":"['folder/archive.tar', 'gz']\n['a', 'b:c']\n['a:b', 'c']","explanation":["With a maximum of one split, rsplit chooses the rightmost delimiter. This extracts the final extension without splitting the earlier dot.","The left and right variants produce different boundaries when the split count is limited; without a limit their field sequences agree."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":162,"title":"How does the format specification mini-language control numeric display?","label":"Format specifications & alignment","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nFormat specifications describe presentation such as width, alignment, precision, sign, and numeric type. They work in f-strings, format(), and str.format(). The specification is interpreted by the value’s formatting protocol, so the meaning of precision depends on the type and presentation mode. Formatting returns text rather than changing the stored number.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse explicit formatting for aligned tables, percentages, hexadecimal displays, and human-readable numeric reports. Keep numeric values numeric until the presentation boundary.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDisplay rounding is not a change to the original value. A width is generally a minimum rather than a truncation limit, and locale-sensitive formatting needs additional care.","summary":"A format specification controls how a value is displayed. Width pads, alignment positions, and precision selects the requested representation.","examples":[{"title":"Format a number and a percentage","code":"value = 12.345\nprint(f\"{value:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"{0.25:.1%}\")\nprint(value)","output":"12.35\n25.0%\n12.345","explanation":["Fixed-point precision requests two digits after the decimal. Percent formatting multiplies the displayed value by one hundred and adds a percent sign.","The last print shows that formatting did not mutate value. Calculations should use the original numeric object rather than the formatted text."]},{"title":"Align fields with a minimum width","code":"print(f\"|{'cat':<6}|\")\nprint(f\"|{'cat':^7}|\")\nprint(f\"{42:04d}\")","output":"|cat   |\n|  cat  |\n0042","explanation":["The alignment markers place text at the left or center of the requested field. Vertical bars make padding spaces visible.","The integer specification uses decimal digits with zero padding to a minimum width of four. Longer values are not automatically truncated."]},{"title":"Choose integer bases and separators","code":"print(f\"{255:#x}\")\nprint(f\"{10:08b}\")\nprint(f\"{1234567:,}\")","output":"0xff\n00001010\n1,234,567","explanation":["The x type displays hexadecimal, and the # flag includes its prefix. Binary formatting here uses eight zero-padded positions.","The comma option groups decimal digits for display. These outputs are strings, so their visual separators are not part of the underlying integer."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":163,"title":"How do bytes and bytearray differ when editing binary data?","label":"Bytearray & mutable binary data","category":"Memory & objects","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nbytes is an immutable sequence of byte values, while bytearray provides mutable byte storage. Indexing either returns an integer from 0 through 255. bytearray supports element and slice assignment, and bytes(buffer) creates an immutable copy. Text still requires an explicit encoding or decoding step.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse bytearray when assembling or editing binary records in place. Convert to bytes when an API expects immutable data or the result should no longer change.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nEach assigned element must fit into one byte. A bytearray is not a Unicode string; replacing a character in encoded multibyte text requires understanding the encoding.","summary":"bytes is fixed binary content; bytearray is editable binary content. Indexing works with integer byte values, not Unicode characters.","examples":[{"title":"Edit one byte and append another","code":"data = bytearray(b\"cat\")\ndata[0] = ord(\"b\")\ndata.append(ord(\"!\"))\nprint(data.decode(\"ascii\"))\nprint(data[0])","output":"bat!\n98","explanation":["The first assignment replaces byte value 99 with 98. Appending 33 adds an ASCII exclamation mark to the mutable buffer.","Decoding creates text for display, while indexing returns the integer value stored in one byte rather than a one-character string."]},{"title":"Take an immutable snapshot","code":"data = bytearray([1, 2, 3])\nsaved = bytes(data)\ndata[1:3] = b\"XY\"\nprint(list(data))\nprint(list(saved))","output":"[1, 88, 89]\n[1, 2, 3]","explanation":["The slice replacement writes the byte values for X and Y into data. Converting each sequence to a list makes the byte numbers explicit.","The immutable bytes object was copied before the mutation, so its contents stay independent of subsequent changes to the bytearray."]},{"title":"Reject an out-of-range byte","code":"data = bytearray([10])\ntry:\n    data[0] = 256\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"byte must fit in 0..255\")\nprint(list(data))","output":"byte must fit in 0..255\n[10]","explanation":["A byte stores one of 256 possible values. Assignment outside that range raises ValueError instead of wrapping the number automatically.","The failing element assignment leaves the original byte unchanged. Validate numeric input when building binary data from external values."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":164,"title":"How does ChainMap layer dictionaries without merging copies?","label":"ChainMap & layered configuration","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ncollections.ChainMap searches several mappings in order and returns the first matching binding. It references the original mappings instead of copying all entries. Normal assignment and deletion through the ChainMap target its first mapping, while new_child adds a fresh higher-priority layer.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse ChainMap to express command-line overrides, user settings, and defaults as separate layers. The order of layers states which source wins.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA ChainMap is a live view of its mappings, not a merged snapshot. Deleting a key through it only affects the first mapping and can fail if the key exists only in a later layer.","summary":"ChainMap reads through layers from first to last. Writes target the first layer, so overrides can stay separate from defaults.","examples":[{"title":"Resolve an override before a default","code":"from collections import ChainMap\ndefaults = {\"color\": \"blue\", \"size\": 10}\noverrides = {\"color\": \"red\"}\nconfig = ChainMap(overrides, defaults)\nprint(config[\"color\"], config[\"size\"])\nconfig[\"size\"] = 20\nprint(overrides)\nprint(defaults[\"size\"])","output":"red 10\n{'color': 'red', 'size': 20}\n10","explanation":["color is found in the first mapping, while size initially falls through to defaults. Reads do not merge the underlying dictionaries.","Writing size creates an override in the first dictionary. The fallback default remains unchanged and can still be read independently."]},{"title":"Create a temporary child layer","code":"from collections import ChainMap\nbase = ChainMap({\"mode\": \"safe\"})\nchild = base.new_child({\"mode\": \"fast\"})\nchild[\"debug\"] = True\nprint(child[\"mode\"], child[\"debug\"])\nprint(base[\"mode\"])\nprint(\"debug\" in base)","output":"fast True\nsafe\nFalse","explanation":["new_child places a new mapping before the existing layers. Both its override and its later debug assignment belong to that child layer.","The base mapping has no debug entry and still supplies safe. Child layers make scoped overrides explicit without modifying the parent layer."]},{"title":"Observe a live fallback update","code":"from collections import ChainMap\ndefaults = {\"limit\": 5}\nconfig = ChainMap({}, defaults)\nsnapshot = dict(config)\ndefaults[\"limit\"] = 8\nprint(config[\"limit\"])\nprint(snapshot[\"limit\"])","output":"8\n5","explanation":["ChainMap retains the original defaults dictionary, so later changes are visible when a lookup reaches that layer.","dict(config) materializes a separate mapping at that moment. Choose that snapshot when future changes should not alter the resolved configuration."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":165,"title":"What does MappingProxyType protect, and what does it leave mutable?","label":"Read-only mapping proxies","category":"Memory & objects","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ntypes.MappingProxyType exposes a read-only view of a mapping. Assignment through the proxy is rejected, but changes made through the original mapping remain visible. The protection is shallow: a mutable value retrieved from the proxy is still the original mutable value.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a proxy to share mapping contents with code that should not replace or delete entries through that interface. Copy first when a stable snapshot is also required.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA proxy does not freeze nested values or prevent the mapping owner from making changes. Read-only access and deep immutability are different contracts.","summary":"A mapping proxy prevents writes through one interface. It still reflects owner updates, and nested mutable values remain mutable.","examples":[{"title":"Read owner changes through a proxy","code":"from types import MappingProxyType\ndata = {\"mode\": \"safe\"}\nview = MappingProxyType(data)\ndata[\"mode\"] = \"fast\"\nprint(view[\"mode\"])\ntry:\n    view[\"mode\"] = \"other\"\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"read-only interface\")","output":"fast\nread-only interface","explanation":["The owner changes the underlying dictionary successfully, and the proxy lookup returns that new value rather than an old snapshot.","Assignment through the proxy raises TypeError. The protection applies to that access path, not to every reference to the underlying dictionary."]},{"title":"Recognize shallow protection","code":"from types import MappingProxyType\ndata = {\"tags\": [\"python\"]}\nview = MappingProxyType(data)\nview[\"tags\"].append(\"examples\")\nprint(data[\"tags\"])","output":"['python', 'examples']","explanation":["Looking up tags returns the original list. Appending to that list does not replace an entry in the protected mapping.","For a deeply immutable interface, choose immutable nested values or an appropriate conversion rather than relying on a mapping proxy alone."]},{"title":"Make a detached read-only mapping","code":"from types import MappingProxyType\noriginal = {\"count\": 1}\nview = MappingProxyType(dict(original))\noriginal[\"count\"] = 9\nprint(view[\"count\"])\nprint(original[\"count\"])","output":"1\n9","explanation":["dict(original) creates a new outer dictionary before the proxy is constructed. Updating the original mapping no longer changes this outer snapshot.","The snapshot is still shallow. This example uses an immutable integer value, so there is no nested mutable object shared between the mappings."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":166,"title":"When is array.array useful compared with a normal Python list?","label":"Typed numeric arrays","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\narray.array stores values using a specified machine-oriented numeric type code. Unlike a general list, its elements must fit that representation. It supports sequence operations and the buffer protocol, making it useful for compact numeric storage and compatible binary interfaces. Type sizes and native byte order can depend on the platform.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse array for homogeneous numeric buffers when the standard library is sufficient. Use struct or an explicitly specified format when interoperating across machines with different byte orders.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA type code constrains stored values, and native binary output is not automatically a portable file format. Do not assume every integer type code has the same width everywhere.","summary":"array holds one numeric representation rather than arbitrary objects. It is useful for compact buffers, but binary portability needs explicit format decisions.","examples":[{"title":"Store and edit signed integers","code":"from array import array\nvalues = array(\"i\", [1, 2, 3])\nvalues.append(4)\nvalues[0] = -1\nprint(values.tolist())\nprint(values.typecode)","output":"[-1, 2, 3, 4]\ni","explanation":["The i type code selects signed integers, and ordinary sequence updates preserve that homogeneous storage representation.","tolist produces a normal list for display. The type code describes the array storage, while its exact item size should be inspected when relevant."]},{"title":"Round-trip raw bytes with the same type","code":"from array import array\nsource = array(\"h\", [10, 20])\nrestored = array(\"h\")\nrestored.frombytes(source.tobytes())\nprint(restored.tolist())\nprint(restored.itemsize == source.itemsize)","output":"[10, 20]\nTrue","explanation":["The source and destination use the same type code on the same machine, so their native byte representations agree.","This local round trip does not establish a cross-platform format. Portable exchange should state the element width and byte order explicitly."]},{"title":"Enforce an unsigned byte range","code":"from array import array\nvalues = array(\"B\", [0, 255])\ntry:\n    values.append(256)\nexcept OverflowError:\n    print(\"outside unsigned byte range\")\nprint(values.tolist())","output":"outside unsigned byte range\n[0, 255]","explanation":["The B type code stores unsigned byte values. The value 256 cannot fit and is rejected rather than silently truncated.","The unsuccessful append leaves the existing elements unchanged. Type constraints are part of the data structure’s contract, not just a display convention."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":167,"title":"How does struct encode fixed-width binary records with explicit byte order?","label":"Struct & portable binary records","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nstruct translates between Python values and binary records described by a format string. Prefixes such as > and < select big-endian and little-endian layouts with standard sizes. pack creates bytes, unpack decodes a buffer of exactly the required size, and unpack_from can decode a record inside a larger buffer.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse struct for network headers, device messages, and documented binary formats. Specify byte order and field widths to avoid accidental dependence on native alignment.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nThe format must match the data exactly. Native layout can include padding and platform differences, and out-of-range field values raise errors.","summary":"struct uses a format string as a binary layout contract. State byte order and field widths, then pack and unpack using that same contract.","examples":[{"title":"Encode two unsigned short values","code":"import struct\ndata = struct.pack(\">HH\", 1, 258)\nprint(data.hex())\nprint(struct.unpack(\">HH\", data))","output":"00010102\n(1, 258)","explanation":["The > prefix requests big-endian standard layout. Each H field occupies two bytes, so 258 becomes the byte sequence 01 02.","unpack with the same format reconstructs a tuple of Python values. The tuple shape follows the number of fields in the format string."]},{"title":"Read a record from an offset","code":"import struct\ndata = b\"ID\" + struct.pack(\"<I\", 1000)\nprint(struct.calcsize(\"<I\"))\nprint(struct.unpack_from(\"<I\", data, 2)[0])","output":"4\n1000","explanation":["The little-endian I field occupies four standard-size bytes. The leading two marker bytes are not part of that integer.","unpack_from starts decoding at offset 2 without requiring the entire buffer to contain only one record. Offset and available size still need to be valid."]},{"title":"Reject a truncated record","code":"import struct\ntry:\n    struct.unpack(\">I\", b\"\\x00\\x01\")\nexcept struct.error:\n    print(\"need four bytes\")\nprint(struct.pack(\"<H\", 258).hex())","output":"need four bytes\n0201","explanation":["An unsigned standard-size integer requires four bytes with I, so a two-byte buffer cannot satisfy the requested layout.","The final H field uses two little-endian bytes, reversing the byte ordering seen for a big-endian representation of the same number."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":168,"title":"Why does itertools.groupby group consecutive runs rather than all equal keys?","label":"Grouping consecutive runs","category":"Iteration","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nitertools.groupby creates a group whenever its key changes as the input is traversed. Equal keys separated by another key therefore form separate groups. Each group is an iterator sharing the underlying input with the outer iterator, so consume or materialize a group before advancing to the next one.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse groupby for run-length summaries, already ordered records, and streaming groups. Sort by the same key first when the goal is one group for every distinct key.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\ngroupby does not sort the input or retain independent copies of every group. Saving group iterators for later consumption can produce empty results once the outer iterator has advanced.","summary":"groupby splits consecutive runs. Sort first for one group per key, and consume each group before moving to the next.","examples":[{"title":"Group neighboring letters","code":"from itertools import groupby\nfor key, group in groupby(\"AAABBAC\"):\n    print(key, len(list(group)))","output":"A 3\nB 2\nA 1\nC 1","explanation":["The later A starts a new group because B interrupted the earlier run. Grouping depends on adjacent key changes rather than a global lookup.","Materializing each group inside the loop consumes its members before advancing the outer iterator, avoiding shared-input surprises."]},{"title":"Sort records by the grouping key","code":"from itertools import groupby\nrecords = [(\"b\", 2), (\"a\", 1), (\"b\", 3)]\nrecords.sort(key=lambda row: row[0])\nfor key, rows in groupby(records, key=lambda row: row[0]):\n    print(key, sum(value for _, value in rows))","output":"a 1\nb 5","explanation":["Sorting by the exact grouping key places both b records next to each other, allowing a single b group to contain both values.","The group is consumed immediately by sum. Aggregation can stream through a group without building another list of all its rows."]},{"title":"Materialize groups that must outlive iteration","code":"from itertools import groupby\ngroups = [(key, list(items)) for key, items in groupby([1, 1, 2, 3, 3])]\nprint(groups)\nprint(groups[0][1])","output":"[(1, [1, 1]), (2, [2]), (3, [3, 3])]\n[1, 1]","explanation":["Each group is converted to a list while it is current. The stored lists no longer depend on the advancing shared input iterator.","This trades streaming memory behavior for retained results. Keep just counts or other summaries when retaining every element is unnecessary."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":169,"title":"How does itertools.tee copy iteration, and why can it buffer memory?","label":"Tee & independent iterator cursors","category":"Iteration","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nitertools.tee returns iterators that can traverse the same input independently. It does not eagerly copy every source value. Instead, it retains values needed by slower branches as faster branches advance. A large gap between consumers can therefore require substantial storage.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse tee for a few coordinated passes over a finite or similarly consumed stream. A list may be simpler when all values will be retained anyway.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDo not continue consuming the original input separately after splitting it, because tee branches share that source. The retained values are object references, not deep copies.","summary":"tee gives separate cursors over one source. Lagging branches cause buffering, and yielded mutable objects can still be shared.","examples":[{"title":"Advance two branches separately","code":"from itertools import tee\nfirst, second = tee(iter([10, 20, 30]))\nprint(next(first))\nprint(list(second))\nprint(list(first))","output":"10\n[10, 20, 30]\n[20, 30]","explanation":["The second branch starts at its own initial position even after the first has consumed 10. tee retains needed items behind the faster cursor.","The first branch still has its own remaining position. Draining one branch does not rewind or exhaust the other branch’s logical sequence."]},{"title":"Compute two summaries","code":"from itertools import tee\nvalues = (n * n for n in range(4))\nleft, right = tee(values)\nprint(sum(left))\nprint(max(right))","output":"14\n9","explanation":["Both summaries observe the square sequence 0, 1, 4, 9. tee allows separate consumption without running the generator expression twice.","Here sum drains its branch before max begins, so the other branch must retain the full sequence. For large data, consider combining summaries in one pass."]},{"title":"Observe shared yielded objects","code":"from itertools import tee\nleft, right = tee(iter([[1], [2]]))\nitem = next(left)\nitem.append(9)\nprint(next(right))\nprint(next(left))","output":"[1, 9]\n[2]","explanation":["Both branches receive references to the same first list, so mutating it through the first branch changes what the second branch observes.","tee duplicates cursor behavior rather than deep-copying yielded data. Copy values explicitly if the branches must mutate independent objects."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":170,"title":"How does itertools.accumulate expose intermediate reductions?","label":"Accumulate & running state","category":"Iteration","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nitertools.accumulate yields the running result of combining input values. Addition is the default operation, but another binary function can implement running products, minima, or state transitions. An initial value can seed the calculation and is itself included as the first yielded result.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse accumulate for cumulative totals, running extrema, and incremental reports where intermediate results matter. Use a single reduction when only the final result is needed.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nThe result is a one-shot iterator. Providing initial adds an extra output, and the chosen function must accept the accumulated state and the next input value.","summary":"accumulate yields every intermediate reduction. Choose the combining function and initial state to describe the running calculation.","examples":[{"title":"Compute running totals","code":"from itertools import accumulate\nprint(list(accumulate([2, 3, 4])))\nprint(list(accumulate([2, 3, 4], initial=10)))","output":"[2, 5, 9]\n[10, 12, 15, 19]","explanation":["Without an initial value, the first input becomes the first result. Each later result adds the next input to the previous accumulated value.","The explicit initial value is yielded first, then combined with every input. Three inputs therefore produce four outputs in that second call."]},{"title":"Track a running maximum","code":"from itertools import accumulate\nvalues = [3, 1, 5, 2, 7]\nprint(list(accumulate(values, max)))","output":"[3, 3, 5, 5, 7]","explanation":["max compares the previous accumulated maximum with each new value. Smaller incoming values leave the running result unchanged.","The output retains a position for each input even when the maximum does not change, making it useful for aligned running summaries."]},{"title":"Multiply rather than add","code":"from itertools import accumulate\nfrom operator import mul\nprint(list(accumulate([2, 3, 4], mul, initial=1)))\nprint(list(accumulate([], initial=0)))","output":"[1, 2, 6, 24]\n[0]","explanation":["The multiplicative identity 1 seeds the product. The successive states multiply by 2, then 3, then 4.","Even an empty input yields the supplied initial value. This distinction matters when aligning accumulated outputs with the original input positions."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":171,"title":"How does cached_property differ from a property that recomputes every access?","label":"Cached properties & invalidation","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nfunctools.cached_property computes a value on first attribute access and normally stores that value in the instance dictionary under the attribute name. Later accesses use the stored value. Deleting the stored attribute permits recomputation on the next access. Dependencies that change do not invalidate the cache automatically.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a cached property for an expensive derived value on an instance whose inputs remain stable or whose invalidation policy is explicit.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nInstances normally need a writable `__dict__`. Do not assume the getter runs only once under concurrent access; synchronize when duplicate computation would be unsafe.","summary":"cached_property stores a derived value on the instance. Change its inputs only with an explicit plan to invalidate or refresh that stored result.","examples":[{"title":"Count initial computation","code":"from functools import cached_property\nclass Report:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.calls = 0\n    @cached_property\n    def total(self):\n        self.calls += 1\n        return 42\nr = Report()\nprint(r.total, r.total)\nprint(r.calls)","output":"42 42\n1","explanation":["The first access executes the getter and stores 42 on this instance. The second access finds the cached attribute rather than recomputing it.","The counter measures actual getter calls for this sequential example. It does not establish a guarantee against concurrent duplicate computation."]},{"title":"Invalidate a derived value","code":"from functools import cached_property\nclass Basket:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.values = [2, 3]\n    @cached_property\n    def total(self):\n        return sum(self.values)\nb = Basket()\nprint(b.total)\nb.values.append(4)\nprint(b.total)\ndel b.total\nprint(b.total)","output":"5\n5\n9","explanation":["Mutating the input list does not tell cached_property that its stored answer is outdated. The second access therefore still returns 5.","Deleting the attribute removes the cached binding. The next access computes a new total from the updated list and stores 9."]},{"title":"Compare a normal property","code":"class Sensor:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 2\n    @property\n    def doubled(self):\n        return self.value * 2\ns = Sensor()\nprint(s.doubled)\ns.value = 5\nprint(s.doubled)","output":"4\n10","explanation":["A normal property invokes its getter on each access, so the derived value immediately reflects the changed input.","Caching is a design choice rather than an automatic improvement. Recomputing a cheap value can be simpler and avoids an invalidation requirement."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":172,"title":"How do operator helpers replace small lookup and method-call lambdas?","label":"Operator lookup & call helpers","category":"Functions","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\noperator.itemgetter builds a callable that selects one or more items, attrgetter selects attributes and can traverse dotted attribute paths, and methodcaller invokes a named method with stored arguments. These helpers fit APIs such as sorted(key=...) that expect a callable.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse these helpers for straightforward field extraction and method invocation. Prefer a named function when validation, branching, or a substantial explanation belongs in the operation.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nitemgetter uses indexing while attrgetter uses attribute access. Missing keys, indexes, attributes, or methods still raise their normal errors.","summary":"Operator helpers package a simple lookup or method call as a callable. Choose item access, attribute access, or method invocation to match the data.","examples":[{"title":"Sort records by multiple fields","code":"from operator import itemgetter\nrows = [(\"b\", 2), (\"a\", 3), (\"a\", 1)]\nprint(sorted(rows, key=itemgetter(0, 1)))","output":"[('a', 1), ('a', 3), ('b', 2)]","explanation":["itemgetter(0, 1) returns a tuple containing two indexed fields from each row. Those tuples become the sorting keys.","Tuple comparison first compares the first field, then the second when the first fields match, producing the displayed two-level ordering."]},{"title":"Extract a nested attribute","code":"from operator import attrgetter\nfrom types import SimpleNamespace\nperson = SimpleNamespace(address=SimpleNamespace(city=\"Kolkata\"))\nget_city = attrgetter(\"address.city\")\nprint(get_city(person))","output":"Kolkata","explanation":["The dotted attribute path first accesses person.address and then accesses city on that object. It is not dictionary key traversal.","The returned callable can be reused for many similarly shaped objects. Missing attributes still fail rather than producing an automatic default."]},{"title":"Build a stored method call","code":"from operator import methodcaller\nreplace_spaces = methodcaller(\"replace\", \" \", \"-\")\nprint(replace_spaces(\"learn python now\"))\nprint(methodcaller(\"upper\")(\"quiz\"))","output":"learn-python-now\nQUIZ","explanation":["methodcaller stores the method name and its arguments, then looks up and calls that method on each object supplied later.","The helpers do not modify these strings because string methods return new strings. Method behavior still depends on the receiving object’s type."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":173,"title":"When should secrets be used instead of random for unpredictable tokens?","label":"Secrets & unpredictable tokens","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nsecrets provides randomness suitable for security-sensitive choices and tokens using the operating system’s randomness facilities. token_hex and token_urlsafe encode random bytes for common text transports. Specify an explicit byte count when the token size is part of the application’s contract, and treat generated tokens as secrets.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse secrets for password-reset links, session tokens, and other values that must resist guessing. Store and compare them according to the application’s authentication design.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA deterministic random.Random seed is useful for simulation but inappropriate for secret generation. Encoding a token does not encrypt it, and actual security depends on adequate entropy and the full surrounding system.","summary":"Use secrets when unpredictability matters. Choose an explicit random-byte budget and do not expose real tokens in logs or example output.","examples":[{"title":"Inspect token shape without revealing it","code":"import secrets\ntoken = secrets.token_hex(16)\nprint(len(token))\nprint(all(c in \"0123456789abcdef\" for c in token))","output":"32\nTrue","explanation":["Sixteen random bytes become thirty-two hexadecimal characters because each byte needs two hex digits. The generated token itself is not printed.","Only deterministic properties of the token are displayed. A fresh run generates different secret content while preserving those format properties."]},{"title":"Choose from an allowed alphabet","code":"import secrets\nalphabet = \"ABCDEFGHJKLMNPQRSTUVWXYZ23456789\"\ncode = \"\".join(secrets.choice(alphabet) for _ in range(12))\nprint(len(code))\nprint(set(code) <= set(alphabet))","output":"12\nTrue","explanation":["Each position is chosen from the specified alphabet with secrets.choice. The final string has the requested number of positions.","The membership test checks the output alphabet without disclosing the generated code. Length and alphabet determine its entropy budget, not visual complexity alone."]},{"title":"Create a URL-friendly token","code":"import secrets\ntoken = secrets.token_urlsafe(24)\nprint(len(token))\nprint(\"+\" not in token and \"/\" not in token)\nprint(isinstance(token, str))","output":"32\nTrue\nTrue","explanation":["Twenty-four random bytes encode to thirty-two URL-safe Base64 characters here. The URL-safe alphabet avoids plus and slash.","The text is still a bearer secret if used for authentication. URL-safe encoding makes transport convenient but adds neither access control nor encryption."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":174,"title":"How can a local random.Random instance make simulations reproducible?","label":"Independent random generators","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nrandom.Random holds its own pseudorandom generator state. Separate instances with the same seed and the same sequence of operations can reproduce the same sequence. Local instances avoid coupling a simulation to unrelated calls on the module-level generator. State can also be saved and restored.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse local seeded generators for simulations, randomized tests, and examples that need controlled repetition. Record assumptions about Python versions and algorithms when exact reproducibility is a long-term requirement.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nPseudorandom sequences are not suitable for authentication secrets. A seed does not mean successive samples are equal, and different operations advance generator state differently.","summary":"Give each simulation its own Random instance. A seed controls its sequence; getstate and setstate can preserve a specific position.","examples":[{"title":"Repeat the same seeded sequence","code":"import random\na = random.Random(7)\nb = random.Random(7)\nprint([a.random() for _ in range(3)] == [b.random() for _ in range(3)])\nprint(a is b)","output":"True\nFalse","explanation":["Both independent generators begin from the same seeded state and receive the same calls, so their generated sample lists match.","They are distinct objects rather than shared aliases. Advancing one later does not automatically advance the other generator."]},{"title":"Restore a generator position","code":"import random\nrng = random.Random(10)\nsaved = rng.getstate()\nfirst = rng.random()\nrng.setstate(saved)\nprint(first == rng.random())","output":"True","explanation":["getstate captures the generator’s internal position before drawing the first value. Restoring that state repeats the next draw.","This controls an experiment’s pseudorandom input sequence. It is not a security feature and should not be used to make authentication tokens predictable."]},{"title":"Shuffle a copy of the input","code":"import random\noriginal = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nshuffled = original.copy()\nrandom.Random(12).shuffle(shuffled)\nprint(original)\nprint(sorted(shuffled))\nprint(shuffled is original)","output":"[1, 2, 3, 4]\n[1, 2, 3, 4]\nFalse","explanation":["shuffle mutates the supplied list in place, so copying first preserves the original ordering for other consumers.","Sorting the shuffled result verifies its members without depending on a particular displayed permutation. The two lists remain separate objects."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":175,"title":"What can a cryptographic hash verify, and what does it not provide?","label":"Hashlib & content digests","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nhashlib computes digests of byte sequences using algorithms such as SHA-256. The same bytes produce the same digest, and incremental updates hash the concatenation of the supplied chunks. A digest can identify expected content when the expected digest comes from a trusted source; it does not encrypt the content or authenticate an untrusted sender by itself.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse digests for content identifiers and integrity checks against trusted expected values. Encode text explicitly and define whether normalization or line-ending conversion is part of the format.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA plain fast hash is not appropriate password storage. Use a purpose-built password hashing design, and use a keyed authentication construction when authenticity is required.","summary":"A hash maps bytes to a content digest. It is not encryption, and integrity depends on trusting the expected digest.","examples":[{"title":"Hash a known byte sequence","code":"import hashlib\ndigest = hashlib.sha256(b\"abc\")\nprint(digest.hexdigest())\nprint(len(digest.digest()))","output":"ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad\n32","explanation":["SHA-256 produces thirty-two digest bytes for this input. Hexadecimal representation uses two characters per digest byte.","The input is explicitly bytes, so no implicit text encoding is involved. Changing the input bytes produces a different digest in ordinary use."]},{"title":"Hash chunks incrementally","code":"import hashlib\ndigest = hashlib.sha256()\nfor chunk in [b\"py\", b\"thon\"]:\n    digest.update(chunk)\nprint(digest.digest() == hashlib.sha256(b\"python\").digest())","output":"True","explanation":["Sequential updates feed the same byte stream as hashing the concatenated chunks. Chunk boundaries are not included as extra data.","This permits streaming large files without materializing the entire file in memory. Add explicit framing if separate logical records must remain distinguishable."]},{"title":"Copy digest state for two suffixes","code":"import hashlib\nprefix = hashlib.sha256(b\"prefix:\")\nleft = prefix.copy()\nright = prefix.copy()\nleft.update(b\"A\")\nright.update(b\"B\")\nprint(left.digest() != right.digest())\nprint(prefix.digest() == hashlib.sha256(b\"prefix:\").digest())","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["copy duplicates the current hashing state, allowing two independent suffix calculations from the same previously hashed prefix.","Updating the copies does not mutate the original digest state. The prefix object still represents only the original prefix bytes."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":176,"title":"How does HMAC authenticate bytes using a shared secret key?","label":"HMAC & authentication tags","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nHMAC combines a secret key, a message, and a cryptographic hash in a defined authentication construction. Parties with the same secret can compute and verify a message authentication tag. Use hmac.compare_digest for supported same-type tag values rather than an ordinary equality test when comparing secret-derived values.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse a documented HMAC-based protocol when a shared-key authentication design is appropriate. Specify exact message bytes and encoding, keep keys secret, and include protocol-level replay protection when needed.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nHMAC does not encrypt the message or stop replay by itself. A hard-coded demonstration key is only for examples and must not be reused as a real credential.","summary":"HMAC uses a shared secret to authenticate exact message bytes. Verify tags carefully; encryption and replay prevention are separate requirements.","examples":[{"title":"Verify a matching message tag","code":"import hashlib\nimport hmac\nkey = b\"demo-only-key\"\ntag = hmac.new(key, b\"message\", hashlib.sha256).digest()\nexpected = hmac.new(key, b\"message\", hashlib.sha256).digest()\nprint(hmac.compare_digest(tag, expected))\nprint(len(tag))","output":"True\n32","explanation":["The same key and the same message bytes produce the same SHA-256 HMAC tag. Its raw output occupies thirty-two bytes.","The fixed key makes this a reproducible demonstration only. Real systems need appropriate key generation, storage, distribution, and rotation."]},{"title":"Detect changed message content","code":"import hmac\nkey = b\"demo-only-key\"\nfirst = hmac.digest(key, b\"amount=10\", \"sha256\")\nchanged = hmac.digest(key, b\"amount=11\", \"sha256\")\nprint(hmac.compare_digest(first, changed))","output":"False","explanation":["Changing the message changes the tag even though the key and algorithm remain the same. Verification binds the authenticated bytes to the key.","Both values are byte strings of the same type. Applications must also agree on exact serialization so they authenticate the same logical message representation."]},{"title":"Update an authentication tag incrementally","code":"import hmac\nkey = b\"demo-only-key\"\ntagger = hmac.new(key, digestmod=\"sha256\")\ntagger.update(b\"hello\")\ntagger.update(b\" world\")\nprint(hmac.compare_digest(tagger.digest(), hmac.digest(key, b\"hello world\", \"sha256\")))","output":"True","explanation":["Incremental updates authenticate the concatenated bytes, just as a one-shot call would. A chunk boundary is not part of the message.","Streaming computation avoids loading an entire message at once. Distinct logical fields still need unambiguous framing in the surrounding protocol."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":177,"title":"What does Base64 encoding do, and why is it not encryption?","label":"Base64 & binary-to-text encoding","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nBase64 encodes binary data using a text-friendly alphabet. Standard Base64 and URL-safe Base64 use different characters for two alphabet positions. Encoding is reversible without a secret, so it provides neither confidentiality nor authentication. Decoding text should follow the input format’s rules, including any padding requirements.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse Base64 when a transport supports text but must carry binary data. Choose the alphabet and validation behavior required by the protocol.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nNever treat an encoded password or token as encrypted. By default some decoding modes are permissive; request strict validation when the format requires rejecting unrelated characters.","summary":"Base64 makes bytes easier to transport as text. Anyone can decode it, so it is encoding rather than secrecy.","examples":[{"title":"Round-trip binary bytes","code":"import base64\nencoded = base64.b64encode(b\"Python\")\nprint(encoded.decode(\"ascii\"))\nprint(base64.b64decode(encoded))","output":"UHl0aG9u\nb'Python'","explanation":["The encoded bytes contain only the Base64 text alphabet. Decoding them reconstructs the original byte sequence exactly.","decode(\"ascii\") is used only to present the encoded text. It does not change the original binary content or add any security protection."]},{"title":"Compare standard and URL-safe alphabets","code":"import base64\ndata = bytes([251, 255])\nprint(base64.b64encode(data).decode(\"ascii\"))\nprint(base64.urlsafe_b64encode(data).decode(\"ascii\"))","output":"+/8=\n-_8=","explanation":["The URL-safe alphabet replaces plus and slash with minus and underscore. Both outputs encode the same two input bytes.","The equals sign is padding for this input size. Keep or remove padding only according to the transport format’s documented rules."]},{"title":"Reject an invalid character explicitly","code":"import base64\nimport binascii\ntry:\n    base64.b64decode(\"@@@\", validate=True)\nexcept binascii.Error:\n    print(\"invalid Base64\")\nprint(base64.b64decode(\"YQ==\", validate=True))","output":"invalid Base64\nb'a'","explanation":["Strict validation rejects the at-sign characters instead of treating them as ignorable material around the encoded content.","A valid encoded value decodes to bytes. Converting those bytes into text requires a separate encoding decision when the application expects text."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":178,"title":"How do urllib.parse helpers separate URL structure from query encoding?","label":"URL parsing & query parameters","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nurllib.parse separates URLs into structural components and encodes query parameters using defined escaping rules. urlsplit identifies scheme, network location, path, query, and fragment; urlencode builds query text from values. Parsing is structural processing, not a guarantee that a URL is safe, reachable, or appropriate for an application.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse these helpers rather than concatenating unescaped parameter strings. Preserve repeated query parameters when the endpoint defines them, and validate allowed schemes and destinations separately.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nurljoin can let an absolute second URL replace the base host. Do not treat it as a security boundary for untrusted path input.","summary":"Parse URLs into components and encode parameters with a query encoder. Structural parsing does not validate trust or constrain the destination.","examples":[{"title":"Build and parse query values","code":"from urllib.parse import urlencode, parse_qs\nquery = urlencode({\"q\": \"python tips\", \"tag\": [\"a\", \"b\"]}, doseq=True)\nprint(query)\nprint(parse_qs(query))","output":"q=python+tips&tag=a&tag=b\n{'q': ['python tips'], 'tag': ['a', 'b']}","explanation":["urlencode escapes the space and expands the tag sequence because doseq is enabled. Repeated keys remain separate query entries.","parse_qs returns lists of values for each key, preserving the possibility that a parameter appears more than once."]},{"title":"Separate structural URL components","code":"from urllib.parse import urlsplit\nparts = urlsplit(\"https://example.com:8443/docs?q=py#intro\")\nprint(parts.scheme, parts.hostname, parts.port)\nprint(parts.path, parts.query, parts.fragment)","output":"https example.com 8443\n/docs q=py intro","explanation":["urlsplit identifies components without making a network request. hostname and port are convenient interpretations of the network location.","The query and fragment remain text components. Application-specific validation is still required before using a parsed URL as a request target."]},{"title":"Understand how joining resolves targets","code":"from urllib.parse import urljoin\nbase = \"https://example.com/docs/page\"\nprint(urljoin(base, \"../help\"))\nprint(urljoin(base, \"https://other.example/path\"))","output":"https://example.com/help\nhttps://other.example/path","explanation":["The relative path is resolved against the base URL’s directory, with the parent segment moving up one path level.","An absolute second URL supplies its own scheme and host. Validate or constrain untrusted targets rather than assuming the base host will always be retained."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":179,"title":"How do statistics helpers distinguish central tendency and spread?","label":"Descriptive statistics in Python","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nstatistics provides basic numerical summaries such as mean, median, multimode, and measures of variance. Mean uses all values arithmetically, while median selects the middle of the ordered data. pvariance treats the input as the population; variance estimates population variance from a sample using a different denominator.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse these functions for small descriptive summaries when their definitions match the data and question. Check missing values, units, outliers, and whether the data represents a sample or a whole population.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA mean can hide skew and outliers. Sample variance needs at least two values, and numerical summaries do not by themselves justify causal or statistical conclusions.","summary":"Choose a summary by its definition: mean averages, median finds the center, and sample and population variance use different assumptions.","examples":[{"title":"Compare mean with median","code":"from statistics import mean, median\nvalues = [1, 2, 3, 100]\nprint(mean(values))\nprint(median(values))","output":"26.5\n2.5","explanation":["The mean sums all four values and divides by four, so the unusually large final value strongly affects it.","The median averages the two central ordered values because the input has an even number of members. It answers a different question about the distribution."]},{"title":"Choose population or sample variance","code":"from statistics import pvariance, variance\nvalues = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(pvariance(values))\nprint(variance(values))","output":"0.6666666666666666\n1","explanation":["The squared deviations sum to two. Population variance divides by three because these values are treated as the entire population.","Sample variance divides by two for the sample estimate. Selecting the function should follow the intended interpretation rather than whichever result looks preferable."]},{"title":"Preserve more than one mode","code":"from statistics import multimode, StatisticsError, mean\nprint(multimode([\"a\", \"b\", \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]))\ntry:\n    mean([])\nexcept StatisticsError:\n    print(\"no values to summarize\")","output":"['a', 'b']\nno values to summarize","explanation":["Both a and b appear twice, so multimode retains both equally frequent modes in their first-encountered order.","An empty dataset has no arithmetic mean. Catching the documented exception lets the application distinguish missing data from a numerical result such as zero."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":180,"title":"How should math.isclose compare approximate floating-point results?","label":"Approximate numeric comparisons","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nmath.isclose compares two finite values using relative and absolute tolerances. Relative tolerance scales with the larger magnitude, while absolute tolerance allows a fixed difference that is especially useful near zero. Tolerances are part of the domain’s definition of an acceptable error, not a universal replacement for exact equality.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse approximate comparisons for computed measurements or floating-point calculations when a justified error allowance is appropriate. Keep exact comparisons for exact discrete values and choose Decimal or Fraction when their arithmetic models fit the problem.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nNear zero, relative tolerance alone can reject a small nonzero result. NaN is not close to any value, including itself; infinity is close only to the same infinity.","summary":"isclose asks whether an error fits your chosen tolerances. Relative tolerance scales with magnitude; absolute tolerance handles small values near zero.","examples":[{"title":"Compare a floating-point sum","code":"from math import isclose\nvalue = 0.1 + 0.2\nprint(value == 0.3)\nprint(isclose(value, 0.3))","output":"False\nTrue","explanation":["Binary floating-point cannot exactly represent these decimal fractions, so the computed sum differs slightly from the stored representation of 0.3.","The default closeness test accepts this tiny representation difference. Whether those tolerances are appropriate still depends on the application’s required accuracy."]},{"title":"Set an absolute tolerance near zero","code":"from math import isclose\nprint(isclose(1e-12, 0.0))\nprint(isclose(1e-12, 0.0, abs_tol=1e-10))\nprint(isclose(100.0, 100.05, rel_tol=0.001))","output":"False\nTrue\nTrue","explanation":["Relative tolerance alone scales to the tiny nonzero input and is too small here. An absolute tolerance permits a fixed error around zero.","The final comparison allows roughly a tenth of a percent relative error. Choose such a threshold using the meaning and units of the measurements."]},{"title":"Handle special floating-point values","code":"from math import isclose, inf, nan\nprint(isclose(inf, inf))\nprint(isclose(inf, -inf))\nprint(isclose(nan, nan))","output":"True\nFalse\nFalse","explanation":["Equal infinities are treated as close, while opposite infinities are not. Finite error tolerances do not bridge an infinite difference.","NaN represents an unordered or invalid numerical result and does not compare close even to itself. Detect it explicitly when it requires separate handling."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":181,"title":"How can timeit measure repeated work without misleading example timings?","label":"Timeit & repeatable measurements","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ntimeit runs a statement or callable repeatedly and reports elapsed time. Setup work can be kept outside the timed statement, and repeat performs several batches. Timings vary with hardware, system load, interpreter, and data, so report the workload and environment rather than treating a single duration as a universal ranking.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse timeit for small controlled performance experiments after verifying correctness. Include realistic input sizes and decide whether setup, allocation, and cleanup belong in the measurement.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA callable passed to timeit may mutate state each time it runs. Also note that timeit normally disables garbage collection during timing unless the setup explicitly enables it.","summary":"timeit repeats a defined workload and measures duration. Control the workload and state, and interpret timings as environment-dependent observations.","examples":[{"title":"Count the timed calls","code":"from timeit import timeit\ncalls = []\nelapsed = timeit(lambda: calls.append(1), number=5)\nprint(len(calls))\nprint(elapsed >= 0)","output":"5\nTrue","explanation":["number=5 invokes the supplied callable five times. The list records those calls so the example verifies behavior without asserting a particular speed.","Elapsed duration depends on the current environment. Only its nonnegative shape is printed, avoiding an output that would change on each run."]},{"title":"Separate setup from a repeated statement","code":"from timeit import Timer\nvalues = [1, 2, 3]\ntimer = Timer(\"sum(values)\", globals={\"values\": values})\nresults = timer.repeat(repeat=3, number=10)\nprint(len(results))\nprint(all(value >= 0 for value in results))\nprint(values)","output":"3\nTrue\n[1, 2, 3]","explanation":["repeat returns three elapsed measurements, each for a batch of ten executions of the sum statement. The globals mapping supplies its input.","sum does not modify values, so each repetition sees the same data. This example checks the measurement structure rather than claiming a performance improvement."]},{"title":"Avoid a changing workload by copying","code":"from timeit import timeit\noriginal = [3, 1, 2]\ndef sort_copy():\n    data = original.copy()\n    data.sort()\nelapsed = timeit(sort_copy, number=4)\nprint(original)\nprint(elapsed >= 0)","output":"[3, 1, 2]\nTrue","explanation":["Each call copies the same unsorted source before sorting. Repetitions therefore do not silently become an already-sorted in-place workload.","The copy cost is included in this measurement. Excluding it would require a different experimental design, so describe exactly which operation is being timed."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":182,"title":"When should pprint format nested data instead of plain print?","label":"Pretty-printing nested structures","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\npprint formats Python object representations using indentation and line breaks intended to improve readability. pprint prints directly, while pformat returns the formatted string. Parameters control width, depth, compactness, and dictionary sorting. This is a diagnostic representation rather than a general interchange format.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse pprint when inspecting nested lists and mappings during development. Use JSON or another explicit serialization format when another program must consume the output.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA depth limit hides detail rather than removing it from the original object. Pretty-printing sensitive data can still expose secrets, and arbitrary object representations need not be valid Python literals.","summary":"pprint makes nested Python representations readable. pformat returns the text, and formatting options affect display rather than stored data.","examples":[{"title":"Choose dictionary display order","code":"from pprint import pformat\ndata = {\"z\": 1, \"a\": 2}\nprint(pformat(data))\nprint(pformat(data, sort_dicts=False))","output":"{'a': 2, 'z': 1}\n{'z': 1, 'a': 2}","explanation":["pformat sorts dictionary keys by default when possible, producing a diagnostic order that can differ from insertion order.","Disabling dictionary sorting preserves this mapping’s insertion order in the representation. Neither call changes the underlying dictionary."]},{"title":"Limit nested display depth","code":"from pprint import pformat\ndata = {\"a\": {\"b\": {\"c\": 1}}}\nprint(pformat(data, depth=2))\nprint(data[\"a\"][\"b\"][\"c\"])","output":"{'a': {'b': {...}}}\n1","explanation":["The depth setting abbreviates the deeper dictionary as an ellipsis-containing representation, keeping the outer structure readable.","The original nested value is still present and accessible. A display limit is not a transformation or deletion of the stored data."]},{"title":"Format a self-referential container","code":"from pprint import pformat\nvalues = []\nvalues.append(values)\ntext = pformat(values)\nprint(\"Recursion on list\" in text)\nprint(isinstance(text, str))","output":"True\nTrue","explanation":["The list contains itself, so expanding it recursively without detection would never finish. pprint emits a recursion marker instead.","The marker can include a process-specific object identifier. Checking its stable descriptive portion avoids relying on that changing identifier."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":183,"title":"How do textwrap helpers handle indentation, wrapping, and shortening?","label":"Text wrapping & indentation","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ntextwrap provides tools for display-oriented text layout. dedent removes a common leading whitespace margin, indent prefixes selected lines, fill wraps words into one string, and shorten collapses whitespace and truncates at word boundaries with a placeholder. These operations return new strings.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse textwrap for command-line help, readable embedded templates, and plain-text summaries. Choose widths appropriate to the destination rather than treating character counts as a full terminal-layout engine.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\ndedent removes a shared margin rather than every leading space. Wrapping does not parse Markdown or preserve all semantic formatting, and Unicode display width can differ from string length.","summary":"textwrap adjusts plain-text layout. Remove shared margins, add prefixes, wrap words, or shorten a preview without changing the original string.","examples":[{"title":"Remove the shared indentation margin","code":"from textwrap import dedent\ntext = \"    alpha\\n      beta\\n    gamma\"\nprint(dedent(text))","output":"alpha\n  beta\ngamma","explanation":["All three lines share a four-space left margin, so dedent removes those four spaces from each line.","The two extra spaces on beta remain because they represent indentation relative to the common margin rather than part of that shared margin."]},{"title":"Wrap a short paragraph","code":"from textwrap import fill, indent\nwrapped = fill(\"one two three four\", width=9)\nprint(wrapped)\nprint(indent(\"a\\nb\", \"> \"))","output":"one two\nthree\nfour\n> a\n> b","explanation":["fill chooses word boundaries so each displayed line fits the requested width when ordinary word-breaking rules permit it.","indent prefixes the nonempty lines of its input with the requested marker, which is useful when rendering quoted or nested plain text."]},{"title":"Shorten a preview at a word boundary","code":"from textwrap import shorten\ntext = \"  one   two three four  \"\nprint(shorten(text, width=12, placeholder=\"...\"))\nprint(repr(text[:2]))","output":"one two...\n'  '","explanation":["shorten first collapses whitespace, then retains complete words that fit together with the placeholder inside the chosen width.","The original string still begins with two spaces. These helpers return formatted results rather than rewriting the input string in place."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":184,"title":"How does argparse turn command-line text into validated program options?","label":"Argparse & command-line interfaces","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nargparse describes accepted positional arguments and options, parses a list of strings, and produces a Namespace of values. You can define conversions, defaults, choices, flags, and subcommands. A parser normally reads process arguments, but an explicit list makes examples and tests independent of how the script was launched.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse argparse for command-line tools that need consistent help and input handling. Keep parsing near the entry point and pass the resulting values into ordinary testable functions.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\ntype=bool does not implement the usual yes/no flag behavior because nonempty strings are truthy. Use suitable actions such as store_true or an explicit boolean conversion.","summary":"argparse defines a command-line contract and returns parsed values. Use explicit types and actions, then keep application logic separate from argument parsing.","examples":[{"title":"Parse a typed option and a flag","code":"import argparse\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\nparser.add_argument(\"--count\", type=int, default=1)\nparser.add_argument(\"--verbose\", action=\"store_true\")\nargs = parser.parse_args([\"--count\", \"3\", \"--verbose\"])\nprint(args.count, args.verbose)","output":"3 True","explanation":["The count text is converted to an integer by the declared type. The presence of the verbose flag sets its boolean value to True.","Providing the argument list explicitly makes this snippet independent of the actual process command line and straightforward to test."]},{"title":"Collect positional values","code":"import argparse\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\nparser.add_argument(\"files\", nargs=\"+\")\nparser.add_argument(\"--mode\", choices=[\"fast\", \"safe\"], default=\"safe\")\nargs = parser.parse_args([\"a.txt\", \"b.txt\"])\nprint(args.files)\nprint(args.mode)","output":"['a.txt', 'b.txt']\nsafe","explanation":["nargs=\"+\" collects one or more positional values into a list. The two filenames therefore belong to a single parsed field.","The optional mode was omitted, so its explicit default applies. choices defines the accepted values when the caller supplies that option."]},{"title":"Separate subcommand behavior","code":"import argparse\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\ncommands = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", required=True)\nshow = commands.add_parser(\"show\")\nshow.add_argument(\"name\")\nargs = parser.parse_args([\"show\", \"report\"])\nprint(args.command, args.name)","output":"show report","explanation":["The first argument selects the show subparser, whose own contract then consumes the name argument. The chosen command is stored explicitly.","Subcommands allow related operations to share one executable while keeping their accepted arguments and help messages separated."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":185,"title":"How does ConfigParser read typed settings from INI-style configuration?","label":"INI configuration & typed getters","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nconfigparser reads INI-style sections and options. Stored values are strings, while getint, getfloat, and getboolean perform explicit conversions. DEFAULT values are available as fallbacks in sections. Basic interpolation can substitute other option values, and option names are case-insensitive under the default normalization.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse ConfigParser for human-editable settings with a section-and-option structure. Keep defaults, parsing, and domain validation explicit so a configuration error is distinguishable from a valid setting.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nbool(\"false\") is True because the string is nonempty. Use getboolean for supported boolean spellings. Disable interpolation when literal percent signs should be read unchanged.","summary":"INI options begin as text. Use typed getters, understand inherited defaults, and choose interpolation behavior deliberately.","examples":[{"title":"Convert numeric and boolean values","code":"import configparser\nconfig = configparser.ConfigParser()\nconfig.read_string(\"[app]\\nretries = 3\\ndebug = false\\n\")\nprint(config.getint(\"app\", \"retries\") + 1)\nprint(config.getboolean(\"app\", \"debug\"))\nprint(type(config[\"app\"][\"retries\"]).__name__)","output":"4\nFalse\nstr","explanation":["getint and getboolean apply explicit parsing rules. The false text becomes False rather than the truthiness of a nonempty Python string.","Direct section lookup still returns the stored string. Typed getters do not replace the underlying text with converted Python objects."]},{"title":"Inherit defaults and interpolate","code":"import configparser\nconfig = configparser.ConfigParser()\nconfig.read_string(\"[DEFAULT]\\nroot = /srv\\n[app]\\npath = %(root)s/data\\n\")\nprint(config[\"app\"][\"root\"])\nprint(config[\"app\"][\"path\"])\nprint(config.get(\"app\", \"missing\", fallback=\"none\"))","output":"/srv\n/srv/data\nnone","explanation":["The app section can read root through DEFAULT even though root is not declared directly in that section.","Basic interpolation inserts the resolved root into path. A missing requested option uses the explicit fallback rather than raising an error."]},{"title":"Preserve a literal percent sign","code":"import configparser\nconfig = configparser.ConfigParser(interpolation=None)\nconfig.read_string(\"[job]\\nProgress = 50%\\n\")\nprint(config[\"job\"][\"progress\"])\nprint(list(config[\"job\"]))","output":"50%\n['progress']","explanation":["Disabling interpolation lets the percentage text remain literal instead of being interpreted as interpolation syntax.","The default option-name transformation still normalizes Progress to progress. Section names and option names have distinct handling rules."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":186,"title":"How do temporary-file context managers define cleanup boundaries?","label":"Temporary files & resource lifetimes","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ntempfile creates temporary files and directories with managed names and cleanup behavior. TemporaryDirectory removes its directory tree on context exit. TemporaryFile supplies an open file-like object, while NamedTemporaryFile offers a filesystem name with platform-sensitive reopening and deletion considerations.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse temporary resources for isolated tests, intermediate artifacts, and operations that require a path without retaining permanent output. Use context managers to make the lifetime clear.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nOn Windows, reopening a still-open named temporary file can encounter sharing restrictions. Close it or use a suitable documented deletion strategy before another component needs to open its path.","summary":"A temporary resource is still a real file or directory. Use a context manager to define its lifetime and account for platform-specific reopening rules.","examples":[{"title":"Clean up an isolated directory","code":"from tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwith TemporaryDirectory() as folder:\n    path = Path(folder) / \"note.txt\"\n    path.write_text(\"hello\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    print(path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    print(path.exists())\nprint(path.exists())","output":"hello\nTrue\nFalse","explanation":["The file exists while its TemporaryDirectory context is active. Explicit UTF-8 encoding makes the text round trip independent of platform defaults.","Leaving the context removes the temporary directory and its contents. Keeping a Path object does not keep the referenced file alive."]},{"title":"Rewind an open temporary stream","code":"from tempfile import TemporaryFile\nwith TemporaryFile(mode=\"w+t\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as stream:\n    stream.write(\"alpha\\nbeta\")\n    stream.seek(0)\n    print(stream.readline().strip())\n    print(stream.read())\nprint(stream.closed)","output":"alpha\nbeta\nTrue","explanation":["After writing, the cursor is at the end. seek(0) moves it back so the subsequent reads can retrieve the stored text.","The context closes the stream on exit. A temporary file’s cursor and closed state follow normal file-object rules."]},{"title":"Reopen a named file after closing it","code":"from tempfile import NamedTemporaryFile\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwith NamedTemporaryFile(mode=\"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\", delete=False) as stream:\n    stream.write(\"saved\")\n    path = Path(stream.name)\ntry:\n    print(path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\nfinally:\n    path.unlink()\nprint(path.exists())","output":"saved\nFalse","explanation":["delete=False keeps the named file available after its creation stream closes, avoiding an open-handle reopening conflict in this example.","The finally block supplies explicit deletion because automatic deletion was disabled. It still runs if reading the temporary file fails."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":187,"title":"How do SQLite parameters and transactions separate data from SQL commands?","label":"SQLite parameters & transactions","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nsqlite3 provides a database interface to SQLite. Parameter placeholders bind values separately from SQL text, avoiding manual quoting of those values. Transactions group related changes so they can be committed together or rolled back. A connection context manager manages a pending transaction; it does not itself close the connection.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse SQLite for local structured storage and small application databases. Define constraints and use bound parameters for values; validate any dynamic table or column identifiers separately.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nDo not build SQL by interpolating untrusted values. Also do not assume that leaving with connection closes the connection: close it explicitly or use a closing context manager.","summary":"Bind SQL values as parameters and group related writes in a transaction. Commit keeps changes, rollback cancels them, and connection closure is separate.","examples":[{"title":"Bind a value containing a quote","code":"import sqlite3\nfrom contextlib import closing\nwith closing(sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")) as db:\n    db.execute(\"CREATE TABLE people (name TEXT)\")\n    db.execute(\"INSERT INTO people VALUES (?)\", (\"O'Reilly\",))\n    print(db.execute(\"SELECT name FROM people\").fetchone()[0])","output":"O'Reilly","explanation":["The question-mark placeholder receives a separate parameter tuple, so the apostrophe is stored as data rather than interpreted as SQL syntax.","The closing context manager explicitly closes the connection. This in-memory database is isolated to that connection and disappears when it closes."]},{"title":"Roll back a failed group of writes","code":"import sqlite3\nfrom contextlib import closing\nwith closing(sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")) as db:\n    db.execute(\"CREATE TABLE items (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY)\")\n    try:\n        with db:\n            db.execute(\"INSERT INTO items VALUES (?)\", (1,))\n            db.execute(\"INSERT INTO items VALUES (?)\", (1,))\n    except sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n        print(\"rolled back\")\n    print(db.execute(\"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM items\").fetchone()[0])","output":"rolled back\n0","explanation":["The duplicate primary key raises IntegrityError during the transaction. The connection context rolls back the pending first insertion as well.","The table exists outside this group of inserts, so it remains available for the count query. The transaction left it with no committed rows."]},{"title":"Read named columns with a row factory","code":"import sqlite3\nfrom contextlib import closing\nwith closing(sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")) as db:\n    db.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n    row = db.execute(\"SELECT ? AS name, ? AS score\", (\"Ada\", 9)).fetchone()\n    print(row[\"name\"], row[\"score\"])\n    print(row.keys())","output":"Ada 9\n['name', 'score']","explanation":["The Row factory creates result objects that support column-name access as well as positional access. SQL aliases define the names here.","Bound parameters provide the values while the fixed query specifies the structure. Row formatting does not alter the database’s transaction behavior."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":188,"title":"How can subprocess.run launch a child program with explicit arguments and captured output?","label":"Subprocess arguments & result handling","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nsubprocess.run starts a child process, waits for it, and returns a CompletedProcess. An argument list with shell=False passes values as arguments without shell expansion. capture_output retains standard output and standard error, text enables decoding, and check=True raises CalledProcessError for a nonzero exit code.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse subprocess when integrating a real external executable. Specify the intended executable, argument boundaries, encoding, timeouts, and error handling rather than relying on a shell command string.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nshell=False avoids shell interpretation but does not make every argument safe for every executable. An external program still interprets its own options; validate inputs according to that program’s contract.","summary":"Pass an explicit argument list and inspect the child result. Capture output and handle nonzero exits deliberately instead of assuming every launch succeeds.","examples":[{"title":"Capture output from a child interpreter","code":"import subprocess\nimport sys\nresult = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", \"print(2 + 3)\"], capture_output=True, text=True, encoding=\"utf-8\", check=True)\nprint(result.stdout.strip())\nprint(result.returncode)\nprint(result.stderr == \"\")","output":"5\n0\nTrue","explanation":["The current interpreter executable runs a small child program. Captured output belongs to the result object instead of being printed directly by the parent.","check=True permits this successful zero exit status but would raise for a failing child. The chosen encoding states how captured bytes become text."]},{"title":"Preserve an argument literally","code":"import subprocess\nimport sys\ncode = \"import sys; print(sys.argv[1])\"\nresult = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", code, \"a b; c\"], capture_output=True, text=True, encoding=\"utf-8\", check=True)\nprint(result.stdout.strip())","output":"a b; c","explanation":["The final list element is one argument even though it contains spaces and a semicolon. No shell is interpreting those characters.","The child receives the exact supplied text as sys.argv[1]. Its own option and input semantics still need consideration in a real integration."]},{"title":"Handle a nonzero exit code","code":"import subprocess\nimport sys\ntry:\n    subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", \"raise SystemExit(4)\"], check=True, capture_output=True)\nexcept subprocess.CalledProcessError as error:\n    print(error.returncode)","output":"4","explanation":["The child exits deliberately with status 4. Because check=True was requested, run raises CalledProcessError instead of silently returning a failed result.","The exception exposes the return code for application-specific handling. Captured output can also be retained for diagnosis when the child provides it."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":189,"title":"How can zipfile create and read an archive without extracting it?","label":"ZIP archives & in-memory files","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nzipfile reads and writes ZIP archives and can operate on file paths or seekable binary streams such as BytesIO. writestr adds an entry from supplied content, namelist lists entry names, and read retrieves an entry’s bytes. Opening an archive with a context manager finalizes or closes it at the intended boundary.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse in-memory archives for generated downloads and tests, and inspect individual members without extracting an entire archive. Treat untrusted archives as inputs requiring resource and path validation.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nCompressed size can hide a very large expanded payload. Avoid extracting untrusted content without bounds and destination checks, and do not confuse an archive entry name with a trusted filesystem path.","summary":"ZIP stores named entries. You can inspect and read those entries directly, while extraction adds separate path and resource considerations.","examples":[{"title":"Create an archive in memory","code":"from io import BytesIO\nfrom zipfile import ZipFile\nbuffer = BytesIO()\nwith ZipFile(buffer, \"w\") as archive:\n    archive.writestr(\"hello.txt\", \"Hello\")\nbuffer.seek(0)\nwith ZipFile(buffer) as archive:\n    print(archive.namelist())\n    print(archive.read(\"hello.txt\").decode(\"utf-8\"))","output":"['hello.txt']\nHello","explanation":["writestr adds a named member, and closing the writing archive finalizes its directory information before it is reopened for reading.","Reading a member returns bytes. Decoding with UTF-8 recovers the text without extracting the member into a real filesystem directory."]},{"title":"Inspect sizes and integrity","code":"from io import BytesIO\nfrom zipfile import ZipFile, ZIP_DEFLATED\nbuffer = BytesIO()\nwith ZipFile(buffer, \"w\", compression=ZIP_DEFLATED) as archive:\n    archive.writestr(\"data.txt\", \"A\" * 1000)\nbuffer.seek(0)\nwith ZipFile(buffer) as archive:\n    info = archive.getinfo(\"data.txt\")\n    print(info.file_size)\n    print(info.compress_size < info.file_size)\n    print(archive.testzip() is None)","output":"1000\nTrue\nTrue","explanation":["Repeated A characters compress well, so this member occupies fewer compressed bytes than its one-thousand-byte uncompressed content.","testzip checks member checksums and returns None here. Integrity checking is useful but does not authenticate the archive or enforce safe expansion limits."]},{"title":"Select a member without extraction","code":"from io import BytesIO\nfrom zipfile import ZipFile\nbuffer = BytesIO()\nwith ZipFile(buffer, \"w\") as archive:\n    archive.writestr(\"docs/a.txt\", \"alpha\")\n    archive.writestr(\"docs/b.txt\", \"beta\")\nbuffer.seek(0)\nwith ZipFile(buffer) as archive:\n    names = sorted(name for name in archive.namelist() if name.endswith(\"a.txt\"))\n    print(names)\n    print(archive.read(names[0]).decode(\"utf-8\"))","output":"['docs/a.txt']\nalpha","explanation":["Member names can contain directory-like segments without creating directories on disk. The code filters names before reading the chosen member.","No extraction is performed, so the example creates no output files. For untrusted inputs, also limit member sizes and the amount of content read."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":190,"title":"How does singledispatchmethod choose method behavior from the first non-self argument?","label":"Single dispatch for methods","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nfunctools.singledispatchmethod turns a method into a dispatcher based on the runtime type of its first argument after self or cls. Registered implementations specialize supported types, and the generic method supplies a fallback. Matching can use an applicable superclass registration when an exact type is not registered.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse it for one conceptual operation that needs type-specific behavior in a class. Keep explicit branching when dispatch depends on several values or on business rules beyond a single argument type.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nIt is not dispatch on every argument or on the return annotation. When combining it with classmethod, singledispatchmethod must remain the outer decorator to expose register.","summary":"singledispatchmethod picks an implementation using the first non-self argument’s type. Register special cases and keep a deliberate generic fallback.","examples":[{"title":"Register integer and string behavior","code":"from functools import singledispatchmethod\nclass Formatter:\n    @singledispatchmethod\n    def show(self, value):\n        return \"other\"\n    @show.register(int)\n    def _(self, value):\n        return f\"integer:{value}\"\n    @show.register(str)\n    def _(self, value):\n        return value.upper()\nf = Formatter()\nprint(f.show(3))\nprint(f.show(\"python\"))\nprint(f.show(1.5))","output":"integer:3\nPYTHON\nother","explanation":["The runtime types int and str select their registered method bodies. The float has no matching specialized registration and uses the generic fallback.","self is not the dispatch value. The same Formatter instance handles all three requests without changing its class or the method name."]},{"title":"Reject unsupported values intentionally","code":"from functools import singledispatchmethod\nclass Doubler:\n    @singledispatchmethod\n    def apply(self, value):\n        raise TypeError(\"unsupported value\")\n    @apply.register(list)\n    def _(self, value):\n        return value + value\nd = Doubler()\nprint(d.apply([1, 2]))\ntry:\n    d.apply(None)\nexcept TypeError as error:\n    print(error)","output":"[1, 2, 1, 2]\nunsupported value","explanation":["The list registration creates a concatenated list. The fallback explicitly rejects unsupported types instead of silently manufacturing a default answer.","A deliberate fallback contract makes type-specific APIs easier to use. Registered methods should still express the same overall conceptual operation."]},{"title":"Specialize bool separately from int","code":"from functools import singledispatchmethod\nclass Kind:\n    @singledispatchmethod\n    def name(self, value):\n        return \"other\"\n    @name.register(int)\n    def _(self, value):\n        return \"integer\"\n    @name.register(bool)\n    def _(self, value):\n        return \"boolean\"\nk = Kind()\nprint(k.name(True))\nprint(k.name(7))","output":"boolean\ninteger","explanation":["bool is a subclass of int, but its more specific registration takes precedence for True. Ordinary integers still use the int implementation.","Subclass relationships affect dispatch. Register bool explicitly when boolean meaning must differ from the treatment of numerical integer values."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":191,"title":"How do dataclasses.replace and asdict handle copying and derived fields?","label":"Dataclass replacement & conversion","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ndataclasses.replace constructs another instance using the dataclass constructor with selected field changes, so `__post_init__` runs again. It does not automatically deep-copy unchanged mutable fields. asdict recursively converts dataclass instances, mappings, lists, and tuples, using deep copying for other values. These operations serve different purposes.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse replace for a modified record while preserving the original record’s field bindings. Use asdict when a recursive dictionary representation is appropriate, while checking whether its copying behavior and resulting types match the next consumer.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nreplace can share mutable fields with the source. asdict is not a promise of JSON compatibility, and init=False fields are not constructor overrides for replace.","summary":"replace creates a new dataclass instance with chosen changes; unchanged nested objects may be shared. asdict recursively converts and copies for a dictionary representation.","examples":[{"title":"Replace one field on an immutable record","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass, replace\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Point:\n    x: int\n    y: int\nfirst = Point(1, 2)\nsecond = replace(first, y=9)\nprint(first)\nprint(second)\nprint(first is second)","output":"Point(x=1, y=2)\nPoint(x=1, y=9)\nFalse","explanation":["replace supplies the unchanged x value and the requested new y value to a new Point construction. The original frozen instance stays unchanged.","The result is a separate object. A frozen outer record prevents ordinary field reassignment but does not make every possible nested value immutable."]},{"title":"Compare shared replacement with converted output","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass, replace, asdict\n@dataclass\nclass Record:\n    tags: list\noriginal = Record([\"python\"])\nchanged = replace(original)\nconverted = asdict(original)\nchanged.tags.append(\"code\")\nprint(original.tags)\nprint(converted[\"tags\"])","output":"['python', 'code']\n['python']","explanation":["The replacement receives the original tags list because no different value was supplied. Mutating that list is visible through both instances.","asdict constructed an independent list representation before the mutation, so its output does not gain the later code element."]},{"title":"Recompute an init-false derived field","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass, field, replace\n@dataclass\nclass Line:\n    price: int\n    quantity: int\n    total: int = field(init=False)\n    def __post_init__(self):\n        self.total = self.price * self.quantity\nfirst = Line(3, 2)\nsecond = replace(first, quantity=4)\nprint(first.total, second.total)","output":"6 12","explanation":["The total field is excluded from constructor arguments and computed by __post_init__. Initial construction computes 3 times 2.","replace constructs a new instance with quantity 4, so initialization recomputes the derived total as 12 rather than copying the earlier total blindly."]}],"batch":"latest-36","minutes":2},{"id":192,"title":"How does tomllib read TOML configuration into Python values?","label":"TOML configuration with tomllib","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ntomllib parses TOML configuration into dictionaries and native Python values. Tables become nested mappings, arrays become lists, and supported scalar types include strings, booleans, integers, floats, and date/time values. loads accepts text, while load expects a binary file object. The standard-library module reads TOML but does not write it.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse TOML for structured human-edited configuration and inspect pyproject.toml with the same parser. Validate the resulting keys and domain constraints after parsing succeeds.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nSuccessful parsing establishes TOML syntax, not the validity of application settings. Open files in binary mode for load, and do not assume tomllib provides a dump function.","summary":"tomllib reads TOML into Python values. Parse first, then validate your application’s required settings; use binary files with load.","examples":[{"title":"Parse tables and typed values","code":"import tomllib\nconfig = tomllib.loads('[app]\\nname = \"Lab\"\\nworkers = 3\\ndebug = false\\n')\nprint(config[\"app\"][\"name\"])\nprint(config[\"app\"][\"workers\"] + 1)\nprint(config[\"app\"][\"debug\"])","output":"Lab\n4\nFalse","explanation":["The app table becomes a nested dictionary. The integer and boolean are converted to their corresponding Python types rather than left as strings.","Application code can use those parsed types directly, but should still verify that worker counts and other values satisfy its own constraints."]},{"title":"Load a binary stream","code":"import tomllib\nfrom io import BytesIO\nstream = BytesIO(b'tags = [\"python\", \"practice\"]\\n[limits]\\nretries = 2\\n')\nconfig = tomllib.load(stream)\nprint(config[\"tags\"])\nprint(config[\"limits\"][\"retries\"])","output":"['python', 'practice']\n2","explanation":["load receives a binary stream and handles the TOML text encoding. A real configuration file would similarly be opened with mode rb.","The tags array and limits table remain separate structured values, avoiding the need to manually split strings to reconstruct lists or nested settings."]},{"title":"Catch invalid TOML syntax","code":"import tomllib\ntry:\n    tomllib.loads(\"count = [1, 2\")\nexcept tomllib.TOMLDecodeError:\n    print(\"invalid TOML\")\nprint(tomllib.loads(\"count = 0\")[\"count\"])","output":"invalid TOML\n0","explanation":["The unfinished array violates TOML syntax, so the parser raises TOMLDecodeError before returning a configuration dictionary.","The second document is syntactically valid even though an application might reject a zero count. Syntax validation and domain validation are separate steps."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2},{"id":193,"title":"How does ipaddress validate addresses and reason about networks?","label":"IP addresses & network membership","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nipaddress provides value objects for IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, interfaces, and networks. Factories parse text into the appropriate address family. Network objects support address membership, prefix information, and iteration helpers. They reason about numeric addresses without contacting a network or resolving hostnames.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse these objects to validate address syntax and compare network ranges. Treat address parsing as one step in a larger networking policy rather than evidence that a destination is reachable or trusted.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nip_network normally rejects host bits in a strict network specification. Use strict=False only when intentionally normalizing an address to its containing network, and define policies for both IPv4 and IPv6.","summary":"ipaddress turns address text into structured numeric values. Network membership is a local calculation, not a connectivity test.","examples":[{"title":"Check address family and membership","code":"from ipaddress import ip_address, ip_network\naddress = ip_address(\"192.0.2.10\")\nnetwork = ip_network(\"192.0.2.0/24\")\nprint(address.version)\nprint(address in network)\nprint(ip_address(\"192.0.3.1\") in network)","output":"4\nTrue\nFalse","explanation":["The parsed address is IPv4. Comparing it with a network checks whether its numeric value falls within that network’s prefix range.","The checks make no network requests. The final address is outside the selected /24 prefix even though much of its text looks similar."]},{"title":"Normalize an interface address intentionally","code":"from ipaddress import ip_network, ip_interface\nnetwork = ip_network(\"192.0.2.17/24\", strict=False)\ninterface = ip_interface(\"192.0.2.17/24\")\nprint(network)\nprint(interface.ip)\nprint(interface.network)","output":"192.0.2.0/24\n192.0.2.17\n192.0.2.0/24","explanation":["With strict=False, host bits are masked out to produce the containing network. The original host address is not retained in the network object.","An interface object preserves both the host address and its network. Choose the object type that represents the information the application needs."]},{"title":"Inspect a small network and invalid input","code":"from ipaddress import ip_network, ip_address\nnetwork = ip_network(\"192.0.2.0/30\")\nprint([str(address) for address in network.hosts()])\ntry:\n    ip_address(\"999.1.2.3\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"invalid address\")","output":"['192.0.2.1', '192.0.2.2']\ninvalid address","explanation":["For this ordinary IPv4 /30 network, hosts excludes its network and broadcast addresses, leaving two usable host addresses.","The invalid octet is rejected during parsing. Avoid materializing every host of a very large network simply to test membership or inspect its size."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2},{"id":194,"title":"How do fnmatch patterns differ from regular expressions and filesystem searches?","label":"Filename patterns with fnmatch","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nfnmatch compares strings with shell-style patterns: * matches any sequence, ? matches one character, and bracket expressions select characters. fnmatchcase applies case-sensitive matching without platform case normalization. The functions match supplied names rather than walking a filesystem, and path separators do not have the special traversal role they have in glob.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse fnmatch for filtering a list of names or applying a simple wildcard policy. Use glob or Path.glob when you actually need filesystem traversal.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nA wildcard pattern is not a regular expression. Choose fnmatchcase for consistent case-sensitive behavior across platforms, and do not assume a filename pattern validates a safe filesystem destination.","summary":"fnmatch applies simple wildcards to strings you already have. fnmatchcase makes case sensitivity explicit; filesystem discovery is a separate task.","examples":[{"title":"Match extensions consistently","code":"from fnmatch import fnmatchcase\nnames = [\"a.py\", \"b.txt\", \"C.PY\", \"test.py\"]\nprint([name for name in names if fnmatchcase(name, \"*.py\")])","output":"['a.py', 'test.py']","explanation":["The star permits any leading sequence, but the literal extension must match case exactly because fnmatchcase is used.","This filters an existing list rather than finding files on disk. The mixed-case C.PY name is intentionally excluded by this policy."]},{"title":"Select a single digit position","code":"from fnmatch import fnmatchcase\nnames = [\"log1.txt\", \"log9.txt\", \"log12.txt\", \"logA.txt\"]\nprint([name for name in names if fnmatchcase(name, \"log[0-9].txt\")])\nprint(fnmatchcase(\"ab\", \"a?\"))","output":"['log1.txt', 'log9.txt']\nTrue","explanation":["The bracket range matches exactly one digit, so the two-digit name and the letter name do not satisfy the full pattern.","The question mark also matches exactly one character. It is not the regex optional-quantifier syntax, so wildcard and regex patterns should not be interchanged."]},{"title":"Understand slash handling","code":"from fnmatch import fnmatchcase\nprint(fnmatchcase(\"docs/a.txt\", \"*.txt\"))\nprint(fnmatchcase(\".hidden\", \"*\"))\nprint(fnmatchcase(\"notes.md\", \"*.txt\"))","output":"True\nTrue\nFalse","explanation":["fnmatch treats the slash as an ordinary character for matching, so the star can include the directory-like prefix in this string.","Leading dots also match normally. Filesystem glob tools can apply different traversal and hidden-name rules, so select the helper whose semantics match the task."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2},{"id":195,"title":"How do itertools product, permutations, and combinations count different selections?","label":"Combinatorial iterators","category":"Iteration","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nitertools.product enumerates Cartesian products, permutations enumerates ordered selections without reusing an input position, and combinations enumerates selections in input order without reusing positions. combinations_with_replacement allows a position to be selected again. These tools yield results incrementally, but consume and retain finite input pools.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse them for test cases, small search spaces, and candidate generation when the selection rules are clear. Estimate the output count before exploring a large space.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nThese functions distinguish input positions rather than deduplicating equal input values. Lazy output does not prevent a combinatorial explosion or make an infinite input pool suitable.","summary":"Choose whether order matters and whether reuse is allowed. Combinatorial iterators generate candidates lazily, but their total number can still be huge.","examples":[{"title":"Build a Cartesian product","code":"from itertools import product\nprint(list(product([\"A\", \"B\"], [1, 2])))\nprint(list(product([0, 1], repeat=2)))","output":"[('A', 1), ('A', 2), ('B', 1), ('B', 2)]\n[(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0), (1, 1)]","explanation":["product pairs every element of the first pool with every element of the second. The rightmost pool advances fastest.","repeat=2 uses the same pool in two independent positions, allowing repeated values such as the pair 1, 1."]},{"title":"Compare ordered and unordered selections","code":"from itertools import permutations, combinations\nprint(list(permutations(\"ABC\", 2)))\nprint(list(combinations(\"ABC\", 2)))","output":"[('A', 'B'), ('A', 'C'), ('B', 'A'), ('B', 'C'), ('C', 'A'), ('C', 'B')]\n[('A', 'B'), ('A', 'C'), ('B', 'C')]","explanation":["Permutations include both A,B and B,A because changing selection order produces a different ordered result.","Combinations preserve the input position order and choose each pair once. They do not sort the input values to establish that order."]},{"title":"Allow replacement and inspect repeated inputs","code":"from itertools import combinations, combinations_with_replacement\nprint(list(combinations_with_replacement(\"AB\", 2)))\nprint(list(combinations(\"AAB\", 2)))","output":"[('A', 'A'), ('A', 'B'), ('B', 'B')]\n[('A', 'A'), ('A', 'B'), ('A', 'B')]","explanation":["Allowing replacement permits selecting the same pool position more than once, which creates the A,A and B,B results.","The two A positions in the second input remain distinct positions. That is why the displayed A,B value pair occurs twice rather than being deduplicated."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2},{"id":196,"title":"What does pickle preserve when serializing trusted Python object graphs?","label":"Pickle & trusted object graphs","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\npickle serializes supported Python objects into a Python-specific binary representation and reconstructs them on loading. It can preserve shared references and cycles within a serialized object graph. It is not a safe parser for untrusted input: unpickling can execute arbitrary code. Use a safer data format such as JSON for simple untrusted interchange.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse pickle only when the data and its provenance are trusted and the Python-specific representation is appropriate. Plan for importable class definitions and compatibility when storing custom instances across program versions.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nNever load a pickle received from an untrusted party. Pickle bytes are not encrypted, and successful serialization does not guarantee compatibility with every Python version or future class definition.","summary":"Pickle can preserve a Python object graph, including sharing and cycles. Only unpickle trusted data; it is neither a secure interchange parser nor encryption.","examples":[{"title":"Round-trip built-in data","code":"import pickle\noriginal = {\"name\": \"Lab\", \"scores\": [2, 4]}\npayload = pickle.dumps(original)\nrestored = pickle.loads(payload)\nprint(restored == original)\nprint(restored is original)\nprint(isinstance(payload, bytes))","output":"True\nFalse\nTrue","explanation":["The payload is created locally from trusted data and then loaded immediately. The reconstructed mapping compares equal but is a different object.","Printing only stable properties avoids depending on a particular protocol’s byte layout. A serialized representation is a data format, not a security boundary."]},{"title":"Preserve a shared nested reference","code":"import pickle\nshared = [1]\noriginal = [shared, shared]\nrestored = pickle.loads(pickle.dumps(original))\nrestored[0].append(2)\nprint(restored)\nprint(restored[0] is restored[1])\nprint(shared)","output":"[[1, 2], [1, 2]]\nTrue\n[1]","explanation":["Pickle records the repeated reference within the graph, so both restored outer positions refer to the same reconstructed inner list.","The restored graph is separate from the original graph. Mutating its shared inner list does not change the original shared list."]},{"title":"Preserve a cycle in trusted data","code":"import pickle\noriginal = []\noriginal.append(original)\nrestored = pickle.loads(pickle.dumps(original))\nprint(restored[0] is restored)\nprint(restored is original)","output":"True\nFalse","explanation":["The graph contains a reference back to its own root. Pickle’s graph tracking reconstructs that relationship without expanding it forever.","The new root is distinct from the original root, even though the same cyclic shape is preserved. No externally supplied pickle is loaded in this example."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2},{"id":197,"title":"How does total_ordering derive comparisons from equality and one ordering method?","label":"Derived comparisons with total_ordering","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nfunctools.total_ordering supplies missing rich comparison methods when a class defines equality and at least one of less-than, less-than-or-equal, greater-than, or greater-than-or-equal. It reduces repetitive code but relies on the supplied methods expressing a consistent ordering. Unsupported operand types should normally return NotImplemented.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse it for small value classes where one ordering key defines a clear total order. Implement all methods explicitly if profiling shows the derived methods or stack traces are a meaningful cost.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nThe decorator does not make an inconsistent comparison rule mathematically valid. Defining equality also affects default hashability, so review the hash contract before using such objects as dictionary keys.","summary":"total_ordering derives missing comparisons from your existing rules. Define consistent equality and ordering, and return NotImplemented for unsupported types.","examples":[{"title":"Derive comparison operators for a value class","code":"from functools import total_ordering\n@total_ordering\nclass Score:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Score):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.value == other.value\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Score):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.value < other.value\nprint(Score(2) <= Score(2))\nprint(Score(3) > Score(2))\nprint(Score(1) >= Score(4))","output":"True\nTrue\nFalse","explanation":["The class provides equality and less-than directly. The decorator derives the missing operators using those supplied comparisons.","Every comparison uses the same numeric field, so the derived operators have a consistent interpretation across instances of this class."]},{"title":"Sort by a compound ordering key","code":"from functools import total_ordering\n@total_ordering\nclass Entry:\n    def __init__(self, priority, name):\n        self.key = (priority, name)\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Entry):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.key == other.key\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Entry):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.key < other.key\nitems = [Entry(2, \"b\"), Entry(1, \"z\"), Entry(2, \"a\")]\nprint([item.key for item in sorted(items)])","output":"[(1, 'z'), (2, 'a'), (2, 'b')]","explanation":["The tuple key compares priority first and name second, giving a deterministic tie-breaker when two entries share a priority.","sorted uses the class’s less-than rule. total_ordering is useful for the other rich comparisons but does not replace the need to design that rule."]},{"title":"Handle unrelated operand types","code":"from functools import total_ordering\n@total_ordering\nclass Rank:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Rank):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.value == other.value\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Rank):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.value < other.value\nprint(Rank(1) == 1)\ntry:\n    print(Rank(1) < 1)\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"unsupported ordering\")","output":"False\nunsupported ordering","explanation":["Returning NotImplemented lets Python try the other operand’s comparison behavior. Unsupported equality ultimately produces False here.","Ordering unrelated types has no valid implementation in this example, so Python raises TypeError rather than guessing an arbitrary ordering."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2},{"id":198,"title":"Why can UserDict be easier to customize than subclassing dict directly?","label":"Custom mappings with UserDict","category":"Classes & design","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ncollections.UserDict is a mutable mapping wrapper whose underlying dictionary is exposed as data. Its mapping operations are designed to work through overridable methods, making policies such as key normalization or assignment validation easier to express than relying on every built-in dict operation to call an overridden special method.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse UserDict when building an application mapping with a consistent normalization or validation policy. Define all relevant access paths so lookups, writes, deletions, and membership agree.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nThe data attribute is still directly accessible and can bypass your methods. A convenience wrapper is not a security boundary, and normalizing only writes does not automatically normalize every read.","summary":"UserDict wraps a dictionary with customizable mapping methods. Keep the key policy consistent across operations, and remember that direct access to data can bypass it.","examples":[{"title":"Normalize keys consistently","code":"from collections import UserDict\nclass LowerKeys(UserDict):\n    def __setitem__(self, key, value):\n        self.data[key.lower()] = value\n    def __getitem__(self, key):\n        return self.data[key.lower()]\n    def __delitem__(self, key):\n        del self.data[key.lower()]\n    def __contains__(self, key):\n        return key.lower() in self.data\nsettings = LowerKeys({\"MODE\": \"safe\"})\nsettings.update({\"LEVEL\": 2})\nprint(settings[\"Mode\"])\nprint(\"level\" in settings)\nprint(sorted(settings))","output":"safe\nTrue\n['level', 'mode']","explanation":["Initialization and update route assignments through the custom setter. Stored keys use lowercase, while the getter and membership method normalize requests.","Deletion is normalized as well, keeping this string-key mapping’s access policy consistent. Supporting arbitrary key types would require a different contract."]},{"title":"Validate values on assignment and update","code":"from collections import UserDict\nclass NonNegative(UserDict):\n    def __setitem__(self, key, value):\n        if value < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"negative value\")\n        self.data[key] = value\ncounts = NonNegative({\"a\": 2})\ntry:\n    counts.update({\"b\": -1})\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)\nprint(dict(counts))","output":"negative value\n{'a': 2}","explanation":["The update attempts to assign b through the custom setter. Validation raises before that individual value is stored in the underlying dictionary.","A multi-entry update is not automatically transactional: earlier successful assignments can remain if a later entry fails. Validate all inputs first when atomic updates are required."]},{"title":"Distinguish a wrapper policy from a boundary","code":"from collections import UserDict\nclass Positive(UserDict):\n    def __setitem__(self, key, value):\n        if value <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"must be positive\")\n        self.data[key] = value\nvalues = Positive({\"ok\": 1})\nvalues.data[\"bypass\"] = -3\nprint(values[\"bypass\"])\nprint(len(values))","output":"-3\n2","explanation":["Writing directly to data uses the underlying dictionary, bypassing the wrapper’s custom assignment method and its validation.","The example makes the limitation visible rather than recommending the bypass. Keep ownership and interfaces explicit when callers must respect a policy."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2},{"id":199,"title":"How does calendar handle leap years and month layouts without manual date arithmetic?","label":"Calendar calculations & month grids","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\ncalendar offers helpers for leap-year rules, month lengths, weekdays, and month grids. monthrange returns the weekday of the first day and the number of days in the month, with Monday numbered zero. Calendar objects can customize the first day of each displayed week. Zero entries in month-day grids represent padding outside the selected month.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse calendar for date-picker layouts, monthly reports, and Gregorian calendar calculations. Use datetime when representing a specific date or a timezone-aware instant.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nWeekday numbering and padding conventions must be read carefully. A calendar grid is a layout of dates, not a timezone conversion or a statement that all months contain the same number of weeks.","summary":"calendar supplies Gregorian month rules and display grids. Check weekday numbering, first-weekday settings, and zero padding when building a monthly view.","examples":[{"title":"Apply Gregorian leap-year rules","code":"import calendar\nprint(calendar.isleap(2000))\nprint(calendar.isleap(1900))\nprint(calendar.isleap(2024))","output":"True\nFalse\nTrue","explanation":["Years divisible by four are normally leap years, but century years must also be divisible by four hundred. The year 2000 qualifies while 1900 does not.","The helper applies these rules directly, avoiding an incomplete manual test that would incorrectly classify every year divisible by four."]},{"title":"Inspect a month boundary","code":"import calendar\nfirst_weekday, days = calendar.monthrange(2024, 2)\nprint(first_weekday)\nprint(first_weekday == calendar.THURSDAY)\nprint(days)","output":"3\nTrue\n29","explanation":["The first day of February 2024 is weekday index 3 because Monday is index 0. The explicit comparison confirms that index 3 denotes Thursday.","The month contains 29 days because 2024 is a leap year. The weekday index and day count are separate parts of the returned pair."]},{"title":"Choose the start of a calendar week","code":"import calendar\nmonday = calendar.Calendar(firstweekday=calendar.MONDAY)\nsunday = calendar.Calendar(firstweekday=calendar.SUNDAY)\nprint(monday.monthdayscalendar(2024, 2)[0])\nprint(sunday.monthdayscalendar(2024, 2)[0])","output":"[0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\n[0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3]","explanation":["The month begins on Thursday, so a Monday-first row needs three leading padding cells before day 1.","A Sunday-first row needs four leading padding cells. Zero means a cell outside the selected month, not an actual day numbered zero."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2},{"id":200,"title":"How does ElementTree read and construct a small XML document?","label":"XML trees with ElementTree","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nxml.etree.ElementTree represents XML elements as objects with tags, attributes, text, and child elements. fromstring parses an XML string, find and findall select elements using supported paths, and tostring serializes a tree. Namespace-qualified names use expanded notation with the namespace URI inside braces.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nUse ElementTree for small structured XML documents when its supported features fit the format. Treat external XML as potentially hostile and apply appropriate parser and resource protections for that input source.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nElementTree is not a full XPath engine. Also, a parsed tree is not a guarantee of schema validity, and application code should test an element against None when checking whether a lookup found anything.","summary":"ElementTree turns XML into nested elements. Read tags, attributes, and text explicitly, and use namespace-aware names for namespaced documents.","examples":[{"title":"Read an element and its attribute","code":"from xml.etree import ElementTree as ET\nroot = ET.fromstring('<library><book id=\"7\">Python</book></library>')\nbook = root.find(\"book\")\nif book is not None:\n    print(book.get(\"id\"))\n    print(book.text)\nprint(root.find(\"missing\") is None)","output":"7\nPython\nTrue","explanation":["find returns the matching element or None. The explicit identity check distinguishes a missing element from an element that happens to have no children.","Attributes are accessed separately from text. XML parsing establishes structure, while application rules decide whether an id or text value is acceptable."]},{"title":"Construct and escape text content","code":"from xml.etree import ElementTree as ET\nroot = ET.Element(\"note\")\nchild = ET.SubElement(root, \"text\", language=\"en\")\nchild.text = \"A & B < C\"\nserialized = ET.tostring(root, encoding=\"unicode\")\nprint(serialized)\nprint(ET.fromstring(serialized).findtext(\"text\"))","output":"<note><text language=\"en\">A &amp; B &lt; C</text></note>\nA & B < C","explanation":["Serializing the tree escapes reserved XML characters in the text content. Those escapes preserve the intended characters without creating markup.","Parsing the serialized tree restores the original text value. Use tree construction rather than string concatenation when inserting ordinary text into XML."]},{"title":"Look up a namespace-qualified element","code":"from xml.etree import ElementTree as ET\nroot = ET.fromstring('<root xmlns=\"urn:lab\"><item>one</item><item>two</item></root>')\nprint(root.find(\"item\") is None)\nprint([item.text for item in root.findall(\"p:item\", {\"p\": \"urn:lab\"})])","output":"True\n['one', 'two']","explanation":["The default XML namespace applies to each item, so the unqualified lookup does not match those namespace-qualified names.","The lookup prefix is a local alias for the namespace URI. It does not need to reproduce a prefix used in the original document."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2},{"id":201,"title":"How do HTML escaping and HTMLParser differ when handling text and markup?","label":"HTML escaping & parsing","category":"Python toolkit","source":"extension","body":"### Answer\n\nhtml.escape converts reserved characters in text to HTML character references, with optional quote escaping. html.unescape performs character-reference decoding. HTMLParser consumes HTML-like markup and sends events to handler methods for tags and text. Neither unescape nor a basic parser is an HTML sanitizer.\n\n### When is this useful?\n\nEscape ordinary text before inserting it into an HTML text context, and use a proper context-aware template system for complete pages. Use HTMLParser for controlled extraction from markup when its event model is sufficient.\n\n### Common mistake to avoid\n\nHTML escaping is not a universal encoding for JavaScript, CSS, or URL contexts. Parsing or removing a few tags does not establish that untrusted markup is safe to render.","summary":"Escape text when inserting it into HTML; parse markup when extracting structure. Decoding references and parsing tags do not sanitize untrusted HTML.","examples":[{"title":"Escape text and decode character references","code":"from html import escape, unescape\ntext = '<b title=\"x\">A & B</b>'\nencoded = escape(text)\nprint(encoded)\nprint(unescape(encoded) == text)","output":"&lt;b title=&quot;x&quot;&gt;A &amp; B&lt;/b&gt;\nTrue","explanation":["escape replaces the angle brackets, ampersand, and quotes so the supplied value can be represented as text rather than interpreted as those tags.","unescape reverses the character references for this value. Decoding is not sanitization and can restore markup characters that need escaping before later rendering."]},{"title":"Collect text events from markup","code":"from html.parser import HTMLParser\nclass TextCollector(HTMLParser):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.parts = []\n    def handle_data(self, data):\n        self.parts.append(data)\nparser = TextCollector()\nparser.feed(\"<p>Hello <b>Python</b> &amp; friends</p>\")\nparser.close()\nprint(\"\".join(parser.parts))","output":"Hello Python & friends","explanation":["The parser sends ordinary text segments to handle_data and decodes supported character references under its default behavior.","Joining the segments extracts this document’s text. The handler does not preserve layout or implement a browser’s complete rendering rules."]},{"title":"Inspect a tag attribute as data","code":"from html.parser import HTMLParser\nclass LinkCollector(HTMLParser):\n    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):\n        if tag == \"a\":\n            print(dict(attrs).get(\"href\"))\nparser = LinkCollector()\nparser.feed('<a href=\"/search?q=py&amp;page=2\">Search</a>')\nparser.close()","output":"/search?q=py&page=2","explanation":["The handler receives a tag name and attribute pairs. The encoded ampersand is decoded in the resulting href value.","Extracting a URL does not validate its scheme, host, or intended use. Apply separate URL and destination rules before treating extracted links as trusted."]}],"batch":"latest-10","minutes":2}];
